1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Sir Richard V Talbot of Richard's Castle
2 CONT * Born: 1278, Eccleswall Manor, Herefordshire, England
2 CONT * Died: Before 10 Oct 1328, Richard's Castle, Ludlow (Shrops), Herefordshire, England
2 CONT * Father: Sir Richard IV Talbot , of Eccleswall,  b: ABT 1250 in Linton Manor, Bromyard, Herefordshire, England 
2 CONT * Mother: Sarah de Beauchamp b: ABT 1250 in Elmley Castle, Worcestershire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Married
2 CONT #  Richard married Joan de Mortimer, Heiress of Richard's Castle, daughter of Hugh de Mortimer and Matilda, before 14 Feb 1315-1316.  Her 2nd husband: she m. 1st by 12 Aug 1305 Thomas de Bykenore (dsp c1316)
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan DE MORTIMER Heiress of Richard's Castle was born before 24 Nov 1291 in Caerphilly Castle, Cardiff, Glamorganshire, Wales and died before 12 Jan 1341-1342 in Richard's Castle, Ludlow (Shrops), Herefordshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT # John TALBOT , of Richard's Castle, Sir b: 29 SEP 1317 in Richard's Castle, Ludlow, Herefordshire, England.  Married Juliane de Gray
2 CONT #  Richard (John) TALBOT , Sir b: ABT 1319 in Richard's Castle, Ludlow, Herefordshire, England.  Married Margaret de HARDRESHULL
2 CONT # Thomas TALBOT Born: Abt 1321, Richard's Castle, Hereford, England
2 CONT
2 CONT ==comments==
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Talbot (m. Joan la Zouche), who thus acquired Richard's Castle, and d. by 12 Jan 1341/2, apparently leaving issue by him which died out by 1388. [Burke's Peerage, p. 3101]
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Talbot, a younger son, of Richard's Castle, Herefs, whose line appears to have expired on the death without issue of his great grandson in 1388. [Burke's Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Apparently Richard's ggs was a John Talbot, son of John & Catherine Talbot, who dsp 3 Jul 1388.
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations 
2 CONT # Title: Burke's Peerage & Baronetage, 106th Edition, Charles Mosley Editor-in-Chief, 1999 Page: 2604, 3101
2 CONT # Title: Burke's Peerage & Baronetage, 106th Edition, Charles Mosley Editor-in-Chief, 1999 Page: 2604
2 CONT # Title: Burke's Peerage & Baronetage, 106th Edition, Charles Mosley Editor-in-Chief, 1999 Page: 3101 Text: no date, 2nd husband
2 CONT
2 CONT LinksFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia  These were the paternal arms of Gwenllian, the daughter and heiress of Rhys Mechyll  (d.1244) (Prince of the Welsh House of Dinefwr, grandson of Rhys ap Gruffydd ), and wife of Gilbert T
2 CONC albot (d.1274), grandfather of Gilbert Talbot, 1st Baron Talbot(d.1345/6) assumed by Talbot as arms of alliance  of a great heiress, superseding his own former paternal arms of Bendy of 10 pieces argent and gules 
2 CONT
2 CONT Baron Talbot is a title that has been created twice. The title was created first in the Peerage of England. On 5 June 1331, Sir Gilbert Talbot was summoned to Parliament, by which he was held to have become Baron Talbot.  The titl
2 CONC e Lord Talbot, Baron of Hensol, in the County of Glamorgan, was created in the Peerage of Great Britain  in 1733 for Charles Talbot , a descendant of the John Talbot, 2nd Earl of Shrewsbury  (the 8th Baron of the first creation), 
2 CONC the Earl Talbot .  Barons Talbot (1331) Gilbert Talbot (1276-1346), Lord Chamberlain of the Household  to King Edward III, was summoned to Parliament as Lord Talbot in 1331, which is accepted as evidence of his baronial status at 
2 CONC that date.  Ancestry He was descended from Richard Talbot, a tenant in 1086 of Walter Giffard at Woburn and Battledsen in Bedfordshire. The Talbot family were vassals of the Giffards in Normandy. Hugh Talbot, probably his son, mad
2 CONC e a grant to Beaubec Abbey, confirmed by his son Richard Talbot in 1153. This Richard (d. 1175) is listed in 1166 as holding three fees of the Honour of Giffard in Buckinghamshire. He also held a fee at Linton in Herefordshire, fo
2 CONC r which his son Gilbert Talbot (d. 1231) obtained a fresh charter in 1190. Gilbert's grandson Gilbert (d. 1274) married Gwenlynn Mechyll, daughter and sole heiress of the Welsh Prince Rhys Mechyll , whose armorials the Talbots the
2 CONC nceforth assumed in lieu of their own former arms. Their son Sir Richard Talbot, who signed and sealed[6] the Barons' Letter, 1301  held the manor of Eccleswall  in Herefordshire  in right of his wife Sarah, sister of William de B
2 CONC eauchamp, 9th Earl of Warwick. In 1331 Richard's son Gilbert Talbot (1276-1346) was summoned to Parliament, which is considered evidence of his baronial status. Succession The first baron's grandson, the 3rd Baron Talbot, died in 
2 CONC Spain supporting John of Gaunt 's claim to the throne of Castile . Richard, the fourth Baron, married Ankaret, 7th Baroness Strange of Blackmere , daughter and heiress of John le Strange, 4th Baron Strange of Blackmere. In 1387, d
2 CONC uring his father's lifetime, Richard 4th Baron was summoned to Parliament as Ricardo Talbot de Blackmere in right of his wife. His son [Gilbert], the fifth Baron, also succeeded his mother as eighth Baron Strange of Blackmere.  On
2 CONC  the early death of the 5th Baron, the titles passed to his daughter, Ankaret, the sixth and ninth holder of the titles. However, she died a minor and was succeeded by her uncle, John seventh Baron Talbot. John married Maud Nevill
2 CONC , 6th Baroness Furnivall, and, in 1409, he was summoned to Parliament in right of his wife as Johann Talbot de Furnyvall. In 1442 John was created Earl of Shrewsbury  in the Peerage of England and in 1446 Earl of Waterford in the 
2 CONC Peerage of Ireland .  Barons Talbot (1733) The title was created in 1733 when Charles Talbot  was raised to the Peerage of Great Britain  as Lord Talbot, Baron of Hensol, in the County of Glamorgan. He was eldest the son of Willia
2 CONC m Talbot , Bishop of Oxford , of Salisbury  and of Durham  and a descendant of Sir Gilbert Talbot (died 1518), third son of John Talbot, 2nd Earl of Shrewsbury .  The title fell into abeyance between the three daughters of Gilbert
2 CONC  Talbot, 7th Earl of Shrewsbury  until the deaths of two of them without issue.  List of titleholders Barons Talbot (1331) 	Gilbert Talbot, 1st Baron Talbot  (1276-1346)  	Richard Talbot, 2nd Baron Talbot  (c. 1305-1356)  	Gilbert
2 CONC  Talbot, 3rd Baron Talbot  (c. 1332-1387)  	Richard Talbot, 4th Baron Talbot  (c. 1361-1396)  	Gilbert Talbot, 5th Baron Talbot, 8th Baron Strange of Blackmere  (c.1383-1419)  	Ankaret Talbot, 6th Baroness Talbot, 9th Baroness Str
2 CONC ange of Blackmere  (d. 1421)  	John Talbot, 1st Earl of Shrewsbury, 1st Earl of Waterford, 7th Baron Talbot, 10th Baron Strange of Blackmere  (1387-1453) (created Earl of Shrewsbury in 1442)  	John Talbot, 2nd Earl of Shrewsbury, 
2 CONC 2nd Earl of Waterford, 8th Baron Talbot, 11th Baron Strange of Blackmere, 7th Baron Furnivall  (1413-1460)  	John Talbot, 3rd Earl of Shrewsbury, 3rd Earl of Waterford, 9th Baron Talbot, 12th Baron Strange of Blackmere, 8th Baron 
