2 CONT ---------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Madog ap Maredudd
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia - http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madog_ap_Maredudd
2 CONT
2 CONT Madog ap Maredudd (died 1160) was the last Prince of the entire Kingdom of Powys and for a time held the Fitzalan Lordship of Oswestry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Madog was the son of Maredudd ap Bleddyn and grandson of Bleddyn ap Cynfyn. He followed his father on the throne of Powys in 1132. He is recorded as taking part in the Battle of Lincoln in 1141 in support of the Earl of Chester, a
2 CONC long with Owain Gwynedd's brother Cadwaladr ap Gruffydd and a large army of Welshmen.  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Lincoln_(1141)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1149 he is recorded giving the commote of Cyfeiliog to his nephews Owain Cyfeiliog and his brother Meurig. The same year Madog was able to seize Oswestry from William Fitzalan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Defeat by Gwynedd
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time the King of Gwynedd, between 1149 to 1150, Owain Gwynedd was exerting pressure on the borders of Powys, despite the fact that Madog was married to Susanna, Owain's sister. Madog made an alliance with Ranulf, Earl of C
2 CONC hester, but Owain defeated them at Coleshill in 1150 and took possession of Madog's lands in Iâl (English="Yale"]. In 1157 when King Henry II of England invaded Gwynedd he was supported by Madog, who was able to regain many of his
2 CONC  lands, though he had to return the Lordship of Oswestry to William Fitz Alan. Madog died in 1160, and was buried in the church of St. Tysilio at Meifod, the mother church of Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Succession Shared
2 CONT
2 CONT Madog's eldest son, Llywelyn, was killed soon after his father's death and Powys was shared between a number of sons and nephews. Powys was never subsequently reunited, being separated into two parts Powys Fadog and Powys Wenwynwy
2 CONC n. Madog's death enabled Owain Gwynedd to annex part of northern Powys. The poet Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr in his elegy on Madog said:
2 CONT
2 CONT     While Madog lived there was no man
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dared ravage his fair borders
2 CONT
2 CONT     Yet nought of all he held
2 CONT
2 CONT     Esteemed he his save by God's might ...
2 CONT
2 CONT     If my noble lord were alive
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwynedd would not now be encamped in the heart of Edeyrnion 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mabinogion
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mabinogion tale The Dream of Rhonabwy is set during Madog's reign. The central character, Rhonabwy, is one of Madog's retainers sent to bring in Madog's rebellious brother Iorwerth. His titular dream contrasts his own time wit
2 CONC h the grandeur of King Arthur's period.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Llywelyn ap Madog
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Gruffydd Maelor
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Gwenllian ferch Madog, married Rhys ap Gruffydd, prince of Deheubarth
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Marared ferch Madog, married Iorwerth Drwyndwn and was the mother of Llywelyn the Great
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Gantz, Jeffrey (translator) (1987). The Mabinogion, pp. 177–191. New York: Penguin. ISBN 0-14-044322-3.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * John Edward Lloyd (1911). A history of Wales: from the earliest times to the Edwardian conquest. Longmans, Green & Co. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Line 176B-26
2 CONT
2 CONT Madog ap Maredudd b abt 1091, Powys, Wales, d 1160, Winchester, Hampshire, England. He md Susanna verch Gruffydd abt 1130, daughter of Gruffydd ap Cynan and Angharad verch Owain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Madog ap Maredudd and Susanna verch Gruffydd were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd-Maelor ap Madog, Lord of Bromfield, b abt 1146.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marared verch Madog b abt 1150, Aberffraw Castle, Anglesey, Wales. She md Iorwerth-Drwyndwn ap Owain abt 1168, son of Owain-Gwynedd ap Gruffydd and Gwladys verch Llywarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Madog III ap Maredudd, Tywysog Powys1,2,3,4
2 CONT
2 CONT b. circa 1091, d. 1160
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	Maredudd I ap Bleddyn, Tywysog Powys5,2 b. 1047, d. 1132
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother	Hunydd ferch Einudd of Dyffrin Clwyd6,2 b. circa 1066
2 CONT
2 CONT      Madog III ap Maredudd, Tywysog Powys was the successor of Maredudd I ap Bleddyn, Tywysog Powys; Prince of Powys.7,8 Madog III ap Maredudd, Tywysog Powys was born circa 1091 at Montgomeryshire, Wales. He was the son of Maredud
2 CONC d I ap Bleddyn, Tywysog Powys and Hunydd ferch Einudd of Dyffrin Clwyd.5,2,6 Madog III ap Maredudd, Tywysog Powys married Susanna ferch Gruffudd ap Cynan o Gwynedd, daughter of Gryffydd ap Cynan, King of Gwynedd and Angharad ferch
2 CONC  Owain of Tegeingl, before 1129.9 Prince of Powys Fadog at Wales between 1132 and 1160.2,10 Chronicle of Ystrad Fflur 1160: "In this year Madog ap Maredudd, prince of Powys died at Winchester."11 Madog III ap Maredudd, Tywysog Pow
2 CONC ys died in 1160 at Winchester, Hampshire, England. "Madoc Powysorum princeps obiit."9,2,12,13 He was the predecessor of Llywelyn ap Madog, Prince of Powys; Prince of North Powys.14
2 CONT
