1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Children: 1. Gilbert Basset 2. Thomas Basset 3. Fulke Basset 4. Katherine Basset b: Abt 1190 5. Aline (Aliva) Basset b: Bef 1200 6. Philip Basset b: Abt 1184 in Wycombe, Buckshire, England 7. Hawise Basset b: Bef 1
2 CONC 217
2 CONT
2 CONT ====FMG: English Lords [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3.htm#AlanBassetdied1231B A–C]
2 CONT * '''ALAN Basset''', son of THOMAS Basset [I] of Headington, Oxfordshire & his wife Alice de Dunstanville (-before 30 Sep 1230).  "Thomas Bass" granted "manerium meum de Comptun cum terra de Berewic" to "Alano filio meo" by charte
2 CONC r dated to [1180/82?], witnessed by "…Waltero de Dunstanvile, Alano de Dunstanvile, Fulcone Basset…Thoma Basset juvene…"[473].  "Thomas Basset", with the consent of "Gilleberti primogeni filii mei", granted "Cumtonam…cuius medieta
2 CONC s fuit maritagium matris mee, altera…medietatis…fuit maritagium uxoris mee matris eorum" to "Alano filio meo fratri suo" by charter dated to [1180/82?], witnessed by "…Walterus de Dunstanvill, Alanus de Dunstanvill, Fulco Basset…T
2 CONC homas Basset juvenis…"[474]. The Roll of Honour dated [1186/87] records that "Gilberto Basset…Alano Basset" held land in the honour of Walingford[475]. Military fee certifications in the Red Book of the Exchequer, in 1166, record 
2 CONC that "Alanus Basset" held one knight fee "in Wokinges et Mapeldorwelle…ex dono Regis Ricardi" in Surrey, and one knight fee in Wycombe, Berkshire[476]. This entry must be redated to after 1189 if it is correct that the knight fees
2 CONC  were granted by King Richard I. King John confirmed that “Waltero de Dunstanvill” had granted the manor of Winterbourne to “Alan Basset”, under a charter confirmed by King Richard I, by charter dated 10 Apr 1200[477].  The Red Bo
2 CONC ok of the Exchequer records "Alanus Basset" holding "Berwike…de feodo Adæ de Port" and two knights’ fees "de honore Walingforde…in Wottone" in Wiltshire, and land "in Wokinges" in Surrey, in [1210/12][478]. Henry III King of Engla
2 CONC nd ordered "Petro de Maulay" to release "…Alanum Basset" from custody dated 21 Dec 1216[479]. A charter dated 1217 records that "Alanum Basset" granted the manor of Yarlington, Somerset to "Isobel quondam uxorem Willelmi de Monte 
2 CONC Acuto" [as her dower] by charter dated 1217, witnessed by "Gilberto Basset, Warino Basset…"[480]. Alan presumably died before 30 Sep 1230, the date of the grant to his son Gilbert cited below. 
2 CONT * ['''m''' firstly [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3D-K.htm#AliceGreyMAlanBasset '''ALICE de Grey'''], daughter of --- (-before 15 Apr 1206). William Reedy, in the genealogical tables in the introduction t
2 CONC o his collection of Basset charters, names "Alice de Gray" as the first wife of Alan Basset and mother of the sons shown below[481]. He does not cite the primary source which confirms that this is correct.  Alice has not been conn
2 CONC ected with the Grey families. The similarity between “Alice de Gray” and “Aline de Gai” (Alan’s supposed second wife) is striking. Unless another source emerges which confirms the separate identity of Alan’s two supposed wives, it
2 CONC  appears likely that he had only a single wife. In any case, the chronology of Alan’s sons, in particular the likely dates of the marriages of Gilbert and Philip, suggests that Aline de Gai (first named in 1206, see below) was the
2 CONC ir mother[482].
2 CONT * '''m [secondly] (before 16 Apr 1206, [1203 or before?]) ALINE de Gai, daughter of ---.''' A charter dated 16 Apr 1206 records a final settlement between "Thomam filium Ricardi et Aliciam uxorem suam" and "Alanum Basset et Alinam
2 CONC  uxorem suam" relating to one knight’s fee in Broad Town, Wiltshire[483]. William Reedy names her “Aline de Gai”, as mother of the two older daughters named below (no source cited)[484]. '''''The primary source which confirms her 
2 CONC family origin has not been identified.''''' The Victoria County History of Buckinghamshire records that King John granted Wycombe in Buckinghamshire to Alan Basset (with Robert Vipont) in 1203 and notes that “this grant represents
2 CONC  the manor of Bassets Bury, which was held of the honour of Wallingford by one knight’s fee”[485]. The grant is also recorded (as Nova Feudamenta) in the Testa de Nevill, dated to 1212[486]. The Testa de Nevill (1212) also include
2 CONC s "Alanus Basset de hereditate uxoris sue ii milites" in its list of tenants of the honour of Wallingford[487]. The reference to two knights’ fees indicates that this was a different land holding. Nevertheless, the apparent connec
2 CONC tion between Wallingford and Alan’s wife suggests that she may also have held some undefined interest in Wycombe. If that is correct, the couple may have married in 1203 or before. 
2 CONT * '''Alan & his [first/second] wife had five children:''' 
2 CONT ** 1. '''THOMAS Basset''' (-before 1230). King Henry III granted "terram quam Thomas Basset frater ipsius Gilberti habuit in Kertlinton" to “Gilberto Basset...fratri et heredi ipsius Thome” dated 1230[488]. "Gilbert Basset" was gr
2 CONC anted "land of Kertlinton…as the king had granted it to Thomas Basset, brother of the said Gilbert" dated 4 May 1235[489]. 
2 CONT ** 2. '''GILBERT Basset''' [II] (-31 Jul 1241). King Henry III granted "terram quam Thomas Basset frater ipsius Gilberti habuit in Kertlinton" to “Gilberto Basset...fratri et heredi ipsius Thome” dated 1230[490]. "Gilbert Basset" 
2 CONC was granted "rent which Alan Basset his [father] used to pay yearly to the exchequer", dated 30 Sep 1230[491].  The Victoria County History of Buckinghamshire records that Alan’s son Gilbert paid a fine in 1231 for entering posses
2 CONC sion of his father’s lands[492]. "Gilbert Basset" was granted "land of Kertlinton…as the king had granted it to Thomas Basset, brother of the said Gilbert" dated 4 May 1235[493]. Bracton records a claim, dated 1234/35, by "Gregori
2 CONC us de la Dun" against "Giliberto Basset" concerning an agreement with "Alanum Basset patrem eiusdem Giliberti"[494]. By notice dated 18 Jun 1240, “G. Marshal, earl of Pembroke” surrendered “the castle of Pevenese and all the land 
2 CONC late of G. de Aquila” to the king, except “the town of Greywell given…as a marriage portion to Gilbert Basset with Isabel, daughter of William de Ferrariis, the earl’s niece…”[495]. “William de Ferr[ariis]” granted an option to ha
2 CONC ve “the manor of Mildehale”, in exchange for “Pery manor”, at the end of four years to “Gilbert Basset and his heirs by Isabella, William’s daughter”, dated “Tuesday after Holy Trinity, 25 Henry [III]” [28 May 1241][496]. Matthew 
2 CONC Paris names "…Gilbertus Basset, cujus casum sinistrum præscripsimus…" among those who died in 1241[497].  In the earlier passage, Matthew Paris described that the accident while hunting of "prædictus G[ilebertus] Basset…in Autumno
2 CONC " which “dissipatis ossibus et nervis dissolutis" such that he died a few days later, noting also that afterwards “filius ejusdem G[ileberti] unicus et hæres, parvulus” died “in octavis Assumptionis Sanctæ Mariæ”, following which 
2 CONC “Fulconem Basset, decanum Eboracensem, fratri dicti G[ileberti]” inherited[498]. '''m''' (before 18 Jun 1240) as her first husband, [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL1.htm#IsabelFerrersMReynoldMohun ''
2 CONC 'ISABEL de Ferrers'''], daughter of WILLIAM de Ferrers Earl of Derby & his first wife Sibyl Marshall of the Earls of Pembroke (-before 26 Nov 1260). By notice dated 18 Jun 1240, “G. Marshal, earl of Pembroke” surrendered “the cast
2 CONC le of Pevenese and all the land late of G. de Aquila” to the king, except “the town of Greywell given…as a marriage portion to Gilbert Basset with Isabel, daughter of William de Ferrariis, the earl’s niece…”[499]....Gilbert & his 
2 CONC wife had one child:...
2 CONT ** 3. '''FULK Basset''' (-1259). The Victoria County History of Buckinghamshire records that Fulk “Dean of York” was heir of Gilbert Basset [II][504], who was presumably his next surviving oldest brother: Matthew Paris, after desc
2 CONC ribing Gilbert Basset’s hunting accident (see above), records that afterwards “filius ejusdem G[ileberti] unicus et hæres, parvulus” died “in octavis Assumptionis Sanctæ Mariæ”, following which “Fulconem Basset, decanum Eboracense
2 CONC m, fratri dicti G[ileberti]” inherited[505].''' Dean of York. Bishop of London 1241'''. A charter of King Edward II records that “Philippus Basset miles” confirmed the foundation of Bicester priory, Oxfordshire, for the souls of "
2 CONC …Fulconis Basset quondam Londoniensis episcopi fratris mei"[506]. 
2 CONT ** 4. '''WARIN Basset''' (-Cardiff Castle 1233). A charter dated 1217 records that "Alanum Basset" granted the manor of Yarlington, Somerset to "Isobel quondam uxorem Willelmi de Monte Acuto" [as her dower] by charter dated 1217, 
2 CONC witnessed by "Gilberto Basset, Warino Basset…"[507].  Henry III King of England appointed judges in a claim by "Warinum Basset et Katerinam uxorem eius" and "Mauricium de Borham tenentem de…terre…in Heles et…in Bradeford" dated 18
2 CONC  Apr 1229[508].  A charter dated 6 Apr 1231 records a final settlement between "Warinum Basset et Katerinam uxorem eius" and the dean of Exeter relating to the advowson of the church of Veryan which they had "ex dono Johannis de M
2 CONC onte Acuto patris predicte Katerine"[509].  Matthew Paris records the death in 1233 of "Warinus Basset in obsidione et insultu castri de Kaerdif”[510].  '''m [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3L-O.htm#Kather
2 CONC ineMontaguMWarinBasset KATHARINE de Montagu]''', daughter of JOHN de Montagu & his wife Lucy --- (25 Nov — -after 6 Apr 1231). Bracton notes a claim, dated 1226, by "Willelmo de Monte Acuto fratri eiusdem Johannis" against "Johann
2 CONC es de Monte Acuto et Lucia uxor eius" claiming that "garciam Katerinam…filiam et heredem eorum" was not the daughter of John and his wife, while the bishop of Winchester stated that Lucy’s daughter was born "in vigilia S. Katerine
2 CONC , unde vocata fuit Katerina"[511]. Henry III King of England appointed judges in a claim by "Warinum Basset et Katerinam uxorem eius" and "Mauricium de Borham tenentem de…terre…in Heles et…in Bradeford" dated 18 Apr 1229[512].  Br
2 CONC acton records a claim, dated 1229, by "Warinus Basset et Katerina uxor eius" against "abbatem de Gresten" concerning "terre…in Merse" of which "Johannes […de Monte Acuto] pater ipsius Katerine" was seised[513]. A charter dated 6 A
2 CONC pr 1231 records a final settlement between "Warinum Basset et Katerinam uxorem eius" and the dean of Exeter relating to the advowson of the church of Veryan which they had "ex dono Johannis de Monte Acuto patris predicte Katerine"
2 CONC [514]. 
2 CONT ** 5. '''PHILIP Basset''' (-29 Oct 1271, bur Stanley, Wilts). William Reedy names Philip as son of Alan by his first wife[515].  However, the name Aline given by Philip to his oldest daughter suggests that Aline de Gai may have be
2 CONC en his mother. The suggested birth date of Philip’s daughter Aline also suggests that her father would have been born after 1206 when Alan’s wife Aline is first named (see above). The Victoria County History of Buckinghamshire rec
2 CONC ords that Philip Basset “appears to have acquired an interest in Wycombe...for during Fulk’s tenure of the manor his name appears in various suits concerning small portions of land in the parish”[516].  "Philip Basset son of Alan 
2 CONC Basset" was granted "land in Saham…", dated 9 Mar 1235[517].  A charter of King Edward II records that “Philippus Basset miles” confirmed the foundation of Bicester priory, Oxfordshire, for the souls of "…Fulconis Basset quondam L
2 CONC ondoniensis episcopi fratris mei"[518]. A writ dated 6 Nov "55 Hen III", after the death of "Philip Basset", names "Aline his daughter, wife of Roger Bygod earl of Norfolk, marshal of England, late the wife of Hugh le Despensir, a
2 CONC ge variously stated as 22 and more, 24 and more, 26 and 30 and more, is the heir", adding that Philip died "on the morrow of SS Simon and Jude last"[519]. '''m''' firstly [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BRABANT,%20LOUVAIN.htm#Ha
2 CONC wiseLovainMPhilipBasset '''HAWISE de Lovaine'''] of Little Easton, Essex, daughter of MATHIEU de Lovaine & his wife Muriel ---. A writ dated 6 Nov "55 Hen III", after the death of "Philip Basset", records "Wykes manor in the hundr
2 CONC ed of Tendring [Essex]…of the inheritance of Helewise his wife, in chief of Matthew de Luviayn"[520]. '''m''' secondly ([25 Nov 1254/23 Mar 1255]) as her second husband, [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIE
2 CONC VAL1.htm#ElaLOngespeedied12971298 '''ELA Longespee'''], widow of THOMAS de Warwick Earl of Warwick, daughter of WILLIAM Longespee Earl of Salisbury & his wife Ela of Salisbury (-9 Feb 1298, bur Oseney Abbey, Oxfordshire).  The Boo
2 CONC k of Lacock names “Isabellam de Vescy…Elam…Idam de Camyle” as the daughters of “Guillelmus Longespe ex…Ela”, adding that Ela married “Comes Warwik, et postea Philippus Basset” but was childless[521].  Pope Innocent IV issued a dis
2 CONC pensation to “Ela de Warwick of the diocese of Worcester to contract marriage with Philip son of Alan knight who is connected with her in the third degree of affinity”, dated 13 Nov 1254[522].  Pope Alexander IV issued an indult t
2 CONC o “Philip knight son of Alan deceased of the diocese of London and his wife Ela de Warwec...to remain in the marriage they have contracted notwithstanding that they are related in the fourth degree of consanguinity”, dated 23 Mar 
2 CONC 1255[523]. A writ dated 6 Nov "55 Hen III", after the death of "Philip Basset", records "Clinton Aston manor [Buckingham] held in exchange from William Muntagu for the life of the said Philip and Lady Ella his wife"[524]. The Chro
2 CONC niculum of Geoffrey le Baker of Swinbrook records the death in 1300 of “domina Ela comitissa Warwici” and her burial “Oseneye”[525]. Philip & his first wife had two children:...
2 CONT * '''Alan & his [second] wife had three children:''' 
2 CONT ** 6. '''ALINE Basset'''. The Testa de Nevill includes a list of landholdings in Dorset, dated 1219, which includes "Aluina que fuit uxor Drogonis de Monte Acuto est de donatione domini regis", adding that "est in custodia Alani B
2 CONC asset per Regem Johannem et est maritanda et terra sua in Pideltun valet xx.l"[529].  "Alan Basset" made a fine "by Richard Talbot" for marrying "Aline who was the wife of Drogo de Montagu…without the king’s licence", dated [Oct] 
2 CONC 1224[530]. '''m''' firstly [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3L-O.htm#DreuxMontagudied1216 '''DREUX de Montagu'''], son of WILLIAM de Montagu & his wife Isabel --- (-before 1219). '''m''' secondly ( [1219/24
2 CONC ] ) [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3T-Z.htm#RichardTalbotdied1234 '''RICHARD''' (V) '''Talbot'''] of Linton, son of GILBERT Talbot of Linton & his wife --- (-before 13 Apr 1234). 
2 CONT ** 7. '''--- Basset''' (-[after 11 May 1226]). William Reedy, in the introduction to his collection of Basset charters, states that Alan Basset paid a fine to marry his daughter to the son and heir of William de Lanvalay in [1212/
2 CONC 14][531]. It does not appear that this marriage was completed.  [Betrothed ([1212/14]) [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/enguntlo.htm#LanavalayMdauAlanBasset '''WILLIAM''' (IV) '''de Lanvalay'''], son of WILLIAM [II] de Lanvalay &
2 CONC  his wife Hawise de Bocland ([after 1190]-[1214/18 May 1216])]. 
2 CONT ** 8. ['''KATHERINE'''] '''Basset'''. The Complete Peerage records her parentage and marriage, noting that Philpot calls her Katherine and “has notes of a grant by their son John for the souls of Alan Basset and Aline his wife”[53
2 CONC 2]. It is not known whether this daughter was the same as the daughter betrothed earlier to William de Lanvalay (see above). '''m''' (before Aug 1216) '''JOHN Lovel''', son of WILLIAM Lovel & his wife Isabel --- (-before 23 Dec 12
2 CONC 52).
