1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * G E Cokayne. ''The Complete Peerage'', new edition, Vol. VIII, St Catherine Press 1932, p. 214, LOVEL - her first name is identified in footnotej
2 CONT * Anderson, James.'' A Genealogical History of The House of Yvery'' (H. Woodfall, 1742) Vol. 1, https://www.google.com/books/edition/_/RyoAAAAAQAAJ?hl=en&newbks=1&gbpv=1&pg=PA24&printsec=frontcover Google Booksl p. 248].
2 CONT * ''Oxford Dictionary of National Biography'', Online entry for 'Basset, Alan', print and online 2004, revised online 2008, available online through some libraries
2 CONT
2 CONT ===="Marriage Date of Sir Alan Basset and Aline de Gay" [https://groups.google.com/g/soc.genealogy.medieval/c/BxYZJ4pAmkc/m/ZyYPqzL_6yEJ Google Groups] – 
2 CONT >Douglas Richardson royalancestry@msn.com
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >Dear Newsgroup ~
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >I had a chance today to briefly examine William T. Reedy's good work, Basset Charters c. 1120 to 1250 (Pubs. Pipe Roll Soc. n.s 50) (1995). Specifically, I examined his comments regarding the marriage of Sir Alan Basset, of Wycom
2 CONC be, Buckinghamshire, and his wife, Aline de Gay. I also looked at some of the charters involving the Gay family inheritance.
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >On page 164, Mr. Reedy states that Alan Basset "was married to his first wife Alice into at least Easter term 1205 ... He married Aline de Gay sometime before 16 April 1206." Mr. Reedy cites as his evidence for these dates and ma
2 CONC rriages two references to Curia Regis Rolls, which items read as follows:
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >Date: Easter term 1205 Surr'.- Gillebertus de Abbingewrth' Gillebertus de Baseville Willelmus Malbanc Adam de Aldeham, missi ad sciendum quem Alicia uxor Alani Basset vellet ponere loco suo versus Thomam filium Ricardi de placito
2 CONC  feodi unius militis in Bradeton', dicunt quod posuit loco sio Alanum virum suum vel Walterum Clericum etc. [Reference: Curia Regis Rolls, 3 (1926): 274].
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >Date: 1206 Wilt'. - Alina uxor Alani Basset ponit loco suo Alanum virum suum vel Matheum Brand' versus Thomam filium Ricardi et Aliciam uxorem suam de placito terre in Lasbradeton' etc.; et Alicia ponit loco suo Thomas virum suum
2 CONC . [Reference: ''Curia Regis Rolls'', 4 (1929): 114].
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >We see above the first mention of the suit dated 1205 which indicates that "Alice" wife of Alan Basset appoints her husband Alan or Walter a priest her attorneys in a plea against Thomas Fitz Richard involving one knight's fee in
2 CONC  Broadtown, Willtshire. The following year, in what is surely a continuation of the same suit, "Aline" wife of Alan Basset appoints Alan her husband or Matthew Brand to represent her in a plea of land in Broadtown, Wiltshire again
2 CONC st Thomas Fitz Richard and Alice his wife. There can be no question that Alan Basset's wife in both parts of this lawsuit is his wife, Aline de Gay. We can be sure of this as Broadtown, Wiltshire was part of Aline de Gay's inherit
2 CONC ance. Also, the same defendant, Thomas Fitz Richard, in named in both records.
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >Elsewhere, on page 163, I find Mr. Reedy has unwittingly provided evidence which gives us a more accurate dating of Alan and Aline's marriage. This evidence is found in a renunciation document issued by Stephen prior and the conv
2 CONC ent of Monkton Farleigh, Wiltshire of their claims against Alan Basset concerning the advowson of the church of Wootton Bassett, Wiltshire. Mr. Reedy has correctly dated this document as being sometime before 21 April 1191, when P
2 CONC rior Stephen's successor was deposed [Reference: Knowles, Brooke, and London, Heads of Religious Houses, pg. 120]. Since Wootton Bassett, Wiltshire was part of Aline de Gay's inheritance, this record is good evidence that Sir Alan
2 CONC  Basset and his wife, Aline de Gay, were married sometime before 21 April 1191. Alan Basset would have no interest in the advowson of the church of Wootton Bassett, except through his wife, Aline.
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >From his comments in his notes, Mr. Reedy seems to suppose that Wootton Bassett, Wiltshire was part of Alan Basset's inheritance from his father. On this point, Reedy is again mistaken. Wootton Bassett, Wiltshire (which was known
2 CONC  simply as Wootton in this time period) was definitely part of Aline de Gay's inheritance. I've previously posted a document which associates Aline's widowed mother, Cecily, with Wootton, Wiltshire in this same approximate time pe
2 CONC riod. Besides Wootton Bassett, Wiltshire, Aline de Gay's inheritance included as stated above Broadtown, Wiltshire, as well as Northbrook (in Kirklington), Oxfordshire (the latter property fell to Aline on the death of her sister,
2 CONC  Cecily, sometime after 1190).
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >I believe the above discussion demonstrates the importance of tracing property ownership in both male and female lines when attempting to assemble a reliable account of a medieval baronial family. In this instance, Mr. Reedy made
2 CONC  several assumptions based on inadequate or bad information regarding the descent of Gay family estates. This in turn caused him to make some erroneous statements regarding Sir Alan Basset and his wife, Aline de Gay.
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >For evidence that Alan Basset was a knight, see Agnes M. Leys, Sandford ''Cartulary (Oxfordshire'' Rec. Soc. 19) (1938): 99-100 (charter dated c. 1217 witnessed by "domino Alan Basseth").
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >Best always, Douglas Richardson, Salt Lake City, Utah
2 CONT >Website: www.royalancestry.net
