2 CONT Isabel Mauduit married William de Beauchamp Baron of Elmley Castle, Earl of Warwick, son of Walter de Beauchamp and Joane de Mortimer 748.,749 (William de Beauchamp Baron of Elmley Castle, Earl of Warwick was born about 1217-1218 
2 CONC in Elmley Castle, Gloucester, England and died 7-9 Jun 1298 in England 748,985.)
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT Cookhill Priory was a Cistercian nunnery near Cookhill in Worcestershire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT History
2 CONT See also: History of Worcestershire
2 CONT The Priory is believed to be founded by Isabel de Mauduit, wife of William de Beauchamp, 9th Earl of Warwick in 1260, but it most likely dates to some years before then.[1] It is on record that she was buried at Cookhill when she 
2 CONC died, and that she had become a nun there by the time of his death in 1298. A tomb with a broken dedication was still present in the chapel in seventeenth century.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Priory was noted for its poverty and repeatedly exempted from taxation. Volume 2 of the History of the County of Worcester says:
2 CONT
2 CONT The poverty of the house of Cookhill is indeed almost the chief feature of its known history. Almost every reference to the nuns is to speak of their poverty, to exempt them with other slenderly endowed houses from payment of any 
2 CONC extraordinary taxation or to grant them respite for the arrears already owing to the king.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The numbers of nuns present was small, probably around seven, as at the time of dissolution. The Priory was dissolved in 1540, two or three years later than most, and the nuns given pensions.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is now a Grade II* listed building[2] and occupied by a recording studio, VADA Studios.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In February 2008, it was filmed for the 2008 British horror film The Children. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Burials
2 CONT Isabel de Mauduit, wife of William (III) de Beauchamp
2 CONT Tax dispute
2 CONT The tax status of the property at its sale in 2001 after the death of Mrs Rosemary Antrobus made for two legal cases, known as Antrobus I and Antrobus II. These have influenced the current understanding of the definition of farm p
2 CONC roperties for agricultural property relief. HMRC agreed that the property was a farmhouse, but that it was not entitled to be viewed as a solely agricultural building.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Willis-Bund & Page 1971, pp. 156–158
2 CONT  Cookhill Priory, British Listed Buildings
2 CONT  "Main page". Vada Recording Studios. Retrieved 12 May 2018.
2 CONT  "Tom Shankland, Cookhill Priory, Warwickshire". 12 February 2008.
2 CONT  Butler 2016, pp. 77–79
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Willis-Bund, J W; Page, William, eds. (1971). "Houses of Cistercian nuns: Priory of Cookhill". A History of the County of Worcester: Volume 2. London: Victoria County History. pp. 156–158.
2 CONT Butler, Julie (2016). Tax Planning for Farm and Land Diversification. Bloomsbury Publishing. ISBN 978-1780439792.
2 CONT Pevsner, Nikolaus; Brookes, Alan (2007), "Cookhill", Worcestershire, The Buildings of England (Revised ed.), London: Yale University Press, pp. 238–239, ISBN 9780300112986, OL 10319229Mwww.findagrave.com
2 CONT
