2 CONT Child John de BEAUCHAMP Born: abt 1331 shown is born after Walter's death! 
2 CONT
2 CONT "However, generous endowments were given to the two younger sons, Walter and John: Walter was granted lands in south-west Warwickshire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter was also to follow in the family's tradition of administrative service; in Prestwich's words he was ‘well schooled in the established tradition of the household’ and was a highly suitable choice for the post of steward of t
2 CONC he royal household, an appointment which suited both his bureaucratic and military skills. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter was appointed as steward in 1289, became sole steward in 1292, and held this position until his death in early 1303. He served with the king in Flanders and Scotland, fighting alongside Edward in the battle of Falkirk and a
2 CONC ppears to have been a man much admired for his military prowess, but criticised for his arrogance; the Song of Caerlaverock describes Walter as ‘a knight who would have been one of the best of all, according to my opinion, if he h
2 CONC ad not been too proud and rashly insolent, but you won't hear anyone talk of the steward without a "but"’." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Walter Beauchamp was the son of William Beauchamp and Isabel Mauduit.1 He was born before 1287. He died in 1303.3 
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived at Alcester, Warwickshire, England.3 He lived at Powick, Worcestershire, England.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Sir Walter Beauchamp: Giles Beauchamp+ d. Oct 1361 
2 CONT
