1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==concerns==
2 CONT '''Stirnet's "Talbot01" page (link below) does not mention this John's marriage to Juliane de Grey.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT attested in The History of Parliament's article on his son-in-law Ralph Parles
2 CONT
2 CONT ==links==
2 CONT * https://stirnet.com/genie/data/british/tt/talbot01.php#son1 (membership required in order to view without interruption)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==content to clean up==
2 CONT Sir John Talbot, of Richard's Castle  1 2
2 CONT * Birth: 29 SEP 1317 in Richard's Castle, Ludlow, Herefordshire, England 3 4
2 CONT * Death: 20 SEP 1355 2
2 CONT * Father: Sir Richard V Talbot , of Richard's Castle b: 1278 in Eccleswall Manor, Herefordshire, England 
2 CONT * Mother: Joan Mortimer , Heiress of Richard's Castle b: BEF 24 NOV 1291 in Caerphilly Castle, Cardiff, Glamorganshire, Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT Married
2 CONT # Juliane de Grey b: ABT 1320 in Ruthyn Castle, Denbighshire, Wales Married: BEF 14 FEB 1329/30 in Ruthyn Castle, Denbighshire, Wales 2.  She was the daughter of  Roger Grey, Baron Grey of Ruthin (d. 1353) & Elizabeth de Hastings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT #  John Talbot , of Richard's Castle, Sir b: 1337 in Richard's Castle, Ludlow, Herefordshire, England.  Married Catherine Talbot.
2 CONT #  Gilbert Talbot , of Wadley, Sir b: ABT 1341 in Richard's Castle, Ludlow, Herefordshire, England.  Married Margaret Howard.
2 CONT # Katherine Talbot, wife of Ralph Parles
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT photo is present day Richard's Castle (ruins) in Herefordshire, England, UK:
2 CONT
2 CONT The castle which gave its name to this small ancient borough pre-dates the Conquest by about 16 years, though it was thoroughly Norman. Edward the Confessor, more Norman than English, encouraged the settlement of Norman kinsmen an
2 CONC d friends in Herefordshire, and gave them lavish gifts of land. This plot, a few miles south-west of Ludlow, was a grant to Richard FitzScrob, who built himself a strong castle on it about 1050, further elevating the hilltop and b
2 CONC edrock with earth on which to site his keep. The castle continued in his line for several generations before it passed by marriage to the family of Talbot, under the overlordship of the Mortimers.
2 CONT
2 CONT By Leland's survey of 1540 the castle is reported as still mainly standing, but ruinous, and later it housed a farm and its buildings, with a dovecote in one of the towers. The property passed through several hands before coming f
2 CONC inally into the possession of the Salwey family, which continued to hold in for 370 years
2 CONT
2 CONT ==comments==
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [http://newsgroups.derkeiler.com/Archive/Soc/soc.genealogy.medieval/2008-05/msg00240.html Katherine Talbot, wife of Ralph Parles, and her daughter, Margery Parles, wife of John Hervey and William Argentine]  May 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT Douglas Richardson writes:
2 CONT
2 CONT > .... Thus, it would appear that Ralph Parles first wife was Katherine Talbot, not Joan Talbot, she being a daughter of '''John Talbot, Knt., of Richard's Castle, Herefordshire''', by his wife, Juliane, daughter of Roger de Grey,
2 CONC  Knt., 1st Lord Grey of Ruthin. Katherine Talbot's mother's descent from Geoffrey Plantagenet can be found in my book, Plantagenet Ancestry (2004).
2 CONT
2 CONT >Ralph Parles, Esq., and his wife, Katherine Talbot, had one daughter, Margery Parles, who married (1st) John Hervey, Esq., of Thurleigh, Bedfordshire, and (2nd) William Argentine, Knt., of Halesworth, Suffolk and Great Wymondley,
2 CONC  Hertfordshire. Margery Parles had four known children by her Hervey marriage, but none by her Argentine marriage. There are many modern descendants of the Hervey marriage.  ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT # Title: Newsgroup: soc.genealogy.medieval, at groups - google.com Page: Douglas Richardson, 5 Oct 2003 Text: says John was eldest son, not Richard (as CP stated)
2 CONT # Title: Complete Peerage of England Scotland Ireland Great Britain and the United Kingdom, by G. E Cokayne, Sutton Publishing Ltd, 2000 Page: XII/1:630
2 CONT # Title: Complete Peerage of England Scotland Ireland Great Britain and the United Kingdom, by G. E Cokayne, Sutton Publishing Ltd, 2000 Page: XII/1:630 Text: 29 Sep 1317-19, but being eldest son makes 1317 more likely.
2 CONT # Title: Newsgroup: soc.genealogy.medieval, at groups - google.com Page: Chris Phillips, 5 Oct 2003 Text: likely to be 1317, as eldest son.
2 CONT
