1 NOTE {geni:about_me} There is an effigy of Payne de Turbeville at the Church of St Mary, Coity, Bridgend, Wales. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1309 28 Feb 1309: "Protection, for three years, for Payn Turberville, going on pilgrimage to the Holy Land." [An Inventory of the Ancient Monuments in Glamorgan: Volume III - Part 1b: Medieval Secular Monuments the Later Castles f
2 CONC rom 1217 to the present, p 435]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1315 Sheriff of Glamorgan in December 1315
2 CONT
2 CONT 1312 25 Jun 1312: "Quit-claim by John de Boneuyle, son and heir of Henry de Boneuyle, to Margam Abbey, of 20 s/z. out of 40 s/2. rent due to him by the Abbey, for a tenement of Boneuyleston, for £20 beforehand. Under seal of Sir P
2 CONC agan de Turberuyle and Leysan de Auene.
2 CONT Witnesses:—John Le Botyler, Peter Baudewyn, William Turberuyle, William de Corneli, William Grammus."
2 CONT
2 CONT Commitment during pleasure to Payn Turvill of the keeping of the castles and lands in Glamorgan and Morgannou in Wales late of Gilbert de Clare, earl of Gloucester and Hertford, deceased, tenant in chief.
2 CONT
2 CONT Calendar of Fine Rolls, 9 Edw. II, p. 253 26 Jul 1317, Nottingham
2 CONT
2 CONT Order to the keepers of the lands of Glamorgan and Morgannou in Wales to levy by distraints and other means all debts which Payn de Turbevill, late keeper of the said lands, who has given the king to understand that divers sums of
2 CONC  money are due to the king in those parts of the time when he was keeper, for which he is held to answer to the king, or Payn's attorney can shew them to be due to the king in those parts, and to deliver the same to the said Payn 
2 CONC or his attorney, so that he may answer therefor...
2 CONT
2 CONT Calendar of Fine Rolls, 11 Edw. II, p. 335
2 CONT
