1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir Gilbert de Turberville was the eldest son of Payne de Turberville and his wife Gwenllian Talbot.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Cecily Beauchamp [& probably an earlier marriage too, as his heir Gilbert was born too early to be the son of Cecily. Probably the Matilda below]. Cecily married about 1347/48 to Roger Seymour soon after Gilbert's death
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT The Topographer and Geneologist Vol 2 page 271 pub 1853 1335 Charter of Gilbert de Turbeville relating to the manor of Landimore in Gower 9 Edward III 1335 Gilbert de Turbevlle. Lord of Landimore, confirmed to Sir Robert de Penres
2 CONC  certain lands in that Lordship, which Sir Robert had aquired from the family of Braose, the chief lords of Gower; one of whom, Lord William de Braos, had formerly disseised the ancestor of the grantor, a former Sir Gilbert de Tur
2 CONC beville, of the Manor of Landymore, which he held in fee tail, conjointly with his wife Matilda. . One of the witnesses was John de la Bere. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert died on the 20th August 1347, just two weeks after the Battle of Calais, where both he and his brother Richard fought for the king, so it is likely that Gilbert was mortally wounded there. He had the right to be buried at 
2 CONC Ewenny Priory, Ewenny, Vale of Glamorgan.
2 CONT
2 CONT His only heir, another Gilbert de Tuberville died without issue in 1349, leaving his uncle Richard de Turberville [this Gilbert's brother ] as his heir. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173392255932 (September 25, 2018; Anne Brannen, curator)
