2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gruffudd_Fychan_II
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Griffith Vychan, Lord of Glyndwrdwym in Merionethshire, representativ of Grifitth Maelor, Lord of Brimfield, eldest som of Madoc ap Meredith, last Price of Poweys.  Source: http://www.maximiliangenealogy.co.uk/burke1/Royal%20Desce
2 CONC nts/hughesofgwerclas_2.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference:
2 CONT * The Seize Quartiers of the Family of Bryan and Frances Cooke, 1857
2 CONT * Date:	1857
2 CONT * Text:	"... Emrall,=: Agnes, daughter and heir of Sir John Wysham, Knight Banneret, of Gloucester. 
2 CONT * Married Sir Richard Pyvelesdon de Emral de Puleston 
2 CONT * Richard he is the son of Sir Roger e Puleston and Margaret  verch Gruffydd ap Llewelyn
2 CONT  * Puleston of Emrall, Knight,: second son and heir, living 1345, 2d Edward IIL..."
2 CONT * Child of Agnes de Wysham Puleston and Sir Richard Pyvelesdon de Emral de Puleston is Roger Puleston of Emrall, Knight, second son and heir living 1345.
2 CONT * Roger Puleston of Emrall, Knight, married  Gwenhwyfar Llewelwyn, the daughter of Ap Ynyr, Lord of Gelligynan in Llewelwyn.
2 CONT * Child of Roger Puleston of Emrall, Knight and Gwenhwyfar Llewelwyn is ''''''Richard Puleston of Emrall, Esq'''''' = Lleuki,temp Richard ll, married daughter of Madoc Voel, Lord of Eglwysegle. They are descended from Cynric Efell
2 CONC .
2 CONT * Child of Richard Puleston of Emrall, Esq and daughter of Madoc Voel is
2 CONT * Robert of Puleston of Emrall, Esq son and heir, was slain 1st Henry IV, living AD 1399. =  Lowrie, Married the daughter of ''''''Griffth Vychan, of Rhuddalt and Lord glynwrdwt. She is a sister of Owen Glyndwr.''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/89716311/gruffudd-fychan?
2 CONT
2 CONT Baron of Glyndyfrdwy and Lord of Cynllaith, hereditary Prince of Powys Fadog. He was father to Owen Glendower, Prince of Wales by his wife Elen ferch Tomas ap Llywelyn of Deheubarth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Named after Saint Asaph the village is known to have existed since at least c.600 AD and was originally called Llanasaph meaning the Church of Saint Asaph. It was where the tomb and relics of Saint Asaph were enshrined before they
2 CONC  were moved to the larger St Asaph Cathedral sometime before 1281. This ancient parish church was later the burial place of Gruffudd Fychan (the father of Owain Glyndŵr). The stone slab which covered his tomb survives and is engra
2 CONC ved with the words HIC LACET GRVFVD VACHAN or "here lies Gruffudd Fychan". This slab, according to records, was originally in the centre of the south aisle and it would appear that Gruffudd Fychan was buried somewhere in this chur
2 CONC ch sometime between 1350 and 1370. His mortal remains have not been found. (shared by Geoffrey Gillon)
2 CONT Inscription
