2 CONT =<nowiki>----------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT
2 CONT From Charles Cawley's Medieval Lands database (http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/WALES.htm):
2 CONT
2 CONT '''GWLADUS, daughter of RHIWALLON ap Cynfyn''' & his wife ---.  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records in 1106 that "Cadwgan son of Bleddyn and Gwladus daughter of Rhiwallon, the mother of Nest were cousins, as Bleddyn and 
2 CONC Rhiwallon, sons of Cynvyn, were brothers"[429]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m '''RHYS ap Tewdr''' (-killed in battle near Brecknock Castle [Mar/Apr] 1093).  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annales Cambriæ record that "filius Teudur Resus" started to rule in 1077[422].  The Gwentian Chronicle records that "Rhys son of Tewdwr came from Llydaw and put in a claim to the principality of South Wales as lawful heir" in
2 CONC  1077[423].  He succeeded in 1078 as King of Deheubarth.  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Rhys son of Tewdwr began to reign" in 1077[424].  The Annales Cambriæ record that "Resus filius Teudur" was expelled fro
2 CONC m his kingdom by "filiis Bledint, scilicet Madauc, Cadugan et Ririt" in 1087[425].  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Rhys son of Tewdwr was expelled from his territory by the sons of Bleddyn, sons of Cynvyn, to 
2 CONC wit Madog, and Cadwgan, and Rhirid, and he himself retreated into Ireland, and immediately afterwards he collected a fleet of the Gwyddelians and returned again, and then the battle of Llych Crei took place, and the sons of Bleddy
2 CONC n were slain" in 1087, adding that "Rhys son of Tewdwr gave an immense sum of money to the mariners, the Scots and Gwyddelians who had come to assist him"[426].  Florence of Worcester records that "Res Walanorum rex" was killed in
2 CONC  battle during Easter week "iuxta castellum Brecheniean" in [1093], after which "kings ceased to reign in Wales"[427].  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Rhys son of Tewdwr king of South Wales was killed by the F
2 CONC rench, who inhabited Brecheiniog, and then fell the kingdom to the Britons" in 1091[428].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhys & his wife had four children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         '''GORONW''' (-beheaded 1093).  The Gwentian Chronicle records that "Goronwy son of Rhys" was beheaded after the death of his father, dated to 1091 in the text, but dateable to 1093 according to the other sources quoted
2 CONC  above[430]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         '''NEST''' .  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales names, in 1106, "Nest daughter of Rhys son of Tewdwr and wife of Gerald the steward" and "Gwladus daughter of Rhiwallon, the mother of Nest", when recording that "Owai
2 CONC n [son of Cadwgan son of Bleddyn]…accompanied by a small retinue [visited] her as his kinswoman" in the castle in which his father organised a feast and later reentered the castle and abducted her "with her two sons and daughter a
2 CONC nd also another son that he [=her husband] had by a concubine"[431].  Giraldus Cambrensis names "Henricus…regi Henrici primi filius…ex nobili Nesta, Resi filii Theodori filia" in South Wales[432].  She was abducted by Owain son of
2 CONC  Cadwgan ap Bleddyn from castle Ceanrth Bychan in 1109.  m (1100) GERALD FitzWalter of Windsor, son of WALTER FitzOther of Windsor & his wife Beatrice --- (-before 1136).  Custodian of Pembroke Castle.  Mistress of HENRY I King of
2 CONC  England, son of WILLIAM I "the Conqueror" King of England & his wife Mathilde de Flandre (Selby, Yorkshire Sep 1068-Château de Lyon-la-Forêt, near Rouen 1 Dec 1135, bur Reading Abbey, Berkshire).  Mistress of STEPHEN Constable of
2 CONC  Cardiff Castle, by whom she had one illegitimate child: 
2 CONT >>i)          ROBERT FitzStephen .  The Expugnatio Hibernica records that "Robertus filius Stephani" was freed from prison in Wales, naming "matre…Nesta, Resi magni filia"[433].  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales names "Robert
2 CONC  son of Stephen by Nest daughter of Rhys son of Tewdwr" when recording that "the lord Rhys" had removed him from "the castle of Aberteivi" which he had destroyed[434].  m ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT c)        ''' GRUFFYDD ap Rhys''' ([1090]-murdered 1137).  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Gruffudd son of Rhys son of Tewdwr, king of South Wales, came from Ireland to Dyved…returned to his patrimony" in 1112 
2 CONC adding that he "passed about two years, sometimes with Gerald steward of Pembroke Castle, his brother-in-law who had married his sister Nest…"[437]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT d)         '''HYWEL''' (-after 1112).  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "Gruffudd son of Rhys…and Howel his brother" went to "Gruffudd son of Cynan" in 1112, adding that "this same Howel had been in the prison of
2 CONC  Ernulf son of Roger, the lord of Castle Baldwin" and "escaped in a maimed state with broken limbs out of the prison"[438]. 
2 CONT =<nowiki>--------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: Gwladus Verch Rhiwallon 1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sex: F 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: ABT 1041 in Powys, Montgomeryshire, Wales 1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: Deceased 1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT ID: I03401 Gwladus verch Rhiwallon
2 CONT
2 CONT "Note: Her marriage to Walter Fitz Other is indicated in many internet sources. However it is not backed by Burke's Peerage. Additionally her daughter Nesta is documented to be the wife of Walter's son Gerald Fitz Walter which wou
2 CONC ld not have been likely if Gerald's mother were Gwladus. She may have been mother of some of Walter's children, instead of Beatrice?"
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps the answer to your above quandary is the possiblity that the Gladys/Gwladys that married Walter fitzOtho was not the dau of Rhywallon ap Cynfyn, Prince of Powys. Turton indicates Walter's wife was Gladys dau of Havallon ap
2 CONC  Eynfyn Prince of North Wales (no further info on him). My notes do not show that Turton listed a second marriage for Walter to Beatrice.
2 CONT
2 CONT This, if true (& with Turton, that's a BIG if), would eliminate the problem of the son of Walter marrying the dau of Gwladus verch Rhiwallon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regards,
2 CONT
2 CONT Curt
2 CONT
2 CONT [JohnFaye (8 Jun 05).FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT No primary and few secondary sources support this story. It more than likely arises from a confusion with the next generation (Gerald of Windsor and Nest verch Rhys). There is also no existing record of any such person as "Havallo
2 CONC n ap Eynfyn" in any known spelling.
2 CONT
