1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==brief biography and family==
2 CONT apparent Lord of the Manor of Melbury Sampford
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT From Dorset OPC's [https://www.opcdorset.org/MelburySampfordFiles/MelburySampford.htm "Melbury Sampford" page]:
2 CONT
2 CONT >Melbury Sampford is a small estate parish in the Beaminster area of Dorset. It is thought the first part of the name ‘Melbury’ comes from the joining of two Old English words, ‘maele’ and ‘burh’, meaning ‘multi-coloured fortified
2 CONC  place’, hinting at long forgotten battles in ancient times. 
2 CONT >
2 CONT >The second part of the name is a Manorial addition in honour of the [https://www.geni.com/surnames/saunford Saunford] family, Lords of the Manor here from the late 13th century. In medieval times this place was often alternativel
2 CONC y called Melbury Turbeville from the family of that name. By the 15th century, the Manor had passed to the [https://www.geni.com/surnames/brouning Brouning] family.
2 CONT >
2 CONT >The parish primarily consists of Melbury Park, an attractive deer park, with woodland and agricultural land of around 300 hectares. Formerly, there was a village of Melbury Sampford, whose disappearance may have been due to the e
2 CONC nclosure of the deer park and the 1530 rebuilding of Melbury House by Sir Giles Strangways, Henry VIII’s Dorset Commissioner for the Dissolution of the Monasteries.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==comments==
2 CONT Surname has also been reported to be:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''[https://www.geni.com/surnames/sanford Sanford]
2 CONT * '''[https://www.geni.com/surnames/stanford de Stanford]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==sources==
2 CONT * ''Proceedings of the Dorset Natural History and Antiquarian Field Club'', Volume 28.  [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Proceedings_of_the_Dorset_Natural_Histor/6JQ3AAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA236-IA1&printsec=frontcover pp. 
2 CONC 236-237, memorial brass of Laurence (Laurencius) Sampford].
