1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Evidence needed to support https://www.geni.com/people/Aufrica-de-Connoght/6000000042233022615?through=6000000001745096178 as mother or as wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LVC7-GRL
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Montagu-92
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sir Simon de Montagu
2 CONT * Born: After 1250, Shepton Montagu, Wincanton, Somerset, England
2 CONT * Marriage: 1)  Hawise de Saint-Amand 2) Isabel 
2 CONT * Died: 26 Sep 1316, Yardlington, Wincanton, Somersetshire, England 
2 CONT * Buried: 2 Nov 1316, Bruton Abbey, Somersetshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Other names for Simon were Symon, DE MONTACUTE and DE MONTAIGU.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon de Montagu came from the ''Montagu'' family, which was traced back to the Norman Drogo de Montagu, who had come to England during the Norman conquest. He was a son of ''William de Montagu'' and his wife ''Berthe''. At the de
2 CONC ath of his father in 1270, he was still a minor, but a little later he must have taken over his father's inheritance, which mainly included possessions in Somerset.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Montagu died 26 Sep 1316  and was buried in Bruton Priory in Somerset. His heir was his son William Montagu (around 1285–1319).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Montagu-92
2 CONT ''Montagu-92 created 2 Oct 2014 | Last modified 9 Aug 2023''
2 CONT
2 CONT Extracted from ''Oxford Dictionary of National Biography''
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Simon was descended from Drogo de Montagu, a Norman who came to England with William the Conqueror. Simon was the son of William de Montagu, a Somerset landowner, and his wife, Berthe. He was under age at his father's death, in 
2 CONC 1270, but fought in Edward I's wars in Wales in 1277 and 1282, and in 1283 was summoned to parliament at Shrewsbury. In 1290 he surrendered his lands in Dorset, Devon, Buckinghamshire, and Oxfordshire to the king, and was then reg
2 CONC ranted them with remainders to his sons William and Simon. He was summoned to fight in Gascony in 1294, where he served as marshal at Blaye. In 1296 he succeeded in breaking the French siege of Bourg-sur-Mer, taking a ship loaded 
2 CONC with victuals through the line of French galleys. He returned to Gascony with John Hastings on royal service in 1302. It was in Scotland, however, that Montagu served the crown most. In 1298 he attended the parliament at York at w
2 CONC hich the Scottish war was discussed, and may have been present on the Falkirk campaign later that year. He certainly took part in the Caerlaverock expedition of 1300; the poet who described the army placed him at the rear of the 3
2 CONC rd division. Montagu's particular contribution to the wars lay in naval warfare in the Irish Sea and around the Western Isles. In 1300 he provided two substantial ships for the Scottish war, a galley and a barge, manned by 100 men
2 CONC  between them. In 1307 he was captain and governor of the English fleet, and admiral in 1310. He continued to be summoned to campaign against the Scots until his death."[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://roots-boots.net/ft/l-montgu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT [Simon de Montacute (d. 1316)] married 1st Hawise de St Amand [1,2,6,8,16,21] daughter of Almeric, Lord St. Amand, before 1270.[22,23,24] Died c.1287.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 2nd Isabella,[4,11,21] after 1287.[23] Was still alive in the spring of 1290.[21,23]
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources claim that Simon married Aufrica of Man,[22] however reliable secondary sources and primary sources give no evidence of this.[23] See elsewhere for a discussion on this.[12] (“Aufricia "of Man" and why she didn't marr
2 CONC y Simon de Montacute.” < [http://roots-boots.net/ft/aufricia.html link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/St_Amand-28
2 CONT
2 CONT Hawise, daughter of Amaury de Saint Amand of Cainhoe, Bedfordshire, married Simon de Montagu, Knt., shortly after 24 October 1270 (grant of his marriage)".[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon and Hawise had three sons and two daughters:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sons
2 CONT # William de Montagu, Knt., 2nd Earl of Salisbury. William married Elizabeth, daughter of Sir Peter or Piers de Montfort of Beaudesert, Worcestershire (died 1287), and Maud de la Mare.
2 CONT # John de Montagu
2 CONT # Simon de Montagu
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughters
2 CONT # Hawise[1]
2 CONT # Mary (nun at Amesbury)[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hawise died before 1287, when Simon married (2) Isabel ______.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT However, from http://roots-boots.net/ft/l-montgu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Simon de Montacute and Hawise de St Amand:
2 CONT *    i. Isabella de Montacute, born c.1260, Shepton-Montacute,         Somerset .[4,7,14,19,20].  Married Robert de Brent, 1279, Cossington, Somerset.[1,3,5,8-10]
2 CONT *       ii. Maude de Montacute, born c.1265, Shepton-Montacute,           Somerset.[13] Married John de la Field, c.1287.[16] Children:            John (abt.1295) of Hertfordshire.[16]
2 CONT * iii. Baron William de Montacute,[1-3,5,6,8,1,21,22] born c.1271,  Shepton-Montacute, Somerset.[2,6]  …. Married Elizabeth de Montfort,   [1,2,5,6,8,16,21] c.1298, at either Salisbury, Wiltshire,[16] or Cassington, Oxfordshire.
2 CONT *iv. Simon de Montacute,[1,2,5,6,8,17,22] born c.1275, Shepton           Montacute, Somerset.[2,6] Knight of the Shire in 1314.[23]           Married unknown. Child: William (1300).[1,2,16]
2 CONT *        v. John de Montacute, born c.1278, Shepton-Montacute,    Somerset.[2,6,18] Sheriff of Somerset in 1305-1306.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources and Notes
2 CONT ''Updated 17 September 2023'' 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From  < [https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simon_de_Montagu,_1._Baron_Montagu German Wikipedia] > retrieved 17 September 2023.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Montagu had probably been married three times. Around 1270 he had married Hawise, a daughter of Amaury de St Amand. After her death before 1287, he married Isabel in a second marriage, whose origin is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Apparently in 1304 he married Aufrica de Connoght. She was probably a relative of Magnus, the last king of the Isle of Man. Therefore, she claimed the island as an heir, which had meanwhile been occupied by England. In 1305, she 
2 CONC ceded her claims to the island to Montagu. 2] Perhaps because of these claims, he conquered the Isle of Man around 1310.[ 3]
2 CONT
2 CONT #Michael Prestwich: Edward I. University of California, Berkeley 1988, ISBN 0-520-06266-3, p. 486
2 CONT # R. Andrew McDonald: The Sea Kings. The late Norse Kingdoms of Man and the Isles, c. 1066–1275. John Donald, Edinburgh 2019, ISBN 978-1-910900-21-5, p. 302.
2 CONT # R. Andrew McDonald: The Sea Kings. The late Norse Kingdoms of Man and the Isles, c. 1066–1275. John Donald, Edinburgh 2019, ISBN 978-1-910900-21-5, p. 303
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [Two generations, Simon I and Simon II, need to be confirmed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simon_Montagu,_1st_Baron_Montagu Wikipedia] > “'''Simon de Montagu, 1st Baron Montagu''' (died 1316) (alias de Montagu, de Montacute, Latinized to de Monte Acuto ("from the sharp mountain")
2 CONC , from the French mont aigu, with identical meaning,[1]) was summoned to Parliament by writ and thereby became the 1st Baron Montagu.[2] He was the ancestor of the great Montagu family, Earls of Salisbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was son of William de Montacute and Bertha, his wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Montagu married twice: firstly to Hawise de St Amand (died 1287), daughter of Amaury de St Amand; and secondly to Isabel, whose parentage is unknown.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT He had three sons, by which wife is unknown. His eldest son and heir was William Montagu, 2nd Baron Montagu (c. 1285 – 18 October 1319). His other sons were named John and Simon.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT * This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: "Montacute, Simon de". Dictionary of National Biography. London: Smith, Elder & Co. 1885–1900. < [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_
2 CONC Biography,_1885-1900/Montacute,_Simon_de link] > 
2 CONT * Duchess of Cleveland, The Battle Abbey Roll with some Account of the Norman Lineages, 3 volumes, London, 1889 < [http://www.1066.co.nz/library/battle_abbey_roll2/subchap181.htm link] > 
2 CONT * 7.  Cokayne, George Edward, Complete Peerage, edited by H.A. Doubleday and Lord Howard de Walden, Vol. IX, London, 1936, p.80
2 CONT * 8.  Mentioned in his father's inquisition post mortem (Cokayne, 1936, p.80)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Source < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Montagu,_2nd_Baron_Montagu Wikipedia] >: "William de Montacute (also known as Montagu), 2nd Baron Montacute (c. 1285-1319) was an English nobleman and courtier of King Edward II.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the '''son of Simon de Montacute''', 1st Baron Montacute in 1299 (1250 - 1316/1317) and either Simon's first wife Hawise, or his second wife, Isabella. The Montagu family was a West Country family with roots going back to t
2 CONC he Conquest, who held extensive lands in Somerset, Dorset and Devon. ..." 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: < [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL1.htm#SimonMontagudied1316B MedLands] >
2 CONT
2 CONT "SIMON de Montagu, son of WILLIAM de Montagu & his wife Berthe --- (after 1250-26 Sep 1316, bur Bruton Priory). Confirmation of charters in favour of the canons of Christchurch Twynham, dated 27 Nov 1313, includes a charter under 
2 CONC which '''"Simon de Monte Acuto, son and heir of Sir William de Monte Acuto"''' confirmed a conveyance made by "William de Monte Acuto" of the town of Pideleton, for the souls of "William his father, and Hawise wife of the said Sim
2 CONC on" dated May 1287. He was summoned to parliament 29 Dec 1299, whereby he is held to have become Lord Montagu.
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ([1270]) HAWISE de Saint-Amand, daughter of AMAURY [II] de Saint-Amand & his wife --- (-before May 1287). Confirmation of charters in favour of the canons of Christchurch Twynham, dated 27 Nov 1313, includes a charter un
2 CONC der which "Simon de Monte Acuto, son and heir of Sir William de Monte Acuto" confirmed a conveyance made by "William de Monte Acuto" of the town of Pideleton, for the souls of "William his father, and Hawise wife of the said Simon
2 CONC " dated May 1287. m secondly (1287 or before) ISABEL, daughter of --- (-after 1290).
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon & his [first/second] wife had one child:  
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. WILLIAM de Montagu (-Gascony 18 Oct 1319). ..." 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: <http://www.geni.com/people/Simon-de-Montagu/6000000001745096178>: "Simon de Montagu, 1st Baron Montagu (c.1250 - 1316) Birthdate: circa 1250 Birthplace: Montacute, Somerset, England Death: Died September 26, 1316 in Prior
2 CONC y, Burton, Somerset, England Occupation: - BARON OF SHEPTON
2 CONT
2 CONT Immediate Family: Aufrica of Man, c. 1245 wife Isabel de Montagu, Baroness Montagu wife Elizabeth de Montagu daughter Hawise de St. Amand wife William Montague, 2nd Lord of Mo... son Simon de Montagu father Aufrica de Montagu moth
2 CONC er William de Montacute, 1210 father Beota de Montagu mother Hawise de Neville sister"
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: <Dictionnaire de la noblesse, contenant les genealogies, l'histoire et la ...> Par Francois-Alexandre Aubert de La Chesnaye Des Bois: "Pierre De Montfort, du nom, Seigneur de Beaudsert, mourut le quinzime du reigne d'Edoua
2 CONC rd I". II avoit Mathilde, de laquelle il laissa: 1. Jean, qui suit; 2. Et Elisabeth, qui s'est mari Guillaume de Montagu, fils de Simon de Montagu." 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: <Heraldry, historical and popular> Par Charles Boutell: "The first English subject who is recorded to have quartered arms, so far as it is at present known, was Symon De Montagu, whose Shield, No. 635, Plate XTX., is marsh
2 CONC alled as follows in the Roll of Edward II., A.d. 1308-1311 :\emdash  "Sire Symon de Montagu, Quartile de Argent e de azure; en les quarters de azure les griffons de or; en les quarters de argent les daunces de goules." (The "daunc
2 CONC es" are equivalent to a group of fusils conjoined in fesse across the shield, which is sometimes blazoned as a "dancette" or a fesse dancette'e.) In this composition two distinct coats of arms borne by the Montagues are marshalled
2 CONC  together by quartering: that is to say,\emdash 1. "Argent, a fesse engrailed (or dancettee) of three pieces gules," for "William Montague," (Roll of Henry III.); and 2. "azure, a griffin segreant or" for " Simon De Montagu," (Rol
2 CONC l of Caerlaverock, A. D. 1300.); Nos. 636 and 636 A, PI. XIX." 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Source <http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=jweber&id=I11492> (Dead link) : "Simon 1st Baron de MONTAGU Birth: BEF 1256 in Shepton Montagu, Wincanton, Somerset, England Death: 26 SEP 1316 in Yardlington, Winc
2 CONC anton, Somerset, England Burial: 2 NOV 1316 Bruton Priory, Somerset, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Simon de MONTAGU b: ABT 1233 in Shepton Montagu, Wincanton, Somerset, England Mother: Aufric de COURCY, Heiress of Isle of Man b: ABT 1238 in Isle of Man, British Crown dependancy
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 1stly, circa 1270, Hawise, daughter of Amauri DE ST. AMAND (a). She was dead in 1287. He married, 2ndly, in or before 1287, Isabel, whose parentage is not known. She was living in the spring of 1290. He died 26 Septembe
2 CONC r, and was buried 2 November 1316, in Bruton Priory. [Complete Peerage IX:78-80, (transcribed by Dave Utzinger)]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Hawise de ST. AMAND b: ABT 1260 in Grendon Underwood, Aylesbury, Buckinghamshire, England Married: ABT 1270 in 1st wife Children 1. William 2nd Baron de MONTAGU, KB b: BEF 1280 in Aston Clinton, Aylesbury, Buckinghamshi
2 CONC re, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 2 Isabel b: ABT 1256 in England Married: BEF 1287 in 2nd wife Children 1. Isabella de MONTAGU b: ABT 1284 in Shepton Montagu, Somerset, England
2 CONT
2 CONT ... Sir Simon [I] married Aufricia, daughter of Fergus, King of Man, descended from Orry, King of Denmark. The Historian records that Aufricia, daughter of Fergus, King of Man, having fled to King Edward when dispossessed by Alexa
2 CONC nder III, King of Scots, Edward bestowed her in marriage upon Simon, Lord Montague, baron of Shipton Montague, who, by the King's assistance, recovered the island and enjoyed it in her right many years. Their issue was William and
2 CONC  Simon, the former succeeded his father and continued the line, the latter was married to Hawise, daughter of Almeric, Lord St. Amand. He had been summoned to parliament from the 28th of Edward I [1300] to the 8th of Edward II [13
2 CONC 15], soon after which he died. [Montagues in Great Britain, Terry and Jason Fritts, Gladstone, Missouri]"
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon married Hawise DE SAINT-AMAND [2603], daughter of Sir Amaury II DE SAINT-AMAND [2608] and Isabel [2623]. (Hawise DE SAINT-AMAND [2603] was born about 1252 in Aylesbury, Buckinghamshire, England, died before 1287 in , , Engla
2 CONC nd and was buried in Bruton Abbey, Somersetshire, England.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Source: < [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Montagu-92 Wikitree] > citing Oxford Dictionary of National Biography.  Marriages:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Montagu's first marriage, about 1270, was to Hawise, daughter of Amaury de St Amand. Shortly after her death in 1287 he married Isabel, whose parentage is unknown. It seems probable that in 1304 he married Aufrica de Connoght, w
2 CONC ho claimed to be heir to the Isle of Man, for in that year she handed over her rights in the isle to him. Man had come under Scottish control in 1265, but was annexed to England by Edward I in 1290." (ibid)
2 CONT
