2 CONT Walter de Beauchamp, died 1236
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Beauchamp-438 And then edited 
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter de Beauchamp was a son of William de Beauchamp.[1][2][3][4][5][6][7][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT The identity of his mother is uncertain (see the profile of his father, William de Beauchamp).
2 CONT
2 CONT His father died in 1197.[8][9] His heir was Wilikin de Beauchamp. This Wilikin died under age in the summer of 1209, when his lands passed to Walter (or Watekin).[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1211 Roger de Mortimer gave 3,000 marks for having the wardship of Walter de Beauchamp and the custody of his lands, and married Walter to his daughter.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT 8 July 1229: For Ralph de Mortimer. To the barons of the Exchequer. The king has pardoned to Ralph de Mortimer, brother and heir of Hugh de Mortimer, for his faithful service, up to £500 of the £511 2s. 4d. that is exacted from th
2 CONC e same Ralph at the Exchequer for the same Hugh of the fine which Roger de Mortimer, father of Hugh and Ralph, made with King John, the king’s father, for having custody of Walter de Beauchamp. Cited Calendar of Fine Rolls 13 Henr
2 CONC y III, No. 255.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sanders[7] stated Walter attained his majority between 1211 and 1214, while Cawley[8] gives his estimated year of birth as c 1195-1197.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Red Book of the Exchequer recorded "Walterus de Bello Campo" holding 207 knights’ fees "in Uptone, Stanes, Graftone, Ayntone, Chedeleswale, Kokesaye, Benetlega, Pancevot, Bouresdale, Costone, Dunclere" in Worcestershire in 121
2 CONC 0-1212.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter de Beauchamp seems to have taken the part of the barons against John for a short time in 1216, but made his peace with the king in August of that year as in that year the king committed the custody of Elmley Castle to Walte
2 CONC r de Lacy, Hugh de Mortimer and Walter de Clifford to keep while Walter de Beauchamp went to the Papal Legate to obtain absolution for his lapse from fidelity to the king.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1234 Walter de Beauchamp received from the king a gift of ten does and three bucks for stocking his park at Elmley.[6][16]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT He died 14 April 1236[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Walterus de Bello Campo obiit die Sancti Guthlac" [1326].[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annals of Tewkesbury recorded the death "VIII Kal Mai" in 1236 of W de Bellocampo.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT NB VCH Worcestershire[6] indicated he died in 1235.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
