1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Maudit,_8th_Earl_of_Warwick
2 CONT
2 CONT William Maudit (or Mauduit), 8th Earl of Warwick (abt 1220 – 8 January 1267), was an English nobleman and participant in the Barons' War.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Alice de Beaumont (daughter of the 4th Earl) and William de Maudit, and so was the grandson of Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick. His father was the lord of Hanslape and hereditary chamberlain of the exche
2 CONC quer, a title that went back to another William Maudit who held that office for Henry I.
2 CONT
2 CONT He adhered to Henry III in the wars with the barons. He was surprised in his own castle, Warwick Castle by John Giffard, the governor of Kenilworth Castle. The walls of the castle were destroyed and the countess taken prisoner to 
2 CONC Kenilworth, and only released on payment of a ransom nineteen hundred marks.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Mauduit made the castle in the corner of Portchester Castle (Portus Adurni) for an unknown reason. This was made in 1090 and is a Norman Castle and had palisades on each side of the castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died without issue and the estates then passed to his sister Isabel de Maudit who had married William de Beauchamp. She died shortly after Warwick's death and the title passed to their son William.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Wikipedia): ...He adhered to Henry III in the wars with the barons. He was surprised in his own castle, Warwick Castle by John Giffard, the governor of Kenilworth Castle. The walls of the castle were destroyed and the countess ta
2 CONC ken prisoner to Kenilworth, and only released on payment of a ransom ninteen hundred marks.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died without issue and the estates then passed to his sister Isabel de Maudit who married William de Beauchamp and inherited the title of the Earl of Warwick by right of his wife.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p25498.htm#i254980
2 CONT
2 CONT William Mauduit, 8th Earl of Warwick1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #254980
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=10 Dec 2007
2 CONT
2 CONT      William Mauduit, 8th Earl of Warwick is the son of William Mauduit.1
2 CONT
2 CONT      William Mauduit, 8th Earl of Warwick gained the title of 8th Earl of Warwick.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct 
2 CONC or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume XII/2, page 367. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He was the son of Alice de Beaumont (daughter of the 4th Earl) and William de Maudit, and so was the grandson of Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick. His father was the Lord of Hanslope and hereditary chamberlain of the exche
2 CONC quer, a title that went back to another William Maudit who held that office for Henry I.
2 CONT
2 CONT He adhered to Henry III in the wars with the barons. He was surprised in his own castle, Warwick Castle by John Giffard, the governor of Kenilworth Castle. The walls of the castle were destroyed and the countess taken prisoner to 
2 CONC Kenilworth and only released on payment of a ransom nineteen hundred marks.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Mauduit made the inner ward in the northwestern corner of Portchester Castle (Portus Adurni) for an unknown reason. This was made in 1090 and is a Norman Castle and had palisades on each side of the castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he died, his estates passed to his daughter, Isabel de Maudit who had married William de Beauchamp. She died shortly after Warwick's death and the title passed to their son William.
2 CONT
