1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Isabel de Beauchamp, d. by 30 May 1306; m. (1) Sir Patrick de Chaworth, d. by 7 July 1283, Lord of Kempsford, co. Gloucester, and Kidwelley, Cymru, son of Patrick de Chaworth, d. 1258, lord of Kempsford, co. Gloucester, by (1) wif
2 CONC e Haiwise, daughter & heir of Thomas of London, lord of Kidwelly; m. (2) by 1286 Sir Hugh le Despenser, b. 1 Mar 1260/1, hanged 27 Oct 1326, Earl of Winchester, son of Hugh le Despenser and Aline Basset.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Magna Charta Sureties]
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT most sources mentioned that she died before 30 May 1306
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=aet%2Dt&id=I9582
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_de_Beauchamp
2 CONT * http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_di_Beauchamp
2 CONT * http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=43145838
2 CONT * http://histfam.familysearch.org/getperson.php?personID=I2580&tree=Nixon
2 CONT * http://histfam.familysearch.org/getperson.php?personID=I8243&tree=EuropeRoyalNobleHous
2 CONT * http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/p100.htm#i12136
2 CONT * http://www.mathematical.com/beauchampisabel.html
2 CONT * http://thepeerage.com/p10687.htm#i106863
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella de Beauchamp, Lady Kidwelly, Lady Despenser (born c. 1263 - died before 30 May 1306), was an English noblewoman and wealthy heiress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was born in about 1263 in Warwickshire, England. She was the only daughter of William de Beauchamp, 9th Earl of Warwick and Maud FitzJohn. She had a brother, Guy de Beauchamp, 10th Earl of Warwick who married Alice de Toe
2 CONC ni, by whom he had seven children. Her paternal grandparents were William de Beauchamp of Elmley Castle and Isabel Maudit, and her maternal grandparents were Sir John FitzGeoffrey, Lord of Shere, and Isabel Bigod.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and issue[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime before 1281, she married firstly Sir Patrick de Chaworth, Lord of Kidwelly in Carmarthenshire, South Wales. The marriage produced one daughter: Maud Chaworth (2 February 1282- 1322), married Henry, 3rd Earl of Lancaster, 
2 CONC by whom she had seven children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Patrick's death in 1286, Isabella had in her possession four manors in Wiltshire and two manors in Berkshire, assigned to her until her dowry should be set forth along with the livery of Chedworth in Gloucestershire and 
2 CONC the Hampshire manor of Hartley Mauditt which had been granted to her and Sir Patrick in frankmarriage by her father.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT That same year 1286, she married secondly Sir Hugh le Despenser without the King's licence for which Hugh had to pay a fine of 2000 marks.[1] He was created Lord Despenser by writ of summons to Parliament in 1295, thereby making I
2 CONC sabella Lady Despenser.
2 CONT
2 CONT Together Hugh and Isabella had four children:[2] Hugh le Depenser, Lord Despenser the Younger (1286- executed 24 November 1326), married Eleanor de Clare, by whom he had issue. Aline le Despenser (died before 28 November 1353), ma
2 CONC rried Edward Burnell, Lord Burnell Isabella le Despenser (died 4/5 December 1334), married firstly as his second wife, John Hastings, 1st Baron Hastings, by whom she had three children. Their descendants became the Lords Hastings;
2 CONC  she married secondly as his second wife, Sir Ralph de Monthermer, 1st Baron Monthermer.[3] Phillip le Despenser (died 1313), married as his first wife Margaret de Goushill, by whom he had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella died sometime before 30 May 1306. Twenty years later, her husband and eldest son, favourites of King Edward II, were both executed by the orders of Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March and Queen Isabella. The couple were by 
2 CONC that time the de facto rulers of England, and along with most of the people in the kingdom, they had resented the power both Despensers wielded over the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT As her husband had been made Earl of Winchester in 1322, Isabella was never styled as the Countess of Winchester.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabel De Beauchamp Despenser was an English noblewoman and a wealthy heiress. She married twice; firstly to Sir Patrick de Chaworth, Lord of Kidwelly, by whom she had a daughter, Maud Chaworth. Her second husband was Hugh le Desp
2 CONC encer, Earl of Winchester, by whom she had four children, including Hugh le Despencer. Her second husband and eldest son were both executed in 1326 by the orders of Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March, and his mistress, Isabella of 
2 CONC France, Queen-consort to King Edward II. The couple were de facto rulers of England at the time. Isabel de Beauchamp had been dead for over twenty years at the time of their executions. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Note:  [scary.ged] Lineage Sources:
2 CONT  Hutchin's History of Dorset, Vol. 1, p. 366.
2 CONT  Lewis' Manuscripts, pp. 54, 56, 22, 88, 161, 186, 223
2 CONT  Welles and Allied Families (Am. Hist. Soc. Pub. 1937), pp. 143, 157, 142, 160.
2 CONT  Dugdale's Baronage of England, Vol. 1, p. 68/9.
2 CONT  Burke's Dormant and Extinct Peerage, pp. 111, 261, 399.
2 CONT
