1 NAME Sir Lawrence de Berkerolles
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1340
2 PLAC East Orchard
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Wales
1 DEAT
2 DATE OCT 1411
1 BURI
2 DATE 18 OCT 1411
2 PLAC St Athan
2 ADDR
3 ADR1 Barry
3 CTRY Wales
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir Lawrence Berkerolles, Knight, was the son of Katherine de Turberville and Sir Roger Bererolles of East Orchard. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother Katherine was the eldest of 4 sisters to Gilbert de Turberville [whose heir Sir Gilbert de Turberville of Coity Castle died without issue], who was succeeded by his brother Richard who also died without issue, so the de
2 CONC  Turberville inheritance was successively inherited by the descendants of Katherine, Margaret, Agnes & Sarah de Turberville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lawrence Berkerolles died in October 1411 & was buried at the church od St Athan, Barry, Vale of Glamorgan, Wales on the 18th October 1411. He was stated to be 72 or 73. His inquisition Post Mortem was held at Newnham on the 23rd 
2 CONC November 1411
2 CONT
2 CONT IPM http://www.inquisitionspostmortem.ac.uk/view/inquisition/19-989/990
2 CONT
2 CONT LAWRENCE BERKEROLLES, KNIGHT 989 Writ. ‡ 27 Oct. 1411 [Smyth]. GLOUCESTERSHIRE AND THE ADJACENT MARCH OF WALES. Inquisition. Newnham. 23 Nov. [Derhurst]. Jurors: John Joce ; John Staure ; John Clerk of Blakemoor ; Walter Boughan ;
2 CONC  Thomas Staure ; Richard Brooke ; William Staure ; Walter Marchall ; John Smyth ; William Wynchull ; Walter Wodeman ; and Richard Wynchull .
2 CONT
2 CONT He held in his demesne as of fee the manors of ‘Estnorchard’, Merthyr Mawr and ‘Lanfey’ in Ogmore in the March,
2 CONT
2 CONT ‘Estnorchard’, annual value 7 marks, and Merthy Mawr, annual value 7 marks, of Richard Lord le Despenser, a minor in the king’s ward, of the lordship of Cardiff, as half a knight’s fee, and ‘Lanfey’ of the king of the duchy of Lan
2 CONC caster as a quarter fee, also 7 marks. Edward and John Stradelyng are his kinsmen and heirs, Edward being the son of Wenthelea sister of Lawrence and John the son of Sarah his other sister, aged 22 years and more and 50 years and 
2 CONC more.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Turburvill held the castle, manor and lordship of Coity and the manors of Newcastle, Newland and Llanharry in Glamorgan in the March. He died seised of them without heirs of his body. They descended to Lawrence, as son and
2 CONC  heir of Katherine sister of Richard; Isabel daughter of Richard Stakepoll , son of Margaret another sister; John de la Beere, knight , son of Agnes the third sister; and William Gamage son of Sarah the fourth sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lawrence held the premises in the right of Isabel, John and William until he died on 18 Oct. 1411 . Joan widow of Richard Vernon, knight , is heir of Isabel, aged 40 years and more; Thomas de la Beere is heir of John, being the so
2 CONC n of John his son, aged 9 years and more, William Gamage is next heir of William Gamage , being the son of Gilbert son of William, and aged 30 years and more.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coity, annual value 84 marks, Newcastle, annual value 100s., Newland, annual value 40s., and Llanharry, annual value 40s., are held of Richard Lord le Despenser of the lordship of Cardiff as half a knight’s fee. Memorandum that in
2 CONC  the quindene of Hilary 13 Henry IV (1412) this writ and inquisition were read in the chancery in the presence of the chancellor, the archbishop of Canterbury , the judges and Serjeants at law, and found to be of no effect because
2 CONC  taken without warrant [sic] and contrary to the law. [separate membrane].
2 CONT
2 CONT [Dorse:] ?Richard ?Skete 'mercer'
2 CONT
2 CONT C 137/88/42 mm. 1-2 (inq., writ), 3 (memorandum)
2 CONT
2 CONT E 152/9/442 #2
2 CONT
2 CONT 990 Writ, que plura . ‡ 18 Dec. 1411 [Wymbyssh]. Regarding 989.
2 CONT
2 CONT GLOUCESTERSHIRE AND THE ADJACENT MARCH OF WALES. Inquisition tam ex officio quam virtute brevis [indented]. Thornbury. 16 Jan. 1412 [Saundres]. Jurors: Thomas fitz Nicholl chivaler; Robert Poyntz ; Alexander Clevedon ; Ralph Botel
2 CONC er ; John Blount ; Edmund Basset ; John Trye ; Thomas Staunton ; Henry Crook ; Robert Coderyngton ; John Rolues ; and John More .
2 CONT
2 CONT He held in his demesne as of fee the manors of ‘Estnorchard’, annual value 20 marks, Merthyr Mawr, annual value £10, and ‘Lanfey’, annual value £10, as above. He also held the manor of Bassaleg in the commote of Wentlloog of Humph
2 CONC rey earl of Stafford , a minor in the king’s ward, as half a knight’s fee, annual value 40 marks. Edward and John Stradelyng, aged 19 years and more and 40 and more, are his heirs as above. Richard Turbervyll held the castle, mano
2 CONC r and lordship of Coity and the manors of Newcastle, Newland and Llanharry, with the advowsons, and granted them to Humphrey de Cherleton, canon of Hereford , his heirs and assigns. He granted them back to Richard, Hawise his wife
2 CONC  and the heirs of their bodies with successive remainders to the heirs of his body, Thomas son of John de la Beere, knight , Gilbert son of Roger Berkerolles, knight , Lawrence son of the same Roger, William son of John Flemmyng ,
2 CONC  Richard son of Richard Stakepoll and Gilbert son of William Gamage , and the heirs male of the body of each in turn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Turbervyll and Hawise, Thomas, Gilbert, Gilbert, Lawrence, William and Richard Stakepoll died without heirs male. After the death of Lawrence on 15 Oct. last the premises should remain to William son of Gilbert Gamage and 
2 CONC his heirs male. He is aged 30 years and more.
2 CONT
2 CONT The premises are held of Richard Lord le Despenser , a minor in the king’s ward, of his lordship of Cardiff as one knight's fee; annual values,
2 CONT
2 CONT Coity 200 marks, Newcastle £10, Newland 100s. and Lanharry 100s. C 137/88/42 mm. 4-5
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173392255932 (September 25, 2018; Anne Brannen, curator)
