1 NAME John /de la Bere/
2 GIVN John
2 SURN de la Bere
2 _MARNM de la Bere
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1308
2 PLAC Kynardesley
2 ADDR
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1366
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Peter Bartrum, https://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/6172/BASSET%201_57.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (December 21, 2016; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source= http://histfam.familysearch.org/getperson.php?personID=I27324&tree=Welsh
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173392255932 (September 25, 2018; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sir John de la Bere was the son of Richard de la Bere. He married Agnes de la Bere in about 1324.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes was a daughter of Payne de Turberville and his wife Gwenllian Talbot. Her eldest brother Gilbert inherited the family lands, but his only son, Sir Gilbert de Turberville of Coyty/Coity Castle died without issue, so his uncle
2 CONC  Richard de Turberville inherited Coity Castle. When Richard also died without issue, his sisters and their issue male became his heirs. His sister were 1. Katherine/Catrin de Turberville who married Sir Roger Berkerolles of East 
2 CONC Orchard, 2. Margaret de Turberville who married Sir Richard Stackpole of Stockpole Elidor, 3. Agnes de Turberville who married John de la Bere of Weobley & 4. Sarah de Turberville who married Gilbert Gamage.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1335 9 Edward III 1335 Charter of Gilbert de Turbeville [brother of Agnes] & his wife Matilda relating to the Manor of Landimore in Gower. One of the witnesses was John be la Bere
2 CONT
2 CONT 1347 John de la Bere, son of Richard de la Bere, knight and Agnes [John’s] his wife, querents, and Richard son of Walter de Duynre, and Agnes,, his wife, deforciants, three weeks from Easter, 21 Edward III [22 April 1347] & afterw
2 CONC ards, two weeks from st Michael in the same year [13 Oct 1347]. Property: 2 messuages, 2 carucates of land, 6 acres of meadow, 10 acres of Wood, 36 shillings of rent and a moiety of 1 messuage and of one mill in Bromyerd, Hodesbac
2 CONC he, Estnore and Parua Tatynton. Plea of covenant. Richard and Agnes, his wife have granted to John & Agnes, his wife, the tenements and have rendered them to them in the court, to hold to John & Agnes, his wife and the heirs of th
2 CONC eir bodies, of Richard and Agnes his wife, and the heirs of Richard forever, rendering yearly 1 rose at the feast of the Nativity of St John the Baptist, and doing to the chief lords all other services. In default of such heirs, t
2 CONC he tenemants shall revert to Richard & Agnes, his wife, and the heirs of Richard, quit of the other heirs of John and Agnes, his wife, to hold of the chief lords for ever. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1363 25 Jan 1363 – 24 Jan 1364 Thomas de Chaundos, knight, to settle messuages, land and rent in Wellington, Fownhope and Snodhill [in Dorstone, Herefordshire] on himself for life, with remainder to his son John, Violet daughter o
2 CONC f John de la Bere, and the heirs of the said John the son, retaining manors in the said places. Hereford. 37 Edward III, National Archives, Kew C 143/348/2 1363 Sir John de la Bere the day after St Martin, 37 Edward III [12 Nov 13
2 CONC 63] John de Oldcastel & Maud his wife, querents, and John de la Bere, knight, John Gour, Hugh de Monyton and David Kyngeyn, deforciants. Property: 2 messuages, 3 carucates of land, 12 acres of meadow, 12 of wood and 20 shillings o
2 CONC f rent Kyngescaple, Baysham & Brokhampton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1371, 20 Nov 1371 [Thursday St Edmund 45 Edward III at Lanhern] John Arundel, knight [1] Richard Deuysier, Vicar of Sent Ide and [2] Matthew Bodrygy, Chaplain [3]. Indenture; Sir John Arundel has by simple charter enfeoffed to 2 &
2 CONC  3 of the manor of Lanhem, with fees and the advowsons of St Colombe & St Maugan; on the following conditions: When Sir John Arundel, by advice of Oliver St John, wishes to have recovery of the manor, fees and advowsons, [1] Richa
2 CONC rd Deuysier, Vicar of Sent Ide and [2] Matthew Bodrygy,shall re-enfeoff him of them for [1] and his heirs male from the body of Isabel daughter of Thomas de Molton, knight, to hold for ever of the chief lords of the fee, by servic
2 CONC es due with remainder, if [1] dies without male heirs of the body of Isabel, to the heirs of body of John son of Sir John Arundel; in default of such heirs, remainder to Oliver St John for terms of his life; after his death, remai
2 CONC nder to John de St John, elder son of Oliver St John, for term of his life; after his death, remainder to the heirs male of Oliver from the body of Elizabeth, daughter of John de la Bere, knight; in default of such heirs, remainde
2 CONC r to Margaret daughter of Sir John Arndel, wife of Reginald Beuyle, for term of her life, after hear death, remainder to Ralph son of Reginald and Margaret, for term of his life; after his death, remainder to Ralph Soor de Taluren
2 CONC  and his heirs male of his body; in default of such heirs, remainder to John Colyn de Lannanta and his heirs male from the body of Rose his wife, cousin to (1); in default of such heirs, remainder to the true heirs of (1) for ever
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Unluckily for the family, but fortunately for genealogists, the male line of the de Turberville sisters "failed" one after the other, leading to complex inquisition Post Mortems to establish who the next heir was. Because of this,
2 CONC  we know that John de la Bere's male line ended with his Great Grandson Thomas de la Bere who died in 1414 while still a minor. His daughter Elizabeth de la Bere who married Oliver St John did leave heirs & they helped manage Thom
2 CONC as's lands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The ultimate heir of the de Turberville lands was William Gamage, descendant of the 4th sister Sarah. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1389 Sir John de la Bere deceased 27 September 1389, possesed of Coity Castle. Its appurtenances were valued at ten marks per annum. His heir was a minor [grandson] named John de la Bere. IMP not held/published until 1397. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References; Petition of William de Braos to the King dated 1317-1320 & held by the UK Archives mentions his Steward David de la Bere. 1292 Welsh Subsidy Roll A Inventory of the Ancient Monuments in Glamorgan Vol 1, By the Royal Co
2 CONC mmision on the Ancient and Historical Monuments in Wales p.11, p.380 & p.381 details the charters that gave the de la Bere family Weobley Castle, Gower & the various documents they signed & witnessed. 
2 CONT
