2 CONT John de la Bere formerly Bere Born about 1300 in Herefordshire, England ANCESTORS ancestors Son of Richard (Bere) de la Bere and Sybil (Chabbonare) de la Bere Brother of Kynard (Bere) de la Bere [half] Husband of Agnes (Turbervill
2 CONC e) de la Bere — married [date unknown] [location unknown] DESCENDANTS descendants Father of Richard (Bere) de la Bere, Sibyl (Bere) Crophill, Elizabeth (Bere) St John and Kinard (Bere) de la Bere Died 1366 in Cornwall, England Pro
2 CONC file manager: Daniel Cox  Profile last modified 15 Nov 2019  Created 4 Dec 2014
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT John (Bere) de la Bere was a member of aristocracy in England. The de Turberville family held the Lordship of Coity from c. 1092 to 1360, which had been founded by Sir Payn de Turberville, one of the legendary Twelve Knights of Gl
2 CONC amorgan of Robert FitzHamon, 1st. Lord of Glamorgan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard de Turberville, seemingly his 6th great grandson, died in 1384 without male heir, leaving his four sisters as co-heiresses:
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine de Turberville, the eldest, had married Sir Roger Berkerolles (d.1351), another descendant of one of the Twelve Knights of Glamorgan, of East Orchard, St Athan. The tomb effigies of Katherine and Sir Roger can be seen in
2 CONC  St Athan's Church. Their son was Sir Lawrence Berkerolles (d.1411), the last of the Berkerolles, who died without progeny. Margaret de Turberville, the second daughter, married Sir Richard Stackpole, whose daughter Joan Stackpole
2 CONC  married Sir Richard Verney. Agnes de Turberville, the third daughter, married Sir John de la Bere of Weobly Castle, Gower. Sarah de Turberville, the fourth and youngest, married William Gamage of Rogiet.[1] Research Notes Did the
2 CONC y have a son John?
2 CONT
2 CONT Son Richard's bio states he is the "second son". Plus: "Little is known of his early life, but he no doubt grew up in the shadow of his elder brother, John, and was lucky to have escaped a life in the church." [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT And, from the Coity Castle Wikipedia entry: "Thomas de la Bere died as a minor on 28 October 1414, following which the lordship reverted to Sarah de Turberville, the youngest sister of Richard de Turberville, who had apparently pr
2 CONC oduced male progeny from her marriage to William Gamage."
2 CONT
2 CONT That seems to tie in with a Post Mortem Inquisition for a John de la Bere, who died in 1403, Inquisition delayed until 1410. His heir, Thomas, then still only 9, was a king's ward. His property was being held by John St John, son 
2 CONC of Elizabeth de la Bere, who is said to be a daughter and one of the heirs of Agnes de la Bere. [3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT And ...
2 CONT
2 CONT "Thomas de la Bere, who was born ca. 1402 (aged 9 in 1411), died 28 Oct. 1414. He was son of John de la Bere who died 24 Sep. 1403, which John was born about 1383 as he was aged 15 in 1397/8 when an inquisition was taken by Sir Jo
2 CONC hn St. John, which found that his father Sir John de la Bere of Weobley, co. Hereford, died in 1380. This Sir John, knight, would have been born say 1335-45, so he either did not marry until later in life, or had no issue by a fir
2 CONC st marriage." [5] The 1397 "inquisition" actually seems to be copied parts of multiple earlier inquisitions and the heir John may have been 15 back in 1380, when the original inquisition took place.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Calendar of Fine Rolls (CFR 14:101) states that certain lands in and around Coytyff came into the hands of Laurence Berkerolles by reason of the minority of Thomas de la Bere, son of John. John Seint John 'chivaler' son of El
2 CONC izabeth one of the sisters of John de la Bere 'chivaler' the father of John the father of the said Thomas de la Bere, and John Basset, esquire, son of Margaret the second sister of the said John de la Beer 'chivaler' are the next 
2 CONC heirs, and of full age." [6] Thomas (d. 1414), son of John (d. 1403), son of John (d. 1380), son of this John?
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources Coity Castle Ford, David Nash; Richard de la Bere J. L. Kirby. "Inquisitions Post Mortem, Henry IV, Entries 700-751," in Calendar of Inquisitions Post Mortem: Volume 19, Henry IV, (London: Her Majesty's Stationery Office, 
2 CONC 1992), 251-269. British History Online, accessed May 11, 2017, [1]. The History of Parliament: the House of Commons 1386-1421, ed. J.S. Roskell, L. Clark, C. Rawcliffe., 1993 [2] artæ et alia munimenta quæ ad dominium de Glamorgan
2 CONC  pertinent ...: 1348-1721, p 53-54 [3] GEN-MEDIEVAL: A follow up on Stackpole and de la Bere. [4] https://gw.geneanet.org/belfast8?lang=en&p=john+de+la&n=bere
