1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gilbert de Turberville was the only son & heir of Gilbert de Turberville. He is most unlikely to be the son of Cecily Beauchamp though, as he was an adult when his father died and so born before 1327 [ when Cecily 
2 CONC was only about six years old].
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert did not long survive his father, leaving his uncle Richard de Turberville as his heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert, son of Gilbert de Turbervill,” died Friday after St. Hilary 22 Edward III [16 January 1348/9]. His next heir was Richard de Turburvile, his uncle, aged thirty or more [b. by 1318]. Gilbert died seised of two parts of the 
2 CONC manor of “Koytyff and la Niwelond,” two parts of the manor of Newcastle, two parts of the manor of Lanhari, and land in Koytechirch [the other third was Cecily’s dower].229 These were held of Hugh le Despenser partly by service of
2 CONC  hunting in the earl’s forest of Newcastle and partly by knights’ service.230 Clark says he married Meiwan, daughter of Cadwgan ap Ithel, lord of the Castle of Alan. On 1 April 1349, order was made to take the fealty of Richard de
2 CONC  Turberville, as heir, the lands being in the king’s hand due to the minority of the Despenser heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173392255932 (September 25, 2018; Anne Brannen, curator)
