1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleanor_de_Montfort
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor de Montfort
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor de Montfort, Princess of Wales and Lady of Snowdon (1252 - 19 June 1282) was a daughter of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester and Eleanor of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor's maternal grandparents were John of England and his queen consort Isabella of Angoulême. Her maternal uncles included Henry III of England and Richard, 1st Earl of Cornwall. Her maternal aunts included Joan of England, Qu
2 CONC een of Scotland, and Isabella of England, and Joan, Lady of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Eleanor was thirteen years old, her father Earl Simon and brother Lord Henry were killed at the Battle of Evesham (4 August 1265). According to the chroniclers, Nicholas Trivet, William Rishanger and others, Earl Simon had ea
2 CONC rlier made an alliance with Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, whereby it was agreed that Llywelyn and Eleanor would marry. After Earl Simon's death, his family was forced to flee the Kingdom of England: Countess Eleanor took her daughter to t
2 CONC he safety of the Dominican nunnery at Montargis, France, a Montfort foundation.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage to Llywelyn ap Gruffydd
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess Eleanor died in Spring 1275, and shortly afterwards Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, the Prince of Wales, and Eleanor de Montfort married by proxy, (per nuncios) per verba de presenti (Canon law endorsed a marital bond that was made
2 CONC  in this way, with the full consent of both of the individuals, before witnesses).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Capture and imprisonment by Edward I
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor began the sea voyage from France to north Wales, avoiding making a land passage through England. The two ships carrying Eleanor, her brother Amaury and their entourage, sailing off the south coast of England, were captured
2 CONC  by sailors from the port of Bristol, just off the Isles of Scilly. Six named men together with the crews of four ships of Bristol were rewarded with a payment of 220 marks (Calendar Patent Rolls, 1272-81, 161; PRO Liberate Rolls 
2 CONC C62). 'Thomas Larchdeacon', 'Thomas the Archdeacon', who masterminded the capture on behalf of her first cousin Edward I of England was paid £20 in May 1276 by the king's orders, through the sheriff of Cornwall. (Calendar of Close
2 CONC  Rolls, 1272-79, 292).
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor was taken by ship to Bristol, then held prisoner at Windsor for nearly three years (PRO: Liberate Rolls, C62/ 52). In 1278, following the signing of the Treaty of Aberconwy, she was released.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Married life
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor and Llywelyn were formally married (secundum formam ecclesie) at the cathedral door, as was the custom, of the cathedral church at Worcester, on the Feast Day of St Edward, 1278; Edward gave the bride, his cousin, away and
2 CONC  paid for the wedding feast. Before the wedding mass was celebrated, Edward insisted that Llywelyn should put his seal to an adjustment to the agreement that they had previously made. Llywelyn had no alternative but to comply, and
2 CONC  he later stated that he did it under duress, 'moved by the fear that can grip a steadfast man' (see Registrum Epistolarum Fratis Johannis Peckham Archiepiscopi Cantuariensis, Lambeth Palace Archives).
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the ceremony, Eleanor became officially known as Princess of Wales and Lady of Snowdon. (see Calendar of Patent Rolls, 1272-81, 306; CPR, 1281-92, 11; Calendar of Ancient Correspondence, 75-76; Foedera I, ii, 576, 584, 5
2 CONC 87).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Death and legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor died in childbirth on 19 June 1282 (see The chronicle of Bury St Edmunds, 74-76) at the royal home Garth Celyn, Aber Garth Celyn on the north coast of Gwynedd; her body was carried across the Lafan Sands to the Franciscan 
2 CONC Friary of Llanfaes, Anglesey (sse Brut y Tywysogion, Peniarth MS20, 223; Peniarth MS20Tr, 117). The Friary, on the opposite shore of the Menai to Garth Celyn, had been founded by Llywelyn Fawr, the grandfather of Llywelyn ap Gruff
2 CONC udd, in memory of his wife Joan.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 12 July 1282, members of Eleanor's personal household were given 'safe-conduct' to travel to England (see Calendar of Welsh Rolls, 234).
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn was killed on 11 December 1282. The child, Gwenllian of Wales, was captured the following year by the armies of King Edward I of England, and taken to be held at Sempringham Priory in Lincolnshire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor de Montfort   
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor was born in October 1252 in Leicester Castle, Leicestershire, England.1   Eleanor's father was Earl of Leicester Simon de Montfort V and her mother was Princess of England Eleanor Plantagenet.  Her paternal grandparents we
2 CONC re Earl of Leicester Simon de Montfort (L'Amaury) IV and Alice de Montmorency; her maternal grandparents were King of England John (Lackland) Plantagenet of England and Isabella De Taillefer of Angouleme. She had five brothers and
2 CONC  two sisters, named Henry, Simon, Guy, Amaury, Richard, Joanna and Isabel.  She was the seventh oldest of the eight children.  She died at the age of 29 on June 18th, 1282 in Aberconway, Carnarvon, Wales.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal Details
2 CONT
2 CONT  Prince of Wales Llewelyn ap Gruffydd
2 CONT
2 CONT Llewelyn was born in 1228 in Nefyn, North Wales.1   He died as a war casualty, Battle of Builty, at the age of 54 on December 11th, 1282 in Builty, Wales.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Eleanor de Montfort
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor was born in October 1252 in Leicester Castle, Leicestershire, England.1   She died at the age of 29 on June 18th, 1282 in Aberconway, Carnarvon, Wales.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT   Catherine verch Llewelyn
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine was born in 1279 in Aberconway, Carnarvon, Wales.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT   Gwenllian Wenceliana verch Llewelyn
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwenllian was born on June 18th, 1282 in Aberconway, Carnarvon, Wales.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Courtesy of fantastically full family tree cf.:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hughes of Gwerclas 1/2/3/4:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.maximiliangenealogy.co.uk/burke1/Royal%20Descents/hughesofgwerclas_1.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.maximiliangenealogy.co.uk/burke1/Royal%20Descents/hughesofgwerclas_2.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.maximiliangenealogy.co.uk/burke1/Royal%20Descents/hughesofgwerclas_3.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.maximiliangenealogy.co.uk/burke1/Royal%20Descents/hughesofgwerclas_4.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Youngest child of Simon de Montfort and wife of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd the Prince of Wales, she was actually named Eleanor (Alyanora) after her mother. Anastasia de Montfort (Countess Nola) was in fact her niece, the daughter of Guy
2 CONC  de Montfort her brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT (The daughter born after Amaury and Guy, and before Richard and Eleanor, was Joanna who died in infancy.)
2 CONT
