1 TITL Lady
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Ferrers
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Ferrers (c. 1250 – c. 1300) was a daughter of William de Ferrers, 5th Earl of Derby, and his second wife Margaret de Quincy (born 1218). Her maternal grandparents were Roger de Quincy, 2nd Earl of Winchester, and Helen o
2 CONC f Galloway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth was married first to William Marshal, 2nd Baron Marshal, and after his death to Dafydd ap Gruffydd, a prince of Gwynedd and brother of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd.[1][2] Dafydd was at that time in favor with King Edward I of En
2 CONC gland but later rebelled and was executed in 1283. Elizabeth's fate is not known for certain, but she is thought to have been buried at the parish church in Caerwys, north Wales, where local tradition identifies a stone effigy on 
2 CONC display in the church as hers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth's daughter Gwladys was sent to Six Hills convent for the rest of her life. Her husband Dafydd is thought to have had other daughters who may have been illegitimate. Her sons Llywelyn and Owain were imprisoned and never r
2 CONC eleased.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://ancestors.familysearch.org/9SJK-1BR/elizabeth-de-ferrers-1244-1297
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Ferrers-103
2 CONT
2 CONT https://gw.geneanet.org/frebault?lang=en&n=de+ferrers&p=elizabeth
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.unofficialroyalty.com/lady-elizabeth-ferrers-princess-of-wales/
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/156461073/elizabeth_marshall-gruffydd
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth FERRERS, daughter of William de FERRER, 5th Earl of Derby and his second wife, Margaret de QUINCY.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married first, in about 1256, William MARSHAL, 2nd Baron Marshall at Hingham, Norfolk, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT William died from wounds sustained at the Battle of Evesham 4 August of 1265. She had been his second wife. He had lands in Norfolk, Cheshire and Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT As the widow of William MARSHAL, she then married Dafydd ap GRUFFYDD about 1277 in Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dafydd ap GRUFFUDD was born about 11 July 1238 and executed by Edward I on 3 October 1283. He was the son of Gruffudda ap LLYWELYN and his wife Senena and the grandson of Llywelyn FAWR.
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT She is said to be buried here
2 CONT at St. Michael's in Caerwys,
2 CONT Wales in the shadow of the
2 CONT castle that once belonged to
2 CONT her husband, Davydd ap GRUFFYD,
2 CONT the brother of Llewelyn ap GRUFFYD.
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT St Michael's Church, Caerwys
2 CONT
2 CONT This church is dedicated to St Michael the Archangel. The cult of St Michael became very popular in Wales in the 8th century, and it's likely the original church on this site was founded at that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest written reference to the church is in 1244, when the Pope chose St Michael's as the venue for a court hearing to decide whether Prince Dafydd ap Llywelyn, son of Llywelyn the Great, had been forced to agree a treaty w
2 CONC ith King Henry III.
2 CONT
2 CONT The next mention of St Michael's is in 1284, when the sum of 60 shillings was paid to Jervasius, the rector, as compensation for the loss of tithe payments and damage done to the church by King Edward I's soldiers during his conqu
2 CONC est of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some parts of the existing church date from the 13th century, including the tower and the nave. Inside the church there's a 13th-century effigy that supposedly depicts Elizabeth Ferrers, wife of Dafydd ap Gruffydd, who was Prince 
2 CONC of Wales in 1282 and 1283. There are also a number of 14th-century sepulchral slabs with crossheads, and stained-glass windows dating from the 15th century. The 19th-century 'Eisteddfod Window' celebrates the Eisteddfod declared i
2 CONC n Caerwys in 1523 and held in 1524. This was a meeting of bards to formalize rules for Welsh poetry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Memorials include a tablet commemorating the Rev John Lloyd, who accompanied writer and naturalist Thomas Pennant on his 'Tours of Wales'. An inscribed tombstone built into the south wall's exterior is said to mark the tomb of Rob
2 CONC ert Evans, known as 'The Boy Rector'. He died in 1582, aged 19.
