0 @I6000000029765930284@ INDI
1 NAME Owain /ap Dafydd/
2 GIVN Owain
2 _MARNM ap Dafydd
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1270
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1325
2 PLAC Bristol Castle
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Please see Darrell Wolcott: Dafydd Goch ap Dafydd - His Real Ancestry; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id116.html. (Steven Ferry, August 22, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Thomas ap Rhodri, Father of Owain "Lawgoch"; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id153.html. (Steven Ferry, August 23, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Some two months before their father's execution in September 1283, the sons of Dafydd ap Gruffudd were brought to Bristol under heavy guard. The eldest, Llywelyn, was probably about sixteen years old, his brother Owain about seven
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT They were imprisoned at Bristol for life. The accounts of the constables of Bristol Castle show that the boys were allowed sixpence a day for their maintenance, along with an annual grant for robes, linen-cloth, shoes and other ne
2 CONC cessaries. At first five grooms were paid to guard them, later three squires.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owain lived on until at least 1325. In 1305, when Owain had already been a prisoner for twenty-two years, King Edward gave the following order:
2 CONT
2 CONT As the King wills that Owain son of Dafydd ap Gruffudd, who is in the Constable’s custody in the castle, should be kept more securely than he has been previously, he orders the Constable to cause a strong house within the castle t
2 CONC o be repaired as soon as possible, and to make a wooden cage bound with iron in that house in which Owain might be enclosed at night.
2 CONT
2 CONT No explanation is given for this instruction. Possibly there had been a security scare, and the king wanted to ensure Owain could not escape
2 CONT
2 CONT Owain's grim, lonely existence is depressing to contemplate. It is likely that the long imprisonment retarded his mental development. In 1312 he sent a petition to Edward II, asking for better treatment:
2 CONT
2 CONT Oweyn, the son of David ap Griffyn, shows that, whereas he is, by order of the King, detained in the Castle of Bristol in strong and close prison, and has been since he was seven years old, for his father's trespass. He prays the 
2 CONC King to grant him, that he may go and play within the wall of the Castle, if he cannot have better grace of the King: and because the Constable of the Castle does not find him sufficiently in food and clothing, as he might, of the
2 CONC  money which he receives from the King for his food, and for his gowns, Oweyn prays the King to order that the money which the Constable receives of the King for the sustenance of the said Oweyn and for his gowns may be delivered 
2 CONC to Oweyn himself, or any other that the King shall please who may be more tender of him than the Constable is.
2 CONT
2 CONT The petition was refused. Owain's joyless life continued until about 1325, though the precise date of death is unknown.
