2 CONC  (Clifford) de Clifford and Roger (Clifford) de Clifford	
2 CONT
2 CONT Died after 1268 in Clifford Castle, Herefordshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT  Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT     Margaret ferch Llywelyn, daughter of Llywelyn ap Iorwerth,[1] married (1) in 1219 John de Brewes, (2) before 2 Nov. 1234 Walter de Clifford[2] (married after 1232[3]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT     John de Briouse, Lord of Bramber and Gower, was the son of William de Briouse & his wife _____[4] (also de Brewes, Breuse, Brause, etc.,[5] de Braose,[6] and Bruse[7]). John was born about 1197 (aged 22 in 1219), son of Willia
2 CONC m by Maud de Clare.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Her children with John de Brewes[5][2] (John de de Briouse Lord of Bramber and Gower[8]): 
2 CONT
2 CONT     William,[8] Knt., 1st Lord Brewes, married (1) Aline de Multon, (2) Agnes de Moels, (3) Mary de Roos[5]
2 CONT     Richard (born before 1232[8]), Knt., married Alice le Rus[5]
2 CONT     Llywelyn de Brewes[2][5][9]
2 CONT     John de Brewes, married Margery ____[5][2][9] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     John was "killed by a fall from his horse near Bramber, Sussex shortly before 16 July 1232".[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     After her first husband's death in 1232, Margaret married before 2 Nov. 1234 Walter de Clifford, Knt., of Clifford, Herefordshire, Cortham (in Diddlebury), Shropshire, Caenby, Lincolnshire, etc., and Bronllys, Breconshire, etc
2 CONC ., Sheriff of Herefordshire, Constable of Cardigan and Carmarthen Castles, son and heir of Walter de Clifford, of Clifford, Herefordshire, by Agnes de Cundy,[8] daughter and heiress of Roger de Condet of Caenby and Glentham, Linco
2 CONC lnshire.[5][10] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Margaret and Walter had one daughter:[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Maud (Matilda[11]) de Clifford, marred (1) William Longespée, Knt., (2) John Giffard, Knt., 1st Lord Giffard[5] (John Giffard had abducted her.[12]) 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Walter de Clifford, Knt.,[5] died December 1263[8] (shortly before 23 Dec. 1263[5]). Margaret was still living in 1268.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     "At her death, her heart was buried in the church of Aconbury, Herefordshire."[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Estimated Birth Date
2 CONT
2 CONT     No estimate of Margaret's birth year is found in Norr, Cokayne, or Richardson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Margaret married 1219. Her first son by John de Brewes, William, is said to have been of "full age 15 July 1245,"[2] thus born by 1224. "Though he was allowed to succeed his father in 1232, before 18 July, he is not stated to 
2 CONC have been of full age in the Fine Rolls until 15 July 1245."[13] 
2 CONT
2 CONT One method of estimation would be to assume, given the facts above, that son William was conceived in 1223 and that the marriage, though arranged earlier, was first consummated in 1223, when Margaret achieved the age of 15. This w
2 CONC ould place Margaret's birth at 1208. Since she is not recognized by Cawley as a daughter of llywelyn's wife Joan, Margaret's mother would have been one of llywelyn's mistresses. This is consistent with other estimates of her birth
2 CONC  year. Day-1904 18:46, 26 February 2016 (EST)
2 CONT
2 CONT     John de Braose was killed at Bramber 16 July 1232, having been bruised in a fall from his horse [Curia Regis Rolls, 490-1; CCR 1231-4, Hen. III, 2:86; Brut (1860), p. 320-1; Brut (1952), p. 102; Brut (1955), p. 231].[13] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     The lands, wardship and marriage of William de Braose, son and heir of John, was in the hands of various nobles until he came of age in 1241, indicating a birth year of about 1220:[13] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Peter des Riveaux was first custodian [see CCR 1231-4, Hen. III, 2:86; CPR 1225-32, Hen. III, 2:490].
2 CONT     Henry de Turberville was granted the lands and marriage in March 1234 [CPR 1232-47, Hen. III, 3:41, 45].
2 CONT     Richard Siward was granted the same in July 1234 [CPR 1232-47, Hen. III, 3:61].
2 CONT     Richard, Earl of Cornwall, the King's brother, was granted the custody, lands and marriage of William de Braose in January 1234/5 until 1241, when William came of age [CPR 1232-47, Hen. III, 3:89; CCR 1234-7, Hen. III, 3:41; C
2 CONC ChR 1:192]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT         b 1205 if 15 at birth of first child by John Braose was born in 1220[14] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     If Margred/Margaret were older, would it not be more logical to conclude she was a daughter of a concubine, such as Tangwystl ferch Llywarch Goch (mother of Llywelyn's eldest son Gruffudd), rather than a child of this daughter
2 CONC  of the King of Man?"[13] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Richardson, 2009, Gen-Medieval Archives: "I think it is a fair assumption that Llywellyn's daughter, Margaret, was born right about 1205, as she had her first known Braose child in 1220. That's providing Margaret was 14 when h
2 CONC er marriage was consumated and 15 when she gave birth to her first known child. If correct, then Margaret would have been born right in the time period Llywellyn was married to the Isles girl."[15] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Llywelyn ap Iorwerth, Prince of North Wales, Prince of Aberffraw, Lord of Snowden, and father of Margaret ferch Llewellyn, was born in 1173.[2] Margaret married her first husband in 1219.[5] Therefore, Margaret was likely born
2 CONC  between 1190 and 1203, being the dates of (1) her father's maturity and (2) the latest year she may have been born and been at least 16 years old at her wedding.[16] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Note - in the 11th century, Welsh women consummated their marriage as early as 14.[17] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Disputed Mother
2 CONT
2 CONT     Margred's parents were Llywelyn the Great and... 
2 CONT
2 CONT     unknown[18]
2 CONT     unknown mistress[3][2]
2 CONT     Tangwystl ferch Lywarch (known mistress)[19]
2 CONT     Joan of England[20][19][21]
2 CONT     daughter of King of Man,[22][23][24] Rǫgnvaldr Guðrøðarson, widow of Rhodri ab Owain, Llywelyn's uncle[25] 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Richardson, 2009, Gen-Medieval Archives: "The subject of the maternity of Llywelyn's daughter, Margaret, could doubtless occupy many posts here on the board. As best I understand the evidence, Margaret was a bit older than Lly
2 CONC welyn's two daughters by Joan, Gladys and Ellen. If we accept the possibility that Margaret might be Joan's daughter, it makes things EXTREMELY crowded in the nursery at the beginning of Llywelyn's marriage to Joan. Besides Gladys
2 CONC  and Ellen, Llywelyn and Joan also had their son and heir, David, at the first part of their marriage. I frankly can't possibly see how Margaret would fit chronologically to be Joan's daughter. Margaret either has to be the child 
2 CONC of one of Llywelyn's earlier two marriages or else illegitimate. My opinion (not conclusion) is that Margaret fits best as a child of her father's marriage to the Isles woman. That's my opinion. Not a conclusion. I'm certainly ope
2 CONC n to further discussion and hopefully new evidence. ... 
2 CONT     "Likewise, having an Isles mother would have afforded her the kinds of connections which would have made her a desirable match for the Braose family."[26] 
2 CONT
