1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT REYNOLD de Briouse (-[5 May 1227/9 Jun 1228]).  His parentage is confirmed by a charter dated 24 Jun 1217 under which King Henry III notified those on the lands of "William de Breouse in Ireland" that "Reginald de Breouse having c
2 CONC ome to his fealty, the king restores to him all the lands which belonged to his father ere"[619].  Another version of his parentage, shown to be incorrect, by this 1217 document, is recorded in the manuscript which narrates the de
2 CONC scents of the founders of Lanthony Abbey and names “Willielmus, Egidius et Reginaldus” as the three sons of “Willelmo Brewes” and his wife “Berta…comitis Milonis secunda filia”, adding that Reynold inherited all the territories of
2 CONC  his older brothers[620].  The Annals of Dunstable record that “Reginaldus frater eius” succeeded on the death of “Ægidius Herefordensis episcopus” in 1216, with the help of “Leulini Regis Walliæ” whose daughter he married[621].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly GRECIA, daughter of WILLIAM Briwere & his wife Beatrix de Vaux.  A manuscript which narrates the descents of the founders of Lanthony Abbey records that “Reginaldus de Brews” married “filiam domini Willielmus de la Bruer
2 CONC e”[622].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([1215/16]) GWLADUS Ddu, illegitimate daughter of LLEWELYN ap Iorwerth Prince of North Wales & his mistress --- (-Windsor 1251).  The Annals of Dunstable record that “Reginaldus frater eius” (referring to “Ægidius Heref
2 CONC ordensis episcopus”) married “Leulini Regis Walliæ…filiam” in 1216[623].  A manuscript narrating the foundation of Wigmore Abbey records that “Radulphus” married “Lewelinus princeps…Gwladusam Duy filiam suam”, who brought her husb
2 CONC and “omnibus terries de Kery et Kedewyn”[624].  She married secondly (1230) Ralph de Mortimer of Wigmore.  The Annals of Worcester record that “Radulphus de Mortuomari” married “filiam Lewelini, conjugem quondam Reginaldi de Breus
2 CONC a” in 1230[625].  The Annales Cambriæ record the death in 1251 of "Gladus filia domini Lewelini" at Windsor[626].]  
2 CONT
2 CONT Reynold & his first wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         WILLIAM de Briouse (-hanged 2 May 1230). 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald de Braose, 9th Baron Abergavenny (Bramber, Sussex, 1182 – June 1228 in Brecon) was the youngest son of William de Braose, 7th Baron Abergavenny and Matilda, also known as Maud, de St. Valery and Lady de Lahaie (born 1148-
2 CONC 1155), who had produced three sons, William, Giles and Reginald.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The de Braoses were loyal to King Richard I but grew in power under King John of England. The dynasty was in conflict with John of England towards the end of his reign and almost lost everything.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald de Braose was a scion of the powerful Marcher family of de Braose, helped manage its survival and was also related by marriage to the Welsh Princes of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Magna Carta Rebel
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Royal Acquiesence & Welsh Vassal
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Welsh Conflict
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 References
2 CONT
2 CONT Magna Carta Rebel
2 CONT
2 CONT He supported his brother Giles de Braose in his rebellions against King John. Both brothers were active against the King in the Baron's War. Neither was present at the signing of Magna Carta in June 1215 because at this time they 
2 CONC were still rebels who refused to compromise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal Acquiesence & Welsh Vassal
2 CONT
2 CONT King John acquiesced to Reginald's claims to the de Braose estates in Wales in May 1216. Reginald became Lord of Brecon, Abergavenny, Builth and held other Marcher Lordships but was also very much a vassal of the Welsh leader Llew
2 CONC elyn Fawr, Prince of Gwynedd who became his father-in-law in 1222 when Reginald married Llywelyn's daughter, Gwladus Ddu, known as Black Gwladys due to her raven hair.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry III restored Reginald to favour and the Bramber estates (confiscated by King John) in 1217.
2 CONT
2 CONT Welsh Conflict
2 CONT
2 CONT At this seeming betrayal, Rhys and Owain, Reginald's Welsh nephews who were Princes of Deheubarth, were incensed and they took Builth, except the castle. Llywelyn Fawr also became angry and his forces besieged Brecon. Reginald eve
2 CONC ntually surrendered to Llewelyn and gave up Seinhenydd (Swansea).
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1221 they were at war again, with Llewelyn again laying siege to Builth. The siege was relieved by King Henry III's forces. From this time on Llewelyn tended to support the claims of Reginald's nephew John de Braose concerning 
2 CONC the de Braose lands in Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald was a witness to the re-issue of Magna Carta by King Henry III in 1225.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died two or three years later in 1227 or 1228 in Brecon and was succeeded by his son, the ill-fated William de Braose, 10th Baron Abergavenny. He is buried at Brecon Cathedral. His daughter Matilda de Braose was the wife of Rhy
2 CONC s Mechyll, Prince of Deheubarth.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald de Braose, 9th Baron Abergavenny ( died June 1228 ) was one of the sons of William de Braose, 7th Baron Abergavenny and Matilda, also known as Maud de St. Valery and Lady de la Haie. Her other children included William an
2 CONC d Giles.
2 CONT
2 CONT The de Braoses were loyal to King Richard I but grew in power under King John of England. The dynasty was in conflict with King John towards the end of his reign and almost lost everything.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald de Braose was a scion of the powerful Marcher family of de Braose, helped manage its survival and was also related by marriage to the Welsh Princes of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald de BRAOSE (1171-1228) [Pedigree]
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of William de BRAOSE Lord Brecknock 5th Baron de Braiose (1144-1211) and Matilda (Maud) de ST. VALERIE (1148-1210)
2 CONT
2 CONT     b. ABT 1171, of Bramber, Sussex, Eng.
2 CONT
2 CONT     d. 9 Jun 1228, Brecon, Breconshire, Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT Married first Grace de BRIWERE (1186-1251)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT William de BRAIOSE 6th Baron de Braiose (1204-1230) m. Eve MARSHALL (1206-1246)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married second Gladys Dhu Princess of Wales (1194-1251)
2 CONT
