1 OCCU BARON OF WIGMORE (5TH), Lord of Wigmore
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Shropshire Walcot Family - Chart X: Mortimer Family; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id104.html. (Steven Ferry, May 6 , 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph de Mortimer - was born in 1190 in Wigmore, Herefordshire, England and died on 6 Aug 1246 in Wigmore, Herefordshire, England . He was the son of Roger de Mortimer and Isabel de Ferrers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph married Gwaldys Verch Llewelyn in 1220 in England. Gwaldys was born about 1205 in Caernarvonshire, Wales. She was the daughter of Llewelyn Ap Iorwerth and Tangwysti Verch Llymarch. She died in 1251 in Windsor, Berkshire, Eng
2 CONC land .
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: (Quick Family Chart)
2 CONT
2 CONT i. 	Sir Roger de Mortimer was born in 1231 in Cwmaron Castle, Radnor, Wales and died on 27 Oct 1282 in Kingsland, Hereford, Eng .
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph de Mortimer
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ranulph or Ralph de Mortimer (befor 1198 to before 2 October 1246) was the second son of Roger de Mortimer and Isabel de Ferrers of Wigmore Castle in Herefordshire He succeeded his elder brother before 23 November 1227 and built C
2 CONC efnllys and Knucklas castles in 1240.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1230, Ralph married Princess Gwladus, daughter of Llywelyn ab Iorwerth. They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger Mortimer, 1st Baron Wigmore, married Maud de Braose and succeeded his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh de Mortimer
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Mortimer
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter de Mortimer
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Remfry, P.M., Wigmore Castle Tourist Guide and the Family of Mortimer (ISBN 1-899376-76-3)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis; Lines 132C-29, 176B-28, 28-29, 67-29, 77-29, 176B-29
2 CONT
2 CONT A history of Wales from the earliest times to the Edwardian conquest (Longmans, Green & Co.) John Edward Lloyd (1911)
2 CONT
