2 CONT >>iii)        [https://www.geni.com/people/Olaf-I-Morsel-King-of-Man-the-Isles/6000000001744995580 OLAV of Man], son of ([1080]-murdered 29 Jun 1153).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names “Lagmannum, Haraldum et Olavum” as the
2 CONC  three sons of [https://www.geni.com/people/Godred-Crovan-King-of-the-Isle-of-Man-and-of-Dublin/1341991?through=6000000001744995580  “Godredus Crovan”] [1288].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that “Murecardum O’Brien re
2 CONC gem Hibernie” sent “Dompnaldum filium Tade” to island as regent for Olav after the death of Lagman, dating the event to 1111[1289].  King of Man.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that the regent ruled for three years wit
2 CONC h great tyranny until he was expelled to the Ireland by the people of Man[1290].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Olav was recalled from exile at the court of Henry I King of England in 1114 and ruled for forty year
2 CONC s[1291].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that King Olav in 1134 gave land in the Isle of Man to Yvo Abbot of Furness on which to build the abbey of Rushen[1292].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that “tres fil
2 CONC ii Haraldi fratris Olavi” who had been brought up in Dublin demanded part of the kingdom of the Isles from their paternal uncle King Olav and that one of them “Reginald” murdered his uncle, dating the murder to 29 Jun 1153 in a la
2 CONC ter passage[1293]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>m [https://www.geni.com/people/Afreca-nic-Fergus-of-Galloway/6000000002553540163?through=6000000001744995580 AUFRICA], daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Fergus-Lord-of-Galloway/6000000000769891469?through=6000000002553540
2 CONC 163 FERGUS Lord of Galloway] & his wife ---.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that “Olavus filius Godredi Crovan” married “Affricam…filiam Fergus de Galwedia”[1294]. Olav & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >1.         [https://www.geni.com/people/Gudrod-the-Black-King-of-Man-the-North-Isles/6000000002076169166?through=6000000001744995580 GODRED] (-Isle of St Patrick, Isle of Man 10 Nov 1187, bur Iona).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insul
2 CONC arum names “Godredum” as the child of “Olavus filius Godredi Crovan” and his wife “Affricam…filiam Fergus de Galwedia”[1295].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that “Godred filius eius” returned from Norway after his fath
2 CONC er was killed, murdered one of his cousins and blinded the other two, after which he reigned for 33 years[1296].  King of Man.  "Guthredus…rex Insularum" exchanged land "Eschedala" for "ecclesia sancti Olavi et villula…Euastad" wi
2 CONC th St Bees by undated charter, witnessed by "…Gillochristo fratre et collactaneo meo…"[1297].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that King Godred was defeated by Somerled Lord of Argyll in 1158 and fled to Norway to seek h
2 CONC elp[1298].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records the death “IV Id Nov” in 1187 of King Godred “in insula Sancti Patricii in Mannia” and his burial the following summer in “insulam…Hy”[1299].  m (1176) [https://www.geni.com/pe
2 CONC ople/Finnguala-mac-Lochlainn/6000000000620695488?through=6000000002076169166  FINGOLA], daughter of --- of Ireland & his wife ---.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that King Godred married “Phingola filia MacLotlen filii
2 CONC  Murkartac regis Hiberniæ”, mother of his son, in a Christian marriage ceremony in 1176[1300].  Godred & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>a)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Olaf-II-the-Black-King-of-Isle-of-Man/1341507?through=6000000000620695488 OLAV] ([1172/73]-21 May 1237, bur Rushen St Mary).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names Olav as the son of Kin
2 CONC g Godred and his wife, adding that he was three years old when his parents were married[1301].  King of Man. OLAV, son of GODRED King of Man & his wife Fingola --- ([1172/73]-21 May 1237, bur Rushen St Mary).  The Chronicon Manniæ
2 CONC  et Insularum names Olav as the son of King Godred and his wife, adding that he was three years old when his parents were married[1329].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that the Manxman chose Olav’s older brother Ragnal
2 CONC l as king, against the wishes of their father, adding in a later passage that Ragnall granted the island of Lewes to Olav but that Olav was later imprisoned by William I King of Scotland, was freed by the latter’s son King Alexand
2 CONC er I and returned to Lewes[1330].  King of Man.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Olav recovered Man from his half-brother in 1226[1331].  The History of Olave the Black King of Man records that “Olave son of Godred 
2 CONC was…King of Man” in 1229 when he resisted Alan Lord of Galloway[1332].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Olav kept Man when he agreed to divide his kingdom with his nephew Godred in 1230, the latter ruling in the Isl
2 CONC es[1333].  "Olavus rex Insularum" donated "boves…oves et porcos" to St Bees by undated charter, witnessed by "…Therkillo filio Nigelli…"[1334].  Henry III King of England made a grant of property to "Olaf King of Man and the Isles
2 CONC " for "his homage and services in guarding at his expense the coast of the English sea towards Ireland and the Isle of Man…" by charter dated 11 Jul 1235[1335].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records the death “XII Kal Jun” in
2 CONC  1237 of “Olavus Godredi filius rex Manniæ et Insularum” and his burial “in abbatia Sanctæ Mariæ de Russin”[1336]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >[https://www.geni.com/people/Gudrod-the-Black-King-of-Man-the-North-Isles/6000000002076169166?through=6000000001744995580 Godred] had three illegitimate children by unknown mistresses: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>b)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Ragnvald-IV-Gudr%C3%B6dsson-King-of-Mann-and-the-Isles/6000000001496413976?through=6000000002076169166 RAGNALD] (-killed in battle Tynwald 1228, bur St Mary of Furness).  The Chronicon Man
2 CONC niæ et Insularum names “Reginaldum, Olavum et Yvarum” as the three sons of King Godred, adding that their father had nominated his legitimate son Olav as his successor, but that the Manxmen chose Ragnald as king because he was old
2 CONC er[1302].  The birth date of his daughter, wife of the Welsh princes, indicates that Ragnald must have been much older than his legitimate half-brother.  King of Man.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Olav recovered 
2 CONC Man from his half-brother in 1226[1303].  "Ragdnaldus…rex Insularum" donated "totam medietatem terre…Ormeshan…apud portum de Corna et aciam terram Asmundertoftes" to St Bees by undated charter, witnessed by "…Gospatricio filio Hen
2 CONC rici…"[1304].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Olav was murdered in 1228 and buried at St Mary of Furness[1305].  m ---, daughter of --.  Her parentage is confirmed by the Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum which records
