1 NOTE Beauclerc means scholarly interest.
2 CONT Succeeded his older brother, William II Rufus. He was very disliked by his charges and nobles. He had many male lovers. Ascended the thorne: 1100.
2 CONT Beauclerc defeated his eldest brother Robert Curthose at the battle of Tinchebroy in Nornamdy 1106
2 CONT thus becoming King of England as well as of Nornamdy. Died at Saint Dinis of food poisoning.
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT Stuart Roderick, W.
2 CONT Royalty for Commoners, 3rd Edit. Published, Genealogical Publishing Co, Inc. Baltomore, MD. 1998,
2 CONT ISBN-0-8063-1561-X Text 324-40
2 CONT Source II
2 CONT Alison Weir, Britains Royal Family A Complete Genealogy 1999, ppg 41-44
1 NOTE He was so hated by his brothers that they vowed to disinherit him in 1087. He waged several wars to consolidate and expand his possessions. William II Rufus King of England may have been ordered killed by his younger brother Henry
2 CONC . However, there is no proof of such. After William's death while hunting in the New Forest in 1100, his brother Henry I succeeded him to the throne and ruled for 35 years. By 1106 he had captured Normandy. His reign is notable fo
2 CONC r important legal and administrative reforms. He restrained the growing power of the barons. Though Robert III, Duke of Normandy, invaded England Robert was compelled to recognize Henry as King. Henry in turn invaded Normandy due 
2 CONC to Robert's misgovernment. Henry captured Robert and held him prisoner, until Robert died on September 28, 1106. Henry defeated Robert at the battle of Tenchebrai and usurped the Duchy of Normandy. Henry had Robert blinded after c
2 CONC apturing him to insure he would never be King. Henry also defeated Louis of France in 1119 at the Bremule. [All.ftw]  Henry I was the fourth and most capable son of William the Conqueror and Matilda, born 1068, and nicknamed "Beau
2 CONC clerc" (fine scholar) for his above average education. He married Eadgyth (who later took the name Matilda), daughter of Malcolm III of Scotland, who bore him two sons and a daughter. One son died very early, and the other, Willia
2 CONC m, died in the wreck of the White Ship in November 1120, leaving the daughter, Matilda, as the sole heir. Eadgyth died in 1118, and Henry married Adelaide of Louvain, but the union produced no offspring. Henry also had two fairly 
2 CONC significant illegitimate children - Robert de Mellent, Earl of Gloucester, and Sibylla, wife of the Scottish King Alexander I. Henry's was the longest reign of the Norman line, lasting thirty-five years. The first years of Henry's
2 CONC  reign were concerned with subduing Normandy. His father divided his kingdoms between Henry's older brothers, leaving England to William and Normandy to Robert. Henry inherited no land, but received £5000 in silver. He played both
2 CONC  sides in his brothers' quarrel, leading both to distrust Henry, and sign a mutual accession treaty barring their brother from the crown. Henry's hope arose when Robert went on the First Crusade; should William die, Henry would be
2 CONC  the obvious choice. Henry was in the woods hunting on the morning of William's death, August 2, 1100. He moved quickly and was crowned king on August 5, his coronation charter denouncing William's oppressive policies and promisin
2 CONC g good government. Robert returned to Normandy a few weeks later, butescaped final defeat until 1106, at the Battle of Tinchebrai. Robert was captured and lived the remaining twenty-eight years of his life as Henry's prisoner. Hen
2 CONC ry was drawn into controversy with theChurch over the lay investiture issue - the practice of selling clergy appoints by the king to gain revenue, heavily opposed by Gregorian reformers in the Church. He ignored the situation unti
2 CONC l he was threatened with excommunication by Pope Paschal II in 1105, reaching a compromise with the papacy: he would officially denounce lay investiture, but prelates were to continue to do homage for their fiefs. In practice, it 
2 CONC changed little - the king still had the deciding voice in appointing ecclesiastical offices - but it a marked a point when kingship was viewed as purely secular, and subservienttothe Church.  A solution to the lay investiture cont
2 CONC roversy and conquest of Normandy were accomplished in 1106, allowing Henry to expand his power. Henry mixed generosity with violence in motivating allegiance to the crown, appointing loyal and gifted men to administrative position
2 CONC s. Roger of Salisbury, the most famous of Henry's servants, was instrumental in organizing a department for collectionof royal revenues, the Exchequer. The Exchequer quickly gained notoriety for sending out court officials to judg
2 CONC e local financial disputes, weakening the feudal courts controlled by local lords, and won the title "Lion of Justice".  The final years of his reign were concentrated on war with France, and succession concerns upon the death of 
2 CONC his son William in 1120. The marriage to Adelaide was fruitless, leaving Matilda his only surviving legitimate heir. She was recalled to Henry's court in 1125 after the death of her husband, Emperor Henry V of Germany; Henry force
2 CONC d the barons to swear they would accept Matilda as Queen upon Henry's death. She was then forced to marry the sixteen-year-old Geoffrey of Anjou (founder of the Plantagenet dynasty) in 1128 to continue the Angevin alliance. Themar
2 CONC riage was unpopular with the Norman barons, but Matilda and Geoffrey produced a male heir, prompting Henry to force another oath from the barons in support of Matilda.  In summer 1135, Henry refused to give custody of certain key 
2 CONC Norman castles to Geoffrey, as a show of good will, and the pair entered into war. Henry's life ended in this sorry state of affairs - war with his son-in-law - inDecember 1135. Source: www.britannia.com
2 CONT !Name is;  Henry I, "Beauclerc" King Of /ENGLAND/
2 CONT
2 CONT !From "Ancestral Roots of 60 Colonist" by WEIS, 6th ed, line 121, page 109; " Henry I, Beauclerc, b 1070 d 1 Dec 1135, King of England, 1100-1135; m 11 Nov 1100, Matilda of Scotland (1-23) b 1079 d 1May 1118. (CP V 736, VII 737: S
2 CONC P I 1-2; CCN 494). He had issue by a number of mistresses. See history books. Several more generations are given.
1 NOTE Henry I "Beauclerc" had at least 16 concubines, including thefollowing:
2 CONT Nest Verch RHYS [a princess of Wales];
2 CONT Sibyl (or Adela or Lucy) CORBET;
2 CONT Isabel (Elizabeth) De BEAUMONT;
2 CONT Edith FITZFORNE;
2 CONT Gieva De TRACY.
1 NOTE [william little,patterson 0963a cd772.FTW]
2 CONT [Genealogy.com, LLC WFT Vol. 72, Ed. 1, Tree #0963, Date of Import: May 13, 2002]
2 CONT King Henry I of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists(7th Ed) by Frederick Lewis Weis, Th.D.; F.A.S.G. (line 1)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was crowned king on Aug. 6, 1100 at Westminster Abbey. His first marriage was to Edith (renamed Matilda in honor of his mother), the elder daughter of Malcolm III, King of Scots, and St. Margaret, the granddaughter of Edmund Ir
2 CONC onside, this reinforcing the strain of Saxon royal blood in the family.
2 CONT He was well educated, learning to read and write Latin and studying English law. All in all, Henry was a wise ruler and skilled diplomatist.
2 CONT
2 CONT His only legitimate son, William, was drowned with a large entourage in the wreck of the White Ship crossing the channel from Normandy. The news so grieved the king that it was said he never smiled again.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1126 Henry designated his daughter Matilda, widow of the Emperor Henry V, to be his heir and chose a second husband for her in the person of Geoffrey Plantagenet, son of Fulk V, Count of Anjou. Although his only legitimate offs
2 CONC pring were William and Matilda, he left a large illegitimate progeny of 21 or more children, among them Robert, Earl of Gloucester, who was to champion his half-sister Matilda in her claim to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT William II (called William Rufus, the "Red King") came to the throne in 1087. He was a harsh ruler and few mourned him when he was killed by an arrow shot by an unknown hand while he was hunting (see William, Kings of England). Ro
2 CONC bert had gone off on the First Crusade, to recover the Holy Land from the Turks. A third son, Henry I, was therefore able to become king without a struggle, in 1100. When Robert returned, Henry crossed the Channel, defeated him, a
2 CONC nd gained Normandy also. He gave both England and western France a peaceful, orderly rule (see Henry, Kings of England). 
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT Excerpted from Compton’s Interactive Encyclopedia
2 CONT Copyright © 1993, 1994 Compton’s NewMedia, Inc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry I exacted a promise from the barons to recognize his daughter Matilda as their ruler. However, when he died, some of the barons broke their promise and instead chose Stephen, a grandson of William the Conqueror. Stephen was 
2 CONC a gallant knight but a weak king. Throughout his reign lawless barons fought private wars, each seeking to increase his power. Twice he was challenged by Matilda and her supporters, who nearly defeated him in 1141. When Stephen di
2 CONC ed (1154), the people were ready to welcome a strong ruler who would restore order.
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT Excerpted from Compton’s Interactive Encyclopedia
2 CONT Copyright © 1993, 1994 Compton’s NewMedia, Inc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry I (of England) (1068-1135), third Norman king of England (1100-1135), fourth son of William the Conqueror. Henry was born in Selby. Because his father, who died in 1087, left him no land, Henry made several unsuccessful atte
2 CONC mpts to gain territories on the Continent. On the death of his brother William II in 1100, Henry took advantage of the absence of another brother—Robert, who had a prior claim to the throne—to seize the royal treasury and have him
2 CONC self crowned king at Westminster. Henry subsequently secured his position with the nobles and with the church by issuing a charter of liberties that acknowledged the feudal rights of the nobles and the rights of the church. In 110
2 CONC 1 Robert, who was duke of Normandy, invaded England, but Henry persuaded him to withdraw by promising him a pension and military aid on the Continent. In 1102 Henry put down a revolt of nobles, who subsequently took refuge in Norm
2 CONC andy, where they were aided by Robert. By defeating Robert at Tinchebray, France, in 1106, Henry won Normandy. During the rest of his reign, however, he constantly had to put down uprisings that threatened his rule in Normandy. Th
2 CONC e conflict between Henry and Anselm, archbishop of Canterbury, over the question of lay investiture (the appointment of church officials by the king), was settled in 1107 by a compromise that left the king with substantial control
2 CONC  in the matter.
2 CONT Because he had no surviving male heir, Henry was forced to designate his daughter Matilda as his heiress. After his death on December 1, 1135, at Lyons-la-Fôret, Normandy, however, Henry's nephew, Stephen of Blois, usurped the thr
2 CONC one, plunging the country into a protracted civil war that ended only with the accession of Matilda's son, Henry II, in 1154."Henry I (of England)," Microsoft (R) Encarta. Copyright (c) 1994 Microsoft Corporation. Copyright (c) 19
2 CONC 94 Funk & Wagnall's Corporation.
