1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Primary Sources
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000066997717828& Inquisitions Post Mortem] for Owen (Audoenus) de la Pole. Writ, 15 Oct. 21 Edw. I [1293]. His death date is not recorded. He died before the 15 Oct. 1293 (date of wr
2 CONC it). Griffin his son, aged 2 years on Palm Sunday last, is his next heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT March 13. 1322 Derby.<br>
2 CONT To Robert de Sapy, keeper of the land of Powys, or to him who supplies his place. Order to deliver to '''Roger de Trumwyne and Joan his wife, late the wife of Owen de la Pole''', all the lands that they held of her dower in Powys,
2 CONC  which were taken into the king's hands, as Roger has found the king security that he will be faithful to him and his heirs. By K.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: 'Close Rolls, Edward II: March 1322', in Calendar of Close Rolls, Edward II: Volume 3, 1318-1323, ed. H C Maxwell Lyte (London, 1895), pp. 426-430. British History Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/cal-close-rolls/ed
2 CONC w2/vol3/pp426-430 [accessed 15 September 2017].
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, http://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/5572/Bleddyn%20ap%20Cynfyn%2030.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (May 6, 2017; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Owen de la Pole (c. 1257 – c. 1293), also known as Owain ap Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn, was the heir presumptive to the Welsh principality of Powys Wenwynwyn until 1283 when it was abolished by the Parliament of Shrewsbury. He became 
2 CONC the 1st Lord of Powis after the death of his father Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn c. 1287. He is not related to the English de la Pole family descended from William de la Pole, Chief Baron of the Exchequer in the following century, later
2 CONC  Earls and Dukes of Suffolk.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owen was born in England sometime after his father was driven into exile there in 1257 by Llywelyn ap Gruffudd the ruler of Kingdom of Gwynedd. It was during this exile that his father probably adopted the surname de la Pole meani
2 CONC ng "of the Pool" and referring to the old name for Welshpool which had become his family's capital. In 1263 following the Treaty of Montgomery his father was restored to some of his lands in return for agreeing to pay homage to Ll
2 CONC ywelyn as Prince of Wales. However, good relations between Powys-Wenwynwyn and Gwynedd were short-lived as Owen and his father were soon implicated in an assassination attempt on the Prince of Wales in 1274. This led to Owen and h
2 CONC is father fleeing to Shrewsbury where they both led border raids against the Principality of Wales on behalf of the English crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Llywelyn's defeat at the hands of Edward I of England in 1277 Owen returned to Wales alongside his father whose lands had been restored. They soon became embroiled in a border dispute with Llywelyn which was one of the c
2 CONC atalysts for a renewed campaign by the princes of Gwynedd against English domination. In 1284, following the Conquest of Wales by Edward I, the de la Pole family surrendered their princely pretentions, but received back their prin
2 CONC cipality in "free barony" as the marcher lordship of Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owen's father Gruffydd died around 1286. In 1290, he endowed his four younger brothers with portions of the lordship, which were to be held of him as his feudal tenants by service in Welsh wars. Two brothers (who were priests) rec
2 CONC eived their portions for life. The shares of the other two were to revert to Owen on failure of issue. Only William left issue, but they enjoyed the Lordship of Mawddwy covering that parish and the majority of Mallwyd for several 
2 CONC generations, before it was divided between coheirs in the early 16th century. One of the coheiresses was Elisabeth de Burgh, who married Sir John Lingen (d. 1505), and left descendants from this princely house.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owen married Joan Corbet the daughter of Robert Corbet and Catherine Le Strange and together they had a daughter and probably five sons; Griffith de la Pole, 2nd Lord of Powis, died 1309 without issue. Hawise de la Pole, "The Lady
2 CONC  of Powys" (inherited the lordship in 1309, died before 1353). She married John Charleton, 1st Baron Cherleton, (1268–1353), and their descendants owned the lordship until the late 16th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived the latter part of his life in Powys Castle in Welshpool and died c.1293. After his death, the lordship of Powis passed to his son Griffith who died in 1309 without issue, and then by his daughter Hawise and her descendan
2 CONC ts from her marriage to John Charleton, 1st Baron Cherleton. _______________________________________________________________________________ Owen ap Griffith, Prince of Powys Wenwynwyn1 b. circa 1230, d. circa 1282 Owen ap Griffit
2 CONC h, Prince of Powys Wenwynwyn|b. c 1230 d. c 1282|p403.htm#i12090|Griffith ap Gwenwynwyn, Prince of Powys-Wenwynwyn|d. b 1218|p386.htm#i26763|Hawyse Le Strange||p233.htm#i26764|Gwenwynwyn a. O. C., Prince of Powys Cyfeiliog||p233.h
2 CONC tm#i26765|Margaret Corbet of Caus|b. c 1181?|p233.htm#i26766|John Le Strange, Sheriff of Salopshire and Staffordshire|b. c 1194 d. 1276|p402.htm#i24533|Lucy de Tregoz|b. c 1210 d. 1294|p406.htm#i24534| Father	Griffith ap Gwenwynwy
2 CONC n, Prince of Powys-Wenwynwyn1 d. before 1218 Mother	Hawyse Le Strange1
