1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Medlands: [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL1.htm#BeatriceMandevilledied1197 England, Earls Created 1138–1142]
2 CONT * a) '''WILLIAM de Mandeville''' (-[1116][730]). “''Godefridus de Magnavilla''” founded Hurley Priory, Berkshire, for the souls of “''uxoris meæ Lecelinæ''…”, by undated charter witnessed by “''Lecelina domina uxor mea, Willielmus
2 CONC  de Magnavilla''…”[731]. ”''Walterus de Gant, filius et hæres Gisilberti de Gant''” restored Bardney Abbey in 1115, witnessed by “''Roberto de ---, Willielmo nepote meo constabulario Cestriæ, Willielmo de Mandevill''…”[732]. 
2 CONT * '''m''' ([1100/05]) as her first husband, '''MARGUERITE''', daughter of EUDES de Rie, dapifer, of Colchester, Essex & his wife Rohese ---. The ''Genealogia Fundatoris'' of Tintern Abbey, Monmouthshire names “''Margareta''” as da
2 CONC ughter of “''Eudoni dapifero Regis Normanniæ''”, adding that she married “''Willielmo de Mandavill''” by whom she was mother of “''Gaufridi filii comitis Essexiæ et iure matris Normanniæ dapifer''”[733]. According to the ''Complet
2 CONC e Peerage'', this genealogy is “probably erroneous” but it does not explain the basis for the doubts[734].  She married secondly ([1116/19]) Ottiwell. This second marriage is suggested by the charter dated [1141/42] under which Em
2 CONC press Matilda made various grants of property including a grant to "''Willelmo filio Otuel fratri…Comitis Gaufredi''"[735]. The only contemporary Ottiwell who has so far been identified was Ottiwell FitzHugh, illegitimate son of H
2 CONC ugh Earl of Chester: maybe he was the same person.  
2 CONT * '''William & his wife had [three] children:'''
2 CONT ** ...ii) '''BEATRIX''' ([1105]–[Rickling, Essex] 19 Apr [1197 or before], bur Walden Abbey). A manuscript listing property of Walden abbey states, quoting a charter of Stephen King of England dated 1147, that “''sororem suam…Beat
2 CONC ricem''” (referring to "''Galfridus Essexiæ comes''") married "''Hugoni Talebot''" from whom she was divorced and secondly "''Willielmo de Saye''"[737].  
2 CONT ** She became the heiress of her nephew William de Mandeville Earl of Essex, her son by her second husband, Geoffrey, being allowed to occupy her place in view of her age[738].  
2 CONT ** The ''History of the foundation of Walden abbey'' records that “''Beatrix de Mandavilla domina de Say, soror Galfridi primi, fundatoris, et amita Willielmi''” succeeded her nephew[739]. The ''History of the foundation of Walden
2 CONC  abbey'' records the death in 1200 of “''Beatrix de Say, soror fundatoris nostri et uxor Willielmi de Say''” and her burial in the abbey[740].  
2 CONT ** '''m''' firstly (divorced) '''[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3T-Z.htm#HughTalbotMBeatriceMandeville HUGH (III) Talbot]''', son of ---.  
2 CONT ** '''m''' ''secondly'' [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3P-S.htm#WilliamSaydied1155 '''WILLIAM de Say'''], son of [JORDAN de Say & his wife Lucy de Rumilly] (-[1155][741]). He fought with his brother-in-la
2 CONC w at the siege of Burwell Castle in Aug 1144.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice de Mandeville
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice de Mandeville was the daughter of William de Mandeville. She married William de Say, son of unknown de Say. She married, firstly, Hugh Talebot before 1098. She and Hugh Talebot were divorced before 1098. She died before 1
2 CONC 197.
2 CONT
2 CONT From before 1098, her married name became Talebot. 
2 CONT From 1098, her married name became Say.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children of Beatrice de Mandeville and William de Say'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * William de Say+1 d. b 1 Aug 1177
2 CONT * Geoffrey de Say+2 b. b 1135, d. bt 1212 - 1214
2 CONT
