1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Montagu,_2nd_Baron_Montagu
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Montagu''', 2nd Baron Montagu (c. 1285 to 18 October 1319) (alias de Montagu, de Montacute, Latinized to de Monte Acuto ("from the sharp mountain")), was an English peer, and an eminent soldier and courtier during the r
2 CONC eigns of Edward I and Edward II. He played a significant role in the wars in Scotland and Wales, and was appointed steward of the household to Edward II. Perhaps as a result of the influence of his enemy, Thomas, 2nd Earl of Lanca
2 CONC ster, Edward II sent him to Gascony as Seneschal in 1318. He died there in October of the following year.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/9H15-ZH3
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Montagu-40
2 CONT
2 CONT Montagu, Earls of Salisbury,
2 CONT
2 CONT Steward to the household of Edward II;From his father's inquisition postmortem, he is known to have had two brothers, John Montagu and Simon Montagu. (1) He was knighted in May 1305 by Edward II. He accompanied Edward on his Scott
2 CONC ish campaigns, and conducted campaigns against the Welsh.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married in 1292 to Elizabeth de Montfort (d. Aug. 1354), daughter of Peter de Montfort, son of Peter de Montfort of Beaudesert Castle(d. bef. March 4, 1287) and Alice Audley. Elizabeth's mother was Maud de la Mare, daughter
2 CONC  and heiress of Sir Henry de la Mare (d. 1257) Justice and Seneschal to William Longspee, 2nd Earl of Salisbury, by his wife Joan Neville, daughter of Sir John Neville and Hawise de Courtenay. Elizabeth survived her husband and ma
2 CONC rried Sir Thomas Furnival. Furnival's son Thomas, married Joan de Verdun, widow of Elizabeth's oldest son John de Montague.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.)  Douch, R. (1951). "The career, lands and family of William Montague, Earl of Salisbury, 1301 44". Bulletin of the Institute of Historical Research. London. 24: 85. doi:10.1111/j.1468-2281.1951.tb00382.x.
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard de Montagu [a] b abt 1125, d bef 1166. He md Alice abt 1148. She was b abt 1130.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Richard de Montagu and Alice was:
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Montagu [b] b abt 1156, d abt 1217. He md Isabel abt 1180. She was b abt 1164.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of William de Montagu and Isabel was:
2 CONT
2 CONT Drew de Montagu [c] b abt 1183, d abt 1216. He md Aline/Aliva Basset abt 1206, daughter of Alan Basset and Aline de Gay.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Drew de Montagu and Aline Basset was:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William de Montagu [d], Knight, b abt 1214, d 23 Sep 1270. He md Bertha abt 1236. She was b abt 1222.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of William de Montagu and Bertha was:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Simon de Montagu [e], Lord Montagu, Knight, b abt 1250, d 26 Sep 1316. He md [1] Hawise de St. Amand abt 1270, daughter of Amauri de St. Amand; and [2] Isabel abt 1287. Hawise de St. Amand was b abt 1256, d bef 1287.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Simon de Montagu and Hawise de St. Amand was:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William de Montagu [f], Lord Montagu, Knight, b abt 1272, d 18 Oct 1319, Gascony, France. He md Elizabeth de Montfort abt 1295, daughter of Sir Piers de Montfort and Maud de la Mare.
2 CONT
2 CONT Identified children of William de Montagu and Elizabeth de Montfort were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William de Montagu b abt 1302.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice de Montagu b abt 1315. She md Sir Ralph Daubeney, Knight, bef 27 Jan 1332/33, son of Sir Helie/Elis Daubeney, Lord Daubeney, and Joan
2 CONT
2 CONT He spent much of his adult life in the wars in Scotland, Wales, and abroad. In May 1304 he was with the King at the siege of Stirling. He, along with his uncle, Amauri de St. Amand, governor of Oxford Castle, and others, were impr
2 CONC isoned in the Tower for an alleged offense at Oxford, but he was soon acquitted. He was ceremoniously knighted, with many others, at the knighting of Prince Edward on 22 May 1306. In 1311 he was commissioned to survey Hastings and
2 CONC  other castles and their defenses and to provide custodians for them, and on 29 Sep he was given charge of Berkhamstead Castle. In May 1313, he accompanied the King and Queen overseas to attend the coronation of Louis X. In 1315/1
2 CONC 6 he was a commander in Wales, and in Jul 1316 was at Bristol to settle the dispute between Sir Bartholomew de Badlesmere and the burgesses. In Jun 1317, the King granted him the manors of Gravesend in Kent and Kingsbury in Somers
2 CONC et, and on 26 Sep he had a grant of free warren in Saxlingham, Norfolk, and other of his manors. He was summoned to Parliament 20 Nov 1317 and later, and in Aug 1318 was made keeper of Abingdon Abbey. In Nov of that year he was ap
2 CONC pointed Seneschal of Gascony, where he died the following year. His widow, Elizabeth, daughter of Sir Piers de Montfort of Beaudesert, co. Warwick, survived him, and on 8 Jun 1322, she married Sir Thomas de Furnival, whom she surv
2 CONC ived.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...more 'potted history from FamilySearch below:
