1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Petronille de Craon
2 CONT *Born: 1185 / 1198, Freiston, Lincolnshire, England
2 CONT *Died: 1259 / 1262, England at age 77
2 CONT
2 CONT Other names: Petronilla de Vaux, Petronille were Petronilla, Petronella, Péronelle, Pernel and DE CROUN.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources, Comments and Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Source <The history and antiquities of Leath Ward: in the county of Cumberland: with ...> Par Samuel Jefferson:
2 CONT
2 CONT "... Oliver de Vaux, who in the 13th King John, gave 500 marks for license to marry Petronell, daughter of Henry dar Mara [!!!], and to enjoy her estate, she being the widow of William de Longcamp, and lived until the 46th of Henr
2 CONC y III.  ..."
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT "GUY de Craon. ... m as her second husband, ISABEL [Basset], widow of ALBERT de Gresley, daughter of [THOMAS Basset of Headington, Oxfordshire & his wife Alice de Dunstanville]. ... Guy & his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) PETRONILLA de Craon . A genealogy of the founders of Freston Priory names "dame Petronil de Croune" as the daughter of "monsieur Wythe de Croune" and his wife "Clarice", adding that she married firstly "baron monsieur William 
2 CONC Longechaumpe" (by whom she had "monsieur Henre de Longechaumpe", and listing several generations of his descendants) and secondly "baron monsieur Oliver Vaux". "Henr de la Mare" made a fine for "terra Petronille de Croun ux sue et
2 CONC  terris suis in Angliam et Willelmus de La Mara frater suus…", dated 1205. "Henr de Mara et Petronill ux eius" claimed "dote…versus…Henr de Longo Campo" in Herefordshire, dated 1207. Her third marriage is confirmed by the Testa de
2 CONC  Nevill which includes a writ of King John dated 1212 which records that "Oliverus de Vallibus" held "Vargeburn in barunia cum uxore que fuit Henrici de la Mare" in Hampshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT *m firstly WILLIAM de Longchamp, [d 1204],  son of [Henry Longchamp d 1203 & Maud]
2 CONT **a) Henry Longchamp m Sybil Heringaud 
2 CONT **b) William Longchamp, constable of the Bishop of Lincoln in 1275
2 CONT **c) Alice Longchamp, d 1275, m William Dungevyn
2 CONT *m secondly HENRY de la Mare , son of --- (-[1210/12]). 
2 CONT *m thirdly ([1211/12]) OLIVER des Vaux, son of --- (-[1238/41]). Oliver & his wife had four children: 
2 CONT **a) ROBERT de Vaux
2 CONT **b) WILLIAM de Vaux
2 CONT **c) John de Vaux b abt 1228, of Freiston, Lincolnshire, England, d 1287. He md Sibyl de Longchamps abt 1255. She was b abt 1240.
2 CONT **d) OLIVER de Vaux."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.knight-france.com/geneal/names/2109.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Source <The history and antiquities of Boston: and the villages of Skirbeck ...> Par Pishey Thompson:
2 CONT
2 CONT "DE CROUN FAMILY.
2 CONT
2 CONT ... Wido was succeeded in the reign of Richard I., by his daughter, Petronilla de Croun, who married William de Longchamp, nephew to William Longchamp, Bishop of Ely, and son of Henry de Longchamp, Abbot of Croyland, from 1191 to 
2 CONC February 1236. Petronilla's son, Henry, by William de Longchamp, died in 1274, and his body, excepting the heart, was buried in Swineshead Abbey; but the latter was interred at Burton Pedwardine, before the altar, in the chapel of
2 CONC  the Virgin Mary. After the death of William de Longchamp, his widow, Petronilla, married one of the family of the De la Meeres, who dying, she married Oliver de Vaux, to whom she gave the manor of Freiston, with certain lands in 
2 CONC the town of Boston, and bore liiin a son, named John.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heniy Longchamp, the eldest son of Petronilla, had a daughter, named Alicia, who married Roger Pedwardine, and bore him five sons, Walter, Henry, Bryan, John, and Richard. Alicia died 15th May, 1330, and was buried in the north si
2 CONC de of the chapel of the Virgin Mary, in Burton Pedwardine, where Dr. Stdkeley saw her monument in 1714.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Source <http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3T-Z.htm#OliverVauxdied1238>: "OLIVER de Vaux (-[1238/41]). ... m ([1211/12]) as her third husband, PETRONILLA de Craon, widow firstly of WILLIAM de Longchamp [NOT the
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-William-de-Longchamp-Lord-Chancellor-of-England/6000000025333218739?through=6000000003148536972 Chancellor], but William d 1204, son of Henry Longchamp d 1204 & Maud] and secondly of HENRY de la M
2 CONC are , daughter of GUY [Wythe] de Craon & his wife Isabel ---. Oliver & his wife had four children: ...
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source <A General and Heraldic Dictionary of the Peerages of England, Ireland, and ...> Par John Burke,Bernard Burke:
2 CONT "... Robert de VAUX ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Oliver, fourth son. The fourth son, OLIVER DE VAUX, was one of the barons in arms against King John. In the 13th of that monarch's reign, he paid five hundred marks, and five palfreys, for license to marry Petronill, widow of Will
2 CONC iam de Longchamp, and daughter and heir of Guy de Croun,t feudal lord of Croun, by whom he had issue,
2 CONT
2 CONT *- Robert & William who both died s. p.
2 CONT *- JOHN, who succeeded to the estates, was steward of Acquitaine, in 1283, and had an allowance of £200 per annum, for his support in that office. He d. in 1288, leaving his two daughters, his heirs, viz. 
2 CONT **- Patronilla, m. to William de Nerford, who, in the division of the estates, had Therston and Shotestram, in the county of Norfolk; Wysete, in Suffolk, the moiety of a messuage in London, called Blaunch - Apleton, and other land
2 CONC s, amounting in all to twenty-five knights' fees.
2 CONT **- Maud, m. to William de Ros, who had the manor of Feston, and other lands in Lincolnshire, with the moiety of the messuage in London, amounting to - nineteen knight's fees. 
2 CONT *- Roger. The youngest son, ROGER DE VAUX, was grandfather of ROBERT WAUX, of Bodenham, in Cambridgeshire, whose son, ..."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ~History of Queens College . . . , "Vaux Pedigree", pg. 106, gives his wife's name as Petronilla de la Mare. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Fitz Baldric "held of the king," the manor of Sourth Warborough, at the time of the Domesday Survey. This manor was passed on to his daughter on her marriage to Guy de Craon, and was held in succession by his son, Alan, then 
2 CONC grandson, Maurice and great-grandson, Guy de Croan. The manor then passed on his daughter Petronilla, was first married to William de Longchamp, secondly to Henry de Mara, and thirdly to Oliver de Vaux, and held the manor jointly 
2 CONC with her respective husbands until her death in 1280. At the time of Petronilla's death, the manor passed on to the son of her first marriage, Henry de Longchamp.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~A History of the County of Hampshire: Volume 3, pp. 378-382
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://finerollshenry3.org.uk/content/calendar/roll_059.html
2 CONT
2 CONT 285 11 April. Westminster. Concerning the relief of a baron. The king has taken the homage of Henry de Longchamp, son and heir of Petronilla de Vaux, for the manor of South Warnborough, which Petronilla, who has recently died, hel
2 CONC d from the king. Order to William de Weilond, the king’s escheator on this side of the Trent, that, having accepted security from Henry that he will answer at the Exchequer for his relief, 1 as a baron, he is to cause him to have 
2 CONC full seisin of that manor without delay. [in the Roll]
2 CONT *1. ‘that he will answer at the Exchequer for his relief’ written over an erasure.
2 CONT **        a. The marginal county heading beside this entry in the originalia roll E 371/26, m. 5, reads ‘Hampshire’. There is also what appears to be a decorative capital ‘A’ in the margin there too.
2 CONT
