1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =ROBERT de Caen=
2 CONT
2 CONT illegitimate son of HENRY I King of England & his mistress --- ([1090]-Bristol 31 Oct 1147, bur Priory of St James, Bristol).  Also known as Robert FitzRoy.  Orderic Vitalis records him as "Robert the king's son", and specifies th
2 CONC at he led a force from the Cotentin in Sep 1123 to quell the rebellion led by Amaury de Montfort Comte d'Evreux and Waleran de Beaumont Comte de Meulan[1868].  According to the Gwentian Chronicle, Robert was the king’s son by "Nes
2 CONC t, daughter of Rhys son of Tewdwr, who was afterwards the wife of Gerald of Pembroke Castle"[1869], but this appears unlikely from a chronological point of view.  He was created Earl of Gloucester in [Jun/Sep] 1122.  He had the cu
2 CONC stody of his uncle Robert ex-Duke of Normandy as a prisoner at Bristol in 1126.  He did homage to King Stephen in 1136, but quarrelled with him in 1137, and his English and Welsh estates were forfeited.  He joined forces with his 
2 CONC half-sister "Empress" Matilda, landing in England with her in Sep 1139.  He became her commander-in-chief in the civil war.  After a series of successes against King Stephen’s forces, including the capture of the King at Lincoln i
2 CONC n Feb 1141, he himself was captured at Stockbridge 14 Sep 1141 and imprisoned at Rochester.  He was freed shortly after in exchange for the king[1870].  The Gesta Stephani Regis records the death of "comes…Glaorniæ" at Bristol, da
2 CONC ted from the context to [1147][1871]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m (before [1112]) '''MABEL [Matilda or Sibylle] FitzRobert, daughter & heiress of ROBERT FitzHamon Lord of Glamorgan and Gloucester & his wife Sibylle de Montgomery''' (-[29 Sep] 1157).  The Chronica de Fundatoribus et Fundatione 
2 CONC of Tewkesbury Abbey names (in order) “Mabiliam, Hawysiam, Ceciliam, Amisiam” as the four daughters of ”Robertum filium Haymonis, dominum de Astramervilla in Normannia”, recording that Mabile married Robert Fitzroy[1872].  Orderic 
2 CONC Vitalis records that “Rodbertus Henrici regis filius” married “Rodberto Haimonis filio...[et] Sibiliam Rogerii comitis filiam...filiam...Mathildem”[1873].  Robert of Torigny records that "filia Roberti Belismensis" was the mother 
2 CONC of "Rogerius Wigornensis episcopus", son of "Robertus comes Gloecestrensis…filius primi Henrici regis Anglorum", clarifying that the bishop's grandfather was "Robertus filius Haimonis dominus de Torigneio"[1874].  The Annals of Te
2 CONC wkesbury record that “Mabilia comitissa Gloucestriæ” died in 1157[1875]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Robert & his wife had [seven] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         '''WILLIAM FitzRobert''' (23 Nov [1112]-23 Nov 1183, bur Keynsham Abbey, Somerset).  The Chronica de Fundatoribus et Fundatione of Tewkesbury Abbey records that “comes Willielmus filius et hæres eiusdem” succeeded on th
2 CONC e death of Robert Earl of Gloucester[1876].  He succeeded his father in 1147 as Earl of Gloucester. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         '''ROGER FitzRobert''' (-Tours 9 Aug 1179, bur Tours).  The Annals of Tewkesbury record that “Rogerus filius comitis Gloecestriæ” was appointed Bishop of Worcester in Mar 1163, and consecrated “X Kal Sep” in 1164[1877].
2 CONC   Robert of Torigny records the death in 1180 of "Rogerius Wigornensis episcopus" at "Turonis", specifying that his father was "Robertus comes Gloecestrensis…filius primi Henrici regis Anglorum" and his mother "filia Roberti Belis
2 CONC mensis"[1878]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         '''HAMON FitzRobert''' (-killed at the siege of Toulouse 1159).  The Chronicle of Melrose records the death in 1159 at Toulouse of "Hamo the son of the earl of Gloucester, deceased"[1879]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.         '''PHILIP FitzRobert''' (-after 1147).  The Gesta Stephani Regis records that "Philippum filium Glaornensis comitis" was given command of Cricklade in succession to "Willelmus…de Dovre" but later joined King Stephen, da
2 CONC ted from the context to [1147][1880].  He went on a pilgrimage to the Holy Land[1881].  "Philippo quoque filio comitis Gloecestrie" is recorded as present in a letter from Hugues Archbishop of Rouen to Emma abbess of St Amand, in 
2 CONC a charter dated 1147[1882].
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.         '''MATILDA FitzRobert''' (-29 Jul 1190).  Robert of Torigny refers to the wife of "Ranulfus comes Cestriæ" as "filia Roberti comitis Gloecestriæ"[1883].  The Rotuli de Dominabus of 1185 records property “Wadinton de feo
2 CONC do comitis Cestrie” held by “Matillis comitissa Cestrie…filia Roberti comitis Gloecestrie, filii regis Henrici primi”[1884].  It was alleged that she and William Peverell of Nottingham poisoned her husband[1885].  "Hugo comes Cest
2 CONC rie" confirmed a donation of land in Thoresby donated by "Willelmus filius Othuer" to Greenfield priory, Lincolnshire, for the soul of "patris mei Randulfi", by charter dated to [1155] witnessed by "Matilla matre sua…"[1886].  The
2 CONC  Annals of Tewkesbury record the death “IV Kal Aug” in 1190 of “Matildis comitissa Cestria”[1887].  m ([1141]) RANULF “de Gernon” Earl of Chester, son of RANULPH “le Meschin” Vicomte de Bayeux & his wife Lucy --- (Château de Gerno
2 CONC n, Normandy before 1100-[murdered] 16 Dec 1153, bur Chester, Abbey of St Werburg). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         '''MABEL FitzRobert.'''  "Mabira daughter of Earl Robert, mother of Jordan de Campo Ernulfi and lady of Maisoncelles [Mansum cellarum]" donated property to the abbey of Saint-Etienne, Plessis-Grimoud by charter dated [1
2 CONC 170][1888].  [1889][m AUBREY de Vere]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.         '''RICHARD FitzRobert''' ([1120/35]-1175).  His parentage is confirmed by the undated charter under which his son "Roger de Croylet, fils de Richard fils du comte de Gloucester" donated property to the abbey of Ardennes
2 CONC , Calvados[1890].  He succeeded his mother as Sire de Creully. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Robert had [four] [illegitimate] children:  
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.          '''RICHARD''' (-3 Apr 1142).  Orderic Vitalis names Richard as son of "the king's son Robert", and records that he was appointed Bishop of Bayeux in 1135[1891], having obtained the Pope's dispensation to hold religious
2 CONC  office despite his illegitimate birth[1892].  The Gesta Stephani Regis records the death of "Roberti Glaornensis comitis filius", dated from the context to [1142][1893].  The son in question is not named and it is not certain tha
2 CONC t this entry refers to Richard. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.          '''ROBERT FitzRobert''' (-1170).  The 1155 Pipe Roll records "Rob. fil. Com. Gloecestr." in Somerset[1894].  Named in charters in [1160], and addressed as Castellan of Gloucester[1895].  Robert of Torigny records the d
2 CONC eath in 1170 of "Roberto filio Roberti comitis Gloecestriæ"[1896].  [1897]m ([1147]) HAWISE de Reviers, daughter of BALDWIN de Reviers Earl of Devon & his first wife Adelisa --- (-[1215]).  The 1194/95 Pipe Roll records "Agnes uxo
2 CONC r Roberti f comitis de Gloecr" in Lincolnshire[1898].  Robert & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT a)         [1899]MABEL .  m firstly JORDAN de Chambernon, son of ---.  m secondly WILLIAM de Soliers, son of ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.       '''MABEL''' .  She is referred to as Earl Robert's illegitimate daughter in Domesday Descendants[1900].  It is not known whether she is the same person as Mabel who is shown above as Earl Robert's legitimate daughter.  m
2 CONC  GRIFFITH ap IVOR Bach . 
2 CONT
2 CONT 11.       [--- .  It is not known whether the parent of Thomas was one of the other illegitimate children of Robert Earl of Gloucester who are named above.  According to Domesday Descendants, Thomas was nephew of Earl William’s fa
2 CONC ther Robert, citing a charter of the Earldom of Gloucester (not yet consulted)[1901].  m ---.]  One child: 
2 CONT a)         THOMAS (-after 1176).  The 1176/77 Pipe Roll names "Tomas nepos comitis Gloecestr et Ricardus filius eius" in Dorset and Somerset[1902].  m ---.  The name of Thomas’s wife is not known.  Thomas & his wife had one child:
2 CONC   i)          RICHARD .  The 1176/77 Pipe Roll names "Tomas nepos comitis Gloecestr et Ricardus filius eius" in Dorset and Somerset[1903]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert,_1st_Earl_of_Gloucester
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Robert, 1st Earl of Gloucester''' (c. 1090 – October 31, 1147) was an illegitimate son of King Henry I of England, and one of the dominant figures of the period of English history sometimes called The Anarchy. He is also known 
2 CONC as Robert of Caen, and Robert "the Consul", though both names are used by later historians and have little contemporary justification, other than the fact that Robert's clerks made a practice of using the Latin word consul rather 
2 CONC than the more common comes for his title of 'Earl'.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Early life===
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was the eldest of Henry's many illegitimate children. He was born well before his father's accession to the English throne, probably in the late 1080s, as he had himself had a son by 1104. Although generally said to have be
2 CONC en the son of Sybil Corbet, his mother is not known for certain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was acknowledged at birth, though in view of the vicissitudes of his father's career between 1087 and 1096 it is unlikely he was raised in his household. He was educated to a high standard, was literate in Latin and had a s
2 CONC erious interest in both history and philosophy, which indicates that he was at least partly raised in a clerical household, a suggestion made all the more likely as his first known child, born around 1104, was born to a daughter o
2 CONC f Samson, Bishop of Worcester (died 1112) who up till 1096 had been a Royal Chaplain and Treasurer of Bayeux. It may be significant that his next brother Richard was brought up in an episcopal household, that of Robert Bloet, bish
2 CONC op of Lincoln. Robert later received dedications from both Geoffrey of Monmouth and William of Malmesbury. William's 'Historia Novella' contains a flattering portrait of the Earl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert appears at court in Normandy in 1113, and around 1114 he married Mabel, eldest daughter and heir of Robert Fitzhamon, who brought him the substantial honour of Gloucester in England, Glamorgan in Wales and the honours of Sa
2 CONC inte-Scholasse-sur-Sarthe and Évrecy in Normandy, as well as Creully. In 1121 or 1122 his father created him Earl of Gloucester.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Career at court===
2 CONT
2 CONT St Marys Church, Luton town centre, founded in 1121 by Robert, 1st Earl of Gloucester.Robert developed a role as one of his father's principal aides and Captains. In 1119, he fought at the Battle of Bremule, and in 1123-24 he was 
2 CONC one of the King's chief commanders during the Norman rebellion. Following the drowning of the King's only legitimate son, William Adelin, in 1120, Robert became increasingly caught up in his father's attempts to ensure the success
2 CONC ion of the Empress Matilda, Robert's half-sister. It was to Robert's custody in his castle of Cardiff that his uncle, the deposed Duke Robert Curthose was eventually confided in 1126. On 1 January 1127 it was Robert who was one of
2 CONC  the first to swear to accept Matilda as Queen after Henry's death. His father at some point gave him the keeping of the castles of Dover and Canterbury, and thus control of Kent and the cross-Channel route. When King Henry fell m
2 CONC ortally ill at Lyons-la-Forêt in Normandy on 25 November 1135, Earl Robert was at his side and was one of the magnates who swore to stay with the King's body until it was buried. The King died a week after falling ill, on 1 Decemb
2 CONC er 1135.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Relationship with King Stephen===
2 CONT
2 CONT After his father's death, Robert attended a series of conferences in Normandy and eventually accepted as King Theobald IV, Count of Blois and King Henry's oldest nephew by his sister Adela. However, during the meeting with Theobal
2 CONC d, news reach the Norman magnates that Theobald's younger brother, Stephen of Mortain and Boulogne, had been accepted and crowned as King in England. Robert eventually accepted this and at Easter 1136 attended the new King's cerem
2 CONC onial court. He does not seem to have seriously considered supporting the Empress Matilda, and did not assist her invasion of southern Normandy. There is evidence in the contemporary source, the Gesta Stephani, that Robert was pro
2 CONC posed by some as a candidate for the throne, but his illegitimacy ruled him out:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Among others came Robert, Earl of Gloucester, son of King Henry, but a bastard, a man of proved talent and admirable wisdom. When he was advised, as the story went, to claim the throne on his father's death, deterred by sounder a
2 CONC dvice he by no means assented, saying it was fairer to yield it to his sister's son (the future Henry II of England), than presumptuously to arrogate it to himself." 
2 CONT
2 CONT This suggestion cannot have led to any idea that he and Stephen were rivals for the Crown, as Geoffrey of Monmouth in 1136 referred to Robert as one of the 'pillars' of the new King's rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert of Gloucester had other distractions in 1136 which put the succession question out of his mind. The Welsh princes of south east Wales rose against the Anglo-Norman settlers of the Welsh Marches in April and Robert spent muc
2 CONC h of the year stabilising the situation in that region. He reached peace treaties with the Welsh and recognised the gains of Morgan ab Owain (died 1158), who called himself King of Glamorgan. In England, Robert of Gloucester soon 
2 CONC became disenchanted with King Stephen, and by the end of 1137 had withdrawn from his Court. It is clear that he was disgruntled that he did not occupy the central place in politics that he had in the last reign. He was also alarme
2 CONC d at the favour with which the King regarded his Flemish mercenary general, William of Ypres, and the rising power of the Beaumont twins, Waleran de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Worcester, and Robert de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Leicester. I
2 CONC n 1138, Robert declared his support for the Empress Matilda, but he was defeated in Normandy by Waleran and his English allies were crushed by Stephen and driven back on his fortress of Bristol.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===The Civil War, 1139-1147===
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Robert took a great gamble and sailed for England with his half-sister, the Empress, his wife and a company of knights. They landed at Arundel on 30 September 1139, and were welcomed into Arundel Castle there, the possession 
2 CONC of Queen Adeliza, Matilda's stepmother. Robert left for Bristol immediately. In his absence the castle was blockaded by King Stephen, opening the possibility that he might seize his dynastic rival. The King in the end let the Empr
2 CONC ess and Countess depart, under escort, to Bristol.
2 CONT
2 CONT With Earl Robert and the Empress in England and based in the West Country and Severn valley, the civil war had begun. The Earl's first moves are revealing. He commanded raids against Wareham in Dorset and Worcester. Both were poss
2 CONC essions of the Beaumonts. He took Robert of Leicester's lands in Dorset for his own. He did much the same to other royalists within his area, mass deprivations which were at the heart of what is called the Anarchy. Although secure
2 CONC  in a heartland of support, Earl Robert did not find it easy to recruit wider support and break out. The King succeeded in containing him along the line of the Cotswold Hills, with such effect that both sides were willing to send 
2 CONC representatives to a peace conference held at Bath in August 1140, though nothing came of it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Robert's big opportunity came at Christmas 1140, when King Stephen fell out with Earl Ranulf II of Chester. Ranulf's failed negotiations with the King to secure Lincoln Castle led him to ally with Robert, his father-in-law. T
2 CONC hey united their forces at Castle Donington in January 1141, including a host of Welsh mercenaries allied to Earl Robert. On 2 February 1141 the Earls met and defeated King Stephen at the Battle of Lincoln. With the King captive, 
2 CONC Empress Matilda should have secured the throne, but a combination of stubborn royalist support, the Empress's miscalculation and military misjudgement led to her failure. On 14 September 1141 Earl Robert and the Empress were trapp
2 CONC ed by a royalist army in an ill-judged attempt to seize control of Winchester. Earl Robert was captured fighting a rearguard action against the forces of Matilda of Boulogne, Stephen's wife, at the river crossing of Stockbridge to
2 CONC  allow his sister to escape. Earl Robert was imprisoned for two months at Rochester Castle before he was released in an exchange with King Stephen. The cross-over point in the joint release was on 1 November 1141 at Winchester, wh
2 CONC ere the two men had a chance to exchange friendly remarks, and the Earl apparently assured the King that there was nothing personal in the fight as far as he was concerned.
2 CONT
2 CONT The war continued and it rapidly became evident that it was a stalemate. The Empress's husband refused to commit the resources to tip the balance in England, only agreeing to discuss matters with the Earl. In June 1142 Robert cros
2 CONC sed from Wareham to Normandy and stayed there till the end of October. He came back with no reinforcements, but with his nephew Henry, the son of the Empress. In the meantime the Empress had been trapped in Oxford. Nothing could b
2 CONC e done to release her, and she had to manage her own escape from the castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert continued the struggle but with less and less hope of ultimate victory. The King also had limited resources, but managed slowly to push towards Robert's centres of Bristol and Gloucester. At the end of 1145 Philip, Earl Rob
2 CONC ert's son and military Captain, defected to Stephen, taking with him the strategic castles of Cricklade and Cirencester. With Gloucester and Bristol under threat, the Earl opened negotiations in the autumn of 1146. The pressure co
2 CONC ntinued in 1147, and it was in a desperate attack on Farnham in Surrey in the late summer of that year that Earl Robert fought his last unsuccessful action of the war. He retired to Bristol to gather new forces, but became feveris
2 CONC h. He died on 31 October 1147 and was buried in the priory of St James he had founded outside the castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family and children===
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, around 1114, Mabel of Gloucester (died 1156), daughter of Robert Fitzhamon and Sibyl de Montgomery. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT *''' William Fitz Robert''', 2nd Earl of Gloucester, died 1183. He married Hawise (died 1197) daughter of Robert II, Earl of Leicester. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Roger, Bishop of Worcester''', (died 9 August 1179, Tours). 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Hamon''', killed at the siege of Toulouse in 1159. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *''' Robert.''' (died before 1157) Also called Robert of Ilchester in documents. He married Hawise, (died after 1210) daughter of Baldwin de Redvers and Adeliz. Their daughter Mabel married Jordan de Cambernon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Matilda,''' (died 1189), wife of Ranulph de Gernon, 2nd Earl of Chester. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *''' Philip, Castellan of Cricklade''', (died after 1147). He took part in the Second Crusade. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Earl Robert had an illegitimate son, '''Richard, bishop of Bayeux''' (1135-1142), by Isabel de Douvres, sister of Richard de Douvres, bishop of Bayeux (1107-1133).
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT
2 CONT Natural son of Henry I of England. [Ped. of Charlemagne]
2 CONT
2 CONT 'The Consul', Earl of Gloucester, 1122-47. [Ped. of Charlemagne]
2 CONT
2 CONT Initially supported Stephen of Blois as successor of his father, Henry I. Robert led a revolt against Stephen in 1138 which stripped Stephen of Caen and half of Normandy. During the next 17 years England was in a constant state of
2 CONC  dissension between the factions wishing to be on the throne. At one point Earl Robert was captured during a battle at Devizes. Later in 1142 Stephen himself was besieged and exchanged himself for Earl Robert. [WBH - England]
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of Mellent, who was created, in 1109, Earl of Gloucester, natural son of Henry I by Elizabeth de Bellomont. [Magna Charta Barons, p. 88]
2 CONT
2 CONT Chief of King Stephen's nobles. A plot which Robert of Gloucester had been weaving from the outset of Stephen's reign came to a head in 1138, and the Earl's revolt stripped Stephen of Caen and half Normandy. [Nations of the World 
2 CONC - England, p. 162-3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lincoln Castle, Feb 1141 -- Robert comes to the aid of Ranulf of Chester who is being beseiged in his castle by King Stephen. Robert's superior forces soon overpower Stephen who refuses to flee. Stephen is captured and held in Rob
2 CONC ert's castle in chains. In November Robert and his half-sister Empress Matilda met Stephen's forces outside Winchester and this time Earl Robert was captured. Robert and Stephen were then exchanged, but Stephen is king again. The 
2 CONC civil war is not over. [Chronicle of the Royal Family, p. 45]
2 CONT
2 CONT Illegitimate son of Henry I; 1st Earl of Gloucester, m. Mabel FitzHamon; father of Maud of Gloucester, Mabira de Caen and William Fitz Robert, 2nd Earl of Gloucester. [The Royal Descents, p. 387, 389, 396]
2 CONT
2 CONT Patron of Geoffrey of Monmouth, the historian who wrote HISTORIA REGUM BRITANNIAE (The History of the Kings of Britain). After his father King Henry I's death in 1135, Robert was the most dedicated defender of the right of his hal
2 CONC f-sister, Matilda, to succeed their father; he and his fellow Marcher Lords refused to acknowledge the authority of Stephen, Henry's nephew and the anointed king of England. [A History of Wales, p. 124]
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of Gloucester; natural son of Henry I, king of England, and Elizabeth de Bellomont; m. Mabel FitzHamon; father of Maud of Gloucester. [Charlemagne & Others, Chart 2917]
2 CONT
2 CONT Natural son of Henry I and a Welsh princess named Nesta who had been made a prisoner during some fighting along the Marches. He was a man of lofty ideals, of great courage and compassion, a capable leader and soldier. Present at h
2 CONC is father's death. [The Conquering Family, p. 9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of Gloucester; d. 1147; m. Mabel, dau. of Robert Fitz Hamon; father of William Fitz Robert. [Ancestral Roots, p. 66]
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Henry I, king of England, by an unknown mistress. [Ancestral Roots, p. 112]
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of Gloucester; m. Mabel Fitz Hamon; father of Philip Fitz Robert; died 31 Oct 1147 of fever in Bristol, England. [Charlemagne & Others, Chart 2968]
2 CONT
2 CONT The open declaration of Robert of Gloucester for Matilda in 1138, and the landing of the empress herself in the following year, were followed by the secession from Stephen of the greater part of Western England. [The Victoria Hist
2 CONC ory of the Counties of England, p. 358]
2 CONT
2 CONT The southwestern counties rose at the instigation of Robert of Gloucester (illegitimate son of Henry I and brother to Matilda), who had thrown off his allegiance to Stephen and fled abroad, allegint that Stephen was a usurper of t
2 CONC he throne. In the fall of 1139, Robert and his sister Matilda came back to England from abroad. The arrival converted the unrest, already manifest, into a civil war which lasted for 14 years. In 1141 King Stephen was defeated. He 
2 CONC was sent to prison in Bristol but the civil war went on. Year after year Matilda lost ground. The death of Robert of Gloucester, in 1147, deprived hr of her chief support and the following year she retired to Anjou and gave up her
2 CONC  struggle. [The Fosters of Flanders, England, and America, p. 9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, Earl of Gloucester, was responsible for the building in masonry of a polygonal keep at Cardiff, probably a precaution taken against the Welsh uprising of 1136, which followed the death of Henry I the previous year, and whi
2 CONC ch resulted in general civil discontent. The keep dominates the castle enclosure not only by its extraordinary height, but by its sheer size. [The Castles of Wales, p. 62]
2 CONT
2 CONT When Robert the Consul became Lord of Cardiff Castle in the 12th century it already had a history going back over a thousand years. The Romans built their first fort on the site almost 2000 years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, Henry I, raised Robert to the earldom of Gloucester and made him lord of Glamorgan in 1122. The earl or 'consul' of Gloucester dominated the political scene in England after the death of his father during the long and 
2 CONC bitter struggle for the throne between Matilda and Stephen. It was 'Robert the Consul', lauded on all sides as a brave soldier, wise statesman and patron of the arts, who is credited with having built the first stone keep of Cardi
2 CONC ff Castle, and it was in the keep that he imprisoned at King Henry's behest, another Robert--the second duke of Normandy and his father's older brother--from 1126 until Robert of Normandy's death in 1134.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert 'the Consul' died in 1147, to be succeeded by his son William. [Cardiff Castle].
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the chief supporter of the royal claimant Matilda during her war with King Stephen of England (reigned 1135-54).
2 CONT ...............................
2 CONT
2 CONT The illegitimate son of King Henry I of England (reigned 1100-35), he was made Earl of Gloucester in 1122. After the death of Henry I and usurpation of power by Stephen (December 1135), Gloucester became the leader of the party lo
2 CONC yal to Matilda, his half sister, who had been designated heir to the throne by Henry I. He took Matilda to England in September 1139 and at the head of her forces won from Stephen most of western England and southern Wales. In Feb
2 CONC ruary 1141 he captured Stephen at Lincoln and imprisoned him in Bristol. Later that year Gloucester was captured at Winchester, Hampshire, and exchanged for the king. He continued to be the mainstay of Matilda's cause until his de
2 CONC ath. The 12th-century chroniclers considered Gloucester an able and sagacious leader.
2 CONT
