2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Maud of Gloucester
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maud_of_Gloucester
2 CONT
2 CONT Maud of Gloucester, Countess of Chester (died 29 July 1190), also known as Maud FitzRobert, was an Anglo-Norman noblewoman, and the daughter of Robert, 1st Earl of Gloucester, an illegitimate son of King Henry I of England. Her hu
2 CONC sband was Ranulf de Gernon, 4th Earl of Chester, whom she allegedly poisoned with the assistance of William Peverel of Nottingham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Maud FitzRobert was born on an unknown date, the daughter of Robert, 1st Earl of Gloucester and Mabel FitzHamon of Gloucester. She had seven siblings including William Fitz Robert, 2nd Earl of Gloucester and Roger, Bishop of 
2 CONC Worcester. She also had an illegitimate half-brother, Richard, Bishop of Bayeux, whom her father sired by Isabel de Douvres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were King Henry I of England and his mistress, Sybil Corbet. Her maternal grandparents were Robert FitzHamon, Lord of Gloucester and Glamorgan, and Sybil de Montgomery, daughter of Roger de Montgomery, 1s
2 CONC t Earl of Shrewsbury and Mabel Talvas of Belleme.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime before 1141, Lady Maud married Ranulf de Gernon, 4th Earl of Chester. She assumed the title of Countess of Chester upon her marriage. Her husband had considerable autonomy in his palatine earldom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after their marriage, in January 1141, Maud was besieged at Lincoln Castle by the forces of King Stephen of England. A relief army, loyal to Empress Matilda and led by her father, defeated the King in the fierce fighting w
2 CONC hich followed, which became known as the First Battle of Lincoln. In return for his help in repelling the King's troops, Maud's father compelled Ranulf to swear fealty to his half-sister Matilda. Ranulf was seized by King Stephen 
2 CONC at court in Northampton on 29 August 1146. Stephen later granted him the castle and city of Lincoln sometime after 1151.
2 CONT
2 CONT Together Ranulf and Maud had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Hugh de Kevelioc, 5th Earl of Chester (1147- 30 June 1181), married Bertrade de Montfort of Evreux, by whom he had five children, including Ranulf de Blondeville, 6th Earl of Chester, Maud of Chester, and Hawise of Chester, 1st
2 CONC  Countess of Lincoln.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Richard of Chester (died 1170/1175), buried in Coventry.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Beatrice of Chester, married Raoul de Malpas
2 CONT
2 CONT Ranulf had an illegitimate son, Robert FitzCount (died before 1166), by an unknown mistress. His date of birth was not recorded. Robert married as her second husband, Agnes FitzNeel.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 16 December 1153, Maud allegedly poisoned her husband with the assistance of William Peverel of Nottingham. In 1172, she founded Repton Priory in Derbyshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rotuli de Dominabus of 1185 records property Wadinton de feodo comitis Cestrie, held by Maud, Countess of Chester.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maud died on 29 July 1190. The Annals of Tewkesbury records the death in 1190 of Maud, Countess of Chester.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
