2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Lovel-19
2 CONT * [https://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vol5/pp135-145] '''Inquisitions Postmortem for John Lovel of Tychemersh, Writ, 1 Oct. 4 Edw. II [1310].''' [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000073398956648&]  
2 CONC mention:  John Lovel his father,  Isabel his wife, Maud their daughter, the wife of William de la Suche.   His death date is not recorded. He died before 1 Oct. 1310 (date of writ). John his son, aged 22, was his next heir.  Maud 
2 CONC his daughter, aged 30 and more was his next heir of the lands, &c. in Dockyngg, as acquired in free marriage.
2 CONT * '''Watney, Vernon James. ''The Wallop Family and Their Ancestry'' Vol. 2 (Oxford: John Johnson, 1928), 504-505.''' See attached document of these pedigree pages in Sources.  [https://www.familysearch.org/library/books/records/it
2 CONC em/206419-the-wallop-family-and-their-ancestry-vol-2?viewer=1&offset=3#page=1&viewer=picture&o=&n=0&q=]
2 CONT * '''Cokayne, George Edward, Vicary Gibbs, H. A. Doubleday, Howard de Walden, eds. '' The Complete Peerage; or, A History of the House of Lords and All Its Members from the Earliest Times'', 2nd ed., Vol 8–Lindley to Moate''' (Lon
2 CONC don: St Catherine Press, 1932), 199-225. -- See attached document of all pages pertaining to the Lovel lineage.m[https://www.familysearch.org/library/books/records/item/594161-redirectiona]
2 CONT * '''''John, Lord Lovell and Holland, also called "the Great Lord Lovel''l"''' by Monika E Simon. "John Lovell VII, who is generally referred to as the fifth Lord Lovell, came of age in 1363 and on 8 June the escheators of several
2 CONC  counties were ordered to give him seisin of his lands.  "At this point, the fortunes of his family were at a distinctly low point. His great-grandfather John Lovell III had been the first Lovell to be summoned to parliament and o
2 CONC nce had been the marshal of Edward I’s army in Scotland. It was the death of his son, John Lovell IV, at the battle of Bannockburn on 24 June 1314, only four years after his father died, that started the longtime of decline. First
2 CONC  came the long minority of his posthumous son John Lovell V. John Lovell V died at the young age of 33 on 3 November 1347. Another long period of guardianship followed as his eldest son; John Lovell VI was only six years old when 
2 CONC his father died. Isabel, the wife of John Lovell V, died two years after her husband in 1449 only a year after the death of Joan de Ros, the grandmother of John Lovell V. This meant that all the Lovell estates were in the hands of
2 CONC  guardians. The long periods of guardianship must have surely had a detrimental effect on the profitability of the Lovell estates. Additionally, the lands were also devastated by the Plague. In Titchmarsh, for example, only four o
2 CONC f the eight tenants survived the Plague. John Lovell VI died a minor on 12 July 1361 and his younger brother, John Lovell VII, who was also underage, inherited his lands."  [https://historytheinterestingbits.com/2021/09/10/guest-p
2 CONC ost-john-lord-lovell-and-holland-also-called-the-great-lord-lovell-by-monika-e-simon/]
2 CONT * '''Monika E. Simon published a book on 31 August 2021 titled ''From Robber Barons to Courtiers: The Changing World of the Lovells of Titchmarsh'''''  [https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/57165132-from-robber-barons-to-courtiers]
2 CONC   Monika studied Medieval History, Ancient History, and English Linguistics and Middle English Literature at the Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität, Munich, from which she received an MA. She wrote her DPhil thesis about the Lovells o
2 CONC f Titchmarsh at the University of York. She lives and works in Munich.
2 CONT *Source: Douglas Richardson. '''Royal Ancestry''': A Study in Colonial and Medieval Families, 5 vols, ed. Kimball G. Everingham (Salt Lake City: the author, 2013), volume III, page 626, LOVEL 10.
2 CONT **"John Lovel, born about 1255. He married (1st) about 1270 Isabel (or Elizabeth) Du Bois, daughter of Arnold du Bois, by Alice, daughter of Henry de Tubney. They had one daughter, Maud. His wife, Isabel, died in or before 1279. H
2 CONC e married (2nd) Joan De Roos, daughter of Robert de Roos, by Isabel, daughter of William d'Aubeney. They had three sons, John [2nd Lord Lovel], James, and Thomas."
