1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Roger Mortimer of Wigmore http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_Mortimer_of_Wigmore
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger de Mortimer (d. before 8 July 1214) was a medieval marcher lord, residing at Wigmore Castle in the English county of Herefordshire. He was the son of Hugh de Mortimer (d. 26 February 1181 and Matilda Le Meschin. He was born 
2 CONC before 1153
2 CONT
2 CONT Before 1196 he married Isabel (d. before 29 April 1252), the daughter of Walchelin de Ferriers of Oakham Castle in Rutland. They had at least four sons and a daughter. Roger was succeeded by his two sons in turn, Hugh de Mortimer 
2 CONC (d.1227) and Ralph de Mortimer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger killed the Welsh prince Cadwallon ap Madog in an ambush in Elfael on 22 September 1179 and was imprisoned for two years in Winchester Castle as punishment by King Henry II. With Hugh de Say of Richards Castle he fought and l
2 CONC ost the battle of New Radnor against Rhys ap Gruffydd in 1196, allegedly loosing some forty knights and an innumerable number of foot in the fight.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources Remfry., P.M., Wigmore Castle Tourist Guide and the Family of Mortimer (ISBN 1-899376-76-3) Cockayne, George E. Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom/13 Volumes Bound in 6 Boo
2 CONC ks, IV:191; IX:272-3
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Roger Mortimer of Wigmore From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Roger de Mortimer (died before 8 July 1214) was a medieval marcher lord, residing at Wigmore Castle in the English county of Herefordshire. He was the son of Hugh de 
2 CONC Mortimer (d. 26 February 1181 and Matilda Le Meschin. He was born before 1153 CEarly life
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger would appear to have been of age in 1174 when he fought for King Henry II against the rebellion of his son, Henry. In 1179 Roger was instrumental in the killing of Cadwallon ap Madog, the prince of Maelienydd and Elfael, bot
2 CONC h of which Mortimer coveted. He was imprisoned until June 1182 at Winchester for this killing. [edit]Children
2 CONT
2 CONT He had married Isabel (d. before 29 April 1252), the daughter of Walchelin de Ferriers of Oakham Castle in Rutland before 1196. With Isabel, Roger had three sons and a daughter: Hugh de Mortimer (d.1227) Ralph de Mortimer (d.1246)
2 CONC . Philip Mortimer Joan Mortimer (d.1225) - married May 1212 to Walter de Beauchamp[1] He is often wrongly stated to have been the father of Robert Mortimer of Richards Castle (died 1219) - married Margary de Say[2], daughter of Hu
2 CONC gh de Say. This Robert was born before 1155 and therefore could not have been a son of Roger. [edit]Lord of Maelienydd
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1195 Roger, with the backing of troops sent by King Richard I invaded Maelienydd and rebuilt Cymaron Castle. In 1196 he joined forces with Hugh de Say of Richards Castle and fought and lost the battle of New Radnor against Rhys
2 CONC  ap Gruffydd, allegedly losing some forty knights and an innumerable number of foot in the fight. By 1200 he had conquered Maelienydd and issued a new charter of rights to Cwmhir Abbey. In the summer of 1214 he became gravely ill 
2 CONC and bought the right for his son to inherit his lands while he still lived from King John. He died before 8 July 1214. [edit]External links
2 CONT
2 CONT Cefnllys Castle The Battle of Radnor FMG on Roger de Mortimer [edit]Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Remfry., P.M., Wigmore Castle Tourist Guide and the Family of Mortimer (ISBN 1-899376-76-3) Cockayne, George E. Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom/13 Volumes Bound in 6 Books, IV:1
2 CONC 91; IX:272-3 William Dugdale, Monasticon IV, Kington St Michael Nunnery, Wiltshire, III Dugdale, Monasticon VI, Wigmore Abbey, Herefordshire, III, Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia Annales de Theokesberia Annales de Wigornia
2 CONT
