2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Roger de Mortimer was a medieval marcher lord, residing at Wigmore Castle in the English county of Herefordshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger was also called Roger II de Mortemer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger would appear to have been of age in 1174 when he fought for King Henry II against the rebellion of his son, Henry. In 1179 Roger was instrumental in the killing of Cadwallon ap Madog, the prince of Maelienydd and Elfael, bot
2 CONC h of which Mortimer coveted. He was imprisoned until June 1182 at Winchester for this killing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger married Milisent de Ferrers, daughter of William I, 3rd Earl of Derby and Goda de Tosny, circa 1182. Later he married Isabel de Ferrers, daughter of William I, 3rd Earl of Derby and Goda de Tosny, before 1188.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger was Lord of Wigmore in Herefordshire, Welsh Marches, England, between 1188 and 1215. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1195 Roger, with the backing of troops sent by King Richard I invaded Maelienydd and rebuilt Cymaron Castle. In 1196 he joined forces with Hugh de Say of Richards Castle and fought and lost the battle of New Radnor against Rhys
2 CONC  ap Gruffydd, allegedly losing some forty knights and an innumerable number of foot in the fight. By 1200 he had conquered Maelienydd and issued a new charter of rights to Cwmhir Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Chronicle of Ystrad Fflur (1202): "In this year ... the Welsh took the castle of Gwerthrynion from Roger de Mortimer and burned it to the ground."
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1214 Roger became gravely ill and bought from King John the right for his son to inherit his lands while he still lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_Mortimer_of_Wigmore for more information.
