1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Primary Sources
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000074896931823& Inquisitions Post Mortem] for Philip le Despenser, Writ, 24 Sept. 7 Edw. II [1313]. <br>
2 CONT His death date is not recorded. He died before 24 Sept. 1313 (date of writ). Philip his son, born on 6 April [1313], was his next heir. Mentions Margaret de Gousill late the wife of the said Philip, and daughter and heir of Ralph 
2 CONC de Gousill.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip le Despenser,  Knight
2 CONT * Birth: 1288 - Barton, Gloucestershire, England
2 CONT * Parents: Sir Hugh le Despenser, Isabella de Beauchamp
2 CONT * Married: 1312 in Goxhill, Lincolnshire, England to Margaret de Goushill 
2 CONT * Death: Bef. 24 Sep 1313 - Goxhill, Lincolnshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Margaret Goushill, daughter  of Sir Ralph de Goushill Knight & Hawise Fitz Warin b 12 May 1294 England d 29 Jul 1349 England.  She married John de Ros 2nd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Sir Philip le Despenser & Margaret Goushill
2 CONT # Sir Philip le Despenser Knight was born 6 Apr 1313 and died 23 Aug 1349.  He married Joan de Cobham
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://boards.ancestry.com/topics.medieval.general/672.2/mb.ashx Philip le Despencer son of Hugh le Despencer and Isabella de Beauchamp?]
2 CONT
2 CONT >Hugh le Despenser - husband of Isabel/Isabella de Beauchamp - called by history "The Elder" and his son Hugh "The Younger" have arguably been classed as two of the most evil nobles in British history as advisors to the despotic K
2 CONC ing Edward II. Both were executed for their crimes in late 1326 by Roger de Mortimer and his lover Queen Isabella. The Despenser/Beauchamp marriage produced 4 children, 3 of them were in the More (Mayflower Passenger) ancestry, Ph
2 CONC ilip being one of those.
2 CONT
2 CONT >* "By 1320 his greed was running free. '''Hugh''' seized the Welsh lands of his wife's inheritance, ignoring the claims of his two brothers-in-law. He forced Alice de Lacy, Countess of Lincoln, to give up her lands, cheated his s
2 CONC ister-in-law Elizabeth de Clare out of Gower and Usk, and allegedly had Lady Baret's arms and legs broken until she went insane. He also supposedly vowed to be revenged on Roger Mortimer because Mortimer's grandfather had murdered
2 CONC  Hugh's grandfather, and once stated though probably in jest that he regretted he could not control the wind. By 1321 he had earned many enemies in every stratum of society, from Queen Isabella to the barons to the common people. 
2 CONC There was even a bizarre plot to kill Hugh by sticking pins in a wax likeness of him. Finally the barons prevailed upon King Edward and forced Hugh and his father into exile in 1321. His father fled to Bordeaux, and Hugh became a 
2 CONC pirate in the English Channel, "a sea monster, lying in wait for merchants as they crossed his path".   ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip le Despenser of Parlington, etc d 24.09.1313 shown by BE1883 as a generation later
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedigree caveat emptor: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * from http://www.multiwords.de/genealogy/despencer1.htm#anne2
2 CONT
2 CONT >There is confusion concerning the various families called Despencer, a name taken from the position of a dispensator or steward. BE1883 appears to mix up, or at least makes it easy for people to view as closely connected, two of 
2 CONC the most important. We show them both on this page to help clear the position with regard to what various sources report. On the second of these families, in Volume IV page 259 note c, TCP reports as follows: "Their pedigree has b
2 CONC een distorted by the unscrupulous efforts of many heralds and genealogists to derive the Spencers of Althorpe from an illustrious origin: with the result that 1 these Despensers, who appear to have been dispensatores of the Earls 
2 CONC of Chester, 2 the Despensers of King's Stanley, co. Gloucester, who were dispensatores Regi, and 3 the above-named now ducal family of Spencer, who emerge from obscurity, as wealthy graziers, towards the end of the 15th century, h
2 CONC ave been associated in a single pedigree in which "fact and fiction are cunningly intertwined." "
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations 
2 CONT # Weis, Frederick Lewis, Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America before 1700 (7th ed., Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., 1992.), 74A-32, Los Angeles Public Library, Gen 974 W426 1992.
2 CONT # Cokayne, George Edward, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct, or Dormant (London: St. Catherine Press, 1910.), 6:42h, Los Angeles Public Library, 929.721 C682.
2 CONT # Weis, Frederick Lewis, The Magna Charta Sureties, 1215 (5th ed., Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., 1999.), pp. 9-6, Los Angeles Public Library, 929.273 W426 1999.
2 CONT # Cokayne, G., CP, 4:289.
2 CONT # Moor, Charles, Knights of Edward I (London: Harleian Society Publications, 1929.), 4:139, Los Angeles Public Library, Gen 942.005 H284.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~agrandchildsheritage/despenser.html
2 CONT * http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~hwbradley/aqwg412.htm#13269
2 CONT
