1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir Patrick was Lord of Kidwelly, in Carmarthenshire, South Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://histfam.familysearch.org/getperson.php?personID=I217015&tree=Welsh
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.mathematical.com/chaworthpatrick1238.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.renderplus.com/hartgen/htm/de-chaworth.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p356.htm#i3557
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.castlewales.com/kidwelly.html [source of photo]:
2 CONT
2 CONT Kidwelly is a mighty and imposing monument of Norman power. It is also a beautiful example of castle development, as the castle was dramatically altered on a number of occasions to conform to the latest thinking in military scienc
2 CONC e. Roger, bishop of Salisbury, the justiciar of England, established Norman power in the area and the ringwork castle (shown below) that he built here was one of a series of strongholds designed by the Normans to secure the new co
2 CONC nquests of south Wales by commanding the river passes here and at Laugharne, Llansteffan and Loughor.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ringwork at Kidwelly was constructed on a steep ridge overlooking the River Gwendraeth at its upper tidal limit. No further strengthening was needed on the riverside, and the present semicircular bank and ditch formed the 12th
2 CONC -century defences which would have been supplemented by a timber palisade on the bank, probably further strengthened by towers and certainly by a gate. In the interior would have been the timber domestic buildings of the lord. Thi
2 CONC s castle fell to the Welsh on a number of occasions in the late 12th and early 13th centuries, including once in 1159 when the Lord Rhys took it and burnt it. He is later credited with rebuilding the castle in 1190. By 1201, howev
2 CONC er, it was back in Norman hands and remained English from then on, despite periodic attacks.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the mid-13th century the de Chaworth family gained possession, and began a long work of building the mighty stone castle that we see today. The earliest parts are best viewed from the centre of the castle, as they consist of th
2 CONC e square inner ward with the four large round corner towers and simple portcullis gates to the north and south. By building this inner ward, set as it is within the outer ward, Pain de Chaworth converted Kidwelly into a strong con
2 CONC centric castle, with an inner and outer ring of defences.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kidwelly passed by marriage in 1298 to Henry, earl of Lancaster, who quickly set about upgrading the accommodation to suit his status. A large first-floor hall reached by a semicircular external stair was built on the east; this h
2 CONC as largely fallen, though the wall footings and a fireplace can still be seen. The chapel, housed in a projecting tower overlooking the river, was also built at this time, and the massive spur buttresses of the tower are a distinc
2 CONC tive feature of the castle and are best seen from the outside. The chapel has white Sutton-stone mouldings around the doors and windows, piscina and sedile, making it one of the finest parts of the castle. A small building on the 
2 CONC south of the chapel house housed the sacristy above the priest's bedchamber. Its fine cruciform roof can be seen from the wall-walk leading from the Great Gatehouse....
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Mauduit and Chaworth Family Sources:
2 CONT  Harleian Society Pub., Vol. 4, p. 123.
2 CONT  Burke's Dormant and Extinct Peerage of England, p. 111.
2 CONT  Wells and Allied Families, pp. 142, 160.
2 CONT
