1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sir John de Beauchamp, 1st Baron Beauchamp de Warwick KG'''
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_de_Beauchamp,_1st_Baron_Beauchamp_(second_creation)
2 CONT
2 CONT (c. 1316 – 2 December 1360) was a younger son of Guy de Beauchamp, 10th Earl of Warwick, and brother of Thomas de Beauchamp, 11th Earl of Warwick, with whom he became a founder and the tenth Knight of the Order of the Garter in 13
2 CONC 48.
2 CONT
2 CONT He attended King Edward III into Flanders in 1338, was in the array at Vironfosse in 1339, and shared the glory of the great naval victory off Sluys in 1340. He carried the Royal Standard at the Battle of Crécy in 1346 and was pre
2 CONC sent at the siege and surrender of Calais, of which town he was appointed captain in 1348. He also held the posts of Admiral of the Fleet, Constable of the Tower of London and Warden of the Cinque Ports. He was summoned to Parliam
2 CONC ent as a Baron in 1350.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died without issue when his barony expired. His remains were interred, between two pillars, before the image of the Virgin, on the south side of the nave of Old St. Paul's Cathedral, where there was a monument to his memory, in
2 CONC correctly later known as Duke Humphrey's Tomb.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT
2 CONT George Frederick Beltz, Memorials of the Most Noble Order of the Garter (1861)
