1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT Sir John le Blount (c. 1298–1358) was a knight of Sodington, Worcestershire, who served as a soldier and administrator during the reign of King Edward III. He was active in public life during the mid-14th century, participating in
2 CONC  royal military expeditions and holding administrative responsibilities in his home county.  
2 CONT In addition to his local duties, Sir John's life was marked by service in the Hundred Years' War and a pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela in 1350. Historical records from the era, such as inquisitions post mortem, confirm his de
2 CONC ath in March 1358 and provide details regarding the manors and lands he held across several English counties at the time of his passing.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Sir Walter le Blount, Kt. (c. 1270–1316)  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Johanna (Joan) de Sodington (c. 1274–after 1331)  
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Born: c. 1298, Sodington, Worcestershire, England  
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 8 March 1358, Mamble, Worcestershire, England  
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage and Issue===
2 CONT Spouse: Isolda de Mountjoy (c. 1307–aft. 1352)  
2 CONT
2 CONT -Note: Older pedigrees often suggest a second marriage to Eleanor Beauchamp, but modern scholarly research frequently notes that this is likely a misidentification or conflation with other family branches.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children (attributed to Isolda):
2 CONT 1. Richard le Blount (b. c. 1344; heir to his father)  
2 CONT 2. Sir John le Blount (d. 1425)  
2 CONT 3. Sir Walter le Blount (d. 1403)  
2 CONT 4. Thomas le Blount
2 CONT 5. Alice le Blount (c. 1349–1414/15)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Military Service===
2 CONT 1324: Recorded as a practiced soldier in Worcestershire.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1347: Served in Gascony under Henry, Earl of Lancaster.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1347: Present at the Siege of Calais under King Edward III.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Factual Information and Relevance===
2 CONT Administrative Role: Served as a joint commissioner in Worcestershire in 1344, tasked with inquiring into landholders.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Pilgrimage: Completed a documented pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela in 1350.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Landholdings: At the time of his death, he held lands in Gloucestershire, and in Staffordshire at Baltederleye, Fenton, Bydulf, Romesore, and Denston.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical Note: The mystery of his wife Isolda’s parentage remains partially unresolved in primary sources, though she is traditionally associated with the Mountjoy family.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Verifiable Sources and Records===
2 CONT Inquisitions Post Mortem (Edward III): Legal records documenting his lands and the status of his heir (Richard) at the time of his 1358 death.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Complete Peerage (Cokayne): Contains references to his marriage to Isolda.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Calendar of Patent and Close Rolls: Administrative documents confirming his commissions and service.
2 CONT
2 CONT Douglas Richardson: Magna Carta Ancestry and Royal Ancestry (widely cited scholarly references for his maternal line and family connections).
2 CONT
2 CONT  Updated: 07/17/2026
2 CONT
2 CONT —-
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Disproved wife
2 CONT John le Blount was not the husband of https://www.geni.com/people/Lady-Eleanor-Blount/6000000003243309842#/tab/discussion
2 CONT
2 CONT From Slektsforum -> Slektsfaglige diskusjoner -> Nyhetsgrupper (arkiv) -> Fora der du MÅ skrive engelsk -> soc.genealogy.medieval -> Mountjoy family - ancestors of the Blounts 18 Feb 2008 Nathaniel Taylor:     Ah--but here the ear
2 CONC lier sources (followed by Coll. Hist. Staffs.) seem to be in error, which has since been corrected by CP (s.n. Mountjoy). As CP shows, Sir John Blount (d. 1358) can only be shown to have had one wife, Isolda de Mountjoy. Older sou
2 CONC rces assign him a second wife, Eleanor Beauchamp (of Hache) who is made to be the mother of his younger sons (including the one whose descendants took the peerage title 'Mountjoy'). On the alleged Blount-Beauchamp marriage, an art
2 CONC icle by Cecil R. Humphery-Smith, "The Blount Quarters," _The Coat of Arms_ 4 (1957), 224-27, is corrected by G. D. Squibb, "The Heirs of Beauchamp of Hatch," ibid., pp. 275-77, showing that the particular claimed marriage cannot h
2 CONC ave happened.
2 CONT
2 CONT More importantly, Isolda is documented as still wife of Sir John Blount in 1352, well after the apparent birth year of Walter, ancestor of the lords Mountjoy. Croke (in his Blount work back in 1823) quoted the 1352 charter but did
2 CONC n't realize the chronological implication, repeating the two-wife fallacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT I think the origin of the fallacious marriage is that the Blounts quartered a chequy coat (like Beauchamp of Hache) whose origin was a mystery for quite some time.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''John le Blount1
2 CONT *'''M, #11197, b. 1298, d. 1358
2 CONT *Father      Sir Walter Blount d. 1322
2 CONT *Mother     Joanna of Sodington b. c 1274, d. a 1331
2 CONT *'''     John le Blount was born in 1298 at of Sodington, Worcestershire, England; Age 39 in 1337. He married Isolda de Mountjoy, daughter of Sir Thomas Mountjoy, Baron Mountjoy, circa 1340.2 John le Blount married Eleanor Beaucha
2 CONC mp, daughter of Sir John de Beauchamp, 2nd Lord Beauchamp of Hacche and Margaret de St. John, in 1347.2 John le Blount died in 1358 at of Passingham, Northamptonshire, England.
2 CONT *'''Family 1      Isolda de Mountjoy b. c 1307, d. 1347
2 CONT *Children
2 CONT **Sir John Blount+ b. 1343, d. 1424
2 CONT **Sir Richard Blount b. 1345, d. a 1358
2 CONT *'''Family 2      Eleanor Beauchamp b. c 1328
2 CONT *Children
2 CONT **Sir Walter Blount+ b. 1348, d. 22 Jun 1403
2 CONT **Sir Thomas Blount b. c 1350, d. 1400
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S2958] Unknown author, Some Early English Pedigrees, by Vernon M. Norr, p. 27; Wallop Family, p. 101; Magna Charta by Wurts, p. 1122; Stemmata Robertson, p. 203.
