1 OCCU Lord, Justice of North Wales, Esquire of the King's Chamber, Steward of the King's Household
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT > '''Sir John de Beauchamp, 1st Baron Beauchamp of Kidderminster''' (died 1388) was an administrator and landowner. He came from Holt, Worcestershire, and belonged to a cadet branch of the great family of Beauchamp, whose head was
2 CONC  the Earl of Warwick. He was the son of another John (born 1319), whom he succeeded in the 1360s. A favourite of the ailing King Edward III, in the years 1370 to 1375 he received several grants of offices, including the constables
2 CONC hip of Bridgnorth Castle. He was elected for Worcestershire to Edward III's last parliament (January 1377) and Richard II's first (October 1377).
2 CONT
2 CONT > Richard II regarded him warmly, and acted as godfather to his son. Retained in the household, Beauchamp soon received substantial further patronage, and by 1384 he had been made Receiver of the Chamber and Keeper of the King's J
2 CONC ewels. He took the order of knighthood on Richard II's entry into Scotland in 1385. That December he was granted for life the office of Justiciar of North Wales, to which was added in August 1386 a charter of liberties within his 
2 CONC recently purchased estate at Kidderminster. Even though the Commons demanded in October that a new Steward of the Household be appointed only in parliament, Richard II refused to comply, and in January 1387 he promoted Beauchamp t
2 CONC o the stewardship. Even more provocative was Sir John's creation on 10 October following as ‘Lord of Beauchamp and Baron of Kidderminster’, a new dignity to be maintained from the estates of Deerhurst Priory. This was the first cr
2 CONC eation of a peerage by letters patent.
2 CONT
2 CONT > Beauchamp's rapid rise from esquire to baron could not be borne by the Lords Appellant, who included his kinsman, Thomas Beauchamp, 12th Earl of Warwick. The latter probably saw the rise of his cousin as a threat to his dominanc
2 CONC e of the midlands.[1] Arrested and imprisoned along with three other household knights, Lord Beauchamp[2] was impeached in the Merciless Parliament in 1388 and condemned by the lords for treason. He was beheaded on Tower Hill and 
2 CONC buried in Worcester Cathedral. Fortunately for his heir, John Beauchamp, 2nd Baron Beauchamp of Kidderminster, then aged eleven, he had entailed certain of his manors, so these were exempt from forfeiture.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT === Notes ===
2 CONT
2 CONT # Saul 179
2 CONT # Although the Lords Appellant disregarded his peerage and addressed him simply as a knight (Ross 564).
2 CONT # Ross 563-564
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference:
2 CONT * http://www.intriguing-history.com/sir-john-beauchamp/
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_de_Beauchamp,_1st_Baron_Beauchamp_(fourth_creation%29 Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * * Sir John Beauchamp, the Beauchamps were a noble family The Beauchamp family were an important family in the middle ages, a family that, following the Norman Conquest, had property in many parts of England.
2 CONT * Sir John Beauchamp of Holt was born sometime between 1320 and  1330. The Beauchamp family had held Holt Manor from the 13th century, when  William Beauchamp gave it to his younger son John, sometime between 1235 and 1269.
2 CONT * The Sir John Beauchamp in this article, was the son of another John, who fought at Sluys in 1340 and at Crecy. * He was one of the knights of the shire in the Parliament of 1352 but died sometime in the early 1360’s.
2 CONT * He was succeeded by his son John, who married Joan daughter and heir of Robert Fitz ? Like his father he served his country in wars in Europe. He served under John of Gaunt in the Spanish Campaign of 1372. In the reign of King R
2 CONC ichard II, John Beauchamp rose rapidly to fame. He fought with the King in France and in 1385, was awarded a knighthood for his service to the King against the Scottish people. He was granted land in Carnarvonshire and made Justic
2 CONC e of North Wales.
2 CONT * Sir John Beauchamp continued his rise up the greasy pole of politics By October 1387, he had risen yet further. He was created a peer and baron of the realm, gaining the title Lord Beauchamp, Baron of Kidderminster, another of h
2 CONC is estates. ‘in consideration of the noble and trusty family from which he sprang, and of his own great sense and circumspection,’
2 CONT * His fall however was as rapid as his rise. * Sir John Beachamp and the 100 years war
2 CONT * The young King Richard II, sought peace with the French by negotiation but this failed to satisfy many of England’s Lords who saw Englands loss of French soil as a treacherous thing.  In 1386, they authorized a commission of nob
2 CONC les known as the Lords Appellant to effectively take over management of the kingdom and act as Richard’s regents. Within two years, a parliament would sit, known as the ‘Merciless Parliament’. Here King Richards closest advisors w
2 CONC ere accused of treason, Sir John Beauchamp amongst them.
2 CONT * By the authority of the Lords Appellants, Sir John was attained of high treason, imprisoned in Dover Castle and then brought to London where he was beheaded in the Tower of London on the 12th May 1388.
2 CONT * It is interesting to note that one of the Lords Appellants was Thomas Beauchamp, Earl of Warwick, a cousin of Sir John Beauchamp.  He was opposed to King Richard and after Richard had regained power he removed himself from polit
2 CONC ics and retired to his estates. He was charged with high treason in 1397 and imprisoned in the Tower of London. The Beauchamp Tower is supposedly named after him. He was saved from execution and released in 1399. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT > '''John Beauchamp, 1st Lord de Beauchamp, Baron of Kidderminster''' was born circa 1319. He was the son of Richard Beauchamp and Eustache (?). He married Joan le Fitzwith between 1327 and 1373. He died on 12 May 1388 at Tower Hi
2 CONC ll, The City, London, England, beheaded.
2 CONT
2 CONT > He fought in the French wars. He was appointed Knight at Scotland. He held the office of Justice of North Wales. He held the office of Esquire of the King's Chamber. He held the office of Steward of the King's Household in 1387.
2 CONC  He was created 1st Lord de Beauchamp, Baron of Kidderminster [England] on 10 October 1387. On 12 March 1387/88 he was impeached by the "wonderful" Parliament, and his honours forfeited. He has an extensive biographical entry in t
2 CONC he Dictionary of National Biography.
2 CONT
2 CONT > Child of John Beauchamp, 1st Lord de Beauchamp, Baron of Kidderminster and Joan le Fitzwith
2 CONT
2 CONT ># John Beauchamp, 2nd Lord de Beauchamp, Baron of Kidderminster+ b. c 1378, d. Sep 1420
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.thepeerage.com/p11859.htm#i118583 The Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT > The Lords Appellant were a group of nobles in the reign of King Richard II, who, in 1388, sought to impeach some five of the King's favourites in order to restrain what was seen as tyrannical and capricious rule
2 CONT
2 CONT > ....
2 CONT
2 CONT > They achieved their goals, first establishing a Commission to govern England for one year from 19 November 1386. In 1387, the Lords Appellant launched an armed rebellion against King Richard and defeated an army under [https://w
2 CONC ww.geni.com/people/Robert-de-Vere-Duke-of-Ireland/6000000064172662169 Robert de Vere, Earl of Oxford] at the skirmish of Radcot Bridge, outside Oxford. They maintained Richard as a figurehead with little real power.
2 CONT
2 CONT > They had their revenge on the king's favourites in the "[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merciless_Parliament Merciless Parliament]" (1388). The nominal governor of Ireland, de Vere and Richard's Lord Chancellor, [https://www.geni
2 CONC .com/people/Michael-de-la-Pole-1st-Earl-of-Suffolk/6000000000165306906 Michael de la Pole, Earl of Suffolk], who had fled abroad, were sentenced to death in their absence. [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-de-Neville-Archbish
2 CONC op-of-York/6000000006444054856 Alexander Neville, Archbishop of York], had all his worldly goods confiscated. [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Robert-Tresilian/6000000022833104032 The Lord Chief Justice, Sir Robert Tresilian], was
2 CONC  executed, as were [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Nicholas-Brembre-Lord-Mayor-of-London/6000000044221857070 Sir Nicholas Brembre, Lord Mayor of London], '''John Beauchamp of Holt''', [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-James-Berner
2 CONC s/4518057335120080047 Sir James Berners], and [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-John-Salisbury/6000000023404281887 Sir John Salisbury].
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lords_Appellant Lords Appellant], Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT > May 12th, 2012
2 CONT
2 CONT > On this date in 1388, [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-James-Berners/4518057335120080047 James Berners], '''John Beauchamp''', and [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-John-Salisbury/6000000023404281887 John Salisbury] were convicte
2 CONC d by the “Merciless Parliament” of treason, and put to immediate death.
2 CONT
2 CONT > ...
2 CONT
2 CONT > This date was the turn for '''Sir John Beauchamp of Holt''' and Sir James Berners (or Barnes), two guys noble enough to suffer “merely” beheading, plus Sir John Salisbury, who was far enough down England’s class hierarchy that h
2 CONC e got to endure the full drawing and quartering treatment.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.executedtoday.com/2012/05/12/1388-three-evil-counselors-of-richard-ii/ Executed Today]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [https://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=89267814 Find a Grave]
