2 TITL John III Comyn, Lord of Badenoch: Biography on Undiscovered Scotland
1 NOTE Comyn, John (Red Comyn), d. 1306, Scottish nobleman
2 CONT  d. 1306, Scottish nobleman. He was called the Red Comyn, to distinguishhim from his father, the Black Comyn. Aiding his uncle, John de Baliol,in the struggle against Edward I, he was for a time held hostage by theEnglish. After t
2 CONC he rout of the Scottish troops at Falkirk (1298), he wasappointed one of the guardians of the realm. He renewed the struggle withEdward, but surrendered in 1304 on condition that he could retain hislands. He was murdered at Dumfri
2 CONC es by Robert the Bruce (later Robert I),probably because Robert feared him as a rival claimant to the throne. Thename also appears as Cumming.
2 CONT
2 CONT  JOHN COMYN (d. 1306), called the” red Comyn,” is more famous. Like hisfather he assisted Baliol in his rising against Edward I., and he was forsome time a hostage in England. Having been made guardian of Scotlandafter the battle 
2 CONC of Falkirk in 1298 he led the resistance eo the Englishking for about five years, and then early in 5304 made an honourablesurrender. Comyn is chiefly known for his memorable quarrel with Robertthe Bruce. The origin of the dispute
2 CONC  is uncertain. Doubtless the tworegarded each other as rivals; Comyn may have refused to join in theinsurrection planned by Bruce. At all events the pair met at Dumfries inJanuary 1306; during a heated altercation charges of treac
2 CONC hery were made,and Comyn was stabbed to death either by Bruce or by his followers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another member of the Comyn family who took an active part in Scottishaffairs during these troubled times is JoHN COMYN, earl of Buchan (d. C.1353). This earl, a son of Earl Alexander, was constable of Scotland, andwas first an al
2 CONC ly and then an enemy of Robert the Bruce.
1 NOTE !From "Ancestral Roots of 60 Colonist" by WEIS, 6th ed, line 95, page 92; "John COMYN, Red Comyn, d 1306, Lord of Badenock; m Joan De VALENCE (154-30) dau of Sir William De VALENCE (or de LUSIGNAN) Earl of Pembroke. (CP II 374 not
2 CONC e c; Gardiner 216; SP I 508-509)".
1 NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> 'Lord of Badenoch'
1 NOTE KNOWN AS "THE RED"; LAIRD OF BADENOCH
1 NOTE Comyn, John, d. 1306, Scottish nobleman. He was called the Red Comyn, to distinguish him from his father, the Black Comyn. Aiding his uncle, John de Baliol , in the struggle against Edward I, he was for a time held hostage by the 
2 CONC English. After the rout of the Scottish troops at Falkirk (1298), he was appointed one of the guardians of the realm. He renewed the struggle with Edward, but surrendered in 1304 on condition that he could retain his lands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of the nobles did not give much support to the efforts of William Wallace <../famous/blfamwallace.htm> to remove the English domination of Scotland but Red Comyn was one who did. As a Guardian of Scotland (1298-1301 and 1302-
2 CONC 1304) he often quarreled with the other nobles - in 1299 he was seen to seize the Earl of Carrick (later Robert I) by the throat. At the Battle of Roslin in 1303, an invading English army led by Sir John Segrave was defeated by Re
2 CONC d Comyn. Following the death of Wallace, Bruce and Red Comyn appeared to be moving to an alliance to rise up against Edward. The two met at Greyfriar's Kirk, Dumfries on 10 February 1306. We don't know the details of what happened
2 CONC  but during the meeting but Robert the Bruce stabbed Red Comyn. Comyn (and his uncle who was also present) were killed and the following month King Robert I ("The Bruce") was crowned at Scone. 
2 CONT Despite the principles of chivalry which prevailed at that time, murder of one noble by another was not unusual in those days. Even so, because it had taken place in a church, Robert the Bruce was excommunicated (at least for a wh
2 CONC ile) by the Pope for his crime. Bruce ruthlessly raided the Buchan lands and destroyed the Comyn family there. When Red Comyn's only son died at the Battle of Bannockburn (fighting for the English) the hereditary office of Constab
2 CONC le of Scotland and the castle of Slains were given to the Hays of Errol (who were distantly related).
2 CONT  The name also appears as Cumming.
