1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William La ZOUCHE of Mortimer
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: ABT 1269, Ashby, Leicestershire, England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 28 Feb 1335/36
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried: Tewkesbury Abbey, Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes: acquired the estates of Alan, Lord la Zouche on his death in 1314, and subsequently took the name "la Zouche" and was eventually summoned to Parliament as Lord Zouche.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Robert De MORTIMER of Richard's Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Joyce La ZOUCHE
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: Alice De TOENI BEF 25 Feb 1316/17
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Alan La ZOUCHE
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Robert La ZOUCHE (b. ABT 1318)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Joyce La ZOUCHE (b. ABT 1320)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 2: Eleanor De CLARE (b. Oct 1292) (dau. of Gilbert De Clare, 3º E. Gloucester and Joan Plantagenet) (w. of Hugh Despencer) ABT 1329 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugh La ZOUCHE (b. 1330, Caerphilly Castle, Caerphilly, Glamorganshire, Wales)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Edward La ZOUCHE
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/ZOUCHE.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fabpedigree.com/s058/f331243.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.familyrecord.net/getperson.php?personID=I48047&tree=CorlissOrdway
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st Lord Zouche William la ZOUCHE MORTIMER
2 CONT  - 1337
2 CONT
2 CONT Name	1st Lord Zouche William la ZOUCHE MORTIMER  Title	1st Lord Zouche  Gender	Male  Died	28 Feb 1336/7 	England    		 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	Robert de MORTIMER 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother	Joyce la ZOUCHE 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 1	Alice de TONY,   b. Abt 1284, England  ,   d. 30 Jun 1337, England   (Age ~ 53 years) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married	Abt 26 Oct 1316 	England    Children 	 	1. Ralph la ZOUCHE,   d. Aft 1339, England  
2 CONT
2 CONT + 2. Alan la ZOUCHE, Knight,   b. 1317, England  ,   d. 12 Nov 1346, England   (Age 29 years)
2 CONT  	3. Robert la ZOUCHE
2 CONT
2 CONT  	4. Philip la ZOUCHE
2 CONT
2 CONT  	5. Joyce la ZOUCHE,   d. Aft 1375/6, England  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 2	Eleanor de CLARE,   b. Bef 23 Nov 1292, Caerphilly, Glamorganshire, Wales  ,   d. 30 Jun 1337, England   (Age ~ 44 years)  Married	Abt 26 Jan 1328/9 	England    Children 	 	1. Hugh la ZOUCHE, Knight,   d. Aft Sep 1369
2 CONT
2 CONT  	2. William la ZOUCHE,   d. Aft 6 Mar 1377, England  
2 CONT
2 CONT •  Sources 	[S67] Magna Carta Ancestry, Douglas Richardson, (Douglas Richardson, Salt Lake City, Utah), ISBN 1460992709., vol. I, pgs. 136-8.
2 CONT
2 CONT •  Notes 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy de Beauchamp, Knight, 10th Earl of Warwick, hereditary Chamberlain of the Exchequer, hereditary Sheriff of Worcestershire, of Worcester and Elmley Castles, Acton Beauchamp, Comberton, Naunton Beauchamp, Pirton, Salwarpe, Sheri
2 CONC ff's Lench, Stoulton, and Wadborough (in Pershore), Worcestershire, Hanslope, Buckinghamshire, etc., eldest surviving son and heir, born about 1271-5 (aged 23-27 in 1298, aged 30 and more in 1301). 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married (1st) after 4 March 1291 (date of letter) and before 11 May 1297 (date of papal dispensation to remain in marriage, they being related in the 4th degree of kindred) Isabel de Clare, daughter of Gilbert de Clare, Knight,
2 CONC  6th Earl of Gloucester, 6th Earl of Hertford, by his 1st wife, Alice, daughter of Hugh XI le Brun (otherwise de Lusignan), Count of La Marche and Angouleme, seigneur of Lusignan in Poitou (uterine brother of Henry III, King of En
2 CONC gland). She was born 10 March 1262/3. They had no issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the king's division at the Battle of Falkirk 22 July 1298, where he distinguished himself for bravery. In consequence of his services, the same year he was granted lands in Scotland belonging to Mowbray, Strivelyn, an
2 CONC d Chartres. He took part in the Siege of Caerlaverock Castle in 1300. He signed the Baron's letter to Pope Boniface VIII in 1301 as "Com' Warr'."
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy and his wife Isabel were separated in or before 1302, and subsequently divorced. She married (2nd) about 1316 Maurice de Berkeley, Knight, 2nd Lord Berkeley. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy served under Edward, Prince of Wales, at the Siege of Stirling Castle in 1304. For his good services, he was granted Barnard Castle, co. Durham, in 1307. Sometime in the period 1307-11 Gilbert de Clare, Earl of Gloucester and 
2 CONC Hertford, Guy de Beauchamp, Earl of Warwick, and other earls and barons, while assembled in the Parliament in London, wrote to the Pope praying for the canonization of Thomas de Cantelowe, late Bishop of Hereford. He was present a
2 CONC t the Coronation of King Edward II in 1308, where he carried one of the Swords of State. He was prominent in procuring the banishment of Peter de Gavaston, Earl of Cornwall in 1308, and alone opposed his recall in 1309. He present
2 CONC ed to the chapel of Elmley Castle, Worcestershire and the church of Acton Beauchamp, Worcestershire in 1309. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy married (2nd) shortly before 28 February 1309/10 Alice de Tony, widow of Thomas de Leybourne (or Leyburne), Knight, of Leybourne, Kent (died shortly before 30 May 1307), and daughter of Ralph de Tony, Knight, of Flamstead, Her
2 CONC tfordwhire, Walthamstow, Essex, Painscastle, Radnorshire, etc., by his wife Mary. She was born about 1682-5 (aged 24 or 27 in 1309). 
2 CONT
2 CONT They had two sons, Thomas, K.G. [11th Earl of Warwick], and John [Lord Beauchamp], and five daughters, Maud, Emme (wife of Roland de Oddingseles), Isabel (wife of John de Clinton), Elizabeth, and Lucy (wife of Roger de Napton). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice was an heiress in 1309 to her brother, Robert de Tony, Knight, Lord Tony, by which she inherited extensive estates throughout England, including the manors of Flamstead, Hertfordshire, Brinkley, Kirtling, Long Stanton, and W
2 CONC hittlesford, Cambridgeshire, Carnanton (in Mawgan), Cornwall, Walthamstow, Essex, Nector, Little Cressingham, and Saham Toney, Norfolk, Garsington, Oxfordshire, East Bergholt, Suffolk, Newton Toney and Stratford Tony, Wiltshire, E
2 CONC lmley Lovett, Worcestershire, etc. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He presented to the church of Pillerton, Warwickshire in 1310. He was one of the Lords Ordainers of Reform in 1310. In June 1312 he seized Gavaston at Deddington, Oxfordshire, and carried him off to Warwick Castle. On the Earl of 
2 CONC Lancaster's arrival, Gavaston was handed over to Lancaster and executed without trial 19 June 1312. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He presented to the churches of Notgrove, Gloucestershire and Naunton Beauchamp, Warwickshire, and to the chapel and chantry of Elmley Castle, Worcestershire in 1313. Although pardoned for his participation in the death of Gavasto
2 CONC n in 1313, Guy and his confederate Earls refused to serve in the Bannockburn campaign the following year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Guy de Beauchamp, 10th Earl of Warwick, died at Warwick Castle 12 August 1315, and was buried at Bordesley Abbey, Warwickshire. He left a will dated 25 July 1315. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His widow Alice married (3rd) by license dated 26 October 1316 (as his 1st wife) William la Zouche Mortimer (formerly de Mortimer), Knight, of Ashby de la Zouche, Leicestershire, Fulbourne and Swavesey, Cambridgeshire, and Nutbour
2 CONC ne (in Pulborough) and Treve (or River) (in Tillington), Sussex, Justice in Eyre for forest pleas in Essex, Joint keeper of Caerphilly Castle, Keeper of Glamorgan and Morganno, Chamberlain of Cardiff, Keeper of the Tower of London
2 CONC , Justice of the Forest south of Trent, 1328, and, in right of his 1st wife, of Walthamstow, Essex, Flamstead, Hertfordshire, Wyke or Rumboldswyke (in Rumboldswyke), Sussex, Cherhill, Wiltshire, Elmley Lovett, Worcestershire, etc.
2 CONC , younger son of Robert de Mortimer of Richard's Castle, Hertfordshire, by Joyce, daughter of William la Zouche. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They had four sons, Ralph, Alan [2nd Lord Zouche], Robert, and Philip, and one daughter, Joyce. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He apparently fought at the Battle of Falkirk in 1298. In 1304 his kinsman, Alan la Zouche, Knight, Lord Zouche, settled the reversion of the manors of Ashby de la Zouche, Leicestershire, Fulbourne and Swavesey, Cambridgeshire, an
2 CONC d Nutbourne (in Pulborough) and Treve (or River) (in Tillington), Sussex on him, on whose death in 1314, he entered into these estates. In 1313 William was pardoned for his participation in the death of Peter de Gavaston. He was s
2 CONC ummoned for service against the Scots, 1313-35, in Gascony, 1324-5, and in Ireland, 1332. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1322-3 John Botetourt and his wife Maud conveyed to William and his wife Alice all their right in the manor of Fulbourn, Cambridgeshire. William was summoned to Parliament rom 26 December 1323 to 14 January 1336/7, by writs dir
2 CONC ected variously "Willelmo la Zoushe," "Willelmo la Zouche de Castro Ricardi" (or "de Mortuo Mari, de Assheby, de Mortimer") whereby he is held to have become Lord Zouche. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife Alice died shortly before 8 January 1324/5. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As a supporter of Queen Isabel, he was present at the extraordinary Council held at Bristol in October 1326, when Prince Edward was chosen as Keeper of the Realm. In November 1326 he was sent with the Earl of Lancaster into Wales 
2 CONC to pursue and capture Edward II. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William married (2nd) about 26 January 1328/9 (when he abducted her from Hanley Castle, Worcestershire) Eleanor de Clare, widow of Hugh le Despenser ("the younger"), Knight, 2nd Lord le Despenser (hanged 23 November 1326), and dau
2 CONC ghter of Gilbert de Clare, Knight, Earl of Gloucester and Hertford, by his 2nd wife, John of Acre, daughter of Edward I, King of England. She was born at Caerphilly, Glamorgan shortly before 23 November 1292 (date of her mother's 
2 CONC churching), and was co-heiress in 1314 to her brother, Gilbert de Clare, Knight, Earl of Gloucester and Hertford. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They had two sons, Hugh, Knight, and William (clerk). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after their marriage, orders were issued for their arrest 5 February 1328/9 by Roger de Mortimer, acting in the King's name. Eleanor was subsequently imprisoned in the Tower of London and then in Devizes Castle. In December 1
2 CONC 329 she was compelled b y the King to surrender the lordships of Glamorgan and Morgannwg, and the manors of Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire, and Hanley, Worcestershire, until they should have paid an impossible fine of L50,000. They r
2 CONC eceived a parson 22 February 1329/30. In January 1330/1 she recovered her lands for a reduced fine of L5,000, which sum was never paid in full during her lifetime. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1332 he had a protection going beyond seas with the Princess Eleanor for her marriage to Renaud, Count of Guelders, in the Low Countries. The same year he had license from the Bishop of Lincoln to have a chapel in his manor-hou
2 CONC se at Flamstead, Hertfordshire. In 1332 Anthony Citroun and Nicholas de Salvo acknowledged that they owed William and his wife Eleanor a debt of L20,000. In 1336 William la Zouche Mortimer, his step-son, Hugh le Despenser, Knight,
2 CONC  and four others acknowledged that they owed debts of L266 to Asselmo Symonete, and L1,600 to Gwido de La Chouche, merchants of Lucca, which they had not paid. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William la Zouche Mortimer, 1st Lord Zouche of Richard's Castle, died 28 February 1336/7 and was buried in Tewkesbury Abbey, Gloucestershire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His widow Eleanor died testate 30 June 1337. [1]
