1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Stradling-39
2 CONT Edward Stradling was a son of Edward Stradling & his wife Eleanor Strongbow. Probably born at St Donat's, Glamorgan, Wales, but possibly at one of the Somerset Estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bristol Merchant in 1339, so estimated to be born about 1318.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married to Gwenlian Berkerolles, daughter of Robert Berkerolles, and one of the heiresses of her brother Sir Lawrence Berkerolles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He is thought to have died about 1394 & his son & heir was William Stradling. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173426968959 Stradling 1 (May 4, 2024; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum. https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173393173925 (August 18, 2022; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173392255932 (September 25, 2018; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.castlewales.com/donats.html
2 CONT
2 CONT In the late 14th century, Edward Stradling was twice Sheriff of Glamorgan and his wife Gwenllian Berkerolles inherited Coity Castle. Their grandson Edward married a daughter of Henry VI's great uncle Cardinal Beaufort and became C
2 CONC hamberlain of South Wales in 1423. He died on pilgrimage to the Holy Land in 1453, four years after his son Henry was captured by the pirate Colyn Dolphyn while sailing to Wales from Somerset and had to be ransomed. Henry married 
2 CONC Elizabeth Herbert of Raglan and their son Thomas died young, leaving a widow who married Sir Rhys ap Thomas. Sir Rhys became guardian of Edward, the heir, later infamous for his numerous and lawless illegitimate sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, http://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/6508/STRADLING%202_1622.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (February 21, 2018; Anne Brannen, curator)www.wikitree.com
2 CONT
2 CONT William Stradling   Born about 1400 in Saint Dunwyd, Glamorganshire, Wales    Son of [father unknown] and [mother unknown]  [sibling(s) unknown]  Husband of Annes ferch John  - married [date unknown] [location unknown] Descendants
2 CONC   Father of Gwenllian Stradling   Died [date unknown] [location unknown] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile manager: Katherine Patterson       Stradling-55 created 21 Feb 2011 Last modified 3 Apr 2015 
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography William Stradling was the son of a Welsh knight, Sir William Stradling of Sain Dunwyd, Glamorganshire. His father went on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, 1407-1408 and was made a Knight of the Holy Sepulchre in Jerusalem, 9 H
2 CONC enry IV.  His family can be traced back to William de Esterling, one of the twelve nights who accompanied Robert FitzHammon, Earl of Gloucester, in the conquering of Glamorgan about 1090..  He married Annes ferch John Gwynn, a dir
2 CONC ect descendant of Bleddyn ap Cynfynn, an 11th century Welsh king of Gwynnedd and Powys, and had three sons and a daughter who later became the mistress of Anthony Woodville, 2nd Lord Rivers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources 	Welsh Genealogies   	Welsh Genealogies AD 1400-1500 (1983), Bartrum, Peter C. (Peter Clement), (18 volumes, with supplements containing additions and corrections. Aberystwyth: National Library of Wales, 1983), , vol. 1 p.
2 CONC  163; vol. 10 p. 1621*.  	The Golden Grove books of pedigrees (filmed 1970), (Manuscript, National Library of Wales manuscript number Castell Gorfod 7. book 5 p. C649
2 CONT
2 CONT . www.castelwales.com/donats.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Mike Salter 1991 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 13th century St Donats was held by the de Haweys family who also had estates in Somerset and Dorset. The heiress John de Hawey married Sir Peter de Stratelynge (Stradling), a Swiss who was in command of the castle at Neath 
2 CONC  in 1296. He died soon afterwards so it was either his sons or Joan's second husband John de Pembridge who built the castle in the early 14th century. In the late 14th century, Edward Stradling was twice Sheriff of Glamorgan and h
2 CONC is wife Gwenllian Berkerolles inherited Coity Castle . Their grandson Edward married a daughter of Henry VI's great uncle Cardinal Beaufort and became Chamberlain of South Wales in 1423. He died on pilgrimage to the Holy Land in 1
2 CONC 453, four years after his son Henry was captured by the pirate Colyn Dolphyn while sailing to Wales from Somerset and had to be ransomed. Henry married Elizabeth Herbert of Raglan and their son Thomas died young, leaving a widow w
2 CONC ho married Sir Rhys ap Thomas . Sir Rhys became guardian of Edward, the heir, later infamous for his numerous and lawless illegitimate sons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Stradling was made a baronet by James I in 1611, despite the family having remained faithful to the Roman church at the Reformation. They fought for King Charles in the Civil War, a Stradling being leader of the Royalist forc
2 CONC e defeated in 1648 at St Fagans. They later declined to the status of local gentry and in 1738 Sir Thomas, the last male heir, was killed in a duel at Montpellier in France. The estate passed to his cousin Bussey, 4th Baron Mansel
2 CONC l of Margam. On his death the estates were divided, St Donats going to Sir John Tyrrwhitt in 1755. His heirs neglected the castle, but in 1862 Dr. Nicholl-Carne, a descendant of the Stradlings, bought the castle and began restorin
2 CONC g it as his residence. In 1901 it was sold to Morgan Stuart Williams who sought a refuge from his coal mines at Aberpergwm and carried out further work on the castle. In 1922 Richard Pennoyer bought the castle, but in 1925 he re-s
2 CONC old it to a fellow American, the newspaper millionaire William Randolph Hearst. The castle was commandeered for training of army officers during the war and then in 1960 was taken over by an international school called Atlantic Co
2 CONC llege, which is still thriving there. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The castle lies on a promontory with precipitous cliffs on the west. An inner ward about 40m across with a polygonal wall is closely surrounded by an outer ward with a dry moat facing the eastern approach. The outer wall mostly su
2 CONC rvives and has a small original tower entirely contained with it on the north, and a square gatehouse on the east. The inner ward is entered by an arch beside the rectangular Mansell Tower on the east side. The western part of the
2 CONC  inner curtain is gone, making room for the early 16th century north and west ranges, and the remainder has buildings of various dates against both sides of it. The late 15th-century hall lies on the south of the ward. Beside it, 
2 CONC squeezed in between the inner and outer curtains, is the Bradenstoke Hall, a modern building re-using the early 14th-century roof. The Lady Ann tower southwest of this is also modern and on the west side of the castle is a large m
2 CONC odern dining hall.
