1 NOTE Domhnall I Earl of Mar - Domhnall mac Uilleim (Donald, William's son) - was the seventh known Mormaer of Mar, ruling from 1276 until his death somewhere between 1297 and 1302.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domhnall was a strong supporter of the Bruce cause during the crisis of the late 13th century. He was at Norham in 1292, probably in the camp of Robert de Brus, then Earl of Carrick.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Helen (sometimes called Ellen), who herself had previously been married to Mormaer Maol Choluim II, Earl of Fife. By Helen, he had three sons, including his successor Gartnait, and two daughters. His daughter Isa
2 CONC bella of Mar was the first wife of Robert I of Scotland and mother of Marjorie Bruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last record of a living Domhnall comes from 1297, and the earliest record of his son Gartnait as Mormaer is from 1305, creating the range of Domhnall's possible year of death to somewhere in between these two points. However, 
2 CONC a document dating to 1302,[1] containing terms of reconciliation between Edward I and Robert, stipulates that Robert should act as warden of Gartnait, implying that Domhnall had just died.
1 NOTE
2 CONT
2 CONT Domhnall I of Mar
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domhnall I of Mar - Domhnall mac Uilleim (Donald, William's son) - wasthe seventh known Mormaer of Mar, ruling from 1276 until his deathsomewhere between 1297 and 1302.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domhnall was a strong supporter of the Bruce cause during the crisis ofthe late 13th century. He was at Norham in 1292, probably in the camp ofRobert de Brus, then Mormaer or Earl of Carrick.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Helen, the daughter of Llywelyn ap Iorwerth, whoherself had previously been married to Mormaer Maol Chaluim II of Fife.By Helen, he had three sons, including his successor Gartnait, and twodaughters. His daughter
2 CONC  Isabella of Mar was the first wife of Robert I ofScotland and mother of Marjorie Bruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last record of a living Domhnall comes from 1297, and the earliestrecord of his son Gartnait as Mormaer is from 1305, creating the range ofDomhnall's possible year of death to somewhere in between these twopoints. However, a d
2 CONC ocument dating to 1302,1 containing terms ofreconciliation between Edward I and Robert, stipulates that Robert shouldact as warden of Gartnait, implying that Domhnall had just died.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT 1. Barrow, Robert Bruce, pp. 122-3
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT Barrow, G.W.S., Robert Bruce and the Community of the Realm of Scotland,(Edinburgh, 1988)knighted 1270.
2 CONT THE old district of Mar was very extensive. It commenced in theneighbourhood of Aberdeen, and extended to the border of Badenoch,comprising nearly the whole of the valleys of the Dee and Don and theterritory lying between them. As
2 CONC  mentioned in the Introduction, in Celtictimes the Mormear was the ruler of the tribe of the land; and the oldEarls of Mar were descended from the Celtic Mormears, and can be tracedfrom the tenth century onward.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1014 Donald, son of Emin, was Mormaer of Mar, and in that year heproceeded to Ireland to assist the Irish in repelling the attacks of theDanes, and he fought and fell in the battle of Clontarf. In the reign ofAlexander I. Ruadr
2 CONC i was Mormaer of Mar, and he became the first Earl ofMar. He was one of the Earls who gave consent to the foundation charterof the Abbey of Scone by Alexander I., in 1120. He was also a witness tothe important charter of David I. 
2 CONC to the monks of Dumfermline, about 1126.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruadri was succeeded by Morgund, second Earl of Mar. Between the years1165 and 1171 he granted the church of Tarland to the canons of St.Andrews, with its tithes and oblations, land and mill, and also thesecond tithes of the Earl’
2 CONC s land, and timber from his woods for buildingpurposes. This grant was confirmed by a charter of William the Lion. EarlMorgund and Agnes, his countess, also granted the church of Migvie to thecanons of St. Andrews.
2 CONT
2 CONT Morgund was succeeded by Gilchrist, third Earl of Mar. It seems probablethat he built the Priory of Monymusk, and by charter he granted to it thechurches of St. Marnan of Leochel, St. Wolock of Ruthven, St. Andrew ofAlford, and In
2 CONC vernochty in Strathdon. He contested the claims of thepatronage of the church of St. Marnan, of Aberchirder, with William theLion and the Bishop of Moray, and granted it to the monks of theMonastery of Arbroath.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilchrist was succeeded by Gratney, fourth Earl of Mar, of whom little isknown. But he appeared, with his son Malcolm, as a witness of charters,under the title of Earl of Mar, and one at least of these charters wasconfirmed by Wil
2 CONC liam the Lion. About the year 1224 he was succeeded byDuncan, fifth Earl of Mar. Earl Duncan granted St. Andrew’s Church, inBraemar, to the Priory of Monymusk, with an acre of land on the otherside of the Water of Clunie. He also 
2 CONC confirmed some of the grants made byhis father, Earl Morgund.
2 CONT
2 CONT A dispute arose between the Earl of Mar and Thomas Durward touching thelegitimacy of Earl Morgund and his son Duncan. Durward asserted thatMorgund and his son were illegitimate, and on that ground he claimed theEarldom of Mar, in 
2 CONC right of his mother, of whom little is known, exceptthat she was the wife of Malcolm Lundin, the King’s hereditarydoor-keeper. it is pretty certain, however, that the King supported theclaim of his doorkeeper, with the aim of brea
2 CONC king up this old Celticearldom. The dispute for a time was settled by a compromise under whichThomas Durward obtained his great domains in Mar—stretching fromInvercanny, on the banks of the Dee, to Alford, on the Don, and fromCoul
2 CONC l, on the West, to Skene, on the east. Yet the Durwards were notsatisfied, and subsequently Thomas Durward’s son claimed the wholeEarldom of Mar, and made the utmost efforts to obtain possession of it.This family took the name of 
2 CONC Durward from their hereditary office ofdoorkeepers to the King, and for a time they rose rapidly to power andinfluence.
2 CONT
2 CONT This seems the proper place to touch briefly on the origin of the Earldomof the Garioch, which afterwards became connected with the Earldom ofMar. The Earldom of the Garioch was created by William the Lion, andgranted by him to hi
2 CONC s brother, David, Earl of Huntingdon. This newearldom mainly consisted of the territory surrounding the old fort ofDunideer, and lying between the Don and its tributary, the Water of Ury.Earl David, the first historic Earl of the 
2 CONC Garioch, was a singularlyimportant personage, inasmuch as he was the ancestor of the subsequentRoyal line of Scotland, and also remotely of Great Britain.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in 1144—the third grandson of David I. He married a sister ofRandolph, Earl of Chester, and by her he had three sons and fourdaughters. His eldest daughter, Margaret, married Alan of Galloway, andit was through her iss
2 CONC ue that John Baliol claimed the Crown of Scotland.His second daughter, Isabella, married Robert de Bruce of Annandale. Hisyoungest daughter, Ada, married Henry de Hastings. And it was thedescendants of these daughters of Earl Davi
2 CONC d, who, after the death of.Alexander III. and his granddaughter, the Maid of Norway, claimed theCrown of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl David died about 1219. Two of his sons, Henry and David, predeceasedhim; and his third son, John, "the Scot;" succeeded to the earldom of theGarioch. On the death of his mother he became Earl of Chester. After thedeath of Ear
2 CONC l John, the earldom of the Garioch reverted to the Crown; andit was eventually granted as a lordship to the Earls of Mar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning, Duncan, Earl of Mar, was one of the witnesses to a charter byAlexander II. to Ness, his physician, of the lands of Banff, in the fiefof Alyth, which was dated at Aberdeen on the 9th of October, 1232. EarlDuncan was succ
2 CONC eeded by his son, William, sixth Earl of Mar. He ismentioned among the great barons of Scotland in the letter of fealtygranted by Alexander II. to Henry III. of England in 1244.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT This Earl, during the minority of Alexander IlI., came into conflict withAlan Durward, who was Justiciary of Scotland, and son of Thomas Durwardmentioned before. Durward had married a natural daughter of AlexanderII., by whom he h
2 CONC ad several daughters; and it was alleged in 1252 that hewas endeavouring to obtain from the Pope the legitimation of his wife, soin the event of the death of the boy Alexander III., his daughter wouldbe the heiress to the Crown of
2 CONC  Scotland. Thus Alan was a great andaspiring personage. He assumed the title and style of Earl of Athole from1233 to 1235; and not content with the very large part of the Earldom ofMar which his father had obtained for him, in 125
2 CONC 7, he claimed the wholeEarldom of Mar. In that year a papal rescript was issued, directing aninquest to be held, proceeding on the narrative that "Our beloved son,the nobleman Alan, called the Durward, hath signified to us that, w
2 CONC hereasthe nobleman William of Mar, of the diocese of Aberdeen, hath withheldthe Earldom of Mar, of right belonging to the aforesaid Alan, and thesame doth occupy to the prejudice of the said Alan, and that Morgund andDuncan, decea
2 CONC sed, to whom the said William asserts his succession to thesaid earldom, were not begotten in lawful matrimony." NotwithstandingAlan’s great efforts, Earl William continued in possession, and Durwardfailed in his aim and ambition.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl William was one of the most powerful barons of his time in Scotland.He was one of those who were removed from the Government of Scotland byHenry III. of England, in September, 1255, while his opponent, AlanDurward, was one of
2 CONC  those who replaced him. Mar was, however, recalled tothe king’s councils in the beginning of the year 1257. In November, 1258,he appears, along with Alan Durward, as one of those whom Henry III.undertook to support in the governm
2 CONC ent of the kingdom. He was named amongthe barons of Scotland to whom Henry III. bound himself to deliver up thechild that his daughter Margaret, queen of Alexander III., was about togive birth in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Great Chamberlain of Scotland in 1252, and againfrom 1263 to 1266. In 1270 he was sent to England, accompanied by theAbbot of Dunfermline, on a mission for the recovery of the Earldom ofHuntingdon. By a chart
2 CONC er dated at Falkland on the 23rd of January, 1268,witnessed by his sons, Donald and Duncan, he confirmed to the canons ofSt. Andrews the grants made to them by his grandfather, Morgund, Earl ofMar, of the church of Tarland, and by
2 CONC  his grandmother, Countess of Mar,of the church of Migvie; and, further, granted an acre of land, lyingbetween the church and the castle of Migvie, for a manse to the vicarserving the cure. One clause in his charters touches on th
2 CONC e questionswhich had been raised as to the legitimacy of Earl Morgund.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl William died in 1273, and was succeeded by his eldest son, Donald,seventh Earl of Mar. He was present at the meeting of the Estates held atScone on the 5th of February, 1284, in which the barons and bishops boundthemselves in
2 CONC  the name of the nation to acknowledge the king’sgranddaughter, Margaret, the Maid of Norway, as the heir of the Crown ofScotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of the Maid of Norway, in September, 1290, it appearsthat the Earl of Mar became a supporter of the claims of Robert Bruce ofAnnandale to the Crown of Scotland. In 1291, Donald, Earl of Mar, one ofthe seven Earls o
2 CONC f Scotland, and the king’s freemen of Moray, appealedfrom William, Bishop of St. Andrews, and John Comyn, Lord of Badenoch,Guardians of Scotland, to Edward I. for redress of wrongs done to them byunder-wardens of Scotland, who had
2 CONC  wasted and plundered their towns andlands in Moray, burned their barns, carried away their goods, and slainmen and women.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl of Mar was present in the chapel of the Castle of Berwick, onthe 3rd of August, 1291, when Edward I. protested that his consent to trythe claims of the candidates for the Scottish Crown within the realm ofScotland, should
2 CONC  not prejudice the exercise of his right as Lord Superiorof Scotland, within the kingdom of England. The same year, on the 24th ofJuly, he was present in the Church of the Friars at Perth, when Edward I.received the fealty of Mary
2 CONC , Queen of Isle of Man, and Countess ofStrathearn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Donald fought at the battle of Dunbar, on the 26th of April, 1296;and shortly after he was taken a prisoner by the English.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Muriel, a daughter of the Earl of Strathearn, by whom he hadissue. He died in 1297, and was succeeded by his son, Gartney, eighthEarl of Mar. He married Christian Bruce, a sister of Robert I.; whileRobert I. married Isa
2 CONC bel, a sister of Earl Gartney. The Earl receivedwith his wife the lordship of Garioch, to be held in free regality. Hedied in 1305, and was succeeded by his son, Donald, ninth Earl of Mar,who was then a boy; and Edward I. ordered 
2 CONC that the heir of Mar should bekept in the Castle of Bristol; and soon after ordered that Donald, Earlof Mar, is to be with the king in his own household. He was detained aprisoner in England till after the battle of Bannockburn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Gartney left two daughters—Ellen of Mar, the eldest, and Janet.Ellen married Sir John Monteith; their daughter, Christian Monteith,married Sir Edward Keith, Great Marischal of Scotland; and theirdaughter, Janet Keith, married
2 CONC  Sir Thomas Erskine; their son, Sir RobertErskine, as a descendant of Ellen of Mar, became heir to the Earldom ofMar, and claimed it in the following century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Donald returned to Scotland in 1314, but it appears that heoccasionally revisited England. He led one of the divisions of theScottish army which invaded England in the summer of 1327. After thedeath of Robert I., the Earl of 
2 CONC Mar joined the cause of his cousin, theyoung Prince David II. On the death of the Earl of Moray, the Regent ofScotland, in 1332, the Earl of Mar was elected Regent of the Kingdom.Shortly after he was slain at the disastrous battle
2 CONC  of Dupplin. He wassucceeded by his son, Thomas, tenth Earl of Mar.
1 NOTE He was one of the Seven Earls of Scotland. He was knighted by Alexander III at Scone on 29 Sep 1270. He was a strong supporter of Robert Bruce.
