2 ADDR
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Lisa Thornbury's family tree (http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/t/h/o/Lisa-Thornbury/WEBSITE-0001/UHP-1712.html)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was in royal custody until he came of age in January 1218, indicating a birth year about 1197. He had been privately nursed by a Welsh woman, at Gower. This John de Braos had grants of lands from King Henry III. and held also t
2 CONC he Barony of Brembye, in Sussex, where he died in 1231, by a fall from his horse, his foot remaining in the stirrup.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicknamed "Tadody" by the Welsh when he was hidden in Gower as a child after King John had his father and grandmother killed. He was later in the custody of Engelard de Cigogny (castellan of Windsor) along with his brother Giles. 
2 CONC Cigogny was ordered to give the two boys up to William de Harcourt in 1214. At this time John became separated from his brother. He was present at the signing of the Magna Charta in 1215.
2 CONT
2 CONT John disputed his uncle Reginald's claim to the Braose lands, sometimes resorting to arms. Llewelyn, Prince of Wales, helped him to secure Gower (1219). In 1221, with the advice and permission of Llewelyn, he repaired his castle o
2 CONC f Abertawy (Swansea). He purchased the Rape of Bramber from Reginald and his son William in 1226. In that year John confirmed the family gifts to Sele Priory, near Bramber, and to the Abbey of St FLorent, Saumur, and added others.
2 CONC  After the death of Reginald (1228) he became Lord of Skenfrith, Grosmont, and Whitecastle, the three Marcher castles, by charter from the king but he lost these in 1230 to Hugh de Burgh at the same time as Gower became a subtenan
2 CONC cy of de Burgh's Honour of Carmarthen and Cardigan.
2 CONT
2 CONT =<nowiki>------------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3.htm#JohnBriouseMMargaretB
2 CONT
2 CONT '''JOHN de Briouse''', son of WILLIAM de Briouse & his wife --- (-1232 before 18 Jul).  Henry III King of England ordered the release of "Johannes, Egidius et Philippus et Walterus filii Willelmi de Braosa" dated [Jan] 1218[1280].
2 CONC   '''Lord of Bramber and Gower'''.  Henry III King of England notified a land agreement between "Reginaldum de Braosa" and "Johannem de Braosa" dated 5 Nov 1226[1281].  The Chronicle of the Princes of Wales records that "John de B
2 CONC ruse died of a cruel death having been bruised by his horse" in 1232[1282]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1219) as her first husband, '''MARGARET of Wales, illegitimate daughter of LLEWELYN ap Iorwerth''' Prince of North Wales & his mistress --- (-1272 or after, bur [Acornbury Priory, Herefordshire]).  The Chronicle of the Princes 
2 CONC of Wales records that "John de Bruse married Margaret the daughter of Llywelyn son of Iorwerth" in 1219[1283].  She married secondly Walter [III] de Clifford of Clifford Castle, Herefordshire.  "Katerina de Lacy filia Walteri de L
2 CONC acy" donated land in Cofham, held by "domino Waltero de Clifford", to Acornbury priory, Herefordshire, for the souls of “fratris mei…et…dicti Walteri de Clifford et Margaretæ uxoris suæ et Margaretæ filiæ ipsius” by undated charte
2 CONC r, witnessed by "domino Willielmo de Evereus, domino Rogero de Clifford, domino Ricardo Tirel, domino Roberto de Lacy, domino Willielmo de Rachesford…"[1284].  "Walterus de Clifford filius Walteri de Clifford et Agnetis de Cundy" 
2 CONC donated land in Cofham to Acornbury priory, Herefordshire, also donated by "Katherinæ filiæ Walteri de Lacy", for the souls of “Margaretæ uxoris meæ et dominæ Mathildis filiæ meæ” by undated charter[1285].  "Margareta quondam uxor
2 CONC  domini Walteri de Clifford" elected burial at Acornbury priory, Herefordshire by charter dated “tertia die dominica proxima ante festum sancti Thomæ apostoli” in 1260, witnessed by "…Henrico de Clifford…"[1286]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.       '''  Sir WILLIAM de Breuse''' (-Findon 1290, bur Sele, Sussex).  "William de Breous son and heir of John de Breus" was granted "a yearly fair at his manor of Horsham", dated 3 Aug 1233[1287].  "Domino Willilemo de Brawoso
2 CONC …" (presumably the donor´s stepson) witnessed the undated charter under which "Walterus de Clifford filius Walteri de Clifford et Agnetis de Cundy" donated land in Cofham to Acornbury priory, Herefordshire, for the souls of “Marga
2 CONC retæ uxoris meæ et dominæ Mathildis filiæ meæ”[1288].  "Willielmus de Breuse miles" confirmed a donation to Acornbury priory, Herefordshire made by “dominus Johannes de Breus pater meus"  by charter dated 22 Jan [1290][1289].  He 
2 CONC sat in the Parliament of 1290, whereby he is held to have become Lord Brewose.  Leland quotes a manuscript which records the death in 1290 "apud Findon" of "Gul de Breosa senior" and his burial "in monaster de Sele"[1290].  m firs
2 CONC tly ALINE, daughter of THOMAS de Multon of Burgh-on-Sans, Cumberland & his wife Maud de Vaux.  m secondly AGNES, daughter of NICHOLAS de Moeles [Mules] of Cadbury, Somerset & his wife ---.  m thirdly (1271 or before) MARY, daughte
2 CONC r of WILLIAM de Ros of Helmsley, Lord Ros & his wife Isabel d´Aubigny of Belvoir (-before 23 May 1326).  Sir William & his first wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >a)         Sir WILLIAM de Breuse (-before 1 May 1326).  Lord Brewose.  m firstly ---.  m secondly ELIZABETH de Sully, daughter of Sir RAYMUND de Sully of Sully, co. Glamorgan & his wife --- ([1295/96]-before 24 Aug 1328).  Sir Wi
2 CONC lliam & his first wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>i)          ALINE de Breuse (-before 20 Jul 1331).  A manuscript record of the Mowbray family states that “Johannes filius [Roger]” married “Elianoram filiam domini Willielmi de Brewes in Wallia”[1291].  m firstly (Swansea 1298)
2 CONC  JOHN de Mowbray, son of ROGER de Mowbray Lord Mowbray & his wife Rohese de Clare (4 Sep 1286-hanged York 23 Mar 1322).  m secondly Sir RICHARD de Peshale .
2 CONT
2 CONT >>ii)         JOAN de Breuse (-before 23 Jun 1324).  m firstly (1301 or before) JAMES de Bohun, son of --- (-1306).  m secondly Sir RICHARD Foliot of Gressenhall, Norfolk, son of ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         '''Sir RICHARD de Briouse''' (before 1232-before 18 Jun 1292, bur Woodbridge Priory).  m (before 9 Sep 1265) as her second husband, ALICE le Rus, widow of RICHARD Longespee, daughter of WILLIAM le Rus of Stinton, Norfol
2 CONC k & his wife Agatha de Clere of Bramley, Surrey (25 Dec 1245 or 1247-before 28 Jan 1301, bur Woodbridge Priory). 
2 CONT -        BREWES Family of STINTON[1292]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =<nowiki>----------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John de Braose''' (born 1197 or 1198 – July 18, 1232), known as Tadody to the Welsh, was the Lord of Bramber and Gower.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Junior branch of the de Braose dynasty==
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the second of the line of the junior branch of the de Braose dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father was William de Braose, son of William de Braose, 7th Baron Abergavenny, and his mother was Matilda de Clare, also known as Maud, (born 1175 in Lincoln) daughter of Richard de Clare, 4th Earl of Hertford of Tonbridge Cas
2 CONC tle in Kent. John was their eldest son and one of four brothers, the others being Giles, Phillip and Walter de Braose.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Royal threat==
2 CONT
2 CONT His grandfather had had his lands seized and his grandmother Maud de St. Valery had been captured by forces of King John of England in 1210. She was imprisoned, along with John's father William, in Corfe Castle and walled alive in
2 CONC side the dungeon. Both mother and son starved to death on the King's orders. This was probably due to John's grandfather's conflict with the monarch, open rebellion and subsequent alliance with Llewelyn the Great. John's nickname 
2 CONC Tadody means "fatherless" in the Welsh.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Hiding and imprisonment==
2 CONT
2 CONT At his family's fall from Royal favour John de Braose was initially hidden on Gower and spent some time in the care of his uncle Giles de Braose, Bishop of Hereford, but finally in 1214 John and his younger brother Philip were tak
2 CONC en into custody. They were imprisoned until after King John had died (in 1216), the throne passing to Henry III. John was released from custody in 1218.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Welsh intermarriage==
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1219 he married Margaret Ferch Llywelyn, (born about 1202 in Caernarvonshire), daughter of the leader of Wales Llywelyn Fawr and his English wife Joan Plantagenet also known as Joan, Lady of Wales, and he received the Lordship 
2 CONC of Gower as her dowry with Llywelyn's blessing.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1226 another surviving uncle Reginald de Braose sold him the title of Lord of Bramber, and he inherited more lands and titles when this uncle died a few years later in 1228.
2 CONT
2 CONT He and Margaret his Welsh wife had three sons, his heir, William de Braose the eldest son, John and Richard (born about 1225 in Stinton, Norfolk) the youngest, (buried in Woodbridge Priory, Suffolk) having died before June 1292.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Death and legacy==
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1232 John was killed in a fall from his horse on his land in Bramber, Sussex at 34 years of age. William de Braose (born about 1230) (died 1291 in Findon, Sussex), his eldest son, succeeded him in the title of Lord of Bramber. 
2 CONC John the younger son became Lord of the manor of Corsham in Wiltshire and also later Lord of Glasbury on Wye.
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Braose (1230 - 1291) also had a son, named William de Braose (born 1274 in Bramber, Sussex / dying "shortly before 1st May 1326".[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Another William de Braose who became Bishop of Llandaff cannot be placed with certainty in this branch of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The de Braose name modified to de Brewes in the Middle Ages 1200 to 1400.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Richardson & Everingham, Magna Carta Ancestry, p137. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==References==
2 CONT
2 CONT Magna Carta Ancestry: A Study in Colonial and Medieval Families, By Douglas Richardson & Kimball G. Everingham, Published 2005, Genealogical Publishing Com 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Lines: 29A-28, 246-30. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_de_Braose"
2 CONT
