2 CONT    Marriage Information:
2 CONT
2 CONT John married Margaret FERCH LLYWELYN, daughter of Llywelyn 'Fawr' (he Great) AP IORWERTH and Tangwystyl FERCH LLYWARCH, about 1219 in Wales 1112. (Margaret FERCH LLYWELYN was born in 1204 in Caernarvonshire, Wales 803, died in 126
2 CONC 3 in Clifford Castle, Clifford, Herefordshire, England 4988 and was buried in Aconbury Chruch, Herefordshire, England 1129.)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Born in Gower, Wales on 1204 to William De Braose and Maud De Clare. John married Margred Verch Llywelyn and had 6 children. He passed away on 1232 in Bramber.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_de_Braose
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Braose (born 1197 or 1198 – 18 July 1232), '''known as Tadody to the Welsh, was the Lord of Bramber and Gower.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Re-establishment of the de Braose dynasty
2 CONT
2 CONT John re-established the senior branch of the de Braose dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father was William de Braose, eldest son of William de Braose, 4th Lord of Bramber and Maud de St. Valery, and his mother was Maud de Clare, (born ca. 1184) daughter of Richard de Clare, 3rd Earl of Hertford of Tonbridge Castl
2 CONC e in Kent. John was their eldest son and one of four brothers, the others being Giles, Phillip and Walter de Braose. Royal threat
2 CONT
2 CONT His grandfather had had his lands seized and his grandmother Maud de St. Valery had been captured by forces of King John of England in 1210. She was imprisoned, along with John's father William, in Corfe Castle and walled alive in
2 CONC side the dungeon. Both mother and son starved to death on the King's orders. This was probably due to John's grandfather's conflict with the monarch, open rebellion and subsequent alliance with Llewelyn the Great. John's nickname 
2 CONC Tadody means "fatherless" in the Welsh. Hiding and imprisonment
2 CONT
2 CONT At his family's fall from Royal favour John de Braose was initially hidden on Gower and spent some time in the care of his uncle Giles de Braose, Bishop of Hereford, but finally in 1214 John and his younger brother Philip were tak
2 CONC en into custody. They were imprisoned until after King John had died (in 1216), the throne passing to Henry III. John was released from custody in 1218.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Welsh intermarriage'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1219 he married Margaret ferch Llywelyn, (born about 1202 in the Kingdom of Gwynedd), daughter of the leader of Wales Llywelyn Fawr and his English wife Joan Plantagenet also known as Joan, Lady of Wales, and he received the Lo
2 CONC rdship of Gower as her dowry with Llywelyn's blessing.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1226 another surviving uncle Reginald de Braose sold him the honour of Bramber, and he inherited more lands and titles when this uncle died a few years later in 1228. Sometime in the 1220s, he established the deer park, Parc le
2 CONC  Breos in the Gower Peninsula.
2 CONT
2 CONT He and Margaret, his Welsh wife, had three sons, his heir, William de Braose the eldest son, John and Richard (born about 1225 in Stinton, Norfolk) the youngest, (buried in Woodbridge Priory, Suffolk) having died before June 1292.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death and legacy'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1232 John was killed in a fall from his horse on his land in Bramber, Sussex at 34 years of age. His widow soon remarried to Walter III de Clifford. William de Braose (born about 1224; died 1291 in Findon, Sussex), his eldest s
2 CONC on, succeeded him in the title of Lord of Bramber. John the younger son became Lord of the manor of Corsham in Wiltshire and also later Lord of Glasbury on Wye.
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Braose (c.1224–1291) also had a son named William de Braose who died "shortly before 1st May 1326".[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Another William de Braose who became Bishop of Llandaff cannot be placed with certainty in this branch of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The de Braose name modified to de Brewes in the Middle Ages 1200 to 1400. See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     House of Braose
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT     Richardson & Everingham, Magna Carta Ancestry, p137.
2 CONT
