2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir WILLIAM de Breuse''' (-Findon 1290, bur Sele, Sussex).  "William de Breous son and heir of John de Breus" was granted "a yearly fair at his manor of Horsham", dated 3 Aug 1233[1287].  "Domino Willilemo de Brawoso…" (presuma
2 CONC bly the donor´s stepson) witnessed the undated charter under which "Walterus de Clifford filius Walteri de Clifford et Agnetis de Cundy" donated land in Cofham to Acornbury priory, Herefordshire, for the souls of “Margaretæ uxoris
2 CONC  meæ et dominæ Mathildis filiæ meæ”[1288].  "Willielmus de Breuse miles" confirmed a donation to Acornbury priory, Herefordshire made by “dominus Johannes de Breus pater meus"  by charter dated 22 Jan [1290][1289].  '''He sat in t
2 CONC he Parliament of 1290, whereby he is held to have become Lord Brewose'''.  Leland quotes a manuscript which records the death in 1290 "apud Findon" of "Gul de Breosa senior" and his burial "in monaster de Sele"[1290].  
2 CONT
2 CONT * m firstly '''ALINE, daughter of THOMAS de Multon''' of Burgh-on-Sans, Cumberland & his wife Maud de Vaux.   
2 CONT * m secondly '''AGNES, daughter of NICHOLAS de Moeles [Mules]''' of Cadbury, Somerset & his wife ---.   
2 CONT * m thirdly (1271 or before) '''MARY, daughter of WILLIAM de Ros''' of Helmsley, Lord Ros & his wife Isabel d´Aubigny of Belvoir (-before 23 May 1326).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William & his first wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >a)         '''Sir WILLIAM de Breuse''' (-before 1 May 1326).  Lord Brewose.  m firstly ---.  m secondly ELIZABETH de Sully, daughter of Sir RAYMUND de Sully of Sully, co. Glamorgan & his wife --- ([1295/96]-before 24 Aug 1328).  
2 CONC Sir William & his first wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>i)          ALINE de Breuse (-before 20 Jul 1331).  A manuscript record of the Mowbray family states that “Johannes filius [Roger]” married “Elianoram filiam domini Willielmi de Brewes in Wallia”[1291].  m firstly (Swansea 1298)
2 CONC  JOHN de Mowbray, son of ROGER de Mowbray Lord Mowbray & his wife Rohese de Clare (4 Sep 1286-hanged York 23 Mar 1322).  m secondly Sir RICHARD de Peshale .
2 CONT
2 CONT >>ii)         JOAN de Breuse (-before 23 Jun 1324).  m firstly (1301 or before) JAMES de Bohun, son of --- (-1306).  m secondly Sir RICHARD Foliot of Gressenhall, Norfolk, son of ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =<nowiki>---------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT Fought in Welsh wars begining in 1257
2 CONT
2 CONT Second wife: Agnes de Moeles - 1 son
2 CONT
2 CONT Third wife: Mary de Ros - 5 children
2 CONT *____________________________
2 CONT *'''Sir William de Breuse, 1st Lord Brewes1
2 CONT *'''M, #137448, b. before 1227, d. 6 January 1290/91
2 CONT *Last Edited=9 Jul 2006
2 CONT *'''     Sir William de Breuse, 1st Lord Brewes was born before 1227.2 He was the son of John de Briouze and Margaret ferch Llywelyn.2 He married, firstly, Aline de Multon, daughter of Thomas de Multon and Maud de Vaux.2 He marrie
2 CONC d, secondly, Agnes de Moels, daughter of Nicholas de Moels and Hawise de Newmarch.2 He married, thirdly, Mary de Ros, daughter of Sir Robert de Ros and Isabel d'Aubigny, before 1271.2 He died on 6 January 1290/91 at Findon, Sussex
2 CONC , England.2,3 He was buried on 15 January 1291 at Sele Priory, England.3
2 CONT *'''     He succeeded to the title of Lord of Bramber, co. Sussex [feudal barony] in 1232.2 He succeeded to the title of Lord of Gower, in Wales [feudal barony] in 1232.2 He lived at Bramber, Sussex, England.1 He was created 1st L
2 CONC ord Brewes [England by writ] in April 1290.2
2 CONT *'''Child of Sir William de Breuse, 1st Lord Brewes and Aline de Multon
2 CONT ** 1.Sir William de Breuse, 2nd Lord Brewes+2 d. c 1 May 1326
2 CONT *'''Children of Sir William de Breuse, 1st Lord Brewes and Mary de Ros
2 CONT ** 1.Richard de Breuse4 b. b 1272, d. 1295
2 CONT ** 2.Sir Piers de Brewose+4 b. c 1272, d. 1311/12
2 CONT ** 3.Margaret de Breuse+5 b. 1285
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Exti
2 CONC nct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume II, page 200. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT * 2.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume II, page 302.
2 CONT * 3.[S2] Peter W. Hammond, editor, The Complete Peerage or a History of the House of Lords and All its Members From the Earliest Times, Volume XIV: Addenda & Corrigenda (Stroud, Gloucestershire, U.K.: Sutton Publishing, 1998), pag
2 CONC e 111. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage, Volume XIV.
2 CONT * 4.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume II, page 308.
2 CONT * 5.[S37] BP2003 See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p13745.htm#i137448
2 CONT *____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT William was only 12 when his father died. The wardship of William and the de Braose lands were granted by Henry III to Peter des Rievaux. On his fall in 1234 these custodies were passed on to the king's brother, Richard, Earl of C
2 CONC ornwall.
2 CONT
2 CONT When William came of age he took control of the Braose lands in Gower, Bramber and Tetbury. He confirmed the grants made by his father of the rents of cottages in Tetbury to the priory at Aconbury, founded in memory of Maud de St 
2 CONC Valery by her daughter Margaret.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was plagued throughout his life by a series of legal battles with his female relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT William died at Findon on "the day of Epiphany" (January 6) in the year 1290/1.
2 CONT  His funeral was at Sele Priory on January 15.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''William de Braose, (alias Breuse, Brewes, Brehuse,[1] Briouze, Brewose'''
