1 OCCU 2nd Baron Wigmore; 2nd Lord Mortimer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_Mortimer,_2nd_Baron_Wigmore
2 CONT
2 CONT Edmund Mortimer, 2nd Baron Wigmore (1251 – July 17, 1304) was the second son and eventual heir of Roger Mortimer, 1st Baron Wigmore. His mother was Maud de Braose. As a younger son, Edmund had been intended for clerical or monasti
2 CONC c life, and had been sent to study at Oxford University. He was made Treasurer of York in 1265. But the sudden death of his elder brother, Ralph, in 1276, made him heir to the family estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT He returned in 1282 as the new Baron Mortimer of Wigmore and immediately became involved in Welsh Marches politics. Together with his brother Roger Mortimer of Chirk, John Giffard, and Roger Lestrange, he devised a plan to trap Ll
2 CONC ywelyn the Last. Edmund sent a message to Llywelyn telling him he was coming to Llywelyn's aid and arranged to meet with him at Builth. But Edmund's brothers secretly forded the river behind Llywelyn's army and surprised the Welsh
2 CONC . In the resulting battle Llywelyn was killed and beheaded. Edmund then send his brother Roger Mortimer of Chirk to present Llywelyn's severed head to King Edward I of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT In September 1285, he married Margaret de Fiennes, the daughter of William II de Fiennes and Blanche de Brienne (herself the granddaughter of John of Brienne by his third wife Berenguela of Leon). Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Isolde Mortimer, married Hugh I de Audley (although FMG has her as the daughter of a mistress, see her profile - Janet Palo-Jackson)
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Maude Mortimer, married Theobald II de Verdun
2 CONT
2 CONT Edmund was knighted by King Edward at Winchester, and served in the king's Gascon and Scottish campaigns. He was mortally wounded in a skirmish near Builth, and died at Wigmore Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Edmund de Mortimer, 1st Lord Mortimer
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #102549, b. between 1270 and 1275, d. 1304
2 CONT
2 CONT Edmund de Mortimer, 1st Lord Mortimer; son of Roger de Mortimer, Lord of Wigmore b. c 1232 d. c 30 Oct 1282 and Maud de Braose d. c 23 Mar 1300; grandson of  Ralph de Mortimer b. 1190 d. 6 Aug 1246 and Gwladus D. d. 1251 and Willi
2 CONC am de Briouze b. 1204 d. 2 May 1230 Eva Marshal 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edmund de Mortimer, 1st Lord Mortimer was born between 1270 and 1275. He was the son of Roger de Mortimer, Lord of Wigmore and Maud de Braose. He married Margaret de Fiennes, daughter of William de Fiennes, Baron of Tingry and Bla
2 CONC nche de Brienne, Dame de La Loupelande. He died in 1304.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Edmund de Mortimer, 1st Lord Mortimer gained the title of 1st Lord Mortimer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Edmund de Mortimer, 1st Lord Mortimer
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Roger de Mortimer, 1st Earl of March+ b. 1287, d. 1330
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Edmund de Mortimer, 1st Lord Mortimer married Margaret de Fiennes, daughter of Sir William de Fiennes, in 1285.2
2 CONT
2 CONT      Sir Edmund de Mortimer, 1st Lord Mortimer lived at Wigmore, Herefordshire, England.1 He gained the title of 1st Lord Mortimer.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Sir Edmund de Mortimer, 1st Lord Mortimer and Margaret de Fiennes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Isolt de Mortimer+1 d. a 1336
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Ex
2 CONC tinct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 347. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. [S2] Peter W. Hammond, editor, The Complete Peerage or a History of the House of Lords and All its Members From the Earliest Times, Volume XIV: Addenda & Corrigenda (Stroud, Gloucestershire, U.K.: Sutton Publishing, 1998), p
2 CONC age 52. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage, Volume XIV.
2 CONT
