2 CONT THOMAS [I] Corbet (-before 2 Nov 1274).  "…Thomas, Hugh and Robert the grantor’s sons…" witnessed an undated charter under which Robert Corbet donated tithes to Shrewsbury abbey[2004].  Henry III King of England ordered that "cast
2 CONC rum de Caos", confiscated from "Robertus [Corbet]", be restored to him after "Thomas filius suus" swore homage to the king dated [Nov] 1217[2005].  Henry III King of England ordered "…Robertus Corbet vel Thomas filius suus…" to en
2 CONC quire into the state of the forests "de comitatu Salopie" dated [Jul] 1219[2006].  "Thomas Corbet, son and heir of Robert Corbet" made a fine for "his relief of the lands that Robert held of the king in chief" in Shropshire, dated
2 CONC  17 Oct 1222[2007].  "Thomas Corbet" was granted "the forest of Teynfrestanes", granted by King Richard to "Robert Corbet his father", dated 10 Jan 1236[2008].  A document dated 5 Jun 1249 records the settlement of a dispute betwe
2 CONC en “Thomas Corbeth, claimant” and “Ralph de Valle Torta, deforciant” concerning “the manor of Syluerton, which manor William de Curtenay & Joan his wife held as the dower of Joan of the inheritance of the aforesaid Ralph on the da
2 CONC y this agreement was made”, with the manor “[which] after Joan’s death ought to revert to Ralph & his heirs for ever, shall then remain to Thomas & his heirs quit of Ralph & his heirs for ever”[2009].  Thomas Corbet "son of Robert
2 CONC  Corbet...Lord of Caures" donated the tenth of lead produced in Schelve Mine to Shrewsbury abbey by charter dated 24 Feb 1270[2010].  Inquisitions dated "Tuesday after St Luke 2  Edw I" following the death of "Thomas Corbeth" name
2 CONC  “Peter his son is his next heir”[2011].  The chronology of his father’s life suggests that Thomas must have been well over 80 years old when he died.  m ([1225/28]) as her second husband, ISABEL de Vautort, widow of ALAN [IV] de 
2 CONC Dunstanville, daughter of ROGER [III] de Vautort & his wife Alice --- (-after 1 Jul 1241).  Her parentage and second marriage are confirmed by inquisitions after a writ of certiorari dated 26 Jan 1277 ["4 Edw I"] relating to "the 
2 CONC barony of Valletorta…", recording in Devon “…Cherleton…land…which Reginald de Valletorta gave to Thomas Corbet and Isabel his sister in free marriage…”[2012].  Her family origin and second marriage are also indicated by inquisitio
2 CONC ns after a writ of certiorari dated 26 Jan 1277 ["4 Edw I"] relating to "the barony of Valletorta, whereof Peter Corbet [Isabel’s son] and Henry de la Pomeroy [descended from Isabel’s sister Joanna, see above] are heirs…of the sai
2 CONC d barony, and of full age" which record disputed holdings in Devon[2013], and by inquisitions after a writ dated 24 Oct 1299 [“27 Edw I”] following the death of "Hawis late the wife of Reginald de Valle Torta" which name “Peter Co
2 CONC rbet [Isabel’s grandson] aged 30 and more, and Henry de la Pomerey aged 32, are next heirs of the inheritance of the said Reginald”[2014].  Her first marriage is indicated by a charter dated 28 Apr 1241 which records a final settl
2 CONC ement between "Willelmum de Englefeld querentem" [grandson of her first husband’s sister Emma] and "Alanum Basset impedientem" [son of his sister Cecily], under which the latter granted half a knight’s fee in Shiplake (Oxfordshire
2 CONC ) to the former, in return for land in Tehidy (Cornwall) and other land in Cornwall "de hereditate que fuit Galfridi de Dunstanvill consanguiney predicti Willelmi" and land “que Isabella que fuit uxor Alani de Dunstanvill tenuit i
2 CONC n eodem comitatu in dotem de dono predicti Alani” which should revert to William when Isabella died but would now instead pass to Alan[2015].  A charter dated 1 Jul 1241 records a final settlement between "Gilbertum de Basevil que
2 CONC rentem" and "Alanum Bassat deforciantem" [both nephews of her first husband] relating to land in Bepton, Sussex and land in Cornwall "quam Isabella que fuit uxor predicti Alani de Dunstanvill tenet in dotem" which was inherited by
2 CONC  Gilbert from "Alani de Dunstanvill avunculi predictorum Gilberti et Alani"[2016].  No record has been found which indicates when Isabel died.  Thomas [I] & his wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. '''PIERS [I] Corbet (-1300 before 10 Aug).  Inquisitions after a writ of certiorari dated 26 Jan 1277 ["4 Edw I"] relating to "the barony of Valletorta, whereof Peter Corbet and Henry de la Pomeroy are heirs…of the said barony,
2 CONC  and of full age" record disputed holdings in Devon[2017].  A writ after the death of "Beatrice late the wife of Peter Corbet of Caus", dated "30 Aug 21 Edw III", names "Thomas Corbet…from whom issued Peter, Alice the elder daught
2 CONC er and Emma the younger", that "Peter" inherited, and after his death "Peter his son" who married "the aforesaid Beatrice" who devised the manor of Bynweston to "one John Corbet"[2018].  Inquisitions dated "Tuesday after St Luke 2
2 CONC   Edw I" following the death of "Thomas Corbeth" name “Peter his son is his next heir”[2019].  He was summoned to Parliament in 1295 whereby he is held to have become Lord Corbet.  [Inquisitions after a writ dated 24 Oct 1299 [“27
2 CONC  Edw I”] following the death of "Hawis late the wife of Reginald de Valle Torta" name “Peter Corbet aged 30 and more, and Henry de la Pomerey aged 32, are next heirs of the inheritance of the said Reginald”[2020].  The age of Pier
2 CONC s Corbet in this document is puzzling: either it is an error or the document refers to Piers [II], in which case Piers [I] would have resigned the inheritance in favour of his son.]  Inquisitions following a writ dated "10 Aug 28 
2 CONC Edw I", after the death of "Peter Corbet of Caus", name “Peter his son, aged 30 and more (is his next heir)”, name “Peter his son and heir and Beatrice his wife”, and also record that the deceased “had a firstborn son named Thomas
2 CONC , who by his father’s will espoused Joan daughter of Alan Plukenet and died without heir himself” and gave dower to her “at the feast of St. Martin 23 Edw I” after her husband died[2021].  m firstly (1253 or before) JOAN de Mortim
2 CONC er, daughter of RALPH [II] de Mortimer & his wife Gwladus of Wales.  m secondly ALICE, daughter of --- (-after 1315).  Piers [I] & his first wife had two children:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          THOMAS [II] Corbet (-before 11 Nov 1295).  Inquisitions following a writ dated "10 Aug 28 Edw I", after the death of "Peter Corbet of Caus", name “Peter his son, aged 30 and more (is his next heir)”, name “Peter his so
2 CONC n and heir and Beatrice his wife”, and also record that the deceased “had a firstborn son named Thomas, who by his father’s will espoused Joan daughter of Alan Plukenet and died without heir himself” and gave dower to her “at the 
2 CONC feast of St. Martin 23 Edw I” after her husband died[2022].  m JOAN Plugenet, daughter of ALAN Plugenet & his wife ---.  Inquisitions following a writ dated "10 Aug 28 Edw I", after the death of "Peter Corbet of Caus", name “Peter
2 CONC  his son, aged 30 and more (is his next heir)”, name “Peter his son and heir and Beatrice his wife”, and also record that the deceased “had a firstborn son named Thomas, who by his father’s will espoused Joan daughter of Alan Pluk
2 CONC enet and died without heir himself” and gave dower to her “at the feast of St. Martin 23 Edw I” after her husband died[2023]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ii)         PIERS [II] Corbet ([1269/70]-before 29 Jan 1322).  A writ after the death of "Beatrice late the wife of Peter Corbet of Caus", dated "30 Aug 21 Edw III", names "Thomas Corbet…from whom issued Peter, Alice the elder dau
2 CONC ghter and Emma the younger", that "Peter" inherited, and after his death "Peter his son" who married "the aforesaid Beatrice" who devised the manor of Bynweston to "one John Corbet"[2024].  [Inquisitions after a writ dated 24 Oct 
2 CONC 1299 [“27 Edw I”] following the death of "Hawis late the wife of Reginald de Valle Torta" name “Peter Corbet aged 30 and more, and Henry de la Pomerey aged 32, are next heirs of the inheritance of the said Reginald”[2025].  The ag
2 CONC e of Piers Corbet in this document is puzzling: either it is an error or the document refers to Piers [II], in which case Piers [I] would have resigned the inheritance in favour of his son.]  Inquisitions following a writ dated "1
2 CONC 0 Aug 28 Edw I", after the death of "Peter Corbet of Caus", name “Peter his son, aged 30 and more (is his next heir)”[2026].  m (3 May 1298) as her first husband, BEATRICE de Beauchamp, daughter of JOHN [I] de Beauchamp of Hatch, 
2 CONC Somerset & his wife Cécile de Vivonne (-before Oct 1347).  Batten records her parentage and two marriages[2027].  She married secondly John de Leyburn Lord Leyburn.  Inquisitions following a writ after the death of "Beatrice late 
2 CONC the wife of Peter Corbet of Caus", dated "30 Aug 21 Edw III", names "Thomas Corbet…from whom issued Peter, Alice the elder daughter and Emma the younger", that "Peter" inherited, and after his death "Peter his son" who married "th
2 CONC e aforesaid Beatrice" who devised the manor of Bynweston to "one John Corbet"[2028].  Inquisitions following a writ dated "10 Aug 28 Edw I", after the death of "Peter Corbet of Caus", name “Peter his son, aged 30 and more (is his 
2 CONC next heir)”, and name “Peter his son and heir and Beatrice his wife”[2029]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Piers & his second wife had [two] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT iii)        JOHN Corbet (25 Mar 1298-before 1347). 
