2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corbet_family
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Feudal barons of Caus and their descendants'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >In the Domesday Book of 1086, Roger FitzCorbet and his brother Robert were listed as some of the most important tenants-in-chief of the king and also of the powerful Marcher Lord Roger, Earl of Shrewsbury[4][5] Roger is generally
2 CONC  believed to have been the first feudal baron of Caus in Shropshire, which was a barony within the marcher lordship of Roger de Montgomerie (died 1094). He was succeeded after 1121 by his son Robert FitzCorbet (d. pre-1155). He wa
2 CONC s succeeded by Roger FitzCorbet, who himself was succeeded by Robert (died 1222), who left a son '''Thomas''' who died in 1274. There followed his son and heir '''Peter Corbet (died 1300)''' who left a son '''Peter''' Corbet (died
2 CONC  1322), who died childless. The barony then passed to his half-brother '''John'''. Although the family soon died out in the senior line, when the barony was lost, cadet branches spread out and thrived.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT '''From''' http://powys.org/pl_tree/ps19/ps19_258.html
2 CONT '''Notes''' for Sir Piers Corbet Lord Corbet m. (1) Joan de Mortimer, (2) Alice le Wafre
2 CONT
2 CONT His and Alice's son John was the last baron of Cause, and who was b. 25 Mar 1148 and d. bef 1347. John's heirs were his aunts Alice m. Robert de Stafford and Emma m. Sir Bryan de Brampton - see CP.
2 CONT ------
2 CONT “In terms of family strategy, it may even have been a deliberate policy to have kin in both camps. Even for staunchly Montfortian kin groups, the picture is not entirely black and white. Bishop Walter de Cantilupe, himself a signi
2 CONC ficant figure in the rebel camp, was head of his family at the time due to a series of unfortunate deaths and minorities. Yet the Cantilupes seemed to have been somewhat divided during the war, with Sir Nicholas de Cantilupe of Gr
2 CONC easley, brother of Bishop Thomas and nephew of Bishop Walter, being rather difficult to place on one side or another depending on the jury being asked about his rebellious actions. '''Similarly, while Peter Corbet was pardoned at 
2 CONC his father’s insistence in 1266, there is little evidence to place him directly on the rebels’ side. After all, he had married Joan Mortimer, between whose family and Earl Simon de Montfort there was a bitter enmity, which culmina
2 CONC ted with Earl Simon allegedly falling in battle at the hand of Roger Mortimer, and chroniclers concurring that body parts were then ‘barbarously’ hacked from the earl’s corpse and sent to Lady Mortimer as gruesome trophies.'''[21]
2 CONC  Nicholas, of course, seemed to have little choice in the matter of his own political leanings, given that the head of the Cantilupe family was the staunch Montfortian Bishop Walter of Worcester, a childhood friend of the rebel ea
2 CONC rl.[22].”
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[21]''' John Sadler, The Second Barons’ War: Simon de Montfort and the Battles of Lewes and Evesham (Barnsley, 2008), 123.
2 CONT '''[22]''' C. H. Lawrence, ‘Cantilupe, Walter de (c.1195-1266)’, Oxford Dictionary of National Biography (Oxford Universtiy Press, 2004), online resource, http://www.oxfordnb.com/view/article/4571 .
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source'''
2 CONT Melissa Julian-Jones, “Family Strategy or Personal Principles? The Corbets in the Reign of Henry III,” in T''hirteenth Century England Authority and Resistance in the Age of Magna Carta: Proceedings of the Aberystwyth and Lampeter
2 CONC  Conference, 2013,'' ed. Janet Burton, Phillipp Schofield, and Björn Weiler, vol. XV (Boydell Press, 2015), p. 73.
2 CONT ----
