2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#_ftnref1056 Chapter 11.  GALLOWAY
2 CONT
2 CONT The province of Galloway included part of Dumfriesshire as well as the earldom of Carrick.  Contemporary charters, especially those included in the Liber Sancte Marie de Melros, show that the Norman settlers seem to have obtained 
2 CONC "a more insecure footing" in Galloway that in other districts in Scotland, as after two or three generations their names disappear from the documentation where Gaelic names resume their predominance[1110]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         FERGUS, son of --- (-[1136]).  Lord of Galloway.  "…Fergus de Galweia…Uchtred filio Fergus" witnessed a charter dated to [1136] by which "David Rex Scotiæ" granted Perdeyc to the church of Glasqow[1111].  "…Fgus de Galw
2 CONC eia…" witnessed the undated charter under which David I King of Scotland donated "decimam meam de meo Chan" to the church of Glasgow[1112].  m ELIZABETH, daughter of ---.  Fergus & his wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         UHTRED of Galloway (-1174).  "…Fergus de Galweia…Uchtred filio Fergus" witnessed a charter dated to [1136] by which "David Rex Scotiæ" granted Perdeyc to the church of Glasqow[1113].  "Uhctredus filius Fergusi" donated 
2 CONC "ecclesiam de Colmanele" to Holyrood abbey by undated charter[1114].  "Uctredus filius Fergi et Gunnild filia Waldef sponsa sua" donated "ecclesiam de Torpennoth" to Holyrood abbey by undated charter[1115].  Malcolm IV King of Sco
2 CONC tland with "Uhtred filio Fergi et Gilebto fratris eius et Rad filio Dunegal et Duuenaldo fratris eius" confirmed the donation of "terra de Dunroden" to Holyrood abbey by undated charter[1116].  "...Huctred son of Fergus, Gilebert 
2 CONC son of Fergus..." witnessed the charter dated to [1166] under which William King of Scotland confirmed the grant of property to “Robert de Brus”[1117].  William of Newburgh names "duo fratres Gilbertus et Uctredus Galwadensis prov
2 CONC inciæ dofuini…Fergusi olim principis eiusdem provincie filii" when recording their quarrels [in 1174] and that Uhtred was killed[1118].  Lord of Galloway.  John of Fordun’s Scotichronicon (Continuator) records that, in the year in
2 CONC  which King William was released from custody, "duce Gilberto filio Fergusii" led "Galwidienses" in rebellion and "X Kal Oct" captured "Ochtredus…filius Fergusii…verus…Scotus", blinded him, cut out his tongue, and murdered him[111
2 CONC 9].  m GUNHILD, daughter of WALTHEOF & his wife Sigrid ---.  The Cronicon Cumbriæ records that “Alanus filius et hæres eiusdem Waldevi” enfeoffed “Ugthredo filio Fergus domino Galwediæ” with property and “Guynolda sorore sua”[1120
2 CONC ].  "Uctredus filius Fergi et Gunnild filia Waldef sponsa sua" donated "ecclesiam de Torpennoth" to Holyrood abbey by undated charter[1121].  Uhtred & his wife had [three] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          ROLAND of Galloway (-Northampton 1200, bur St Andrews).  William of Newburgh names "fratri nefarie interempto filius Rollandus"[1122].  Lord of Galloway.   The Annals of Ulster record the death in 1200 of "Roland son o
2 CONC f Uchtrach king of the Foreign-Irish”[1123]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -         see below. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ii)         --- of Galloway (-killed in battle 1185).  The Chronicle of Melrose records that he fought with his brother Roland against "Gillecolm" but was killed in the battle together with their opponent[1124].  John of Fordun’s 
2 CONC Scotichronicon (Continuator) records that, after the death of "dominus Galwalliæ Gilbertus, filius Fergusii" in 1185, "Rotholandus filius Othredi" fought with "Gilpatricio, et Henrico Kennedy, necnon Samuele" and that "frater Roth
2 CONC olandi" was killed[1125]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT iii)        [FERGUS (-after 22 Sep 1196).  "…Roll constabul, Philipp de Mubray, Willmo de Valloñ, Henr Biset, Thomas de Colville, Adam fil Herb, Ferg fratre Roll, Alexander de Finton" witnessed the charter dated 22 Sep (no year) u
2 CONC nder which William King of Scotland confirmed the donation of "in territorio de Cliftun" to Melrose abbey made by "Walterus Corbet filius Walteri"[1126].  It is not certain that Fergus was the brother of Fergus of Galloway, but th
2 CONC e conjunction of the names (which are unusual) make this probable.  If this is correct, Fergus was not the same person as the unnamed brother who was killed in 1185, as Roland is named in the document as constable, an appointment 
2 CONC which he assumed after the death of William de Morville in 1196.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         AUFRICA of Galloway .  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that “Olavus filius Godredi Crovan” married “Affricam…filiam Fergus de Galwedia”[1127].  m OLAV “Morsel” King of Man, son of GODFRED “Crovan” King of Man 
2 CONC & his wife --- ([1080]-killed 1153). 
2 CONT
2 CONT c)         GILBERT of Galloway (-1 Jan 1185).  Malcolm IV King of Scotland with "Uhtred filio Fergi et Gilebto fratris eius et Rad filio Dunegal et Duuenaldo fratris eius" confirmed the donation of "terra de Dunroden" to Holyrood 
2 CONC abbey by undated charter[1128].  "...Huctred son of Fergus, Gilebert son of Fergus..." witnessed the charter dated to [1166] under which William King of Scotland confirmed the grant of property to “Robert de Brus”[1129].  William 
2 CONC of Newburgh names "duo fratres Gilbertus et Uctredus Galwadensis provinciæ dofuini…Fergusi olim principis eiusdem provincie filii" when recording their quarrels [in 1174], commenting that "Gilbertus natu major"[1130].  John of For
2 CONC dun’s Scotichronicon (Continuator) records that, in the year in which King William was released from custody, "duce Gilberto filio Fergusii" led "Galwidienses" in rebellion and captured "Ochtredus…filius Fergusii…verus…Scotus", bl
2 CONC inded him, cut out his tongue, and murdered him[1131].  John of Fordun’s Scotichronicon (Continuator) records the death of "dominus Galwalliæ Gilbertus, filius Fergusii" in 1185[1132].  m ---.  The name of Gilbert’s wife is not kn
2 CONC own.  Gilbert & his wife had one child: 
