2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_of_Galloway_(bastard) https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_of_Galloway t is possible that Alan had hoped that his illegitimate son, Thomas, would be able succeed to the lordship.[219] Celtic cust
2 CONC om would not have barred the latter from the succession. According to feudal practice, however, Alan's nearest heirs were his three daughters.[9][note 27] As it turned out, the Gallovidian succession was quickly seized upon by the
2 CONC  Scottish crown.[219] The prospect of Alan's illegitimate son succeeding to the lordship threatened to reignite Gallovdian interests in the Isles, and thereby threatened the welfare of the Scottish realm.[9] On the other hand, the
2 CONC  husbands of Alan's daughters were prominent men of Anglo-Norman descent, and the prospect of bringing about the demise of the semi-autonomous lordship, through its division between such imminent Englishmen, was an advantageous op
2 CONC portunity that Alexander could not pass up.[220][note 28]
2 CONT
2 CONT Although it is possible that the people of Galloway regarded Alan's illegitimate son as their rightful lord,[222] the Chronicle of Melrose states that they appealed to Alexander to take the whole lordship into his own hands,[223] 
2 CONC suggesting that whilst the Gallovidians were willing to accept that illegitimacy indeed excluded Alan's son from the inheritance, they were unwilling to accept female succession.[224] Whatever the case, Alexander denied the Gallov
2 CONC idian's their request, quickly crushed their subsequent retaliatory uprising, and went ahead with the tripartite partitioning.[225] One of the Scots who played a key part in the king's destruction of the Gallovidian resistance was
2 CONC  Ferchar mac in tSagairt, Earl of Ross (died c. 1251), a rising star in Alexander's administration, who happened to be Amlaíb Dub's father-in-law.[226] (Christina,  Fearchar's daughter, was married to Amlaibh, the King of Mann and
2 CONC  the Isles Following Alan's death, the constableship passed to Roger de Quincy, husband of Alan's eldest surviving daughter, Helen.[227] Alan's Irish lands were not inherited by his daughters. Forty years after his death, these la
2 CONC nds are recorded in the hands of the Bissets, although precisely how this came about is uncertain. The succession of the Irish and Gallovidian lands of Alan's brother, Thomas, is also uncertain.[228] In fact, Thomas' lands should 
2 CONC have passed to his son, Patrick (died 1242), had the latter not died on the eve of his inheritance (under suspicious circumstances, apparently at the hands of the Bissets).[229]
