2 CONT
2 CONT James de ALDITHLEY, Justice of Chester 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 1225, Heleigh, Stafford, England  Married:  
2 CONT Died: 11 Jun 1272  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orthographic variations: de AUDLEY, AUDITHELE, ALDITHELE  
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes: Relationship to compiler: 21/23 x great-grandfather  
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes: Inquisition Post Mortem  788. James de Aldithele alias de Audithele. Writ of certiorari, 16 July, 56 Hen. III, on the complaint of Ella, late the wife of the said James, that the escheator had taken into the king's
2 CONC  hand the manors of Stratton and Wechewik, which were of her free marriage of the gift of her father William Lungespei. Inq. (undated) James, his son, aged 22 and more, is his heir. Oxford. Stretton and Wrechewic manors, are held 
2 CONC of the fee of Henry de Laci, and were given in free marriage with the said Ella to the said James. C. Hen. III. File 41. (1.)  
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Information: James married Ela de LONGESPÉE, daughter of Sir William de LONGESPÉE, 2nd Earl of Salisbury, and Idoine de CAMVILLE. (Ela de LONGESPÉE was born in 1228 in Salisbury, Wiltshire, England and died before 22 Nov 
2 CONC 1299.)  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Calendar of Inquisitions Post Mortem, and other analogous documents, preserved in the Public Record Office, Vol. I, Henry III, p. 261
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT James Audley (1220 – 1272), Aldithel or Alditheley, was an English baron.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT James Audley was born in 1220 to Henry Audley and Bertha de Mesnilwarin,[3] and was, like his father, a lord-marcher.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1257 he accompanied Richard, king of the Romans, to his coronation at Aachen (Matt. Paris), sailing on 29 April (Rymer) and returning to England in the autumn to take part in the Welsh campaign (1257-1260). In the following yea
2 CONC r (1258) he was one of the royalist members of the council of fifteen nominated by the Provisions of Oxford, and witnessed, as 'James of Aldithel,' their confirmation by the king (18 Oct.). He also, with his brother-in-law, Peter 
2 CONC de Montfort, was appointed commissioner to treat with Llewelyn (18 Aug.), and two years later he acted as an itinerant justice. On Llewelyn of Wales attacking Mortimer, a royalist marcher, Audley joined Prince Edward at Hereford, 
2 CONC 9 January 1263 to resist the invasion. But the barons, coming to Llewelyn's assistance, dispersed the royalist forces, and seized on his castles and estates. He is wrongly said by Dugdale and Foss to have been made 'justice of Ire
2 CONC land' in this year, but in December he was one of the royalist sureties in the appeal to Louis of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of the battle of Lewes (May 1264) he was in arms for the king on the Welsh marches (Matthew Paris), and he was one of the first to rise against the government of Simon de Montfort. On Gloucester embracing the royal cau
2 CONC se, early in 1265, Audley joined him with the other marchers, and took part in the campaign of Evesham and the overthrow of the baronial party. He appears to have gone on a pilgrimage to Galicia in 1268, and also, it is stated, to
2 CONC  Palestine in 1270; but though his name occurs among the 'Crucesignati' of 21 May 1270, it is clear that he never went, for he was appointed justiciary of Ireland a few months later, his name first occurring in connection with tha
2 CONC t office 5 September 1270.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also served as High Sheriff of Staffordshire and Shropshire in 1261 and 1270.[4] During his tenure as Justiciar of Ireland he led several expeditions against 'the Irish rebels,' but died by 'breaking his neck' about 11 June 127
2 CONC 2 (when he is last mentioned as justiciary), and was succeeded by his son James, who did homage 29 July 1272.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT  "(Sir) James DE AUDLEY Knight, Justiciar of Ireland". washington.ancestryregister.com. Archived from the original on 20 March 2015. Retrieved 20 March 2015.
2 CONT  Lloyd, Simon (2004). "Audley, James (d. 1272), magnate". Oxford Dictionary of National Biography (online) (online ed.). Oxford University Press. doi:10.1093/ref:odnb/894. (Subscription or UK public library membership required.)
2 CONT  p.220, Walter Chetwynd's History of Pirehill Hundred], published in 'Collections for a History of Staffordshire', Vol. XXII New Series (1909): and by Bertha, the daughter of Ralph de Mesnilwarin (with whom he had in frank-marriag
2 CONC e ye manors of Smalwood and Snelston, and half ye town of Pickmere, co. Cestr.), left issue James Ld. Audley
2 CONT  Collections for a history of Staffordshire. Staffordshire Record Society. 1912. p. 276.
2 CONT Attribution
