1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Agnes Arderne=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Sir John Arderne, of Aldford and Margery verch Gruffydd 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000008190669140&photo_id=6000000189948966895&position=0 '''The History of the County Palatine and City of Chester: Compiled, Volume 2, by George Ormerod.''']
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married:  '''Sir John Wettenhall, Kt., of Acton,''' on 1 June 1330 in Cheshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sons:[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Robert de Wettenhall2 d. a 1316 m. 	Amicia de Alpraham
2 CONT # John, m. Maude, daughter of Robert Fulleshurst
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Arderne-79
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes is the child of John Arderne and Margaret Gruffudd. She married John de Wetenhall.[1][2][3] "This John de Wetenhale...and Agnes his wife, obtained the manor of Wetenhale, by fine from Margery daughter to John de Arderne, II 
2 CONC Edw. II."[4] This statement is derived from Williamson's Cheshire Evidences which is reported to say: "Jo. de Wetenhall Senr & Agnes his w. bt. [bought] from Margery d to Jo de Ardern ye Manr of Wetenhall cm p[] Nic de West put in
2 CONC  his claime." [5][6] This is presumably what established the relationship; per one writer: "This document contains no explicit statement of relationship between Agnes and John Arderne. However, because such transfers frequently to
2 CONC ok place to resettle an inheritance within a family there has been a long history among antiquarians of assuming a relationship based on the existence of a fine."[7] Furthermore, in a discussion of the children of John Arderne and
2 CONC  Margaret Griffin/Gruffydd, their issue included "other daughters who had settlements of land."[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT After John's death, Agnes transferred the manor of Wetenhale to Robert de Paris in 21 Edward III (~1347).[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000190231091832&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT # ↑ Ormerod, Vol. II, p. 85 (text buried in book gutter, see p. 84)
2 CONT # ↑ Ormerod, 1819 ed. Vol. II, p. 42
2 CONT # ↑ Ormerod, Vol. II, p. 195
2 CONT # ↑ Ormerod, p. 192
2 CONT # ↑ http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/GEN-MEDIEVAL/2015-06/1435442908
2 CONT # ↑ Report, p. 115 (While this abstract calls it "a tenement in Wetenhale", the original says "ye Manr of Wetenhall".)
2 CONT # ↑ http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/GEN-MEDIEVAL/2015-06/1435528777
2 CONT # ↑ Miscellanea, p. 81
2 CONT # ↑ Report, p. 48
2 CONT # Ormerod, George, Daniel King, William Smith, William Webb, Peter Leycester, and Thomas Helsby. 1882. The history of the county Palatine and city of Chester; comp. from original evidences in public offices, the Harleian and Cotto
2 CONC nian mss., parochial registers, private muniments, unpublished ms. collections of successive Cheshire antiquaries, and a personal survey of every township in the county; incorporated with a republication of King's Vale royal and L
2 CONC eycester's Cheshire antiquities. London: G. Routledge. 1882. 2nd Ed. Vol. II. (Also 1819 ed. Vol. II)
2 CONT # Ormerod, George. 1851. Miscellanea Palatina: consisting of genealogical essays illustrative of Cheshire Domesday roll, compiled from original authorities. London: T. Richards, printer.
2 CONT # Roberts, Gary Boyd. 2014. The royal descents of 600 immigrants to the American colonies or the United States: who were themselves notable or left descendants notable in American history : with a 2008 addendum, coda, and final ad
2 CONC dition. p. 507
2 CONT # The Twenty-eighth Annual Report of the Deputy Keeper of the Public Records (28 Feb 1867). (London: 1867)
2 CONT # http://www.masland.org/wp-content/themes/MaslandFamily/ancestors-of-femiv/a26.htm#i8099
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes daughter of Knight John Arderne.
