1 NAME Robert /Puleston/
2 GIVN Robert
2 SURN Puleston
2 _MARNM Puleston
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1358
2 PLAC Emral
2 ADDR
3 CITY Worthenbury
3 CTRY Wales
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1399
2 PLAC Emral
2 ADDR
3 CITY Worthenbury
3 CTRY Wales
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Peter Batrtum, https://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/5559/puleston.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (March 2, 2017; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Ancestry of Owain Glyndwr; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id200.html. (Steven Ferry, September 13, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Puleston was a brother-in-law and supporter of Owain Glyndŵr, at the time of his rebellion against King Henry IV of England in the early 15th century and afterwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was from a well established Welsh Marcher family [1]. Pulestons had settled during the reign of King Henry III in Newport, Shropshire initially, in Pilston village and manor, from where they derive their surname.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Sir Roger de Puleston (died 1294) established himself at Emral in Maelor Saesneg, and was appointed the first High Sheriff of Anglesey by King Edward I of England in 1293. His first task there was to impose the new English taxes
2 CONC  (one fifteenth of all moveables) that unsurprisingly led to the revolt of Madog ap Llywelyn, at the height of which the Welsh mob seized the Sheriff and hanged him following a raid on Caernarvon borough.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another Puleston, Richard de Puleston, was at this time King Edward's High Sheriff of Caernarvonshire and had been appointed the same date as Sir Roger, so is very likely to have been a close relation, probably a brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Puleston was son of Richard Puleston. He was a witness in the Scrope v. Grosvenor Trial at Chester in 1386, alongside another witness Owain Glyndŵr.
2 CONT
2 CONT This trial was to settle a dispute between Sir Richard le Scrope of Bolton and Sir Robert Grosvenor of Hulme concerning ownership of a coat of arms. During King Richard II's military campaign in Scotland in 1385 three knights had 
2 CONC borne the same coat of arms. Also involved was Carminow of Cornwall.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Court was presided over by the Duke of Gloucester as Constable of England who also adjudicated on the evidence presented by each party and their many witnesses. The trial was to run for five years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Glyndŵr gave evidence on behalf of Grosvenor saying he had seen Grosvenor bear the arms and that in the counties of Flintshire, Chester and Denbighshire they were accepted as being his rightfully. Glyndŵr's younger brother Tudur a
2 CONC lso testified to this, as did Puleston. However, eventually Scrope won.
2 CONT
2 CONT Puleston later took part in Glyndŵr's rebellion and his extensive lands in the county of Chester, in Shropshire, and Flintshire were declared forfeit before 1401. However as part of the programme of Royal Pardons meted out by the 
2 CONC new King Henry V he received his old lands back, restored to him after the rebellion had petered out around 1415.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Puleston married Owain Glyndŵr's younger sister, Lowry. They had a son called John Puleston, whose will was proved in 1444. He married Angharad, a daughter of Griffith Hanmer, of the same family as Owain Glyndŵr's wife, Mar
2 CONC garet Hanmer. Angharad was a granddaughter of Gronw ap Tudor of Anglesey. Another son, Roger Puleston (died 1469), who was a staunch ally of Jasper Tudor, Earl of Pembroke holding Denbigh Castle as Deputy Constable to Jasper Tudor
2 CONC  in 1460 and 1461.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT *http://www.robertsewell.ca/puleston.html
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Puleston
2 CONT *_______________ See Peter Bartrum, http://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/5212/Bleddyn%20ap%20Cynfyn%205.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (December 27, 2016; Anne Brannen, curator) 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference:
2 CONT * The Seize Quartiers of the Family of Bryan and Frances Cooke, 1857
2 CONT * Date:	1857
2 CONT * Text:	"... Emrall,=: Agnes, daughter and heir of Sir John Wysham, Knight Banneret, of Gloucester. 
2 CONT * Married Sir Richard Pyvelesdon de Emral de Puleston 
2 CONT * Richard he is the son of Sir Roger e Puleston and Margaret  verch Gruffydd ap Llewelyn
2 CONT  * Puleston of Emrall, Knight,: second son and heir, living 1345, 2d Edward IIL..."
2 CONT * Child of Agnes de Wysham Puleston and Sir Richard Pyvelesdon de Emral de Puleston is Roger Puleston of Emrall, Knight, second son and heir living 1345.
2 CONT * Roger Puleston of Emrall, Knight, married  Gwenhwyfar Llewelwyn, the daughter of Ap Ynyr, Lord of Gelligynan in Llewelwyn.
2 CONT * Child of Roger Puleston of Emrall, Knight and Gwenhwyfar Llewelwyn is ''''''Richard Puleston of Emrall, Esq'''''' = Lleuki,temp Richard ll, married daughter of Madoc Voel, Lord of Eglwysegle. They are descended from Cynric Efell
2 CONC .
2 CONT * Child of Richard Puleston of Emrall, Esq and daughter of Madoc Voel is
2 CONT *'''''' Robert of Puleston of Emrall, Esq son and heir, was slain 1st Henry IV, living AD 1399.'''''' =  Lowrie, Married the daughter of Griffth Vychan, of Rhuddalt and Lord glynwrdwt. She is a sister of Owen Glyndwr.
