2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "De la Pole", in this case derives from Welshpool, in Montgomeryshire, and does not necessarily indicate a connection to the later Earls/Dukes of Suffolk. (There ''may'' be a connection, but any hard evidence has long since been l
2 CONC ost.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Said to be the son of a William de la Pole who was the grandson (via another William) of Gruffudd ap Gwenwynwyn (Griffin de la Pole), c. 1200-1286, and Hawise Le Strange (c. 1234-after 1310). But Darrell Wolcott (http://www.ancien
2 CONC twalesstudies.org/id167.html) points out some chronological problems regarding Gwenwynwyn that do nothing to clarify the situation.
2 CONT
2 CONT His position in the family tree looks like it should be in between two Williams: 1) William de la Pole (of Mawddwy), d. before 1319, younger brother of Owen de la Pole and, like him, a son of Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn of Powys; and 2
2 CONC ) William (ap Gruffydd) Mawddwy. Gruffydd (Griffin) was "of age" (probably 21 or older) by 1319, but other dates are obscure. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_la_Pole_(of_Mawddwy; ref: G. T. O. Bridgeman, 'The Princes of u
2 CONC pper Powys', Montgomeryshire Collections I (1868), 52 59 77ff.
