2 DATE ABT 1097
2 PLAC Ikeston, Derbyshire England
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} >In the Treasury at Durham, there is a writ of king Stephen addressed to Ranulf de Muscamps and C. his sister, commanding them to restore to St. Cuthbert the land of "Hadereslawe," which Thomas de Muschamps, their 
2 CONC brother, whose heirs they are, gave when he became a monk. The heiress of the barony of Muschamps married Stephen de Bulmer, and her name was Cecily. They had a son Thomas, who succeeded his father in or shortly before 1172, and, 
2 CONC adopting his maternal patronymic, was called Thomas de Muschamps.[https://archive.org/details/earlyyorkshirech02farruoft/page/127/mode/1up 1]
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >"The barony of Wooler was first granted to Robert de Muschamp by Henry I. There was a dispute with Ranulf Flambard, bishop of Durham, for in 1107-8, the king issued a writ to Robert de Muschamp concerning his possession of the ma
2 CONC nor of Ross in Islandshire. This Robert was probably the father of Thomas, Reginald and Cicely de Muschamp. Thomas entered the monastery of Durham, and in 1138, king Stephen ordered Reginald and Cicely to give Hetherslaw to the mo
2 CONC nks of Durham, which Thomas had given them. Reginald succeeded to the barony before 1131, for in that year, he made a payment of 20s. for a hunting hound. He must have died before 1158 for Stephen de Bulemer and another paid 10 ma
2 CONC rks for Wooler."[https://etheses.durham.ac.uk/id/eprint/4373/1/4373_1893.pdf?UkUDh:CyT 2]
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >"Muschamp (Feudal Barons of Wooler, co. Northumberland; descended from Robert de Muschamp, who obtained divers lordships from Henry I., left an only dau. and heir. Cicely Muschamp, m. Sir Stephen de Blumer, second son of Blumer, 
2 CONC of Sheriff Hutton, co. York, and her descendants assumed the name of Muschamp; Robert de Muschamp, Baron of Wooler, great-grandson of Sir Stephen and Cicely, d. 1249, leaving three co-heiresses: Cicely, m. Odinel de Ford; Mary, m.
2 CONC  Valise, Earl of Strathearn, in Scotland; and Isabella, m. William de Huntercombe)."[https://archive.org/details/generalarmoryofe00burk/page/718/mode/1up 3]
2 CONT <nowiki>———————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT >WOOLER NORTHUMBERLAND
2 CONT >Robert Muschamp, who was granted the lordship of Wooler by King Henry I, was succeeded post II1I by his eldest son Thomas who became a monk. Ranulf, br. and h., d.s.p. sometime in Stephen's reign when his heir was his sister CECI
2 CONC LY d. circa 1180.
2 CONT >CECILY m. Stephen Bulmer d. 1171-2. Thomas, s. and h., who took his mother's patronymic, d. 1190 leaving Robert d. 1250 when his heirs were the child of his daughter CECILY and his two daughters MARGERY and ISABEL.
2 CONT >>Regesta, ii, no. 1001; Fees, p. 200; Red Book, p. 562; E.Y.C. ii, pp. 127-9; Pipe Roll 18 Hen. II, p. 67; idem, 26 Hen. II, p. 142; idem, 2 Rich. I, p. 21; C.I.P.M. i. no. 202. The three coheirs were charged £100 relief, i.e. 50
2 CONC  m. each. In 1166 the barony consisted of 5 knights' fees de veteri and of 1¾ de novo. Later scutage was charged on 4-5 knights' fees (Ex. F.R. ii, p. 90; Red Book, pp. 439-40; Fees, pp. 554, 1119; Pipe Roll, 14 Hen. II, p. 171; i
2 CONC dem, 18 Hen. II, p. 67; idem, 1 John, p. 122; idem, 13 John, p. 209; Pipe Roll, E. 372/68, N'humb.; idem, E. 372/96, N'humb.; idem, E. 372/123, N'humb.).
2 CONT >Sanders, ''English Baronies'', p.100. [https://archive.org/details/englishbaroniess0000ivor/page/100/mode/1up Archive.org] (requires free login)
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/englishbaroniess0000ivor/page/100/mode/1up
2 CONT ----
