1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Geoffrey (II) de Say (1135–1214) was an English nobleman, Lord of West Greenwich, and Magna Carta surety.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held land at Edmonton and Sawbridgeworth. He had family claims to larger estates, but they had gone to the kinsman Geoffrey Fitz Peter, 1st Earl of Essex.
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoffrey was the son of William de Saye II and Beatrice de Mandeville. He married (1) Alice de Chesney and (2) Alice de Vere. He had a son named Geoffrey with each of his wives, and an older son named William with his first wife. 
2 CONC His son Geoffrey (III) (1155-1230) by his first marriage inherited the honour of West Greenwich and was a Magna Carta Surety.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3P-S.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT GEOFFREY de Say ([1155]-Poitou 1230 before 26 Aug, bur Dover, Hospital of St Mary).  “Galfridus de Say, filius Galfridi de Say et Aliciæ de Ver, dominus de Rikelyng” donated property to Waldon abbey by undated charter, witnessed b
2 CONC y “domino Willielmo de Mandevilla comite Essexiæ…”.  The Red Book of the Exchequer records "Galfridus de Say junior" holding 7 knights´ fees "de honore Patricii" in Kent in [1210/12].  "Galfr de Say" made a fine for "terris q fuer
2 CONC unt G. de Say patris sui" in Sussex and Middlesex, dated 1214.  The Annals of Tewkesbury record the death in 1230 of “Gaufridus de Sey” and his burial “apud Dovere”.  m ---.  Geoffrey & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT (a)       WILLIAM de Say .  "Upon the death of Geoffrey de Say…William de Say his son and heir" paid homage to the king "for all that Geoffrey held of the king in chief" [in Bedfordshire], dated [Sep] 1230
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He was involved in financial transactions and law suits against his cousin and nephew by marriage, Geoffrey fitz Piers, to gain the lands of the Mandeville Earls of Essex
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He was involved in financial transactions and law suits against his cousin and nephew by marriage, Geoffrey fitz Piers, to gain the lands of the Mandeville Earls of Essex.
