2 CONT * a) '''EUDES de Rie''' (-1 Mar 1120, bur Colchester). His parentage is confirmed by the ''History of the foundation of St John’s'' abbey, Colchester which names “''Eudoni…major domus regiæ''”, “''pater…eius…Hubertus de Ria, qui i
2 CONC nternuntius et sequester inter ducem Normanniæ et regem Angliæ''…”, his three brothers “''Radulfus...custodia castelli et comitatus Notingehamiæ, Hubertus…turris Norwici…Adam…in Cantia”, and “Roasya uxor eius…Gilbertum comes, Roha
2 CONC isæ frater''”[788]. "…''Eudo dapifer''" witnessed the charter dated to [1072] under which William I King of England summoned knights to attend Easter at Clarendon[789]. "…''Eudo de Ria fitz Hubert…Engelramn fitz Hubert'' [=Hilbert
2 CONC ]…" witnessed the charter dated 14 Jul 1080 under which William I King of England confirmed the foundation of the abbey of Lessay[790]. The ''Chronicon Rameseiensis'' records the donations made by "''Eudo…dapifer Regis…sororis suæ
2 CONC  Muriellæ''" dated to [1080], witnessed by "…''Radulfo fratre Ilgeri, Rogero vicecomite et Osberno sororio Eudonis et Walgero filio eius''…"[791]. "…''Eudo, Adam brother of Eudo…Richard de Meri, Geoffrey de Sai, Robert de Oilli''"
2 CONC  witnessed the charter dated 1084 under which William I King of England confirmed a donation by Roger de Albini to Lessay Holy Trinity[792]. ''Domesday Book'' records “''Eudo fitzHubert''” holding Ashe in Overton Hundred in Hampsh
2 CONC ire; St Leonards in Ripplesmere Hundred in Berkshire; "Eudo the Steward" holding numerous properties in Essex, in Norfolk and in Suffolk[793].  "…Eudo dapifer, Ivo dapifer, Hanno dapifer…" witnessed the charter dated 27 Jan 1091 u
2 CONC nder which William II King of England confirmed the status of Bath abbey[794]. King William II’s grant of "''manerio Deremanni quod Lefstanus frater eius habebat''" to “''Eudonem dapiferum meum''” in an undated charter[795], dated
2 CONC  by the ''Regesta Regum Anglo-Normannorum'' to [1093/97][796]. "''Eudo dapifer''" donated "the whole tithe of the forest of Tison...[and rights] in all his honour of Préaux [Pratellis]" to Saint-Amand by undated charter[797]. “''E
2 CONC udo dapifer domini regis''” founded Colchester St John, for the souls of King Henry I, Queen Matilda “...''uxore mea Roaysia''”, by undated charter[798]. The ''History of the foundation of St John’s'' abbey, Colchester records the
2 CONC  death “''pridie Kal Mar 1120''” of “''Eudoni…major domus regiæ''”, and that “''Waltherius eius nepos''” brought his body for burial[799]. 
2 CONT * '''m ROHESE''', daughter of RICHARD FitzGilbert de Brionne & his wife Rohese Giffard (-7 Jan 1121, bur Le Bec, Normandy[800]). “''Eudo dapifer domini regis''” founded Colchester St John, for the souls of King Henry I, Queen Mati
2 CONC lda “...''uxore mea Roaysia''”, by undated charter[801]. Her parentage is confirmed by the undated charter under which “''Rohais uxor Eudonis dapiferi''” donated “''manerium de Halingberi sicut dominus meus Eudo die qua vivus et m
2 CONC ortuus fuit illud habebat” and land which “Gelebertus frater meus” gave her, for the souls of “Eudonis dapiferi mariti mei et Gilberti fratris mei''”[802], which is corroborated by the undated charter under which  “''Walterus fili
2 CONC us Roberti''” donated “''terram de teia''” to Colchester St. John, for the souls of “''patris mei Roberti filii Ricardi et matris mee Matildis et...Rohaise amite mee que ecclesiam Sancti Johannis fundavit et fratrum suorum''”, to 
2 CONC Colchester St. John[803]. The ''History of the foundation of St John’s'' abbey, Colchester also names “''Eudoni…major domus regiæ''” and “''Roasya uxor eius…Gilbertum comes, Rohaisæ frater''”[804]. Other sources suggest a differen
2 CONC t parentage for Rohese. According to Guillaume de Jumièges and the ''Genealogia Fundatoris'' of Tintern Abbey, she was Rohese, widow of Richard FitzGilbert de Brionne, daughter of Gauthier Giffard & his wife Ermengarde (-after 111
2 CONC 3, bur [Colchester]).  Guillaume de Jumièges names "''Galterium Giffardum primum''” as father of “''secundum Galterium Giffardum et filias plures''” of whom “''una...Rohais''” married “''Richardo filio comitis Gisleberti''”[805]. 
2 CONC According to the ''Genealogia Fundatoris'' of Tintern Abbey, Monmouthshire, ”''Rohesia''” married secondly “''Eudoni dapifero Regis Normanniæ''” after the death of “''Ricardo filio comitis Gisleberti''” and that they were both bur
2 CONC ied “''tempore Henrici primi''” in “''castrum Clecestriæ…cœnobio in honore sancti Johannis''” which Eudo constructed[806]. The ''Complete Peerage'' says that this parentage is “probably erroneous”[807]. From a chronological point 
2 CONC of view, the connection would be tight, assuming that the death date of Richard FitzGilbert is correctly estimated to [1090] and the birth of Rohese’s granddaughter by her alleged second marriage, Beatrix, is correctly assessed at
2 CONC  [1105]. This supposed different parentage is disproved by the three sources quoted above. 
2 CONT * '''Eudes & his wife had [one possible child]:''' 
2 CONT ** i)  '''MARGUERITE''' ([1080/90]-). The ''Genealogia Fundatoris'' of Tintern Abbey, Monmouthshire names “''Margareta''” as daughter of “''Eudoni dapifero Regis Normanniæ''” and “''Rohesia''”, adding that she '''married''' “''Wil
2 CONC lielmo de Mandavill''” by whom she was mother of “''Gaufridi filii comitis Essexiæ et iure matris Normanniæ dapifer''”[808]. According to the ''Complete Peerage'', this genealogy is “probably erroneous” but it does not explain the
2 CONC  basis for the doubts[809]. Marguerite’s second '''marriage''' is suggested by the charter dated [1141/42] under which Empress Matilda made various grants of property including a grant to "''Willelmo filio Otuel fratri…Comitis Gau
2 CONC fredi''"[810]. The only Ottiwell has been identified was the illegitimate son of Hugh Earl of Chester. 
2 CONT ** '''m''' ''firstly'' ( [1100/05] ) '''[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL1.htm#WilliamMandevilledied1130 WILLIAM de Mandeville],''' son of GEOFFREY de Mandeville & his first wife Adelisia --- (-[1116]
2 CONC ).  '''William & his wife had [three] children'''
2 CONT *** i) '''GEOFFREY''' ([1100/05]-Mildenhall, Suffolk 14 or 16 Sep 1144, bur 1163 New Temple Church). The ''Genealogia Fundatoris'' of Tintern Abbey, Monmouthshire names “''Gaufridi filii comitis Essexiæ et iure matris Normanniæ da
2 CONC pifer''” as son of “''Willielmo de Mandavill''” and his wife “''Margareta''”[736]. He was created '''Earl of Essex''' in 1140 and 1141.- see below.
2 CONT *** ii) '''BEATRIX''' ([1105]-[Rickling, Essex] 19 Apr [1197 or before], bur Walden Abbey). A manuscript listing property of Walden abbey states, quoting a charter of Stephen King of England dated 1147, that “''sororem suam…Beatri
2 CONC cem''” (referring to "''Galfridus Essexiæ comes''") married "''Hugoni Talebot''" from whom she was divorced and secondly "''Willielmo de Saye''"[737]. She became the heiress of her nephew William de Mandeville Earl of Essex, her s
2 CONC on by her second husband, Geoffrey, being allowed to occupy her place in view of her age[738].The History of the foundation of Walden abbey records that “''Beatrix de Mandavilla domina de Say, soror Galfridi primi, fundatoris, et 
2 CONC amita Willielm''i” succeeded her nephew[739]. The History of the foundation of Walden abbey records the death in 1200 of “''Beatrix de Say, soror fundatoris nostri et uxor Willielmi de Say''” and her burial in the abbey[740]. '''m
2 CONC  firstly''' (divorced) '''HUGH [III] Talbot''', son of ---. '''m secondly WILLIAM de Say''', son of [JORDAN de Say & his wife Lucy de Rumilly] (-[1155][741]).He fought with his brother-in-law at the siege of Burwell Castle in Aug 
2 CONC 1144.
2 CONT *** iii) ['''ALICE'''. Round suggests Alice’s parentage and marriage based on a charter in which "''Adelid''” Capra names William Earl of Essex as her "''nepos''"[742]. '''m WILLIAM "Capra"''', son of ---. Empress Matilda made var
2 CONC ious grants of property by charter dated to [1141/42] including a grant of "''terram patris sui''" to "''Willelmus Cap''"[743].] 
2 CONT ** '''m''' ''secondly'' ( [1116/19] ) '''[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL.htm#Ottiwelldied1120 OTTIWELL, {maybe OTTIWELL FitzHugh}]''', illegitimate son of HUGH Earl of Chester & his mistress ---] (-
2 CONC drowned off Barfleur, Normandy 25 Nov 1120). '''Otuel & his wife had [one] child:'''
2 CONT *** a) ['''WILLIAM FitzOtuel''' ([1120]-after [1166/75]). Empress Matilda made various grants of property including a grant to "''Willelmo filio Otuel fratri…Comitis Gaufredi''" (identified as Geoffrey de Mandeville Earl of Essex)
2 CONC [50]. It is not certain that "''Otuel''" was the same person as the illegitimate son of Earl Hugh, although as noted above no other person of this name has yet been identified. The co-identification appears confirmed by the follow
2 CONC ing two charters. "''Hugo comes Cestrie''" confirmed a donation of land in Thoresby donated by "''Willelmus filius Othuer''" to Greenfield priory, Lincolnshire, for the soul of "''patris mei Randulfi''", by charter dated to [1155]
2 CONC  witnessed by "''Matilla matre sua''…"[51]. "''Willelmus comes de Essex''" confirmed a donation of land in Aby and South Thoresby donated by "''Willelmus filius Otueli avunculus meus''" to Greenfield priory, Lincolnshire by charte
2 CONC r dated to [1166/75] witnessed by "Simone de Bello Campo…"[52].  "…''Willelmo filio Otueri, Rannulfo de Seis, Ingeramo Bagot''…" witnessed the charter dated to the reign of King Henry II under which "''Matildis de Stafford''" gran
2 CONC ted land in Theddlethorpe, Lincolnshire to "''Matildi filie Roberti filii Gilberti filiole mee''", with the consent of "''Johannis filii mei et Radulfi nepotis mei''"[53].]  
