2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Clinton-214
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan de Clinton, daughter of Ida de Odingsells and John de Clinton, married first to Edmund Deincourt.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT A writ was issued on 8 January, 20 Edward II [1327], regarding Edmund Deyncourt alias Dayncourt. On 13 January, 20 Edward II, 1327, an Inquisition found Edmund Deyncourt's next heir was his kinsman, William Dayncourt, aged 26 and 
2 CONC more, and among the properties Edmund held was a messuage, 312a land, 14 (?) and 3r meadow, and 52s 4d rent in Elmerton in Derbyshire, which ought to remain to Hamond de Mascy and Joan his wife for the life of Joan.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT A writ was issued on 26 January, 2 Edward II [1328], regarding Joan, late the wife of Hamo de Mascy, and the subsequent Inquisition held at Derby on the Friday after the Purification, 2 Edward III, [5 February, 1328], found that J
2 CONC oan held for life a messuage, 300a land and 14a meadow, in Elmeton, of the king in chief as of the crown, of the inheritance of William de Eynecourt or de Deyncourt, by service of a quarter of a knight's fee.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes for Edmund Deyncourt
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquisition Post Mortem of Edmund Deyncourt [Lord Deincourt] 13 Jan, 20 Edward II [1326/1327]:[12] Holmesfeld and Elmeton. The manors (extents given), held for life of the king in chief as of the crown by service of 1/2 knight's f
2 CONC ee ; which manors by the fine levied as abovesaid ought to remain to the abovesaid William &c, excepting a messuage, 312a. land, 14(a. ?) and 3r. meadow, and 52s. 4d. rent in Elmeton, which ought to remain to Hamond de Mascy and J
2 CONC oan his wife for the life of the said Joan.
2 CONT
2 CONT 08 Feb 1326/1327:[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT >To Simon do Grymesby, eseheator beyond Trent. Order not to inter-meddle further with a messuage, 312 acres of land, 14 acres and 3 roods of meadow, and 52s. 4d. of rent in Elmeton, and to restore the issues thereof to Hamund de M
2 CONC ascy and Joan his wife, as the king learns by inspection of part of a fine levied before William de Bereford and his fellows, the late king's justices of the Bench, between William son of William de Emeleye, demandant, and Edmund 
2 CONC Deyncourt, deforciant, and by inquisition taken by the escheator that William granted to Edmund Deyncourt the premises for life, with remainder to the said Hamund and Joan for the term of Joan's life, with remainder to Isabella, d
2 CONC aughter of John Dayncourt, and the heirs male of her body, with remainder to the right heirs of Edmund, and Hamund and Joan have appeared in chancery and have prayed that the tenements may be delivered to them, and William Deyncou
2 CONC rt, kinsman and heir of Edmund, has confessed before the king in chancery that the fine was levied in form aforesaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Presumably the Isabella concerned was Isabella, daughter of Edmund, son of John, son of Edmund [Lord Deincourt].
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan, the widow of Edmund Deincourt, died shortly before 26 Jan 1327/1328.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes for Hamon VI Massey:
2 CONT
2 CONT >After the death of his only son, Hamon VI fell in love with Joan Clinton, sister of the Earl of Huntingdon, and he looked for any excuse he could find to marry her. His station of rank was such that he was able to have the church
2 CONC  declare that his marriage to Alice Beauchamp, the mother of his children, illegal under a religious tenet: ie his marriage to Alice was invalid because she was a sister of his first wife. So the Church by a fiat declared that Ham
2 CONC on's marriage to have been meretricious, with Alice declared to have borne bastard children, and thus the children were declared illegitimate. Baron Hamon VI married Joan Clinton after he was freed from Alice, but no children were
2 CONC  born from this union. Joan Clinton before her death in 1329, proved to be as extravagant as Hamon VI was profligate. First he sold his life estate to Dunham-Massey as well as other estates to one Robert De Frodesham. Afterward he
2 CONC  sold the reversion rights thereto to Oliver De Inghon who was the justice of Cheshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
