1 NOTE {geni:about_me} After Domesday all Cheshire belonged to the Norman Overlord Hugh Lupus (Hugh 'The Wolf') who continued to allow Erchebrand use of the land but under the watchful eye of the Norman William Fitznigel. By about 1290 A
2 CONC D it had passed to the Norman Overlord William De Tabley (under King Edward I) who in turn, granted Nether Knutsford to his vassal Sir Richard Massey of Tatton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Knutsford over the moor
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard applied for a market charter in 1292 AD which upset his Overlord William, who only reached agreement over it in 1294 AD, dividing manorial rights and profits at 38 burgesses to himself and 19 to Richard. This division cont
2 CONC inued right up to 1590 AD when the lands of Knutsford passed entirely into the ownership of the Lords of Tatton.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Medieval wars it is highly likely that Knutsford provided archers to the nationally renowned 'Cheshire Bowmen'.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just after the battle of Agincourt a survey of the Bucklow Hundred taken in 1417 AD showed that almost a quarter of Cheshire bowmen came from this area making up 107 out of the 439 recorded.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.virtual-knutsford.co.uk/frameset.php?main=/archaeology/o_knutsford.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Research Notes: From A Genealogical and Heraldic Dictionary of the Landed Gentry of Great Britain and Ireland, Part II, p. 986 "MASSEY."
2 CONT
2 CONT SIR WILLIAM MASSEY, Knt., 16 HENRY III, son of WILLIAM MASSEY, of Tatton, grandson of ROBERT MASSEY, of Sale, and great-grandson of ROBERT MASSEY, living anno 1124, who was son of HAMON MASSEY, 1st Baron of Dunham Massey, temp. WI
2 CONC LLIAM the Conqueror, m. Margaret, dau. and co-heir of Humphrey Rosthorne, of Rosthorne, and was father of
2 CONT
2 CONT '''SIR RICHARD MASSEY, Knt., sheriff of Cheshire, 6 EDWARD I, ancestor of the MASSEYS of Denfield, whose representative,'''
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM MASSEY, of Denfield and Moss, co. Chester, aged 63 at the Heralds' Visitation of that co. in 1663; m. Dorothy, dau. of George Cotton, of Combermere, co. Chester, and dying in 1682, was s. by his son and heir, "
2 CONT
2 CONT Noted events in his life were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT • Living: 1124. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.virtual-knutsford.co.uk/frameset.php?main=/archaeology/o_knutsford.htm
