1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Edward Montagu (or Edward''' de Montacute), 1st Baron Montagu (d. 14 July 1361) was an English peer. He fought at the Battle of Crecy. His first wife, Alice of Norfolk, the granddaughter of Edward I, died as the
2 CONC  result of an assault committed by Montagu and his retainers.
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Montagu,_1st_Baron_Montagu
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the youngest son of William Montagu, 2nd Baron Montagu (d. 18 October 1319), by Elizabeth Montfort (d. August 1354), daughter of Sir Piers Montfort of Beaudesert, Warwickshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife was Alice of Norfolk, daughter of Thomas of Brotherton, and granddaughter of Edward I. In March 1339 the King ordered William Trussell, escheator, to deliver to Montagu and his wife Alice her share of her father's l
2 CONC ands.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought at the Battle of Crecy in 1346, and was summoned to Parliament from 20 November 1348 to 20 November 1360 by writs directed Edwardo de Monte Acuto, 'whereby he is held to have become Lord Montagu'.[6][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice died shortly before 30 January 1352 as the result of an assault by Montagu and his retainers, a crime for which he and some of his followers were indicted.[7][5] In 1361 one William Dunche of Bungay was pardoned for his part
2 CONC  in her death, as well as other felonies.[7][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Montagu died 14 July 1361, his only son and heir by Alice having predeceased him. His place of burial is unknown. His heir was his seven-week-old son by his second marriage, Edward Montagu, who survived him by only three months. A
2 CONC t his death on 4 October 1361 any barony created by writ fell into abeyance, according to modern doctrine, among the surviving daughters of his father.[7][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT His arms were Argent, three lozenges in fess, on each an eagle displayed with a label of three points.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://thepeerage.com/p10206.htm#i102059
