2 CONT GILBERT de Oilly. “R. de Oileo” founded Oseney Abbey, Oxfordshire, with the consent of “Editha uxore mea et filiis meis Henrico et Gilleberto”, by undated charter[774]. “Editha, Roberto de Oilly conjugali copula juncta” donated pr
2 CONC operty to Thame Abbey, for the souls of “Henrici et Gilberti filiorum meorum”, by undated charter witnessed by “Fulco de Oilly, Fulco Luval, Henrico filio Roberti filii Aumari”[775]. “Henricus de Oleio” donated property to Oseney 
2 CONC Abbey, Oxfordshire, with the consent of "Roberto filio regis fratre meo”, for the souls of "…patris mei Roberti de Oleio…fratris mei Gilberti", by undated charter[776].  
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_D%27Oyly_%28Osney%29 Robert and Edith had at least two children—Henry, buried at Osney in 1163,[1] and Gilbert. Henry became 4th Baron Hocknorton and Kings Constable and married Margery, daugh
2 CONC ter of Humphrey de Bohun, Earl of Hereford.
2 CONT * Crossley, Alan.  ''Victoria County History of Oxford'' Volume IV (1969)
2 CONT * Betham, William.  ''The Baronetage of England''. Vol. 2, page 401.  < [https://books.google.com/books?id=QS8wAAAAYAAJ&dq=baron%20hocknorton&pg=PA401#v=onepage&q=baron%20hocknorton&f=false GoogleBooks] >
2 CONT * https://www.multiwords.de/genealogy/doyly1.htm
2 CONT * Eleanor Chance, Christina Colvin, Janet Cooper, C J Day, T G Hassall, Mary Jessup, Nesta Selwyn, 'Medieval Oxford', in A History of the County of Oxford: Volume 4, the City of Oxford, ed. Alan Crossley, C R Elrington (London, 19
