2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Basset
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Basset (died between 1135 and 1144) was a royal judge and sheriff during the reign of King Henry I of England. His father was also a royal justice. In about 1122 Basset married the eventual heiress of another justice; the 
2 CONC marriage settlement has survived. In 1129–30 Basset was co-sheriff of eleven counties. Basset and his wife founded a monastic house in 1125 from their lands, which before the donation were equivalent to 15 knight's fees.
2 CONT
2 CONT Basset was the son of Ralph Basset,[1] who was a royal justice under Henry I. While it is not known whether Richard was Ralph's eldest son, Richard inherited Ralph's estates in Normandy, which were near Montreuil-au-Houlme. He als
2 CONC o inherited his father's English estates at Colston Basset, Kingston Winslow, and Peatling Parva.[2] The bulk of Ralph's English lands did not go to Richard, however.[3] Basset's brother Nicholas signed over his own inheritance to
2 CONC  Richard.[2] Ralph Basset was considered one of the "new men" of Henry I.[4][5] William Basset, the abbot of the Benedictine monastery of the abbey of St Benet of Hulme, may have been a relative, as he granted lands to Richard Bas
2 CONC set in return for a £10 annual rent.[6] Another relative may have been the Robert Basset who witnessed nine charters of Ranulf de Gernon, Earl of Chester.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lands 
2 CONT
2 CONT Basset's lands did not form a compact estate, as they were spread over 11 counties.[12] In 1135, Basset's lands totalled 184.25 carucates of land, and were later considered 15 knight's fees.[13] In Leicestershire, Basset held most
2 CONC  of the lands held by Robert de Buci at the time of the Domesday Survey of 1086. The lands were held by Basset of the king by right of his wife, but how the lands had passed into her family is unclear. In addition, Basset held lan
2 CONC d in Leicestershire from both King David I of Scotland and from Robert de Beaumont, the Earl of Leicester.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1125, Basset and his wife founded an Augustinian Order priory at Launde in Leicestershire,[15] This priory, Launde Priory, was endowed with the village of Loddington in Leicestershire and a number of churches in that county and
2 CONC  others.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and death
2 CONT
2 CONT Basset married Matilda, the daughter and eventual heiress of Geoffrey Ridel (d. 1120), sometime between 1120 and 1123.[1] Matilda had a brother, Robert, who was mentioned in her marriage settlement. By the terms of the settlement,
2 CONC  Robert Ridel was placed under the guardianship of Richard Basset until he was knighted and married to Basset's niece.[2] The marriage settlement describes Matilda's dowry as being worth four knight's fees.[17] Basset also receive
2 CONC d the right to arrange marriages for Matilda's sisters. Robert's lands were to come to Basset if Robert had no children.[17] Not long after the settlement was written, Basset was in possession of the lands that should have been Ro
2 CONC bert's.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Basset witnessed a royal charter in 1135 but was dead by 1144 when his lands were granted by the Empress Matilda and her son Henry to Richard's son 
2 CONT # Geoffrey Ridel. His other sons were 
2 CONT # Ralph Basset, who held lands near Drayton, and 
2 CONT # William Basset, who held lands near Sapcote.[1] William became a royal justice and sheriff like his father.[11] Richard also had two daughters: 
2 CONT # Sibil, who married Robert de Cauz, and 
2 CONT # Matilda, who married John de Stuteville.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph inherited the ancestral lands in Normandy.[2] The Norman chronicler Orderic Vitalis wrote that Basset built a tower on his ancestral lands of Montreuil in Normandy purely to demonstrate his status and wealth.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
