2 CONT Last modified: August 28, 1998
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT William married Alivia Basset, daughter of Thomas Basset and Phillipa Malbank. (Alivia Basset was born in Headington, England.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Malet, Baron of Curry Malet. Known as William the Surety. Magna Carta signatory. From 1210 to 1214 was the Sheriff of Somerset and Dorset. Following the signing of the Magna Carta his lands were confiscated and he was 
2 CONC excommunicated by the Pope in 1216..
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT William II Malet, the Surety of the Magna Charta, was mentioned in 1194 as a minor, in connection with an expedition made that year into Normandy. His principal estate was Curry-Malet. From 1210 to 1214 he was Sheriff of the count
2 CONC ies of Somerset and Dorset. When he joined with the barons against King John and became one of the twenty-five Sureties his lands in four counties were confiscated and given to his son-in-law, Hugh de Vivonia, and to his father-in
2 CONC -law, Thomas Basset, and Malet was excommunicated by the Pope in 1216. He was also fined 2,000 marks, but this remained unpaid until after his death, and at that time 1,000 marks were remitted, being found due him for military ser
2 CONC vice to King John at Poitou. It is interesting to note that there were five contemporary relatives named William Malet, and they all held lands in England or in Jersey. He died about 1217, having married Mabel (Alice) (Alicia) Bas
2 CONC set, daughter of Thomas Basset, of Headington. She survived him and married (2) John Biset. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William and his wife had two sons who died in their father's lifetime and three daughters as follows:
2 CONT *1. Hugh Malet, whose descendants did not survive.
2 CONT *2. William, d.s.p.
2 CONT *3. Hawise Malet. See below.
2 CONT *4. Mabel Malet, whose inheritance of one-third increased to one-half, married (1) Nicholas Avenal; and before November 1223 (2) Hugh de Vivonia (de Forz), baron of Chewton, co. Somerset, and Steward of Poitou, who held West Kingt
2 CONC on in 1214. They had the following children:
2 CONT **1. John de Vivonia, d.s.p. in 1314.
2 CONT **2. William de Vivonia de Fortibus, married Maud Ferrers, and died in 1259. He had four daughters, all minors at his death, as follows:
2 CONT ***1. Joan Vivonia, married Reynold FitzPiers.
2 CONT ***2. Sibyl Vivonia, married Guy de Roche Chinard.
2 CONT ***3. Mabel Vivonia, married Fulk de L'Orty.
2 CONT ***4. Cecily Vivonia, heiress to her cousin, John, married John de Beauchamp who died in 1283. In the 5th year of King Edward I. he was made Governor of the Castles of Caermerdin and Cardigan. They had a son, John de Beauchamp, wh
2 CONC o took part in the wars of Scotland and soon after, in the 14th year of King Edward II., he succeeded to the very extensive landed possessions of his mother, in cos. Dorset, Wilts, Surrey, and Cambridge. Ten years afterwards he wa
2 CONC s made Governor of the Castle of Bridgewater. He died in 1336, and was succeeded by his son, John de Beauchamp, 2nd Lord Beauchamp of Hacche. He died in 1343, having taken part in the French wars of King Edward III. He had issue.
2 CONT **3. Helewise Vivonia, who married Walter de Wahull, son of Saiher de Wahull, who died in 1250. His death occurred in 1269, when he was succeeded by his son, John de Wahull 
2 CONT *5. Bertha Malet, who inherited one-third of the barony, was probably the youngest daughter. She did not marry and d.s.p. before Easter 1221.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Ref: L. J. Sanders, "English Baronies - A Study of Their Origin and Descent 1086-1327" (1960), and Wurts, "Magna Charta, Vol 1," pp. 95-97).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *William II Malet Baron Curry Malet – born about 1172 Enmore, Somerset, England
2 CONT
