2 CONT born abt 1140 Ermore, Somerset, England
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir William Malet (circa 1180 - circa 1215)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Malet |b.c1180\nd.c1215|p516.htm#i18345|Gilbert Malet|d.1194|516.htm#i30842||||||||||||||||
2 CONT
2 CONT He is probably a descendant of William Malet who was High Sheriff of Yorkshire from 1068-9. Although a Norman by birth he may have been grandson of Earl Leofric of Mercia, and he fought at the Battle of Hastings, supposedly being 
2 CONC responsible for the burial of King Harold's body. On becoming Sheriff, he received substantial Yorkshire estates to augment his East Anglian lands centred on the manor of Eye, Suffolk. He was almost certainly the rapacious Sheriff
2 CONC  criticised for seizing the property of Archbishop Ealdred, and was custodian of York during the attack by Edgar the Aetheling in 1068 and the subsequent 'Harrying of the North'. He seems to have been largely responsible for the f
2 CONC ire which practically destroyed York and which allowed Danes to seize the city in September 1069, when he and his family were captured. He was released, but was replaced as Sheriff and had no further connection with Yorkshire. He 
2 CONC was briefly Sheriff of Suffolk, and was sent on campaign against Hereward in 1071, but died soon afterwards. He was married to Esilia, dauaghter of Gilbert Crispin, a Norman nobleman, and was succeeded by his son Robert, who becam
2 CONC e Master Chamberlain to Henry I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Malet married Alice Basset, daughter of Thomas Basset and Phillippe Malbank. She was co-heir of her father, and in her issue sole heir of the barony of Headington, Oxon. Sir William Malet was born circa 1180. There is 
2 CONC no clear evidence of his parentage. He was the son of Gilbert Malet.
2 CONT
2 CONT William died circa 1215 at Curry Mallet, Somerset.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Child of Sir William Malet and Alice Basset:''' Hawise Malet+ b. c 1200, d. a 4 May 1247
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Lord of Curry-Malet, Somersetshire
2 CONT -Magna Charta surety
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT >William was of Curry Malet in Somerset, England.
2 CONT >He was also called Sir William Malet.
2 CONT >He was an adult in 1196.
2 CONT >He was Sheriff of Somerset and Dorset in England in 1209.
2 CONT >He married Alice Basset (not our ancestor), daughter of Thomas II Basset of Headington and Philippa Malbank. 
2 CONT >William was Lord of Curry-Mallet in Somerset, England, circa 1215. He was present at the signing of the Magna Carta on 15 June 1215 at Runnymede, between Windsor and Staines, Surrey, England. He was a Surety Baron there.
2 CONT >'''See "My Lines"''' ( http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/p385.htm#i8275 )
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT From ''Notices of an English Branch of the Malet Family'', by Arthur Malet, pp. 75-76, Titled "Gilbert Malet of Curry and Shepton:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"We have in the time of Henry II, but without date Gilbert Malet the Seneschal a witness to a deed concerning the Danegeld and other liberties of the Church of Christ at Canterbury [Rymer, Fadæder, vol I, p. 43, temp. Henry II]; 
2 CONC and the next is an excerpt of Sir William Pole's [Sir A. M.'s MSS, vol I, Sup. I, p. 34], in which Gilbert names 'William Malet my heir, Robert and Ralph his brothers, William Malet my grandson.' Thus we are not left in ignorance 
2 CONC as to his family, but have more than usual evidence of the relationship of the three last Barons of this branch. "Gilbert was succeeded by his eldest son William Malet."
2 CONT
