2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Fitzhamon (died March 1107), or Robert FitzHamon, Sieur de Creully in the Calvados region and Torigny in the Manche region of Normandy, was Lord of Gloucester and the Norman conqueror of Glamorgan, southern Wales. He became
2 CONC  the leader of Glamorgan in 1075.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a kinsman of the Conqueror and one of the few Anglo-Norman barons to remain loyal to the two successive kings William Rufus and Henry I of England, he was a prominent figure in England and Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known about his earlier life, or his precise relationship to William I of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert FitzHamon (born c. 1045-1055, d. March 1107 Falaise, Normandy) was the son of Haimo the Sheriff of Kent and grandson of Haimo Dentatus ('The Toothy', i.e., probably buck-toothed). His grand-father held the lordships of Tori
2 CONC gny, Creully, Mézy, and Evrecy in Normandy, but following his death at the Battle of Val-ès-Dunes in 1047, the family might have lost these lordships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Few details of Robert's career prior to 1087 are available.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert probably did not fight at Hastings, and does not appear in the Domesday Book, although some relatives may.
2 CONT
2 CONT The chronology of Fitzhamon's conquest of Glamorgan is uncertain, but it probably took place in the decades after he received Gloucester.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert FitzHamon (died March 1107), Sieur de Creully, Calvados region and Torigny in the Manche region of Normandy, Lord of Gloucester and the Norman conqueror of Glamorgan, southern Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Hamon & his wife
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Sibylle de Montgomery
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Mabel (married Robert FitzRoy)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Hawise
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Cecile
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Amice
2 CONT
