1 NAME Richard /de Grenville/ I
2 GIVN Richard
2 NSFX I
2 _MARNM de Grenville
2 NICK Ricus
2 NICK de Granville
2 NICK Earl of Corbeil
2 NICK Baron of Thorigny and Granville
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1094
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bideford
3 CTRY England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1147
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir Richard de Grenville (died after 1142) (alias de Grainvilla, de Greinvill, etc.) was one of the Twelve Knights of Glamorgan who served under Robert FitzHamon (died 1107),[1] in the conquest of Glamorgan in Wale
2 CONC s. He obtained from FitzHamon the lordship of Neath in which he built Neath Castle and in 1129 founded Neath Abbey. He is by tradition the founder and ancestor of the prominent Westcountry Grenville family of Stowe in the parish o
2 CONC f Kilkhampton in Cornwall and of Bideford in Devon, the later head of which family was John Granville, 1st Earl of Bath (1628–1701). The surname of his supposed descendants the Westcountry Grenville family was spelled by tradition
2 CONC  "Grenville" until 1661 when it was altered to "Granville".
2 CONT Richard de Grenville is thought to have married twice. Firstly, according to Ordericus Vitalis (died c. 1142), to Isabel Giffard, the only daughter of Walter Giffard, 1st Earl of Buckingham (died 1102) and Lord of Longueville in N
2 CONC ormandy, co-heiress with her aunt Rohesia (wife of Richard FitzGilbert, Lord of Clare) of the great possessions and lordships of the Giffard family.[6] Grenville's second wife was a certain "Constance", mentioned as his wife in hi
2 CONC s foundation charter of Neath Abbey. She acted jointly with him in the founding of that Abbey, which suggested to Round that she may have been a native-born Welsh lady and the heiress of Neath.[3] Granville (1895) suggested she wa
2 CONC s the daughter of Caradoc ap Arthur, Lord of Glyn Nedd.[6] No surviving charter of his mentions any children and the fact that the lordship and castle of Neath escheated to the overlord the Earl of Gloucester and Lord of Glamorgan
2 CONC , the son-in-law and heir of Robert FitzHamon, suggested to Round that he left no children to inherit his possessions.[7] Likewise, he held a few manors in Devon from the feudal barony of Gloucester, one of these being Naissa (Ash
2 CONC  Reigny) which was re-granted after his death by the overlord to the de Reigny family.[7] This evidence that the lands of Richard de Grenville did not descend to the later Westcountry Grenville family suggested to Round that thoug
2 CONC h the latter family claimed to descend from the lord of Neath, this was not the case.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to tradition and as confirmed retrospectively by the 1661 warrant of King Charles II, Richard de Grenville was claimed to have been the brother and heir male of Robert FitzHamon, who was supposed to have been Earl of Cor
2 CONC boile and Lord of Thorigny and Granville in Normandy. Although FitzHamon's lands passed via his daughter Mabel FitzHamon (1090–1157) to her husband Robert Fitzroy, 1st Earl of Gloucester (died 1147) and to their descendants, FitzH
2 CONC amon's Norman titles were inexplicably claimed to have passed to his brother Richard de Grenville as his heir male; Round points out that in fact, under ancient Norman law, titles could indeed be inherited via female lines.[8] It 
2 CONC was from the 1661 royal warrant that the Granville family of Stowe and Bideford, who claimed to be descended from Richard, were granted licence to use these titles, even retrospectively on monuments they later erected to their anc
2 CONC estors. The titles were mere historical curiosities and carried no rights in the Peerage of Great Britain.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Round, Richard de Grenville died after June 1142.[4] This deduction follows from his last being recorded as having signed (as Richard de Greinvill) a charter dated "probably June 1142" as a sworn pledge giver to Rober
2 CONC t, 1st Earl of Gloucester in his treaty of alliance with Miles Earl of Hereford.
2 CONT
2 CONT By tradition Richard de Grenville is said by Prince (died 1723),[9] (apparently following Fuller's Worthies)[7]) after he had founded Neath Abbey and bestowed upon it all his military acquisitions for its maintenance, to have "ret
2 CONC urned to his patrimony at Bideford where he lived in great honour and reputation the rest of his days". However, according to Round no proof exists that Richard de Grenville ever held the manor of Bideford, which was later one of 
2 CONC the principal seats of the Westcountry Grenville family. It was however certainly one of the constituent manors of the Honour of Gloucester granted by King William Rufus to Robert FitzHamon."[10] Richard de Grenville is known to h
2 CONC ave held seven knight's fees from the Honour of Gloucester, either granted to him by his brother FitzHamon or the latter's son-in-law and heir Robert, 1st Earl of Gloucester (1100–1147). Round supposes instead that the Grenvilles 
2 CONC of Bideford and Stowe were descended from a certain "Robert de Grenville" (alias de Grainville, de Grainavilla, etc.) who was a junior witness to Richard's foundation charter of Neath Abbey and who in the 1166 Cartae Baronum retur
2 CONC n was listed as holding one knight's fee from the Earl of Gloucester, feudal baron of Gloucester. Robert's familial relationship, if any, to Richard is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Grenville family pedigree dated 1639 stated that in his old age Richard undertook a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, in which city he died.[4] This story appears to derive from the Gwentian Chronicle or Aberporgwm Brut, which adds that 
2 CONC he brought back from the land of Canaan a man named Lalys who was "well-versed in the science of architecture, who erected monasteries, castles and churches" and who afterwards went to London as architect to King Henry I.
2 CONT Richard de Grenville - Wikipedia https://share.google/IPy8ts6c7RqAdMXJd
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT
