2 CONT Hugh "Le Meschin", Earl of Chester[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh II de Kevelioc 5th Earl  The 5th earl was Ranulf’s son Hugh II de Kevelioc who was born in Wales. His father's northern lands were given to King David of Scotland, known as the Earldom of Huntingdon.
2 CONT Scotland had been divided into two and ruled by David and his brother William. William died in 1124 without issue so David succeeded to the whole of Scotland. He had received a Norman education and Anglo-Saxon culture influenced h
2 CONC im. He granted lands to Anglo-Saxon friends and later to Anglo-Normans. David became Earl of Nottingham and Huntingdon through marriage to Maud the daughter of Hugh de Kevelioc. Their son John was to become the 7th and last Earl o
2 CONC f Chester. Hugh II held lands in England and Normandy, where he spent most of his time overseeing his lordships of Avranches and Bayeux. In 1169 he married Beatrice de Montford daughter of Simon III de Montford and cousin to Henry
2 CONC  II who gave her away in marriage. Her father later became the leader in the baronial revolt against Henry II in 1173/4. Hughe's son Ranulf III de Blunderville later became the 6th earl.
2 CONT
2 CONT His daughters made good marriages with important leaders of the 12th century. Maud married David of Scotland, 8th Earl of Huntingdon Mabel married William de Aubigny, 3rd Earl of Arundel Agnes married William de Ferrers, 4th Earl 
2 CONC of Derby Hawise, Countess of Lincoln, married Robert II de Quincy Another daughter married a Welsh prince and an illegitimate daughter Amice married Ralph de Mainwaring. Hugh II was aware of the problems between Henry and Thomas B
2 CONC ecket in 1170. Hugh died in 1181 at Leek in Staffordshire and was buried in St. Werburgh Abbey.
2 CONT
