2 CONT *'''Sir Hugh de Courtenay
2 CONT *'''Birth: 	Mar. 25, 1251  Okehampton, Devon, England
2 CONT *'''Death: 	Feb. 28, 1292  Colyton, Devon, England
2 CONT *'''Baron of Okehampton, Earl of Devon. Knight of Buckinghamshire. Son of''' Sir John de Courtenay and Lady Isabel de Vere. '''Grandson of''' Sir Robert de Courtenay, Sheriff of Devon and Oxfordshire and Mary de Vernon, Hugh de Ve
2 CONC re, 4th Earl of Oxford and Hawise de Quincy.
2 CONT *'''Husband of Eleanor le Despenser''', daughter of Sir Hugh le Despenser and Alivia Bassett. '''Father of three sons and four daughters:
2 CONT **Hugh de Courtenay, 9th Earl of Devon
2 CONT **John Courtenay
2 CONT **Philip Courtenay
2 CONT **Isabel de Courtenay, who married John de Saint John, 1st Lord St John of Basing
2 CONT **Aveline de Courtenay
2 CONT **Egeline de Courtenay
2 CONT **Margaret de Courtenay
2 CONT *'''Eleanor's maritagium included the manors of Wooton and Dunstredon.
2 CONT *'''Hugh paid fines to the king twice in order to avoid military service, December of 1276 and June of 1287, but was in the army of West Wales in 1282 and summoned to attend the King at Shrewsbury 28 June 1283.
2 CONT *'''The date of his death, 28th of February, has been recorded in the Forde Abbey records, but the Fine Rolls changed his death to March 5th. His Buckingham estate records state his death as "after the Feast of St Peter and to the
2 CONC  Feast of the Exalted St Crucis."
2 CONT *Family links: 
2 CONT *''' Parents:
2 CONT *  John de Courtenay (1224 - 1274)
2 CONT *  Isabel de Vere Courtenay (____ - 1299)
2 CONT *''' Spouse:
2 CONT *'''  Eleanor le Despenser de Courtenay (____ - 1328)*
2 CONT *''' Children:
2 CONT **  Hugh de Courtenay (1273 - 1340)*
2 CONT **  Egelina De Courtenay Scales (1282 - 1335)*
2 CONT *'''Burial:  Cowick Priory, Exeter, City of Exeter, Devon, England
2 CONT *Maintained by: Anne Shurtleff Stevens
2 CONT *Originally Created by: Jerry Ferren
2 CONT *Record added: Aug 03, 2010 
2 CONT *Find A Grave Memorial# 55817932
2 CONT *From: http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=55817932 __________________
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *1.         JOHN de Courtenay of Okehampton (-3 May 1274, bur Forde Abbey, Devon).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey names “dominum Johannem de Courtenay” as son of “Robertus de Courtenay” and his wife “domina
2 CONC  Maria, juniore filia domini Willielmi de Redveriis comitis Devoniæ”[569].  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey records the death “V Non Maii 1273” of “Johannes de Courtenay” and his burial at Ford[570].  Inquisi
2 CONC tions after a writ dated 11 May "2 Edw I" following the death of "John de Corteney alias de Curtenay" name “Isabel sometime his wife...Hugh his son aged 24 is his next heir”, and record that “William de Vernoun earl of the Isle ga
2 CONC ve the manor [of Cruk] to Robert de Curtenay father of the said John in free marriage with Mary his daughter” and that “Hawis de Curtenay gave to [Forde abbey]...land of Hargrave...and Robert de Curtenay her son and heir confirmed
2 CONC  the gift”[571].  m as her first husband, ISABEL de Vere, daughter of HUGH de Vere Earl of Oxford & his wife Hawise de Quincy (after 1223-11 Aug 1299 or after, bur Exeter Dominican Church).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia 
2 CONC of Forde Abbey records that “dominum Johannem de Courtenay” married “Isabellam filiam domini Johannis de Veere comitis Oxoniæ”, that she married “Olivero de Dineham” after the death of her first husband, died “III Id Aug” and was 
2 CONC buried “apud Fratres Prædicatores Exoniæ”[572].  She married secondly Olivier de Dineham.  The primary source which confirms her second marriage has not yet been identified.   John & his wife had [two] children: 
2 CONT **'''a)         HUGH de Courtenay of Okehampton (-Colcombe 28 Feb 1292, bur Cowick near Exeter).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey names “Hugonem de Courtnay primum” as son of''' “dominum Johannem de Courtenay
2 CONC ” and his wife “Isabellam filiam domini Johannis de Veere comitis Oxoniæ”[573].  Inquisitions after a writ dated 11 May "2 Edw I" following the death of "John de Corteney alias de Curtenay" name “Isabel sometime his wife...'''Hugh
2 CONC  his son aged 24 is his next heir”[574].  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey records the death ”apud Colecombe III Kal Mar 1291” of “Hugonem de Courtnay primum” and his burial “apud Cowick prope Exon.”[575].  m 
2 CONC ELEANOR Le Despencer''', daughter of HUGH Le Despencer & his wife Aline Basset of Wycombe, Buckinghamshire (-30 Sep 1328, bur Cowick near Exeter).  '''The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey records that “Hugonem de 
2 CONC Courtnay primum” married “Elianoram''' filiam…domini Hugonis le Despencer primi, patris etiam domini Hugonis le Despencer…postea comitis Winton”[576].  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey records the death ”1328 
2 CONC II Kal Oct” '''of “Hugonem de Courtnay primum…[vidua] domina Alianora” and her burial''' “prope maritum…apud Cowick”[577].  '''Hugh & his wife had [seven] children: 
2 CONT ***i)          [ELEANOR (-before 6 Jun 1301).  According to the Complete Peerage, the wife of Henry de Grey was “said to have m 1stly Eleanor daughter of Hugh de Courtenay”[578].  The same work suggests that “if this is correct” h
2 CONC er father was Hugh who married Eleanor le Despencer.  However, this does not appear to be an ideal fit from a chronological point of view, assuming that Hugh le Despencer is identified as the Hugh who was killed at the battle of E
2 CONC vesham in 1265.  The Complete Peerage records that this Hugh le Despencer married “in or before 1260” Aline Basset, their son Hugh le Despencer being born 1 Mar 1261[579].  This would fit approximately with the estimated birth dat
2 CONC e of Hugh de Courtenay, son of Hugh de Courtenay and Eleanor, in [1275].  However, the wife of Henry de Grey would presumably have been born in the early 1260s, assuming that her marriage is correctly estimated to before [1281/82]
2 CONC .  If that last date is correct, Eleanor, wife of Henry, would more likely have been born in the previous Courtenay generation.  The problem is that a “Hugh de Courtenay” has not yet been identified in that earlier generation.  m 
2 CONC (before [1281/82]) as his first wife, HENRY de Grey of Codnor, Derbyshire, son of JOHN de Grey & his wife Lucy de Mohun of Dunster, Somerset (-Sep 1308).  He was summoned to Parliament in 1299 whereby he is held to have become Lor
2 CONC d Grey.] 
2 CONT ***ii)         HUGH de Courtenay ([1275]-23 Dec 1340, bur Cowick, near Exeter).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey names “Hugonem secundum…ac Philippum postea dominum de Mourton prope Dartmore” as sons of “Hugo
2 CONC nem de Courtnay primum” & his wife[580].  He succeeded his father in 1292 at Okehampton.  He was summoned to parliament 6 Feb 1299, whereby he is held to have become Lord Courtenay.  He was declared Earl of Devon 22 Feb 1335. 
2 CONT ***-         see below. 
2 CONT ***iii)        PHILIP de Courtenay (-killed in battle Stirling 24 Jun 1314).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey names “Hugonem secundum…ac Philippum postea dominum de Mourton prope Dartmore” as sons of “Hugonem
2 CONC  de Courtnay primum” & his wife, adding that Philip was killed “VIII Kal Jul 1314 in bello Scotiæ apud Striveling”[581]. 
2 CONT ***iv)       ISABEL (-after Feb 1323).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey names “Isabellam domini Johannis de St John uxorem, Avelinam domini Johannis Gifford militis uxorem, necnon Egelinam Roberti de Scales u
2 CONC xorem, et Margaretam Johannis de Mulis…uxorem” as the four daughters of “Hugonem de Courtnay primum” & his wife[582].  A manuscript recording the founding of Boxgrove Priory, Sussex names "Isabellam filiam nobilis viri Hugonis de 
2 CONC Cortenay" as the wife of “Johannes secundus [de sancto Johanne]”[583].  m as his first wife, JOHN de St John, son of JOHN de St John of Basing, Hampshire & his wife Alice de FitzPiers ([1271/74]-before 4 Apr 1329).  He was summone
2 CONC d to Parliament in 1299 whereby he is held to have become Lord St John of Basing. 
2 CONT ***v)        AVELINE .  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey names “Isabellam domini Johannis de St John uxorem, Avelinam domini Johannis Gifford militis uxorem, necnon Egelinam Roberti de Scales uxorem, et Margar
2 CONC etam Johannis de Mulis…uxorem” as the four daughters of “Hugonem de Courtnay primum” & his wife[584].  m JOHN Giffard, son of [OSBERT Giffard of Winterborne Houghton, Dorset & his wife Joan ---] (-after 1327). 
2 CONT ***vi)       EGELINE (-10 Oct 1335 or before).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey names “Isabellam domini Johannis de St John uxorem, Avelinam domini Johannis Gifford militis uxorem, necnon Egelinam Roberti de 
2 CONC Scales uxorem, et Margaretam Johannis de Mulis…uxorem” as the four daughters of “Hugonem de Courtnay primum” & his wife[585].  m ROBERT de Scales, son of ROBERT de Scales Lord Scales & his wife Isabel --- ([1278/79]-20 Mar 1324). 
2 CONC  He succeeded his father in 1305 as Lord Scales. 
2 CONT ***vii)      MARGARET .  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey names “Isabellam domini Johannis de St John uxorem, Avelinam domini Johannis Gifford militis uxorem, necnon Egelinam Roberti de Scales uxorem, et Marga
2 CONC retam Johannis de Mulis…uxorem” as the four daughters of “Hugonem de Courtnay primum” & his wife[586].  m JOHN de Mulis, son of ---. 
2 CONT **b)         .... etc.
2 CONT *HUGH de Courtenay, '''son of HUGH de Courtenay of Okehampton, Devon & his wife Eleanor le Despencer''' ([1275]-23 Dec 1340, bur Cowick, near Exeter).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey names “Hugonem secundum”
2 CONC  as '''son of “Hugonem de Courtnay primum” & his wife'''[591].  He succeeded his father in 1292 at Okehampton.  He was summoned to parliament 6 Feb 1299, whereby he is held to have become Lord Courtenay.  He was declared Earl of D
2 CONC evon 22 Feb 1335. 
2 CONT *m (1292) AGNES de St John, daughter of JOHN de St John of Basing, Hampshire & his wife Alice FitzPiers ([1274/75]-Tiverton 11 Jun 1345, bur 27 Jun 1345 Cowick, near Exeter).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey 
2 CONC records that ”dominus Hugo secundus” married “dominæ Agnetæ sorori sororii sui domini Johannis de St. John” when she was 17 years old[592].  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Forde Abbey records the death “1340 die Dominic
2 CONC a in festo sanctæ Trinitatis et sancti Barnabæ Apostoli…apud Tiverton” of “domina Agneta de Courtnay comitissa Devoniæ” and her burial “apud Cowick prope Exon.”[593]. 
2 CONT *Hugh & his wife had six children: 
2 CONT ** .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL1.htm#HughCourtenaydied1292 _______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Courtenay (M) d. between 28 February 1291 and 1292, #911
2 CONT
2 CONT      Hugh Courtenay was the son of John Courtenay and Isabel Vere. Hugh Courtenay married Eleanor Despenser, daughter of Hugh Despenser Justicar of England. Hugh Courtenay died between 28 February 1291 and 1292.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Child of Hugh Courtenay and Eleanor Despenser:
2 CONT     Hugh Courtenay Earl of Devon+   b. c 1276, d. 23 Dec 1340 
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: Hugh de Courtenay Prefix: Sir Given Name: Hugh Surname: de Courtenay Sex: M _UID: B0122AFA5118D811BE490080C8C142CC560B Change Date: 16 Nov 2003 Birth: BET 1248 AND 1250 Death: 28 FEB 1292
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: John de Courtenay  Mother: Isabel de Vere b: ABT 1222
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Eleanor de Spencer b: ABT 1252 Children
2 CONT  Hugh de Courtenay b: 14 SEP 1275 in Okehampton, Devonshire, England
2 CONT  Isabel de Courtenay b: 1283
2 CONT  Egeline de Courtenay b: ABT 1282
2 CONT  Eleanor de Courtenay b: ABT 1267
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh took part in the expedition to Wales in 1282, later participating in more expeditions.  He had many contests with the monks of Ford regarding services owed to them as patron of their abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.familysearch.org/Eng/search/AF/individual_record.asp?recid=6614882&lds=0&frompage=99
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Hugh de Courtenay (1251–1292) was the son and heir of John de Courtenay, feudal baron of Okehampton, Devon, by Isabel de Vere, daughter of Hugh de Vere, 4th Earl of Oxford. His son inherited the earldom of Devon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Hugh de Courtenay, born 25 March 1251, was the son and heir of John de Courtenay of Okehampton, Devon, by Isabel de Vere, daughter of Hugh de Vere, 4th Earl of Oxford, and Hawise de Quincy. John's father, Robert de Courtenay (
2 CONC d. 26 July 1242), son of Renaud de Courtenay (d.1190) by Hawise de Curcy (d.1219), heiress of the feudal barony of Okehampton, married Mary de Redvers (sometimes called 'de Vernon'), daughter of William de Redvers, 5th Earl of Dev
2 CONC on (d.1217).
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to avoid military service Courtenay paid a fine on 12 December 1276. He was called to arms on the emergency against the Welsh princes, fighting in the 1282 campaign. He attended upon the King at Shrewsbury on 28 June 1283
2 CONC . In 1284, he came into possession of The Abbey, Sutton Courtenay, which he first leased to Solomon of Rochester. He again absented himself from the wars on 14 June 1287 by paying the King's justice a fine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Courtenay married Eleanor le Despenser (d.1328), daughter of Hugh le Despencer, 1st Baron le Despencer, Justiciar of England, of Loughborough, Leicestershire and Ryhall, Rutland by his wife Aline Basset, daughter of Sir Philip Bas
2 CONC set, Justiciar of England, of Wycombe, Buckinghamshire and Compton Bassett and Wootton Bassett, Wiltshire. By his wife he had four[6] sons and five[6] daughters:
2 CONT    1- Hugh de Courtenay, 1st/9th Earl of Devon (1276–1340) of 
2 CONT        Tiverton Castle, eldest son and heir.
2 CONT    2- Sir Philip Courtenay (d.1314) of Moreton Hampstead in 
2 CONT        Devon, slain at Stirling on 24 June 1314, according to 
2 CONT        Vivian.[6] Died childless, when Moreton Hampstead was 
2 CONT        inherited by his elder brother the Earl of Devon.
2 CONT    3- John Courtenay, died young.
2 CONT    4- Robert Courtenay, died young.
2 CONT    5- Isabel de Courtenay, wife of John de Saint John, 1st Baron 
2 CONT        St John (died 1329) of Basing.
2 CONT    6- Aveline de Courtenay, wife of Sir John Giffard
2 CONT    7- Egeline (or Eleanor) de Courtenay, wife of Robert le Scales.
2 CONT    8- Margaret (or Margery) de Courtenay, wife of John de 
2 CONT        Moels. Other sources give her husband as Nicholas de 
2 CONT        Moels, 2nd Baron Moels (d.1316), feudal baron of North 
2 CONT        Cadbury, Somerset. Without progeny.
2 CONT    9- Alice Courtenay, died young
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT Courtenay died at Colcombe, Devon, on 28 February 1292. He was buried at Cowick Priory, near Exeter.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_de_Courtenay
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Courtenay-12
