1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hugh Courtenay Earl of Devon (M)
2 CONT
2 CONT b. circa 1276, d. 23 December 1340, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Courtenay Earl of Devon was born circa 1276. He was the son of Hugh Courtenay and Eleanor Despenser. Hugh Courtenay Earl of Devon married Agnes St. John, daughter of John St. John Governor of Aquitaine and Alice Fitzpiers, ci
2 CONC rca 1292. Hugh Courtenay Earl of Devon died on 23 December 1340.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Hugh Courtenay Earl of Devon and Agnes St. John:
2 CONT
2 CONT #     Margaret Courtenay   d. 2 Aug 1385 
2 CONT #     Hugh Courtenay Earl of Devon+   b. 12 Jul 1303, d. 2 May 1377  
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh was Earl of Devon, Lord Courtenay
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh de Courtenay, 9th Earl of Devon (1276-1340) (cousin; declared Earl 1335) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh de Courtenay was probably born in 1275. His parents were Sir Hugh de Courtenay of Okehampton and Eleanor le Despenser, daughter of Lord Hugh le Despenser, the significant advisers to King Edward II. He was grandson of John de
2 CONC  Courtenay of Okehampton by Isabel de Vere, daughter of Hugh, Earl of Oxford. John's father Robert had married Mary, the youngest daughter of William de Redvers, who had died in 1293. According to the Complete Peerage Hugh became 
2 CONC 9th Earl of Devon through the maternal line on extinction of the de Redvers inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 28 Feb 1292, about the time of his marriage, Hugh succeeded to the Okehampton estate and to those de Reviers estates that had not yet been alienated to the Crown. He may have been styled Earl of Devon, the first of the Courtena
2 CONC y family, although was not recognised in the de facto Earldom until 1333. He did homage to Edward I on 20 June 1297 and was granted his own livery stable. At the time the King was with his army crossing the Tweed into Scotland. It
2 CONC  is probable that the honour was in acknowledgement of Hugh's military achievements. That July the English defeated and humiliated the Scots at Irvine. However the following year the tables were turned on the advent of the remarka
2 CONC ble campaign of William Wallace.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following February, 1298 he was summoned as a Lord in Parliament. He remained an important noble at Parliaments into the reign of Edward III. He was summoned as Hugoni de Curtenay with the specious suffix of senior being known
2 CONC  as Lord Courtenay.
2 CONT
2 CONT Courtenay joined King Edward at the long siege of Caerlaverock Castle, just over the Solway Firth for a fortnight in July 1300. He proved himself a fine soldier and loyal adherent to the English crown. He had not been present at t
2 CONC he disastrous encounter outside Stirling Castle in 1298, during which half the English contingent were killed, including commander Hugh Cressingham. But Edward was determined to march into Ayrshire to devastate Robert Bruce's esta
2 CONC tes. Unfortunately the English army melted away into the forests as the army moved further northwards. Courtenay may have been with the English King when he sat down in Sweetheart Abbey to receive Winchelsea, Archbishop of Canterb
2 CONC ury who had travelled north with a demanding missive from Pope Boniface to cease hostilities. The King could not ignore this order. In September he disbanded troops and withdrew over the Solway Firth to Carlisle. The campaign had 
2 CONC failed due to a shortage of money, so Parliament was recalled for January 1301. Before returning to London the English drew up a six months truce.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parliament met at Lincoln. The agenda included redrafting the Royal Forest Charter, which had no precedent since it was first introduced in the reign of Henry II, 150 years earlier. Local juries were expected to "perambulate the f
2 CONC orests" to gather evidence. But the King needed money and was required by Parliament to surrender his absolute authority and ownership of what became community forests.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1306 the Prince of Wales was despatched into Scotland; the vanguard led by Aymer de Valence, the King's cousin. On 22 May, Courtenay was knighted by the Prince, presumably for his efforts against the Scots. In June the English 
2 CONC occupied Perth. On 19 June, Valence, who had cut a swathe through the Lowlands fell on the Scots army at Methven in the early dawn. The Bruce fled into the hills. Edward I was merciless as many prisoners were punished. That autumn
2 CONC  the army returned to Hexham. The war was all but over: there were however sieges at Mull of Kintyre and Kildrummy Castle, Aberdeenshire. Edward I committed many atrocities rounding up the Scots aristocracy and their women.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then as Robert Bruce returned from exile in Ireland the English army started losing battles. The ailing King had one last campaign in which Courtenay played a major part. Struggling into the saddle to the Solway Firth, Edward I di
2 CONC ed at Burgh-on-Sands awaiting a crossing. In 1308 a new campaign was sent to quell Robert the Bruce, and Courtenay was made a knight banneret, one of the King's elite household.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the reign of Edward II he was made a Lord Ordainer, one of the ruling council in the Lords. He was appointed to the King's Council on 9 Aug 1318. He was appointed the Warden of the coast of Devon and Cornwall in 1324 and th
2 CONC en again in 1336, because his estates stretched across what is now Exmoor and Dartmoor. But he took the honours reluctantly and played a guarded game with King and Parliament. A veteran campaigner he aimed to ingratiate himself wi
2 CONC th the young Edward III, and so refused the Third Penny from the Exchequer. He was investigated; and on 22 Feb 1335 elevated to the Earldom of Devon, restored to his ancestral line.
2 CONT
2 CONT The styling of Hugh is often confused. He was the 9th Earl, but the first in the Courtenay creation. He died at Cowick near Exeter on 23 dec 1340, and was buried there on 5 Feb 1341. It is possible that his body lay in state for r
2 CONC oyal visitors to come to pay respects to a great soldier and statesman because his honours and titles were confirmed by Parliamentary writ on 3 Jan 1340/1.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Agnes de St John, daughter of John St John of Basing, Hampshire by Alice, daughter of Reynold FitzPiers. They had six children
2 CONT
2 CONT (Wikipedia)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Hugh de Courtenay, 1st Earl of Devon1 
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #9221, b. circa 1276, d. 23 December 1340
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=7 Apr 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT      Sir Hugh de Courtenay, 1st Earl of Devon was born circa 1276.1 He was the son of Sir Hugh de Courtenay and Eleanor le Despencer.1 He was born on 12 July 1303 at Okehampton, Devon, England.2 He married Agnes de St. John, daugh
2 CONC ter of John de St. John and Alice FitzPiers, in 1292.1 He died on 23 December 1340.1
2 CONT
2 CONT      In 1292 he inherited the Okehampton estates from his father.1 In 1293 he inherited the unalienated portions of the Reviers estate, also from his father.1 He was created 1st Lord Courtenay [England by writ] on 6 February 1298/
2 CONC 99.1 He fought in the Siege of Carlaverock in 1300.1 He was invested as a Knight in 1306.1 He was invested as a Knight Banneret in 1308.1 He held the office of a Lord Ordainer in 1313.1 He held the office of Warden of Devon and Co
2 CONC rnwall Coast in 1324.1 He was created 1st Earl of Devon [England] on 22 February 1334/35.1 He held the office of Warden of Devon and Cornwall Coast in 1336.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir Hugh de Courtenay, 1st Earl of Devon and Agnes de St. John
2 CONT
