2 CONT ===From Medlands:===
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL1.htm#HughCourtenaydied1340B
2 CONT
2 CONT HUGH de Courtenay, son of Sir HUGH de Courtenay of Okehampton, Devon & his wife Eleanor le Despencer ([1275]-23 Dec 1340, bur Cowick, near Exeter).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Ford Abbey names “Hugonem secundum” as 
2 CONC son of “Hugonem de Courtnay primum” & his wife[472].  He succeeded his father in 1292 at Okehampton.  He was summoned to parliament 6 Feb 1299, whereby he is held to have become Lord Courtenay.  He was declared Earl of Devon 22 Fe
2 CONC b 1335.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1292) AGNES de St John, daughter of Sir JOHN de St John of Basing, Hampshire & his wife Alice FitzPiers ([1274/75]-Tiverton 11 Jun 1345, bur 27 Jun 1345 Cowick, near Exeter).  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Ford Abbe
2 CONC y records that ”dominus Hugo secundus” married “dominæ Agnetæ sorori sororii sui domini Johannis de St. John” when she was 17 years old[473].  The Fundationis et Fundatorum Historia of Ford Abbey records the death “1340 die Domini
2 CONC ca in festo sanctæ Trinitatis et sancti Barnabæ Apostoli…apud Tiverton” of “domina Agneta de Courtnay comitissa Devoniæ” and her burial “apud Cowick prope Exon.”[474].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh & his wife had six children
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ''''''Sir Hugh de Courtenay (12 July 1303 – 2 May 1377) was the 2nd Earl of Devon  in England , born probably in Devon. His parents were Hugh , the 1st Courtenay Earl of Devon  by Agnes de St John, daughter of Sir John St John of 
2 CONC Basing. He was destined to become a great soldier in the Hundred years war  in service of King Edward III. On 11 August 1327, still only 23 years old he was made knight banneret , and joined the elite group of knights who protecte
2 CONC d the King's body. He was made a founding and 12th Knight of the Order of the Garter  in 1348 on its investiture at Windsor Castle . Courtenay fought with the heroes of Crecy on 26 August 1346 in the famous of the encounters in Fr
2 CONC ance. The victory formed the basis for Courtenay's inclusion as a Knight of the Garter in 1348, by personal invitation of the King himself
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh, a badly behaved fellow, was self-important and puffed up. In 1335 The King granted his wish to be elevated to the earldom of Devon. No sooner he was bragging to the peasant folk in Devonshire that he was the only font of jus
2 CONC tice  Courtenay was summoned to Parliament on the assumption of Edward III to full authority over the usurper Roger Mortimer 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Manor of Powderham was mentioned in the Domesday Book. It came into the Courtenay family in the dowry of Margaret de Bohun on her marriage to Hugh de Courtenay, son of the first Courtenay Earl of Devon in 1325. The Courtenays 
2 CONC had come from France in the reign of Henry II and had acquired considerable lands and power in the South West by judicious marriages to wealthy heiresses. They had castles at Okehampton, Plympton and Colcolme near Colyton. Margare
2 CONC t bore her lord eight sons and nine daughters, and from this marriage descends all the subsequent Courtenay Earls of Devon. She outlived him for a number of years, and left Powderham to her sixth son, Philip, in her Will.
