2 CONT *'''Margaret de Courtenay1
2 CONT *'''F, #403395, d. 18 March 1349
2 CONT *Last Edited=20 Oct 2014
2 CONT *'''     Margaret de Courtenay was the daughter of''' Sir Hugh de Courtenay and Eleanor le Despencer.1 '''She married Nicholas de Moels, 2nd Baron Moels''', son of Sir John de Moels, 1st Baron Moels and Maud (?).1 '''She died on 1
2 CONC 8 March 1349.1
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S1916] Tim Boyle, "re: Boyle Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 16 September 2006. Hereinafter cited as "re: Boyle Family."
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p40340.htm#i403395 _________________
2 CONT *Sir Hugh de Courtenay (1251–1292) was the son and heir of John de Courtenay, feudal baron of Okehampton, Devon, by Isabel de Vere, daughter of Hugh de Vere, 4th Earl of Oxford. His son inherited the earldom of Devon.
2 CONT *Sir Hugh de Courtenay, born 25 March 1251,[1] was the son and heir of John de Courtenay of Okehampton, Devon, by Isabel de Vere, daughter of Hugh de Vere, 4th Earl of Oxford, and Hawise de Quincy.[2] John's father, Robert de Cour
2 CONC tenay (d. 26 July 1242),[3] son of Reginald de Courtenay (d.1190) by Hawise de Curci (d.1219), heiress of the feudal barony of Okehampton,[4] married Mary de Redvers (sometimes called 'de Vernon'), daughter of William de Redvers, 
2 CONC 5th Earl of Devon (d.1217).
2 CONT *In order to avoid military service Courtenay paid a fine on 12 December 1276. He was called to arms on the emergency against the Welsh princes, fighting in the 1282 campaign. He attended upon the King at Shrewsbury on 28 June 128
2 CONC 3. He again absented himself from the wars on 14 June 1287 by paying the King's justice a fine.[5]
2 CONT *Courtenay married Eleanor le Despenser, daughter of Hugh le Despencer, 1st Baron le Despencer, Justiciar of England, of Loughborough, Leicestershire and Ryhall, Rutland by Aline Basset, daughter of Sir Philip Basset, Justiciar of
2 CONC  England, of Wycombe, Buckinghamshire and Compton Bassett and Wootton Bassett, Wiltshire. They had three sons and four daughters:[6]
2 CONT **Hugh de Courtenay, 9th Earl of Devon.
2 CONT **John Courtenay
2 CONT **Philip Courtenay
2 CONT **Isabel de Courtenay, who married John de Saint John, 1st Lord St John of Basing (died 1329).
2 CONT **Aveline de Courtenay
2 CONT **Egeline (or Eleanor) de Courtenay
2 CONT **'''Margaret (or Margery) de Courtenay
2 CONT *Courtenay died at Colcombe, Devon, on 28 February 1292.[7] He was buried at Cowick Priory, near Exeter. The date of his death is correctly recorded in the Forde Abbey Chronicles. However the Fine Rolls decided upon his death on 5
2 CONC  March and issued a writ affirming deceased on 24 March.[citation needed] An inquisition post mortem was taken at London from 3 April to 27 May 1292. Records for his Buckinghamshire estates relate that his death was "after the Fea
2 CONC st of St Peter and to the Feast of the Exalted St Crucis".[citation needed]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_de_Courtenay _____________________
2 CONT *Hugh COURTENAY (Sir)
2 CONT *Born: 25 Mar 1249, Oakhampton, Devon, England
2 CONT *Died: 28 Feb 1290/91, Cullicomb, Devon, England
2 CONT *Buried: Cowick, Devonshire, England
2 CONT *Father: John COURTENAY (2° B. Okehampton)
2 CONT *Mother: Isabel De VERE
2 CONT *Married: Eleanor DESPENCER (BET 1245/1260-30 Sep 1328) BEF 1273
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Eleanor COURTENAY
2 CONT **2. Phillip COURTENAY
2 CONT **3. Thomas COURTENAY
2 CONT **4. Avelina (Ada)COURTENAY
2 CONT **5. John COURTENAY
2 CONT **6. Robert COURTENAY
2 CONT **7. Alice COURTENAY
2 CONT **8. Hugh COURTENAY (1° E. Devon)
2 CONT **'''9. Margaret COURTENAY
2 CONT **10. Isabel COURTENAY
2 CONT **11. Egeline COURTENAY
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/COURTENAY1.htm#Hugh COURTENAY (Sir)1 __________________
2 CONT *Sir Hugh de Courtenay
2 CONT *Birth: Mar. 25, 1251 Okehampton, Devon, England
2 CONT *Death: Feb. 28, 1292 Colyton, Devon, England
2 CONT *Baron of Okehampton, Earl of Devon. Knight of Buckinghamshire. Son of Sir John de Courtenay and Lady Isabel de Vere. Grandson of Sir Robert de Courtenay, Sheriff of Devon and Oxfordshire and Mary de Vernon, Hugh de Vere, 4th Earl
2 CONC  of Oxford and Hawise de Quincy.
2 CONT *Husband of Eleanor le Despenser, daughter of Sir Hugh le Despenser and Alivia Bassett. Father of three sons and four daughters:
2 CONT **Hugh de Courtenay, 9th Earl of Devon
2 CONT **John Courtenay
2 CONT **Philip Courtenay
2 CONT **Isabel de Courtenay, who married John de Saint John, 1st Lord St John of Basing
2 CONT **Aveline de Courtenay
2 CONT **Egeline de Courtenay
2 CONT **'''Margaret de Courtenay
2 CONT *Eleanor's maritagium included the manors of Wooton and Dunstredon.
2 CONT *Hugh paid fines to the king twice in order to avoid military service, December of 1276 and June of 1287, but was in the army of West Wales in 1282 and summoned to attend the King at Shrewsbury 28 June 1283.
2 CONT *The date of his death, 28th of February, has been recorded in the Forde Abbey records, but the Fine Rolls changed his death to March 5th. His Buckingham estate records state his death as "after the Feast of St Peter and to the Fe
2 CONC ast of the Exalted St Crucis."
2 CONT *Family links:
2 CONT *Parents:
2 CONT *John de Courtenay (1224 - 1274)
2 CONT *Isabel de Vere Courtenay (____ - 1299)
2 CONT *Spouse:
2 CONT *Eleanor le Despenser de Courtenay (____ - 1328)*
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **Hugh de Courtenay (1273 - 1340)*
2 CONT **Egelina De Courtenay Scales (1282 - 1335)*
2 CONT *Burial: Cowick Priory, Exeter, City of Exeter, Devon, England
