2 CONT NOTES:
2 CONT [1] This family claims ancestry from Walcelin de Douai from Normandy, a man born c. 1040; his name does not appear among those men known to have been at the Battle of Hastings.  Probably a retainer of the Mortimer family of Wigmor
2 CONC e, the manor of Brampton in Herefordshire was acquired c. 1200 by marriage to a Mortimer lady
2 CONT [2] By this marriage, the Harley family acquired the manor and castle of Brampton which they still held during the English Civil War c. 1644.  It is often called Brampton Bryan
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:
2 CONT [1] Visitation of Shropshire of 1623, pp 304 for the chart down to the marriages of the two Brampton ladies c. 1300.  That pedigree contains much anachronistic data which we have emended to follow a realistic timeline
2 CONT [2] ibid, pp 305 for the marriage of Robert Harley to the Corbet lady
2 CONT [3] ibid, pp 476 for the marriage of Julia Harley to Sir Philip Walcot
2 CONT
2 CONT
