2 CONT William de Beauchamp, for his zeal in the cause of the Empress Maud, was
2 CONT
2 CONT dispossessed of the castle of Worcester by King Stephen, to which, and
2 CONT
2 CONT all his other honors and estates, however, he was restored by King Henry
2 CONT
2 CONT II; and in that monarch's reign, besides the sheriffalty of
2 CONT
2 CONT Worcestershire, which he enjoyed by inheritance, he was sheriff of
2 CONT
2 CONT Warwickshire (2nd year of Henry II), sheriff of Gloucestershire (from the
2 CONT
2 CONT 3rd to the 9th year of Henry II, inclusive),and sheriff of Herefordshire
2 CONT
2 CONT (from the 8th to the 16th year of Henry II, inclusive). Upon the levy of
2 CONT
2 CONT the assessment towards the marriage portion of KingHenry's daughters,
2 CONT
2 CONT this powerful feudal lord certified his knight's fees tothe amount of
2 CONT
2 CONT fifteen. He married Maud Braose
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Beauchamp, who, for all his zeal in the cause of the Empress Maud, was dispossessed of the castle of Worcester by King Stephen, to which, and all his other honours and estates, however, he was restored by King Henry II;
2 CONC  and in that monarch's reign, besides the sheriffalty of Worcestershire, which he enjoyed by inheritance, he was sheriff of Warwickshire (2nd Henry II), sheriff of Gloucestershire (from 3rd to the 9th Henry II), sheriff of Herefor
2 CONC dshire (from the 8th to the 16th Henry II, 1167-70, inclusive). Upon the levy of the assessment towards the marriage portion of one of King Henry's daus., this powerful feudal lord certified his knight's fees to amount to fifteen.
2 CONC  He m. Maud, dau. of William Lord Braose, of Gower, and was s. at his decease by his son, William de Beauchamp. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 29, Beauchamp, Earls of Warw
2 CONC ick]
2 CONT
