1 NOTE William de Beauchamp, who, for all his zeal in the cause of the EmpressMaud, was dispossessed of the castle of Worcester by King Stephen, towhich, and all his other honours and estates, however, he was restored byKing Henry II; an
2 CONC d in that monarch's reign, besides the sheriffalty ofWorcestershire, which he enjoyed by inheritance, he was sheriff ofWarwickshire (2nd Henry II), sheriff of Gloucestershire (from 3rd to the9th Henry II), sheriff of Herefordshire
2 CONC  (from the 8th to the 16th HenryII, 1167-70, inclusive). Upon the levy of the assessment towards themarriage portion of one of King Henry's daus., this powerful feudal lordcertified his knight's fees to amount to fifteen. He m. Ma
2 CONC ud, dau. ofWilliam Lord Braose, of Gower, and was s. at his decease by his son,William de Beauchamp. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant and Extinct Peerages,Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 29, Beauchamp, Earls of Warwick]
