1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick (1102 – 12 June 1153), was the elder son of Henry de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Warwick and Marguerite, daughter of Geoffrey II of Perche and Beatrix of Montdidier. He was also kno
2 CONC wn as Roger de Newburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was generally considered to have been a devout and pious man; a chronicle of the period, the Gesta Regis Stephani, speaks of him as a "man of gentle disposition". The borough of Warwick remembers him as the founder of the Hospi
2 CONC tal of S. Michael for lepers which he endowed with the tithes of Wedgnock, and other property; he also endowed the House of the Templars beyond the bridge. In the reign of Stephen he founded a priory dedicated to S. Kenned at Llan
2 CONC gennilth, Co. Glamorgan and he attached it as a cell to the Abbey of S. Taurinus at Evreux in Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT He visited the Holy Land several times and took part in the Second Crusade, and he apparently joined the expedition under Conrad III of Germany after the Siege of Lisbon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and children
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 1130 Gundred de Warenne, daughter of William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey and Elizabeth de Vermandois and had children:
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Beaumont, 3rd Earl of Warwick. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry de Beaumont, was Dean of Salisbury in 1205. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes de Beaumont, married Geoffrey de Clinton, Chamberlain to the King and son of Geoffrey, the founder of Kenilworth Castle and Priory. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret de Beaumont. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gundred de Beaumont, married: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Bigod, 1st Earl of Norfolk; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger de Glanville. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward T. Beaumont, J.P. The Beaumonts in History. A.D. 850-1850. Oxford. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick (1102 – 12 June 1153), was the elder son of Henry de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Warwick and Marguerite, daughter of Geoffrey II of Perche and Beatrix of Montdidier. He was also known as Roger de N
2 CONC ewburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was generally considered to have been a devout and pious man; a chronicle of the period, the Gesta Regis Stephani, speaks of him as a "man of gentle disposition". The borough of Warwick remembers him as the founder of the Hospi
2 CONC tal of S. Michael for lepers which he endowed with the tithes of Wedgnock, and other property; he also endowed the House of the Templars beyond the bridge. In the reign of Stephen he founded a priory dedicated to S. Kenned at Llan
2 CONC gennilth, Co. Glamorgan and he attached it as a cell to the Abbey of S. Taurinus at Evreux in Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family and children
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 1130 Gundred de Warenne, daughter of William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey and Elizabeth de Vermandois and had children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. William de Beaumont, 3rd Earl of Warwick.
