2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p17626.htm#i176256 ___________________________
2 CONT *Thomas de Beauchamp, 11th Earl of Warwick, KG (c. 14 February 1313 – 13 November 1369) was an English nobleman and military commander during the Hundred Years' War. In 1348 he became one of the founders and the third Knight of th
2 CONC e Order of the Garter.
2 CONT *Thomas de Beauchamp was born at Warwick Castle, Warwickshire, England to Guy de Beauchamp, 10th Earl of Warwick and Alice de Toeni. He served in Scotland frequently .... etc.
2 CONT *He married Katherine Mortimer, daughter of Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March. They had five sons and ten daughters:[1]
2 CONT **Thomas b. 16 Mar 1338 d. 8 Aug 1401, who married Margaret Ferrers and had descendants. His son Richard succeeded him as Earl and inherited most of his property.
2 CONT **Guy (d. 28 April 1360). He had two daughters who by entail were excluded from their grandfather's inheritance: Elizabeth (d. c.1369), and Katherine, who became a nun.
2 CONT **Reinbrun, (d. 1361); he was named for a character in Guy of Warwick.
2 CONT **William (c. 1343–1411), who inherited the honour of Abergavenny. Married Joan FitzAlan.
2 CONT **Roger (d. 1361)
2 CONT **'''Maud (d. 1403), who married Roger de Clifford, 5th Baron de Clifford.
2 CONT **Philippa de Beauchamp who married Hugh de Stafford, 2nd Earl of Stafford.
2 CONT **Alice (d. 1383), who married first John Beauchamp, 3rd Baron Beauchamp and then Sir Matthew Gournay.
2 CONT **Joan, who married Ralph Basset, 4th Baron Basset de Drayton.
2 CONT **Isabell (d. 1416) who married first John le Strange, 5th Baron Strange, and then to William de Ufford, 2nd Earl of Suffolk. After the latter's death she became a nun.
2 CONT **Margaret, who married Guy de Montfort and after his death became a nun.
2 CONT **Elizabeth, married Thomas de Ufford, KG
2 CONT **Anne, married Walter de Cokesey
2 CONT **Juliana
2 CONT **Katherine, became a nun at Shouldham
2 CONT *Catherine Montacute, Countess of Salisbury was not his daughter, although she is presented as such in William Painter's Palace of Pleasure and in the Elizabethan play, Edward III that may be by William Shakespeare.
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_de_Beauchamp,_11th_Earl_of_Warwick ____________________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 11
2 CONT *'''Clifford, Roger de''' (1333-1389)     by Thomas Andrew Archer
2 CONT *'''CLIFFORD, ROGER de, ninth Lord Clifford, fifth Baron of Westmoreland''' (1333–1389), was born 10 July 1333 (Scr. and Gros. Roll, text, i. 197). His father (d. 20 May 1344) was Robert de Clifford, second son of Robert de Cliffo
2 CONC rd (1273–1314) [q. v.], the founder of the northern branch of this family; his mother (d. 25 July 1362) was Isabella, daughter of Maurice, lord Berkeley. He succeeded his elder brother, Robert, probably in or before 1352, and cert
2 CONC ainly before 10 Aug. 1354, on which day he made proof of his age (Dugdale, i. 240; Whitaker, pp. 310-11; Hist. Peerage, 117; Hist. of Westmoreland, i. 279; Escheat Rolls, ii. 118, 248).
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *'''Clifford married Maud or Matilda, daughter''' of Thomas de Beauchamp, earl of Warwick, '''who perhaps died in 1402-3 (cf. Escheat Rolls, iii. 286). By her he had two sons''', Thomas, his successor (d. 1391?) [q. v.]; and, as i
2 CONC s said, Sir William Clifford, the governor of Berwick (d. 1419), '''and three daughters''': Mary, who married Sir Philip Wentworth of Wentworth, Yorkshire; Margaret, who married Sir John Melton, knight; and Katherine, the wife of 
2 CONC Ralph, lord Greystock. Dugdale gives him a third son, the Lollard, Sir Lewis Clifford (d. 1404), whom, however, Sir H. Nicolas shows to have been '''probably his brother, but certainly not his son''' (Dugdale, i. 340-2; Whitaker, 
2 CONC 314-16; Nicolas, Scr. and Gros. Roll, ii. 427, &c.) The genealogical table in Whitaker gives Clifford two brothers, John de Clifford and Thomas de Clifford, said to have been the ancestor of Richard de Clifford, bishop of London [
2 CONC q. v.]; also three sisters.
2 CONT *[Whitaker's History of Craven (ed. 1877) contains copious extracts from the account of the Clifford family drawn up by Sir Matthew Hale in the seventeenth century; together with a genealogical table facing p. 311; Scrope and Gros
2 CONC venor Roll, ed. by Sir Harris Nicolas, of which vol. i. contains the text and vol. ii. lives of many of the witnesses, compiled by the editor; for other references see Clifford, Robert de.]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Clifford,_Roger_de_(1333-1389)_(DNB00) ___________________
2 CONT *'''Roger CLIFFORD (5º B. Clifford)
2 CONT *Born: 10 Jul 1333
2 CONT *Died: 13 Jul 1389, Brough Castle, Westmoreland, England
2 CONT *Notes: summoned to Parliament as a Baron between 1357-88. '''He married lady Maud De Beauchamp''' daughter of the Earl of Warwick. Roger was active against the Scots and was one of a commission to correct truce-breakers and decid
2 CONC e border disputes in 1367. In 1372 he was commissioner of Array against the Scots and in 1374 he was called on to settle a dispute over possession of Jedburgh Forest between Henry, lord Percy and William, Earl of Douglas. He was m
2 CONC ade Sheriff of Cumberland and Governor of Carlisle in 1377 and between 1380 and 1385 he was warden of the East and West Marches five times. In 1388 he accompanied Richard Fitzalan, Earl of Arundel, Admiral of the fleet on a victor
2 CONC ious naval expedition against France and Castile. He died on 13th Jul 1389 at home in one of his northern castles and was probably buried at Shap Abbey.
2 CONT *Father: Robert CLIFFORD (3º B. Clifford)
2 CONT *Mother: Isabel BERKELEY (B. Clifford)
2 CONT *'''Married: Maud BEAUCHAMP (d. 1402)''' (dau. of Thomas Beauchamp, E. Warwick and Catherine Mortimer) '''ABT 1356, Ravensworth, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT **'''Children:
