2 NOTE Warwick Castle.
3 CONT
3 CONT Maud de Clifford born here
3 CONT
3 CONT Owned by the de Beauchamps starting 1268
3 CONT
3 CONT The Warwick Castle Facts and Timeline 
3 CONT The History of the site of Warwick Castle covers hundreds of years and many different reigns. This comprehensive Warwick Castle Facts and Timeline has been developed to show the major  interesting events which occurred during the 
3 CONC various rule of each of the Kings and Queens of England and how these events effected the Earl of Warwick of the time. This comprehensive Warwick Castle Timeline of important and interesting facts and dates is simple to follow and
3 CONC  the dates and facts about each of the reigns and the names of the relevant Kings and Queens and the details of the Earls of Warwick are highlighted for ease of reference. All major building events are detailed in the Warwick Cast
3 CONC le timeline. Key events of the history of England have also been described within the Warwick Castle Facts and Timeline table.
3 CONT
3 CONT Warwick Castle Timeline
3 CONT
3 CONT Warwick Castle Timeline of Facts, Events & Dates
3 CONT
3 CONT
3 CONT Warwick Castle - Ancient Timeline of Facts, Dates and Events
3 CONT
3 CONT 79AD
3 CONT
3 CONT Roman fort was established at the site of Warwick by Agricola - the Romans left England in 410AD
3 CONT Dark Ages - 425
3 CONT
3 CONT The Saxons had moved into the Avon valley when the Romans left
3 CONT Dark Ages - 584AD
3 CONT
3 CONT The Anglo-Saxon kingdom of Mercia is founded in England
3 CONT Dark Ages - 793
3 CONT
3 CONT The Vikings invade Britain
3 CONT Dark Ages - 874
3 CONT
3 CONT Large parts of Mercia to the east of Warwickshire were ceded to Danish (Viking) invaders
3 CONT Dark Ages - 886
3 CONT
3 CONT The north-eastern edge of Warwickshire, became the boundary 
3 CONT between the Danelaw (the kingdom of the Danes) to the east and 
3 CONT Mercia to the west.
3 CONT Dark Ages - 914
3 CONT
3 CONT To protect Mercia from the Vikings Edward the Elder, the son of Alfred the Great and his sister Aethelflaed the 'Lady of the Mercians', and daughter of King Alfred the Great, build a Burh at Warwick
3 CONT Dark Ages - 1001
3 CONT
3 CONT The first recorded use of the name Warwickshire  named after 
3 CONT Warwick meaning 'dwellings by the weir'. A weir was a fence or wattle built across a stream to catch or retain fish
3 CONT 1065
3 CONT
3 CONT The Saxon Sheriff of Warwickshire, prior to the Norman Invasion,  was Thorkell of Arden in Warwick (also referred to as Turkil, Turchil, Thurkell, Turchill ) a Saxon Earl believed to be related to Lady Godiva and the King of Merci
3 CONC a. His emblem was the 'bear and ragged staff'. Thorkell is also referred to as the 'Traitor Earl'
3 CONT Warwick Castle Facts, Events, Dates and Timeline - 1000's
3 CONT
3 CONT 1066 - 1087	William the Conqueror
3 CONT 1066	The Normans invade England in September 1066 and commence their strategy of building castles. King Harold is killed at the Battle of Hastings
3 CONT 1067	William the Conqueror is crowned King of England in Westminster Abbey in London
3 CONT 1067-1069	Thorkell of Arden refused to support King Harold and therefore received the gratitude of William the Conqueror allowing him to retain his lordship and many landholdings in Warwickshire. Under orders from William he const
3 CONC ructed a ditch with an entrance gate around the town of Warwick
3 CONT 1067-1069	Under orders from William the Conqueror, Thorkell of Arden constructs a ditch, with a guarded entrance gate, around the town of Warwick
3 CONT 1068	The Normans start to build a timber tower in their Motte and Bailey Castles style at Warwick to subdue the Northern Saxon Lords. William the Conqueror uses enforced Anglo-Saxon labour - burh-hot becomes castle work
3 CONT 1068	William the Conqueror chooses a natural site for Warwick Castle overlooking a river
3 CONT 1068	The Warwick wooden Motte and Bailey Castle was defended by the River Avon on the South side and by wide and deep ditches on all other sides
3 CONT 1068	Henry de Beaumont, also known as Henry de Newburgh, (1045-1114) holds the Motte and Bailey castle for William the Conqueror
3 CONT 1086	The Doomsday Book is completed in England
3 CONT 1086	Thorkell of Arden, the Traitor Earl was one of only two Englishmen 'holding estates of baronial dimensions directly of the King' in the whole of England covered by Doomsday Book
3 CONT 9th September 1087	William the Conqueror dies at Saint-Gervais near Rouen, France
3 CONT 1087 - 1100	William Rufus (son of William)
3 CONT 1088	William Rufus creates the earldom of Warwick for Henry de Beaumont who changes his name to Henry de Newburgh, (1045-1119). He is appointed as Constable of Warwick Castle. William Rufus also gives him the great estate of Thork
3 CONC ell of Arden thereby betraying the Traitor Earl
3 CONT 1088	Not only was the title of the Earl of Warwick assumed by successive Earls, but also the Thorkell emblem of the 'bear and ragged staff'
3 CONT Warwick Castle Timeline of Facts, Events & Dates -1100's
3 CONT
3 CONT 1100 - 1135	Henry I (William Rufus' brother)
3 CONT 1119	Roger de Newburgh became 2nd Earl of Warwick (1102-1153)
3 CONT 1121	Henry I married Adelicia of Louvain, the "Fair Maid of Brabant."
3 CONT 1135 - 1154	Stephen (nephew of Henry I)
3 CONT 1153	William de Newburgh became 3rd Earl of Warwick (1153-1184)
3 CONT 1154 - 1189	Henry II (grandson of Henry I)
3 CONT 1184	Waleran de Newburgh became 4th Earl of Warwick (1184-1204)
3 CONT 1189 - 1199	Richard I (third son of Henry II)
3 CONT 1190	Richard the Lionheart embarks on the Third Crusade.
3 CONT 1193	Prince John plots against King Richard
3 CONT Warwick Castle Timeline of Facts, Events & Dates - 1200's
3 CONT
3 CONT 1199 - 1216	John (fifth son of Henry II)
3 CONT 1204	Henry de Newburgh, became 5th Earl of Warwick (1192-1229)
3 CONT June 15th 1215	Signing of the Magna Carta between King John and the Barons at Runnymede.
3 CONT October 1215	War breaks out again between King John and the Barons
3 CONT 1216 - 1272	Henry III (son of John)
3 CONT  	King Henry III was only ten years old when he was crowned but he immediately started on a strategy to reinforce all of the royal castles
3 CONT 1229	Thomas de Newburgh became 6th Earl of Warwick (1201-1242)
3 CONT 1242	Thomas de Newburgh left no heirs so his sister Margery Newburgh married John de Plessetis who assumed the 7th Earldom of Warwick in her right
3 CONT 1260	The wooden castle is replaced by a fortified stone castle. The two main round towers were built at the front of the castle and the Chapel and large hall were built against the south wall
3 CONT 1263	William Maudit married Alice Newburgh the 6th Earl of Warwick's daughter and assumed the 8th Earldom of Warwick in her right. He had no son and died in 1268
3 CONT 1263 - 1267	The Barons War. The Barons led by Simon de Montfort force the king to submit to government by council leading to the formation of the English Parliament. William Maudit sides with the King
3 CONT 1264	During the Barons War Simon de Montfort successfully attacks and lays siege to Warwick Castle
3 CONT 1268	William Beauchamp (1227-1298) followed his uncle and became 9th Earl of Warwick
3 CONT 1272 - 1307	Edward I (son of Henry III) also referred to as Longshanks
3 CONT 1272 - 1307	King Edward I continued the castle building initiated by his father Henry III and, with his chief architect and builder Master James of St George,  built massive Concentric Castles in England, Scotland and Wales.
