2 CONT England enters the period of civil disorder and political instability known as the Wars of the Roses. Richard Neville was a Yorkist, and when he helped Edward IV the son of the Duke of York, to become king he became very powerful
2 CONT 1460 1460 - The Battle of Wakefield when Richard of York is defeated and killed. Richard Neville, the Earl of Warwick (later called the Kingmaker) captures London for the Yorkists
2 CONT 1461 The battles of Mortimer's Cross and Towton when Richard's son, Edward of York, defeats the Lancastrians and becomes King Edward IV is aided by Richard Neville, Earl of Warwick
2 CONT 1466 Warwick's quarrels with Edward IV begin over the King's marriage to Elizabeth Woodville, and Warwwick forms alliance with King Louis XI of France
2 CONT 1465 - 1470 The mentally unstable and pious Lancastrian King Henry VI and his headstrong and ambitious wife Margaret of Anjou were imprisoned in Warwick Castle
2 CONT 12th March 1470 Warwick is declared a traitor by Edward IV and forced to flee to France
2 CONT 6th October 1470 Warwick returns from France and turns Lancastrian when he defeats Edward IV and restores Henry VI to power
2 CONT 14th April 1471 The Battle of Barnet when Edward IV defeats and kills Warwick the Kingmaker
2 CONT 22nd May 1471 Henry VI, the last Lancastrian king, was murdered in the Tower of London whilst he was at prayer, he was probably murdered on the orders of Edward IV
2 CONT 1471 George (b.1449 - d.1478), Duke of Clarence becomes the 17th Earl of Warwick and marries Isabel Neville - he is executed on 18th February 1478 for treason
2 CONT 1461 - 1483 Edward IV ( youngest son of Edward III )
2 CONT 1478 Edward Plantagenet (b.1474 - d.1499), 18th Earl of Warwick was the son of the Duke of Clarence and named Plantagenet due to his close relationship with the Royal family and, like his father, he is also executed for treason in
2 CONC  1499
2 CONT 1478 Warwick castle became the property of the future Richard III, who had married Anne Neville, the youngest daughter of Neville
2 CONT 1461 - 1483 Edward IV was a notorious womaniser - his affairs led to claims of illegitimacy and ultimately led to the murder of his sons
2 CONT 1483 Edward V (eldest son of King Edward IV and Elizabeth Woodville)
2 CONT 1483 The boy king was on his way to his coronation in London but was intercepted by his uncle, and Protector ( who would become Richard III ). Edward was escorted to London and then to the Tower. On the 16th June 1483  he was join
2 CONC ed by his brother Prince Richard. The coronation was cancelled.
2 CONT 1483 The thirteen year old King and his ten year old brother mysteriously disappeared in the Tower after being declared illegitimate. They were believed to have been murdered and are referred to as the Little Princes in the Tower
2 CONT 1483 - 1485 Richard III (uncle of Edward V)
2 CONT June 25 1483 Parliament declared the two little princes illegitimate and, as next in line to the throne, their uncle and Protector, Richard, Duke of Gloucester, was declared the true King. The two little princes were never seen ag
2 CONC ain
2 CONT 1483 Richard III began building the Bear and Clarence towers at Warwick Castle but they were incomplete due to his death
2 CONT August 22nd 1485 A Lancastrian rebellion rose against the Yorkist Richard and on  he fell in the Battle of Bosworth Field to Henry Tudor. Richard lost both the throne and Warwick Castle when he was killed at the Battle of Bosworth
2 CONC  in 1485. Henry Tudor becomes the owner of Warwick Castle and keeps the castle to himself
2 CONT 1485 - 1509 Henry VII (grandson of Henry V)
2 CONT 1485-1509 Henry VII cemented his succession and settled the friction between the Yorkists and Lancastrians by marrying the Yorkist heir, Elizabeth of York
2 CONT 1499 Edward Plantagenet (b.1474 - d.1499), 18th Earl of Warwick was the son of the Duke of Clarence and a claimant of the throne of England - his title was attained due to his 'treason' for which he was executed. In 1547 this led 
2 CONC to the Second Creation of the Earls of Warwick
2 CONT Warwick Castle Timeline of Facts, Events & Dates - 1500's
2 CONT
2 CONT 1509 - 1547 Henry VIII (Henry VII's second son, Arthur married Katherine of Aragon but died at the age of 15)
2 CONT 1509 King Henry VIII also keeps Warwick Castle to himself ensuring the power and influence of the nobility is contained. Improvements include a new roof for the kitchens, reinforcement of the south front, the building of Spy Tower
2 CONC  and an extension to the State Rooms
2 CONT 1540 Improvements are completed which include a new roof for the kitchens, reinforcement of the south front, the building of Spy Tower and an extension to the State Rooms
2 CONT 28th January 1547 Henry VIII died at Whitehall Palace and was buried at St George's Chapel, Windsor
2 CONT 1547 - 1553 Edward V (Henry's son by Jane Seymour)
2 CONT 1547
2 CONT The Second Creation of the
2 CONT Earls of Warwick The young King restores the title and John Dudley, his chief minister, was created the 1st Earl of Warwick. On Dudley's death the dukedom was forfeit for his treason, but the earldom passed to his son John, and th
2 CONC en his other son Ambrose
2 CONT 1551 John Dudley is given the title Duke of Northumberland which makes him the most powerful noble in England. The young King Edward is sickly and Dudley realises that if the King dies, and either of his sisters take the crown, th
2 CONC at Dudley will lose his power and probably his head 
2 CONT 1552 John Dudley plots with the Earl of Suffolk and arranges the marriage of his son, Guildford Dudley, to the Suffolk's daughter, Lady Jane Grey
2 CONT May 25, 1553 Guildford Dudley and Lady Jane Grey are forced into marriage
2 CONT 1553 Edward died of tuberculosis and he left the throne to 'the Lady Jane and her heirs male.' Lady Jane Grey was John Dudley's daughter- in-law
2 CONT Monday 10 July - 1553 Lady Jane Grey (Queen for just Nine Days)
2 CONT Monday 10th July 1553 Lady Jane Grey was proclaimed Queen of England
2 CONT 19th July 1553 Queen Jane was deposed as Queen
2 CONT 23rd August, 1553 John Dudley, Duke of Northumberland, was beheaded for treason
2 CONT 1553 - 1558 Mary (Henry's daughter by Queen Katherine of Aragon)
2 CONT 1553 - 1558 The eldest daughter of Henry VIII is referred to as 'Bloody Mary' for her persecution of Protestants and political rivals
2 CONT February 12, 1554 Lady Jane Grey and her husband Guildford were both executed
2 CONT 1553 John Dudley (b.c.1528 - d.1554) took the title 2nd Earl of Warwick but died shortly after due to ill health no doubt caused by his nine month imprisonment following his implication in the Lady Jane Grey situation
2 CONT 1554 Ambrose Dudley (d.1590), 3rd Earl of Warwick.
2 CONT 1557 The fortunes of the Dudley's rose again when Ambrose Dudley Dudley used his influence to raise an army for King Philip II of Spain against France. In gratitude for his help all of the Dudley estates and Warwick Castle were re
2 CONC turned
2 CONT 1558 - 1603 Elizabeth I (Henry's daughter by Anne Boleyn)
2 CONT 1563 The Black Death ( Bubonic Plague ) broke out in London
2 CONT 1564 Ambrose Dudley was created Baron Lisle. His brother Robert Dudley was a great favourite of Queen Elizabeth I and was created Earl of Leicester.
2 CONT 1572 Elizabeth I visits Warwick Castle
2 CONT 1590 Ambrose Dudley dies and the earldom of Warwick became extinct until 1618
2 CONT Warwick Castle Timeline of Dates, Events & Facts - 1600's
2 CONT
2 CONT 1603 - 1625 James I of England James VI of Scotland (great-great-grandson of Henry VII)
2 CONT 1604 James I presented Warwick Castle to Sir Fulke Greville who was a poet and contemporary of Sir Philip Sidney. The castle had stood empty for 14 years and was in a dilapidated state. Sir Fulke Greville spent considerable money 
2 CONC on improvements and repairs
2 CONT 1618 The Earldom of Warwick was created for a third time for Robert Rich (d. 1619), 3rd Baron Rich and referred to as the 1st Earl of Warwick . However, unlike previous holders of the title, the Rich family did not control Warwick
2 CONC  Castle
2 CONT Warwick Castle remained in the hands of the Greville family who were eventually given the title in 1759
2 CONT 1619 Robert Rich created 2nd Earl of Warwick (1587-1658) but did not control Warwick Castle
2 CONT 1625 - 1649 Charles I (second son of James)
2 CONT 1628 Sir Fulke Greville was murdered by his steward in 1628 in Warwick Castle - he was stabbed to death and is said to haunt the castle
2 CONT 1628 Sir Robert Greville (c. 1608-1643), am ardent Puritan, became 2nd Lord Brooke and owner of Warwick Castle
2 CONT 1639 Sir Robert Greville was imprisoned by King Charles I at York for refusing to take the oath to fight for the king and became an active member of the parliamentary party
2 CONT 1642 Civil war broke out between King and parliament.
2 CONT 1642 Warwick Castle successfully withstands a siege by Royalist troops
2 CONT 1643 Sir Robert Greville, a general in the parliamentary army was killed at the attack on Lichfield
2 CONT 30th January 1649 Charles I was beheaded on a scaffold outside the Banqueting House in Whitehall, London
2 CONT 1649 - 1659 The Commonwealth under the Cromwells
2 CONT 7 February 1649 The office of King was formally abolished
2 CONT 1658 Robert Rich became the 3rd Earl of Warwick (1611-1659)
2 CONT 1659 Charles Rich became the 4th Earl of Warwick (1619-1673)
2 CONT
2 CONT timeline lifted from
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.castles.me.uk/warwick-castle-facts-timeline.htm
