3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://newsgroups.derkeiler.com/Archive/Soc/soc.genealogy.medieval/2007-05/msg00267.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides the identification of Alice le Boteler's parentage, we are given two additional children of this couple, namely Margaret and Eleanor, both of whom were ignored by Complete Peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Curiously, Complete Peerage reveals in a footnote that "it has been stated that she [Alice] was da. of Sir John Beauchamp of Powick." [Reference: C.P. 12 (1) (1953): 418, footnote k]. However, no sources whatsoever are given for t
2 CONC his statement. So one is left to wonder who and whenever it was stated that Alice, wife of Thomas le Boteler, was a Beauchamp. The author then proceeds to discredit the Beauchamp connection by referring to a mangled record in the 
2 CONC Patent Rolls in which Sir Ralph Boteler, son of Thomas and Alice le Boteler, is called "uncle" of Elizabeth Beauchamp, daughter of Sir William Beauchamp, of Powick. Complete Peerage states: "These facts cannot be made to tally wit
2 CONC h the account of Beauchamp of Powick given by Dugdale, Baronage, vol. i, p. 249."
2 CONT
2 CONT That the published Patent Rolls item cited by Complete Peerage contains a manifest error was caught in recent time by Chris Phillips, of England, who took it upon his himself to examine the original record. His review of the origi
2 CONC nal shows that Sir Ralph Boteler [son of Thomas and Alice] was actually called "kinsman" [consanguinei] to Elizabeth Beauchamp of Powick in the original record, and that it was Walter Beauchamp who was called her "uncle" [Auunculi
2 CONC ]. Walter Beauchamp was in fact Elizabeth Beauchamp's uncle and Ralph Boteler was in fact her near kinsman.
2 CONT
2 CONT As for evidence that Alice, wife of Thomas le Boteler, was a Beauchamp of Powick, her identity and parentage are indicated by no less than three published visitation records:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hawley et al., Vis. of Essex 1552, 1558, 1570, 1612 & 1634 2 (H.S.P. 14) (1879): 563-565 (Misc. Peds.) (Cooke pedigree: "Sr Thomas Butler. = Alice d. of John Beauchamp of Powick.").
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Harvey et al., Vis. of Bedfordshire 1566, 1582, 1634 & 1669 (H.S.P. 19) (1884): 162-163 (Bray pedigree: "Thomas Butler Baron of Sudley = Allice d. and heire of John Beauchampe of Powick.").
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Benolte et al., Vis. of Surrey 1530, 1572 & 1613 (H.S.P. 43) (1899): 219-221 (Vincent pedigree: "Sir Thom. Botteler Knt. =3D Allice
2 CONT
2 CONT d. of Sir John Beauchamp of Powick Knt.").
2 CONT
2 CONT Amazingly, all of these records were in print at the time Complete Peerage published its account of the Boteler family, and all were simply ignored or suppressed.
2 CONT
2 CONT As for direct evidence of Alice's maiden name, I've elsewhere learned that a contemporary seal of Alice le Boteler, Lady Sudeley, has survived, which seal displays the arms of Boteler of Sudeley impaling Alice's own arms, Beaucham
2 CONC p of Powyk. The seal is described below:
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference: Source: Report on the MSS of Lord de L'isle & Dudley 1 (Hist. MSS Comm. 77) (1925): 149:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"1410, February 5. - Indenture whereby John Lonseforde, abbot of Robertsbridge, demises at farm to Alice, widow of Sir John Dalyngregge, part of a meadow called Frerenmede in Iwehurst. Seal: round, 1 1/4 inch, impaling, Boteler o
2 CONC f Sudley, quarterly two bendlets and a fess chequy between six crosses patee fitchee, and Beauchamp of
2 CONT Powyk, a fess between six martlets. Between the shield and border three lizards or crocodiles. SIGILLVM DNE. ALICIE DE BOTELER DNE. DE SUYDLE. (See Archaeologia lxv. Plate xxxiii., 10)." END OF QUOTE.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is regrettable indeed that Complete Peerage gave the account of the Boteler family in Coll. Top. et Gen. such short shift. Had more attention been paid to its statements, the author of Complete Peerage would surely have found t
2 CONC hat its identification of the various Boteler wives was in fact correct.
