2 CONT '''+      2                 i.    William2 Clopton, Gent., of Clopton Hall, Suffolk,''' born in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly, 10 miles southwest of Bury St. Edmunds; died in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, Coun
2 CONC ty Suffolk, possibly.  All Saints Church at Wickhambrook dates from about 1240, too late to be the burial place for William and Anne, although there was probably an earlier church.  A small Saxon figure is on the south wall.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation No. 2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2.  William2 Clopton, Gent., of Clopton Hall, Suffolk''' (Guillaume1 Peche, Lord Of Cloptunna and Dalham)4 was born in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly, 10 miles southwest of Bury St. Edmunds, and died in Cl
2 CONC opton Hall, Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly.  All Saints Church at Wickhambrook dates from about 1240, too late to be the burial place for William and Anne, although there was probably an earlier church.  A small Saxon figu
2 CONC re is on the south wall.  He married Anne Grey, of Buckingham Castle, County Norfolk5, daughter of John Grey, of Buckingham Castle, County Norfolk.  She died in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of William Clopton and Anne Grey is:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''+      3                 i.    Walter3 Clopton, Knt., of Clopton Hall,''' born in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly, 10 miles southwest of Bury St. Edmunds; died Aft. 1154 in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, Count
2 CONC y Suffolk, possibly.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation No. 3
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' 3.  Walter3 Clopton, Knt., of Clopton Hall''' (William2, Guillaume1 Peche, Lord Of Cloptunna and Dalham)6 was born in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly, 10 miles southwest of Bury St. Edmunds, and died Aft. 
2 CONC 1154 in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly7.  He married Chewyt8. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Walter Clopton and Chewyt is:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''+      4                 i.    William4 Clopton, Sir William of Clopton Hall''', born in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly, 10 miles southwest of Bury St. Edmunds; died Aft. 1216 in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook
2 CONC , County Suffolk, possibly.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation No. 4
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4.  William4 Clopton, Sir William of Clopton Hall''' (Walter3, William2, Guillaume1 Peche, Lord Of Cloptunna and Dalham)9 was born in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly, 10 miles southwest of Bury St. Edmunds,
2 CONC  and died Aft. 1216 in Clopton Hall, Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly10.  '
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Cockerell11, daughter of William Cockerell, Sir William. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of William Clopton and Cockerell are:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''+      5                 i.    Walter5 Clopton, of Wickhambrook, born in Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly, 10 miles southwest of Bury St. Edmunds; died in Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly'''
2 CONT .
2 CONT  6                ii.    Richard Clopton, of Wickhambrook.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation No. 5
2 CONT
2 CONT '''5.  Walter5 Clopton, of Wickhambrook''' (William4, Walter3, William2, Guillaume1 Peche, Lord Of Cloptunna and Dalham)12 was born in Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly, 10 miles southwest of Bury St. Edmunds, and died in Wic
2 CONC khambrook, County Suffolk, possibly.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Frances Trussell13, daughter of William Trussell, Sir William. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Walter Clopton and Frances Trussell is:
2 CONT
2 CONT +      7                 i.    William6 Clopton, Lord of Chiperley Manor, born Aft. 1216 in Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly, 10 miles southwest of Bury St. Edmunds; died 1294 in Poslingford, County Suffolk, possibly, and be
2 CONC lieved to be buried at the Church of the Blessed Mary, near Chipley Priory about 3 miles northeast of Clare, County, Suffolk
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation No. 6
2 CONT
2 CONT '''7.  William6 Clopton, Lord of Chiperley Manor''' (Walter5, William4, Walter3, William2, Guillaume1 Peche, Lord Of Cloptunna and Dalham)14 was born Aft. 1216 in Wickhambrook, County Suffolk, possibly, 10 miles southwest of Bury 
2 CONC St. Edmunds15, and died 1294 in Poslingford, County Suffolk, possibly, and believed to be buried at the Church of the Blessed Mary, near Chipley Priory about 3 miles northeast of Clare, County, Suffolk16.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of William Clopton, Lord of Chiperley Manor are:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''+      8                 i.    Walter7 Clopton, Lord of Chiperley Manor''', died 1327 in Poslingford, County Suffolk, possibly, and believed to be buried at The Church of the Blessed Mary, near Chipley Priory.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9                ii.    Adam Clopton, of Chiperley Manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10             iii.    Clement Clopton, of Chiperley Manor17, died Aft. 132318.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1323, Clement sold some land in the village of Cowlinge, about 3 miles west of Wickhambrook, to John de Shardelowe.  "Let all men know that I, Clement son of William Cloptone have given &c., to John de Shardelowe one rood of la
2 CONC nd in the town of Coulynge &c. Given at Coulynge on the Sunday after Trinity in the 16th. Year of King Edward, son of King Edward."
2 CONT
2 CONT The church at Cowlinge, St. Margaret, dates from the 12th century, as evidenced by incorporated fragments in the walls, but the main church is early 14th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT 11             iv.    William Clopton, of Chiperley Manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT 12              v.    Hugo Clopton, of Chiperley Manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT 13             vi.    Robert Clopton, of Chiperley Manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation No. 7
2 CONT
2 CONT '''8.  Walter7 Clopton, Lord of Chiperley Manor''' (William6, Walter5, William4, Walter3, William2, Guillaume1 Peche, Lord Of Cloptunna and Dalham)19 died 1327 in Poslingford, County Suffolk, possibly, and believed to be buried at
2 CONC  The Church of the Blessed Mary, near Chipley Priory20. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married (1) Alice FitzHugh21, daughter of Warin FitzHugh.  She died Aft. 1289 in Poslingford, County Suffolk, possibly, and believed to be buried at The Church of the Blessed Mary, near Chipley Priory22. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married (2) Anwett or Ivetta Weyland23.  She died Aft. 1338 in Poslingford, County Suffolk, possibly, and believed to be buried at Chipley Priory, Clare24.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Walter Clopton and Alice FitzHugh are:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''* +      14               i.    William8 Clopton, Knt., Lord of Toppesfield Manor, died Bet. January 22, 1375/76 and January 14, 1376/77 in England and buried in Babwell Friary'''
2 CONT .
2 CONT
2 CONT +      15              ii.    Thomas Clopton, Knt, of Kentwell Estate, Suffolk, died Bet. March 8, 1381/82 and October 12, 1383 in Long Melford, County Suffolk, and buried at the Church of the Blessed Mary, near Chipley Priory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation No. 8
2 CONT
2 CONT '''14.  William8 Clopton, Knt., Lord of Toppesfield Manor''' (Walter7, William6, Walter5, William4, Walter3, William2, Guillaume1 Peche, Lord Of Cloptunna and Dalham)25 died Bet. January 22, 1375/76 and January 14, 1376/77 in Engl
2 CONC and and buried in Babwell Friary26.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He married (1) Amitia or Ivetta Grey, of Buckenham Castle27, daughter of Thomas Grey, Knt., of Buckenham Castle, Norfolk.  She was born in England.   Buckenham, County Norfolk, is about 8 miles southeast of Norwich.  It is reporte
2 CONC d that her daughter Johane, was born at Paston, Norfolk, about 22 miles north of Norwich.  None of this has been confirmed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married (2) Mary Cockerell, of Toppesfield Manor, Hadleigh28, daughter of William Cockerell, Knt, of Toppesfield Manor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of William Clopton and Amitia Grey are:
2 CONT
2 CONT +      17               i.    William9 Clopton, of Hawstead Manor, died in Hawstead Manor, County Suffolk, possibly, about 4 miles southwest of Bury St. Edmunds and may be buried at All Saints, Hawstead.
2 CONT
2 CONT +      18              ii.    Walter Clopton, of Toppesfield Manor, Hadleigh, died Aft. May 5, 1413 in Toppesfield Manor, probably, and possibly  buried at Holy Trinity Church, Long Melford, County Suffolk.
2 CONT
2 CONT +      19             iii.    Edward Clopton, of Newnham Hall, Ashdon, Essex, died 1389 in Newenham Hall, Ashdon, County Essex.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''* +      20             iv.    Johane Clopton, of Toppesfield Manor, Hadleigh, born in Paston, County Norfolk, possibly, about 22 miles north of Norwich.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT 21              v.    Joan Clopton, of Wickhambrook & Norwich29, died 1404 in England and buried on the north side of the presbytery at Norwich Cathedral, County Norfolk30.  She married Thomas Erpingham, K.G., Lord Marshall of Eng
2 CONC land31 Aft. 138032; born 1357; died 1428 in England and buried on the north side of the presbytery at Norwich Cathedral, County Norfolk33.
2 CONT
2 CONT On a cold and wet morning in 1415, five thousands archers stood waiting at Agincourt.  Henry Bolingbroke, later crowned King Henry IV, called to him the husband of Joan Clopton, the distinguished marshall of his army, Sir Thomas E
2 CONC rpingham.  Sir Thomas brought the men into position.  The elderly knight tossed his baton into the air and cried 'Nestrocque," his command answered with roars which echoed across the gentle countryside soon to be drenched in blood
2 CONC .  And thus began one of the most famous battles in history, the Battle of Agincourt.  One of the highest-ranking officers at Agincourt, Sir Thomas was immortalized by William Shakespeare in his play Henry V.  See "In The Name of 
2 CONC Jesus, Mary and St. George"
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of William Clopton and Mary Cockerell is:
2 CONT
2 CONT  22               i.    Thomas9 Clopton, of Toppesfield Manor34.
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation No. 9
2 CONT
2 CONT '''20.  Johane9 Clopton, of Toppesfield Manor, Hadleigh'''  (William8, Walter7, William6, Walter5, William4, Walter3, William2, Guillaume1 Peche, Lord Of Cloptunna and Dalham)52 was born in Paston, County Norfolk, possibly, about 
2 CONC 22 miles north of Norwich.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She married (1) Walter Walcote, of County Norfolk.   
2 CONT
2 CONT She married (2) Roger Beauchamp, 2nd Baron Beauchamp of Bletsho5354, son of Roger Beauchamp.  He was born Abt. 1363 in Bletsoe, County Bedfordshire, about 24 miles west of Cambridge, County Cambridgeshire55, and died May 3, 140656
2 CONC .  
2 CONT
2 CONT She married (3) John Cavendish, Knt.57. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Johane married into one of the most eminent and powerful families, the Beauchamps.   A companion in arms of the victorious William the Conqueror, the family was represented by the Earls of Warwick and Albemarle, and, the Barons of
2 CONC  St. Amand, Barons of Bletsho, Hache, Kydderminster and Powyke.  Counted among her direct descendants are most of the Kings and Queens of England who have held the throne since Henry, VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Johane Clopton and Roger Beauchamp are:
2 CONT
2 CONT +      29               i.    John10 Beauchamp, 3rd Baron Beauchamp of Bletsho.
2 CONT
2 CONT +      30              ii.    Margaret Beauchamp.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~clopton/peche.htm The Descendants of Guillaume Peche, Lord of Cloptunna and Dalham]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===========================
2 CONT
2 CONT Information from: The Families of LOU DRAPER & CHARLIE MARTIN of Johnson and Henry Counties, Missouri" by James M McMillen, Arlington, Texas
2 CONT
2 CONT Published in .pdf format 13 January 2010
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan de Cloptone (1), married Sir Thomas Erpingham, KG, who was named in Shakespeare's Henry V; no issue.
2 CONT *_________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p11865.htm#i118650
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan Clopton is the daughter of Sir Walter Clopton.1 She married, firstly, Sir Walter Walcot before 1366.1 She married, secondly, Roger Beauchamp, son of Roger Beauchamp, 1st Lord Beauchamp (of Bletsoe), after 1366.1
2 CONT
2 CONT From before 1366, her married name became Walcot.1 From after 1366, her married name became Beauchamp.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S2] Peter W. Hammond, editor, The Complete Peerage or a History of the House of Lords and All its Members From the Earliest Times, Volume XIV: Addenda & Corrigenda (Stroud, Gloucestershire, U.K.: Sutton Publishing, 1998), page 75
2 CONC . Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage, Volume XIV.
2 CONT *___________________
2 CONT *IS THIS REFERENCE CONFUSING TWO JOAN'S? OTHER REFERENCES LIST THE WIFE OF JOHN CAVENDISH AS JOAN DAU. OF WILLIAM CLOPTON. SEE http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/CAVENDISH.htm#John CAVENDISH1 & http://www.thepeerage.com/p24924.htm#i2492
2 CONC 35
