1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT Isolda (or Iseult) de Mountjoy (c. 1307 – after 1352) was a noblewoman of the 14th century and the daughter and heiress of Sir Thomas de Mountjoy. Her marriage to Sir John le Blount of Sodington, Worcestershire, was a significant 
2 CONC historical alliance that secured the transmission of the Mountjoy estates—specifically the manors of Gayton, Yeldersley, and Brushfield in Derbyshire—into the le Blount family.
2 CONT Historically, her status is confirmed through land charters and legal administrative rolls. Her role as an heiress is further memorialized by the subsequent heraldic practice of her descendants, who quartered the Mountjoy coat of 
2 CONC arms (gules, three escutcheons, or) with the le Blount arms. Modern genealogical consensus recognizes her as the wife of Sir John le Blount, correcting historical conflations that previously attributed a second wife to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Sir Thomas de Mountjoy, Kt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Margaret (Documented in secondary sources; primary evidence remains scarce)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Born: c. 1307 (Estimated; specific location unrecorded)
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: After 1352 (Date of final administrative record; specific location unrecorded)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Spouse and Issue===
2 CONT Spouse: Sir John le Blount, Kt. (c. 1298–1358)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT 1. Richard le Blount (Heir to the Sodington estates)
2 CONT 2. Sir John le Blount (d. 1425)
2 CONT 3. Sir Walter le Blount (d. 1403)
2 CONT 4. Thomas le Blount
2 CONT 5. Alice le Blount (c. 1349–1414/15)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Factual Information and Legal Relevance===
2 CONT Inheritance: Through Isolda, the le Blount family acquired substantial manors in Derbyshire. The formal transmission of these lands is the definitive legal evidence confirming her identity and lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Status of Marriage: Primary records, such as the Calendar of Close Rolls, confirm Isolda as the wife of Sir John le Blount. Academic research has clarified that she was his primary (and likely only) wife, invalidating earlier clai
2 CONC ms of a marriage to Eleanor Beauchamp.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heraldic Record: The adoption and quartering of the Mountjoy arms by the le Blount family acts as a formal, verifiable acknowledgement of the marriage alliance and her status as a noble heiress.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Verifiable Sources and Records===
2 CONT The Complete Peerage (Cokayne): Standard academic reference verifying the marriage and the le Blount lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Derbyshire Charters: Primary records of land transactions that document the transfer of the Mountjoy manors (Gayton, Yeldersley, Brushfield) to the le Blount family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Calendar of Close Rolls (Edward III): Official administrative documents confirming her status as Sir John’s wife; the final mention of her presence occurs in 1352.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquisitions Post Mortem: Legal records for the estate of Sir John le Blount (1358) that identify his children and connect them to the Mountjoy inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Updated: 07/17/2026
2 CONT
2 CONT —-
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondary references and notes:
