2 CONT *BLOUNT, John II (aft.1345-1425), of Sodington, Worcs.
2 CONT *b. aft. 1345, 2nd s. and event. h. of '''Sir John Blount (d.1358) of Sodington, prob. by his 1st w. Iseult, da. and h. of Thomas Mountjoy''' of Derbys.; er bro. of Sir Walter*. m. (1) Juliana (?Foulhurst), 2s. d.v.p.; (2) c. Apr.
2 CONC  1383, Isabel, da. of Sir Brian Cornwall† of Kinlet, Salop by Maud, da. of Fulk, 1st Lord Strange of Blackmere, sis. of Sir John Cornwall*, 1s.; ?(3) Ellen. Kntd. bet. Sept. 1403.
2 CONT *John was one of the sons of '''Sir John Blount, himself a younger son''' of Sir Walter Blount† of Rock, Worcestershire, and Joan, the heiress of the manor of Sodington. .... etc. .... '''his father had fought in Gascony under Hen
2 CONC ry, earl of Lancaster, who had granted him for life the manors of Passenham (Northamptonshire) and Tibberton (Gloucestershire).'''
2 CONT *'''When Blount’s father died in 1358 the heir was his eldest son''' Richard, but the latter did not long survive, soon leaving the inheritance to young John. By settlements made in 1356 John had already obtained lands in Balterle
2 CONC y, Biddulph, Fenton and Ramshorn, Staffordshire, from his aunt Margery (widow of William, Lord Blount and at that time the wife of Sir John Crophull of Nottinghamshire), and in June 1358 the King, respiting his homage for these la
2 CONC nds until he should come of age, granted him seisin. The rest of his substantial paternal inheritance (which, besides Sodington, included the manors of Timberlake and Mamble and several other properties in Worcestershire) came to 
2 CONC him only after he attained his majority. In 1364 he was evidently a ward of Nicholas Fitzherbert, for in that year Eleanor, countess of Arundel (daughter of Earl Henry of Lancaster) acknowledged receipt of 11 marks from Fitzherber
2 CONC t as ‘the guardian of the lands of my dear cousin Janckin Blount’. Ten years later Blount, no longer a minor, reached an agreement with his brother Walter that the latter should have all the '''Mountjoy lands in Derbyshire falling
2 CONC  to them on the death of their mother''', he himself to have other family properties in Staffordshire and Worcestershire in lieu; and accordingly, in 1381, Walter relinquished to him all claim to lands in Denstone, Elvaston, Quixh
2 CONC ill and Waterfall.2
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1386-1421/member/blount-john-ii-1345-1425
2 CONT *___________________
2 CONT *BLOUNT, Sir Walter (d.1403), of Barton Blount, Derbys.
2 CONT *3rd s. of '''Sir John Blount (d.1358) of Sodington, Worcs. by his 1st w. Iseult, da. and h. of Thomas Mountjoy''' of Derbys.; yr. bro. of John Blount II*. m. by 1374, Sancha (d.1418/19), da. of Diego Gomez of Toledo, principal se
2 CONC c. of the province of Toledo, by his w. Ines de Ayala, 5s. inc. Thomas II* (1s. d.v.p.), 2da. Kntd. by Mar. 1372.1
2 CONT * .... He must have still been very young when '''his father died in 1358''', since his eldest brother, Richard, was then only 13 years old. .... etc. Richard Blount, too, was a soldier; and Sir Walter agreed to act as his attorne
2 CONC y while he campaigned in Aquitaine with the Black Prince. He was evidently killed in action, for by 1374 John, '''the second of Sir John Blount’s three sons''', had succeeded to the family estates. It was then that John reached an
2 CONC  agreement with Sir Walter, whereby the latter was to '''receive their mother’s manor of Gayton in Staffordshire together with several Derbyshire properties in return for an assurance (made later in 1381) that he would advance no 
2 CONC further title to any other part of the Mountjoy estates'''.5 .... etc.
2 CONT *___________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor Beauchamp was NOT the 'second wife' of Sir John BLOUNT, whose one and only wife was Isolda MOUNTJOY, as per email from Kyle VanLandingham. "Last year I obtained the pages from Complete Peerage, Vol IX, which show the Bloun
2 CONC t line from William le Blund [le Blount] who d. 1280.  The line shows that William's grandson, John le Blount, m. Isoude [Isolda] Mountjoy.  Walter le Blount who m. Sancha de Ayala is clearly shown as the 3rd son of John and Isold
2 CONC a."  
2 CONT
2 CONT http://dgmweb.net/genealogy/FGS/B/BlountJohn-IsoldaMountjoy.shtml
2 CONT
2 CONT ================================
2 CONT
2 CONT Also:
2 CONT
2 CONT  The Blounts were a junior branch of the Blount of Sodington family of Worcestershire. The first Baron was the greatgrandson of Sir John Blount of Sodington who married Isolda Mountjoy and the grandson of Sir Walter Blount, bearer
2 CONC  of the Royal Standard of Henry IV at the Battle of Shrewsbury in 1403 where he was slain.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baron_Mountjoy
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.disnorge.no/slektsforum/viewtopic.php?t=69516  Slektsforum -> Slektsfaglige diskusjoner -> Nyhetsgrupper (arkiv) -> Fora der du MÅ skrive engelsk -> soc.genealogy.medieval -> Mountjoy family - ancestors of the Blo
2 CONC unts] 18 Feb 2008 Nathaniel Taylor:
2 CONT 	    Ah--but here the earlier sources (followed by Coll. Hist. Staffs.) seem  to be in error, which has since been corrected by CP (s.n. Mountjoy).  As CP shows, Sir John Blount (d. 1358) can only be shown to have had one  wife, I
2 CONC solda de Mountjoy. Older sources assign him a second wife,  Eleanor Beauchamp (of Hache) who is made to be the mother of his younger  sons (including the one whose descendants took the peerage title  'Mountjoy'). On the alleged Bl
2 CONC ount-Beauchamp marriage, an article by  Cecil R. Humphery-Smith, "The Blount Quarters," _The Coat of Arms_ 4  (1957), 224-27, is corrected by G. D. Squibb, "The Heirs of Beauchamp of  Hatch," ibid., pp. 275-77, showing that the pa
2 CONC rticular claimed marriage  cannot have happened. 
2 CONT
2 CONT More importantly, Isolda is documented as still wife of Sir John Blount  in 1352, well after the apparent birth year of Walter, ancestor of the  lords Mountjoy. Croke (in his Blount work back in 1823) quoted the 1352  charter but 
2 CONC didn't realize the chronological implication, repeating the  two-wife fallacy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I think the origin of the fallacious marriage is that the Blounts  quartered a chequy coat (like Beauchamp of Hache) whose origin was a  mystery for quite some time. 
2 CONT *______________________________
