1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT Alice le Blount (c. 1349 – 4 November 1414/15) was a noblewoman of the Sodington branch of the le Blount family. She is historically recognized primarily through her significant landholdings, specifically the moiety of the manor o
2 CONC f Belton, Leicestershire. Her life is established through 14th-century land settlement records and Crown administrative rolls, which identify her parentage and her marriages to two prominent English knights. Upon her death without
2 CONC  issue, her legal holdings were devolved through the le Blount family line, confirming her position as a daughter of Sir John le Blount and Isolda de Mountjoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Sir John le Blount, Kt. (c. 1298–1358)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Isolda de Mountjoy (c. 1307–aft. 1352)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Born: c. 1349, Stringston, Somerset, England (Estimated based on date of first marriage)
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 4 November 1414 (or 1415), England
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage and Issue===
2 CONT -Spouse 1: Richard Stafford
2 CONT Date of Marriage: Before 1370
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: None
2 CONT
2 CONT -Spouse 2: Sir Richard Stury, Kt.
2 CONT Date of Marriage: Before 1393
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: None
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Factual Information and Legal Relevance===
2 CONT Land Tenure: Alice le Blount held a moiety (half-share) of the manor of Belton, Leicestershire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legal Settlements (1393): Alice and her second husband, Sir Richard Stury, Knt., formally settled the manor of Belton on themselves for the duration of their lives. The legal fine established "remainders," which directed that the 
2 CONC property return to the heirs of Sir John le Blount of Sodington upon Alice’s death, providing the definitive genealogical link to her paternal family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Documentation of Heirship: Legal inquiries following her death in 1414/15 confirm she died without issue, which necessitated the reversion of her property to her father's collateral heirs, specifically Sir Walter le Blount and his
2 CONC  issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Verifiable Sources and Records===
2 CONT Victoria County History (Leicestershire), Vol. 3: Academic record detailing the descent of the manor of Belton and explicitly identifying Alice le Blount as the daughter of Sir John le Blount of Sodington.
2 CONT
2 CONT Calendar of the Close Rolls, Richard II: Official administrative records of the Crown documenting the legal settlements (land fines) made by Alice and her husband Sir Richard Stury regarding the Belton estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquisitions Post Mortem: Official legal records of the King’s Chancery conducted upon Alice’s death, proving her lack of issue and the subsequent legal transfer of the Belton manor to the le Blount cousins.
2 CONT
2 CONT Feet of Fines: Records of the legal agreements that transferred the manor to the le Blount line after the expiration of Alice’s life interest.
