1 NOTE {geni:about_me} I have seen reference -but not proof to his wife being Joan LeEyre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Middle name given in numerous secondary sources as Richard (though middle names generally were not in use by the English culture at the time that this person was alive).
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://books.google.ca/books?id=QIkUAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA233#v=onepage&q&f=false Peerage of England] by Arthur Collins (1756), gives his birth place as "Lombardy". The source for Hampshire as his birthplace is apparently John Burke, wh
2 CONC o wrote his work on extinct and dormant baronetcies in 1838. Peerage of England claims he arrived to live in England during the reign of Henry IV (1399-1413).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Excerpt of XIII. Rich, Earl of Warwick and Holland, in Peerage of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first I find of this family is John de Rich, Esq. (Pat. 11 H. IV. m. 6); born in Lombardy (northern Italy, land that was occupied by the Lombard League, which was disbanded in the century before his birth, in 1250), who was li
2 CONC ving in England in the reign of King Henry the fourth, (Pedig. of Nobility, by Lilly, penes Joh. Com' Egmont), and had issue Richard Rich of London, Esq.; who died in 26 Henry VI, seized of lands in Springfield, Little Waltham,, G
2 CONC reat Leys, Tirling, and Boreham, (all) in Essex (Efc. 26 H VI), and was buried in the church of St. Lawrence Jewry, London (Lilly praed.), leaving issue, among other children (Ibid) Richard Rich, who was of the company of Mercers 
2 CONC of the City of London, and executed the office of Sheriff of that city, an. 1441, (in the 20th of H. VI.) and founded a certain alms-house at Hodsdon in com' Hertford.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://books.google.pl/books?id=K1kBAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA441&lpg=PA441&dq=Catherine,+%22Richard+Rich%22&source=bl&ots=E9RkY3phem&sig=_co8CcnEoRjR2v_ajKkGOGefiIo&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiq09Ou7f7LAhWDhywKHdRVAHg4FBDoAQgpMAM#v=onepage
2 CONC &q=Catherine%2C%20%22Richard%20Rich%22&f=false "A genealogical and heraldic history of the extinct and dormant baronetcies of England, by J. and J.B. Burke"] by John Burke (1838)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rich's were originally of Hampshire, where:
2 CONT *John le Rich, of Rich's Place, flourished about the reign of Edward II (1307-1327). His son and heir,
2 CONT **Robert le Rich, was living temp. Edward III (1327-1387), and was father of:
2 CONT ***John le Rich, of Rich's Place, who was alive in the 13th of Henry IV (1412), and had two sons, Richard and William.
