1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Reginald de Mohun (born c.1305)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald (alias Reynold) de Mohun (born c.1305), the 4th son of John de Mohun, 1st Baron Mohun (1269–1330), feudal baron of Dunster by his wife Anne Tiptoft, daughter of Paine Tiptoft, married Elizabeth FitzWilliam, heiress of Hal
2 CONC l, Bodinnoc and other valuable estates. His father granted him a life-interest in the manor of Ugborough in Devon. In 1323 he received a royal pardon from King Edward II for having taken part in the rebellion of the Earl of Lancas
2 CONC ter and Roger Mortimer. In 1324-5 he was in Guienne on the King's service, and was abroad again in 1344, with Henry of Lancaster, Earl of Derby.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maxwell Lyte relates a story "of very doubtful origin" that Reginald first met his future wife Elizabeth FitzWilliam when he came "into Fowey harbour with soldiers bound for Ireland, let fly a hawk at some game which came down in 
2 CONC the garden at Hall". The couple were married, but he was soon deprived of his wife by her powerful neighbour Sir John Daunay, who "had designs upon her property". Daunay colluded with the Bishop of Exeter to effect a divorce of th
2 CONC e couple on the canonical ground that Elizabeth had previously been engaged to one of Reginald's elder brothers, namely Thomas Mohun. Daunay "eloigned" Elizabeth from Mohun and having married her off to a certain Henry Deneys, the
2 CONC n received quit-claims from Mohun of his FitzWilliam lands. However, Mohun made a successful appeal to the Pope, and at some time after 1346, he eventually recovered his wife and her lands, together with "enormous damages from two
2 CONC  parsons who had been the accomplices or tools of Sir John Daunay".
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hall,_Lanteglos-by-Fowey#Reginald_de_Mohun_.28born_c.1300.29 wikipedia.org]
