1 NOTE Following the death of Queen Margaret (the Maid of Norway) in 1290, John Comyn's son (also named John but often referred to as "Black Comyn") was one of the six competitors for the throne as a descendant of Donald III. The other c
2 CONC laimants included John Balliol and Robert Bruce the elder (known as "The Great Competitor"). They were descended from Donald's elder brother, King Malcolm III and so technically had better claims. 
2 CONT When King Edward I of England offered to "adjudicate", he selected John Balliol (believing that he could dominate him more easily). The Black Comyn (and Robert the Bruce's grandfather) acknowledged Balliol as king. In a move desig
2 CONC ned to strengthen the claim on the throne, John's son (also named John but usually referred to as "Red Comyn") married Balliol's sister. 
2 CONT Eventually, Balliol rebelled and Edward came north. At this time, Bruce was one of those who rendered homage to Edward and Balliol seized the Bruce lands and gave them to Red Comyn (his nephew). Edward defeated the Scots at the Ba
2 CONC ttle of Dunbar and marched across Scotland and removed such precious items as the Stone of Destiny and the Scottish crown. He humiliated Balliol by removing his royal insignia. He then demanded that all the Scottish landowners sho
2 CONC uld sign an oath of allegiance to him - a document which became known as the "Ragman Rolls". There were nine Comyns of sufficient stature to sign the document (along with another 2,000 signatories).
1 NOTE Comyn, John (Black Comyn), d. c.1300, Scottish nobleman
2 CONT   d. c.1300, Scottish nobleman, known as the Black Comyn. In 1286 hebecame one of the six regents for Margaret Maid of Norway and, as such,agreed to the treaty of 1290, by which Margaret was to marry the eldestson of Edward I of E
2 CONC ngland. After her death, he was at first a claimantfor the vacant throne but then supported the claim of his brother-in-law,John de Baliol, who was awarded the crown by Edward I of England in 1292.Comyn joined Baliol in his revolt
2 CONC  against Edward but submitted to theEnglish king in 1296. The name also appears as Cumming.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT COMYN, JOHN (d. C. 1300), Scottish baron, was a son of John Comyn (d.1274), justiciar of Galloway, who was a nephew of the constable ofScotland, Alexander Comyn, earl of Buchan (d. 1289), and of the powerfuland wealthy Walter Comy
2 CONC n, earl of Mentieth (d. 1258). With his uncle theearl of Buchan, the elder Comyn took a prominent part in the affairs ofScotland during the latter part of the 13th century, and he had interestsand estates in England as well as in 
2 CONC his native land. He fought for HenryIII. at Northampton and at Lewes, and was afterwards imprisoned for ashort time in London. The younger Comyn, who had inherited the lordshipof Badenoch from his great-uncle the earl of Mentieth,
2 CONC  was appointed oneof the guardians of Scotland in 1286, and shared in. the negotiationsbetween Edward I. and the Scots in 1289 and 1290. When Margaret, the Maidof Norway, died in 1290, Comyn was one of the claimants for the Scotti
2 CONC shthrone, but he did not press his candidature, and like the other Comynsurged the claim of John de Baliol. After supporting Baliol in his risingagainst Edward I., Comyn submitted to the English king in 1296; he wassent to reside 
2 CONC in England, but returned to Scotland shortly before hisdeath.
1 NOTE Following the death of Queen Margaret (the Maid of Norway) in 1290, John Comyn's son (also named John but often referred to as "Black Comyn") was one of the six competitors for the throne as a descendant of Donald III. The other c
2 CONC laimants included John Balliol and Robert Bruce the elder (known as "The Great Competitor"). They were descended from Donald's elder brother, King Malcolm III and so technically had better claims. 
2 CONT When King Edward I of England offered to "adjudicate", he selected John Balliol (believing that he could dominate him more easily). The Black Comyn (and Robert the Bruce's grandfather) acknowledged Balliol as king. In a move desig
2 CONC ned to strengthen the claim on the throne, John's son (also named John but usually referred to as "Red Comyn") married Balliol's sister. 
2 CONT Eventually, Balliol rebelled and Edward came north. At this time, Bruce was one of those who rendered homage to Edward and Balliol seized the Bruce lands and gave them to Red Comyn (his nephew). Edward defeated the Scots at the Ba
2 CONC ttle of Dunbar and marched across Scotland and removed such precious items as the Stone of Destiny and the Scottish crown. He humiliated Balliol by removing his royal insignia. He then demanded that all the Scottish landowners sho
2 CONC uld sign an oath of allegiance to him - a document which became known as the "Ragman Rolls". There were nine Comyns of sufficient stature to sign the document (along with another 2,000 signatories).
1 NOTE !From "Ancestral Roots of 60 Colonist" by WEIS, 6th ed, line 95, page 92; "Alianora De BALIOL m 1279/83 Sir John COMYN, Black Comyn (121A-29) d ca 1303, Lord of Badenock. (SP I 507-508, IV 143; CP I 386 (d)."
