2 CONC tates included the castle and lordship of Pembroke  and the lordship erected earldom of Wexford  in Ireland. The custody of Joan's property was entrusted to her husband, along with, apparently, the title of Earl of Pembroke  and E
2 CONC arl of Wexford between 1250 and 1260.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Second Barons' War
2 CONT
2 CONT This favouritism to royal relatives was unpopular with many of the English nobility  a discontent which would culminate in the Second Barons' War . It did not take long for William to make enemies in England. From his new lands in
2 CONC  South Wales , he tried to regain the palatine rights which had been attached to the Earldom of Pembroke , but his energies were not confined to this. The King heaped lands and honours upon him, and he was soon thoroughly hated as
2 CONC  one of the most prominent of the rapacious foreigners. Moreover, some trouble in Wales led to a quarrel between him and Simon de Montfort, who was to become the figurehead for the rebels. He refused to comply with the provisions 
2 CONC  imposed on the King at Oxford  in 1258, and took refuge in Wolvesey Castle  at Winchester , where he was besieged and compelled to surrender and leave the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, in 1259 William and de Montfort were formally reconciled in Paris, and in 1261 Valence was again in England and once more enjoying the royal favour. He fought for Henry at the disastrous Battle of Lewes , and after the de
2 CONC feat again fled to France, while de Montfort ruled England. However, by 1265 he was back, landing in Pembrokeshire , and taking after the battle he was restored to his estates and accompanied Prince Edward, afterwards Edward I, to
2 CONC  Palestine . From his base in Pembrokeshire he was a mainstay of the English campaigns against Llywelyn ap Gruffudd  and later Dafydd ap Gruffudd ; in the war of 1282-3 that led to the conquest of Wales  he negotiated the surrende
2 CONC r of one of Dafydd's last remaining castles, Castell-y-Bere , with its custodian, Cynfrig ap Madog. He also went several times to France on public business and he was one of Edward's representatives in the famous suit over the suc
2 CONC cession  to the crown of Scotland  in 1291 and 1292.
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Valence died at Bayonne  on the 13 June  1296 ; his body is buried at Swanscombe  or at Westminster Abbey .
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT William de Valance, Earl of Pembroke, Knight was born on c. 1225 in Valance, Vienne, Poitou-Chantres, France to Hugh X De Lusignan, Count de la March and Isabella D'Angouleme de Lusignan.  William married Joan de Munchensy, Counte
2 CONC ss of Pembroke on 1248 in Pembroke Castle, Pembroke, Pembrokeshire, Wales and had 7 children:  Agnes de Valance; John de Valance; Margaret de Valance; William de Valance, Sire de Montignac and Bellac; Joan de Valance; Isabel de Va
2 CONC lance; and Aymer de Valance, 2nd Earl of Pembroke.  He passed away on c. 1295 in Bayonne, Pyrenees-Atlantiques, Aquitaine, France.  He is buried in Swanscombe, Dartford, Kent, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William de Valance, Earl of Pembroke, Knight is my 26th great uncle.
2 CONT
