1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography===  
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Talbot-38 and then edited.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mary Talbot''' was the daughter of Sir Richard Talbot, 4th Lord Talbot, and his wife Ankaret le Strange. Sister of Sir John Talbot, Earl of Shrewsbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Talbot married, as her first husband, Sir Thomas Greene, son of Sir Thomas Greene and the daughter of John Mablethorp. They were married before 23 Oct 1398, the date of their deed, and had one son:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Thomas Greene, Knt., born 10 February 1399/1400, died 18 January 1461/2, married Philippe Ferrers and Mary Bellers and had issue with both wives. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Grene, Knight, husband of Mary Talbot, died on 14 December 1417[7] and his heir was his son Thomas Greene, Knight, age 18.[1][2][10][11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 23 April and 28 June 1418 she (described as Marion, Lady de Grene) was resident with two servants at the Talbot estate of Blakemere, Shropshire.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary remarried to John Nottingham before 14 June 1420, the date of the pardon for marrying without a license.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Mary his widow married John Notyngham without licence. They entered the manor of Kegworth against the king’s right and have taken the profits since the death of Thomas".
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary died 13 April 1434[7][8] and was buried at Green's Norton, Northamptonshire.[1][2][13] The Writ for the inquisition post mortem recorded 18 April 1434 for Nottinghamshire stated, "She died on 13 Apr last." Thomas Greene, Knt.
2 CONC , aged 34 or more, was her next heir.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Monuments
2 CONT
2 CONT The Greene monuments at St Bartholomew, Green’s Norton, Northamptonshire, now largely destroyed, were described by Halstead in 1685. There was a tomb for Sir Thomas Greene and Mary Talbot. The Inscription was in Latin, a translati
2 CONC on of which is:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Here lies Thomas Greene, Knight, son and heir of Thomas Greene, Knight, son and heir of Henry Greene, Knight, one of the Justices of King Edward Third, and Mary, his wife, the daughter of Lord Talbot, to whose souls may god be g
2 CONC racious. Amen."
2 CONT
2 CONT On the side of this was a shield bearing Greene impaling Talbot – argent, a lion rampant gules, within a bordure engrailed of the last.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary was also noted on the monument to her grandson at St Bartholomew, Green’s Norton, as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Here lies Thomas Greene, Knight, Lord of Norton, and Matilda, his wife. The said Thomas was son and heir of Thomas Greene, Knight, Lord of the same, and of Philippa, his wife, who was the daughter of Robert, Lord Ferrars of Char
2 CONC teley, and of Elizabeth his wife, daughter of Lord Thomas de Spencer. Thomas Greene, the father of Thomas aforesaid, was son and heir of Thomas Greene, Knight, Lord of Norton aforesaid, and of Mary his wife, daughter of Richard, L
2 CONC ord Talbot, and of Ankarete, his wife, who was the daughter and heir of Lord Strange of Blackmere. Thomas, son of Thomas and Philippa aforesaid, died on the ninth of September, A.D. 1462; and the said Matilda, one of the daughters
2 CONC  of John Throckmorton, Esq., formerly Under-Treasurer of England, died on the …. Of the month of … A.D. 14 … May God be gracious to their souls."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Some internet genealogies state that the wife of Thomas Greene was an Ela Mallory, "Lady of Isham". There is no basis for this assertion.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000223268170827&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT >Source: Richardson, Douglas. 2011. Plantagenet Ancestry: A Study In Colonial And Medieval Families. Edited by Kimball G. Everingham. 2nd ed. Vol. 1. Page 154.
2 CONT ----
