2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John was the son and heir of Sir Pain Tiptoft and Agnes Wrothe.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married twice, he only had children with his second wife Joyce Charleton.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married first before February 24, 1408, to Philippa Talbot (c. 1367–1417), daughter of Sir John Talbot, of Richard's Castle, Herefordshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married secondly Joyce Charleton by royal license dated February 28, 1422. She was the daughter and co-heiress of Edward Charleton, 5th Baron Cherleton by his first wife Eleanor Holland, a descendant of King Edward I.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were the parents of
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tiptoft, 1st Earl of Worcester (1427–1470), whose only son Edward Tiptoft, 2nd Earl of Worcester died in 1485, a minor without issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippa Tiptoft (c.1423 - after January 1487), married Thomas de Ros, 9th Baron de Ros (1427–1467).
2 CONT
2 CONT Joanna Tiptoft (c.1425 - 1494), married, in 1435, Sir Edmund Ingoldsthorpe (c1421 - 1456), of Burgh Green, Cambridgeshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joyce Tiptoft (aft.1425 – c.1470), married Sir Edmund Sutton.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was knighted in 1399. He was returned to Parliament as knight of the shire for Huntingdonshire in January and October, 1404 and again in 1406, when he was elected Speaker of the House of Commons. He represented Somerset in 1414
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed Treasurer of the Household (1406–1408), Chief Butler of England in 1407, Treasurer of the Exchequer (1408–1409), Seneschal of Aquitaine (1415–1423), Councillor of Regency to Henry VI (1422–1443) and Lord Steward o
2 CONC f the Household (1426–1432).
2 CONT
2 CONT On 7 January 1426 he was summoned to the House of Lords whereby he was created Baron Tiptoft.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John, 1st Lord Tiptoft, was buried at Enfield in Middlesex, early in February 1443
