1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Wikitree: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Grey-1883 John Grey]
2 CONT * Son of [father unknown] and [mother unknown]
2 CONT * John was married to Elizabeth D'Abernon Croyser by July 1, 1390.[1]
2 CONT * Both John and Elizabeth were dead before October of 1392.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''CP 25/1/289/56, number 224. Image of document at AALT County:''' Hertfordshire. Bedfordshire. Middlesex. '''Place:''' Westminster. '''Date:''' One week from St John the Baptist, 14 Richard [II] [1 July 1390]. And afterwards t
2 CONC wo weeks from St Michael, 15 Richard [II] [13 October 1391]. '''Parties:''' Thomas de Percy, knight, Master William de Assheton', clerk, Thomas de [Hungerford'], knight, and Robert de Whiteby, clerk, querents, ''and John Grey and 
2 CONC Elizabeth, his wife, deforciants''. '''Property:''' The manors of Maydecroft and Tidbirst [in the county of Hertford and] the manor of Wrastelyngworth' in the county of Bedford and 4 messuages, 1 carucate of land, 20 acres of mead
2 CONC ow and 6 acres of wood in [E?]geswere and Parua Stanmere in the county of Middlesex, which Thomas Barre, knight, and Elizabeth, his wife, hold for the life of Elizabeth. '''Action:''' Plea of covenant. '''Agreement:''' ''John and 
2 CONC Elizabeth, his wife'', have acknowledged the manors and tenements to be the right of Robert, ''and have granted for themselves and the heirs of Elizabeth, the wife of John'', that the manors and tenements - which Thomas Barre and 
2 CONC Elizabeth, his wife, held for the life of Elizabeth ''of the inheritance of Elizabeth, the wife of John'', in the aforesaid vills on the day the agreement was made, and which after the decease [of Elizabeth], the wife of Thomas, '
2 CONC 'ought to revert to John and Elizabeth, his wife, and the heirs of Elizabet''h - after the decease of Elizabeth, the wife [of Thomas], shall remain to Thomas de Percy, Master William, Thomas de Hungerford' and Robert and the heirs
2 CONC  of Robert, to hold of the chief lords for ever. '''Warranty:''' For this: Thomas de Percy, Master William, Thomas de Hungerford' and Robert have given them 200 pounds sterling. '''Note:''' This agreement was made in the presence 
2 CONC of Thomas Barre and Elizabeth, and they did fealty to Thomas de Percy, Master William, Thomas de Hungerford' and Robert in the court.
2 CONT # '''Reference: C 131/44/7 Description: Debtor:''' ''Elizabeth Croyser, widow, of Surrey, now deceased, and William Croyser, her son.'' '''Creditor:''' John Sandhurst, citizen and chandler of London [deceased]. '''Amount:''' £200.
2 CONC  '''Before whom:''' Adam Carlisle, Mayor of the Staple of Westminster. '''When taken:''' 10/07/1392 '''First term:''' 25/12/1392 '''Last term:''' 25/12/1392 '''Writ to:''' Sheriff of Surrey Sent by: Chancery. '''Endorsement:''' ''
2 CONC William Croyser was not found in the bailiwick''. The extent appears in the inquisition. '''Note:''' Date given for return to Chancery: 11/04/1395. The executors of John Sandhurst are Thomas Wight of London, grocer, and Cecily, hi
2 CONC s wife, John Eston, draper, and Gilbert Aghton, fishmonger. '''M.2:''' Inquisition made at Guildford, Wed. after 31/03/1395 before Edward St John, knight, then Sheriff of Surrey. ''William Croyser has no goods or chattels in the b
2 CONC ailiwick. Elizabeth Croyser had on the day of the recognisance and afterwards the manor of Aldebury in Surrey which is worth £28 nett'', from which Elizabeth pays to John Cobham 100s. a year for his life. '''M.3:''' Inquisition ma
2 CONC de at Bookham on Sat. 01/05/1395 before Edward St John, knight, Sheriff of Surrey. ''William, the son of Elizabeth Croyser had on the day of the recognisance and afterwards, 40 acres of land in the parish of Bookham called Fryelan
2 CONC d worth 16s. a year''. '''Dorse:''' Memorandum that 02/07/1395 the lands were delivered to the executors. '''Date:''' 1395 Feb 24 '''Held by:''' The National Archives, Kew Legal status: Public Record Language: English
