1 NAME Edward /Stradling/
2 GIVN Edward
2 SURN Stradling
2 _MARNM Stradling
1 NAME Edward Stradling
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1295
2 PLAC Of St Donat's Glamorganshire
2 ADDR
3 CITY St Donats
3 STAE Wales
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1363
2 PLAC St Dontats Castle St Donats W Glamorgan
2 ADDR
3 CITY Saint Donats
3 STAE Wales
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173426968959 Stradling 1 (May 4, 2024; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Edward Stradling was the second son of Peter Stradling and Joan de Hawey. He was born in 1995 so was only aged about 5 when his father died in 1300. His mother Joan married again to Sir John Pembridge/Pembrigg who was granted 
2 CONC wardship of the two boys & their property.
2 CONT His elder brother John held title of the manors in his own right in 1314, but must have died soon after, dsp, as Edward was confirmed as lord of St Donats in 1316.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dictionary of Welsh Biography
2 CONT https://biography.wales/article/s-STRA-MOR-1275
2 CONT
2 CONT "Joh. de Penbrugg" [stepfather] still held the advowson of Combehay Rectory in Somerset (a Hawey Estate) on 8 November 1312,[5] so Joan's sons were not yet of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed as Lord of St Donat's in 1316 so was aged 21 and his elder brother must have died dsp. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Stradling-358
2 CONT Knight of the Bath, knighted after the deposing of King Edward II on 20 January 1326/27
2 CONT Edward Stradling (first of the name), second son of Sir Peter de Stratelinges by his wife, Joan de Hawey / Lady Stradling, was born about 1295, as he was able to hold title of manors as early as 1316. [1] [2] [3] Although not defi
2 CONC nitively proven, his birthplace was most likely in the de Hawey family estates of Somerset, given he was Lord of Combe Hay/Hawey farmstead in the Hundred of Wellow, Somerset, England during the Nomina Villarum, a survey carried ou
2 CONC t after 5 March 1315/16. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT When Sir Peter died, his widow then married Sir John Penbrigg, who was granted wardship over Sir Peter's estates and both young sons, Edward and his older brother, John, until such time as they attained 21 years of age. [2] "Joh. 
2 CONC de Penbrugg" still held the advowson of Combehay Rectory in Somerset (a Hawey Estate) on 8 November 1312,[5] so Joan's sons were not yet of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon attaining the age of 21 years, Edward gained hereditary titles, along with the knight's fee of St. Donat's and other family estates of his parents. He would use marriage and appointments to expand the Stradling estates within
2 CONC  his lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward and Elena/Eleanor, daughter and heiress of Sir Gilbert Strongbow, were married between 1316 and "in a week of Easter" 1317-8. 
2 CONT https://www.medievalgenealogy.org.uk/fines/abstracts/CP_25_1_286_31.shtml
2 CONT County:	Somerset. Dorset.
2 CONT Place:	Westminster.
2 CONT Date:	Three weeks from Easter, 11 Edward II [14 May 1318].
2 CONT Parties:	Edward de Stradelyng' and Ellen, his wife, querents, by William de Perret, put in the place of Ellen by the lord king's writ, and Michael le Rous, deforciant.
2 CONT Property:	3 messuages, 1 mill, 5 carucates and 2 virgates of land, 31 acres of meadow, 141 acres of wood [and] 7 pounds, 9 shillings and 2 pence of rent in Cumbe Haweye, Wachet Haweye and Hennegraue by Brywton' and the advowson of
2 CONC  the church of Cumbe Haweye in the county of Somerset and 1 messuage, 2 carucates of land, 25 acres of meadow, 15 acres of wood and 108 shillings and 8 pence and 1 halfpenny of rent in Cumpton' Haweye and the advowson of the churc
2 CONC h of the same vill in the county of Dorset.
2 CONT Action:	[Plea of covenant?].
2 CONT Agreement:	Edward has acknowledged the tenements and advowsons to be the right of Michael, as those which Michael has of his gift.
2 CONT For this:	Michael has granted to Edward and Ellen the tenements and advowsons and has rendered them to them in the court, to hold to Edward and Ellen and the heirs of their bodies, of the chief lords for ever. In default of such h
2 CONC eirs, remainder to the right heirs of Edward.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Stradling (heir), married Gwenllian Berkerolles, daughter of Roger Berkerolles of East Orchard, Glamorgan; had issue
2 CONT Unknown son (possibly John), married Sarah Berkerolles, daughter of Roger Berkerolles of East Orchard, Glamorgan; had issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheriff and Escheator of Somerset and Dorset 1343 [11][12]
2 CONT MP for Somerset 1343 [11]
2 CONT Justice of the Peace for Somerset and Dorset 1346–1347 [11]
2 CONT On 11 September 1346, Sir Edward was one of three knights of Somerset at King Edward III's Westminster parliament. [13]
2 CONT Sir Edward likely lived until 1363, when his son, Edward did homage for Combe Hawey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Memorials of the Danvers Family p. 247
2 CONT In the year 1341, Sir Edward Stradling , Lord of St Donats, and his wife Elena made provision with William, Abbot of Neath, for an obit for their souls.
