1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir John Stradling was the second son of Sir William Stradling and Isabel St Barbe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In about 1417 he married Joan Dauntsey, daughter of John Dauntsey & Elizabeth Beverley. She was the heir of her brother Walter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Stradling & Joan Dauntsey had at least two sons; When John died in 1435, his IPM shows his son & heir was Richard Stradling, born about 1422. However, he died young dsp & the eventual heir was Edmund, born about 1428. 
2 CONT
2 CONT IPM 13 Henry IV
2 CONT Heir Richard aged 12 and more. Richard died dsp & heir was Edmund Stradling, aged 28 and more when his mother Joan died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://inquisitionspostmortem.ac.uk/view/inquisition/24-303/index.html
2 CONT JOHN STRADLYNG, KNIGHT
2 CONT 303 Writ. ‡ 30 January 1435. [ Frank ].
2 CONT WILTSHIRE. Inquisition. Salisbury. 12 October 1435. [Bonham].
2 CONT Jurors: David Cervyngton ; Thomas Gereberd ; Robert Blake ; Thomas Ryngwode, junior ; John West ; Richard Rede ; William Thacham ; John Stone ; Richard Thacham ; Peter ?Duyke [ms torn] ; William Pette ; and John Botiller .
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the following in right of Joan his wife, still living.
2 CONT Marden, the manor, annual value 20 marks, held of the king in socage by rent of one rose at Midsummer for all service.
2 CONT Dauntsey, the manor, annual value £20, held of the abbot of Malmesbury by rent of 2s. yearly for all service.
2 CONT Smithcot, the manor, annual value 100s., held of John Fastolfe, knight , by service of one grain of pepper yearly for all service.
2 CONT Wilsford Dauntsey, the manor, annual value 4 marks, held of the earl of Hereford by rent of one grain of pepper yearly for all service.
2 CONT Winterbourne Dauntsey, the manor, annual value 8 marks, held of Humphrey, duke of Gloucester , by rent of a pair of gloves yearly for all service.
2 CONT He died on 23 November last. Richard is his son and next heir, and aged 12 years and more.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.british-history.ac.uk/vch/wilts/vol14/pp65-75#h3-0002
2 CONT In 1066 Alward held Dauntsey of Malmesbury abbey, apparently by lease. By 1086 the abbey had granted it as a knight's fee to Robert. (fn. 64) It apparently passed to Miles of Dauntsey, who was succeeded in turn by his son Miles an
2 CONC d Miles's son Miles; (fn. 65) one of that name held the manor in the late 12th century. (fn. 66) In 1242–3 it was held by the youngest Miles's son Roger Dauntsey. It passed to Roger's brother Gilbert, (fn. 67) on whom it was settl
2 CONC ed for life in 1257 with remainder for life to a third brother Richard. (fn. 68) By 1275 it had passed to Richard's heir, (fn. 69) presumably his son Richard (fl. 1292). (fn. 70) Sir Richard Dauntsey, possibly the younger Richard'
2 CONC s son, held the manor in 1331 (fn. 71) and was succeeded after 1349 (fn. 72) by his grandson Sir John Dauntsey (d. 1391). The manor passed in turn to Sir John's son Sir John (fn. 73) (d. 1405) and to that Sir John's son Sir Walter
2 CONC  (fn. 74) (d. 1420). Sir Walter was succeeded by his sister Joan, wife of Sir John Stradling (fn. 75) (d. 1433), (fn. 76) later wife of John Dewale. From Joan (d. 1457) the manor passed in turn to her son Edmund Stradling (fn. 77)
2 CONC  (d. 1461), Edmund's son John (fn. 78) (d. 1471), and John's posthumous son Edward (fn. 79) (d. 1487). Edward was succeeded by his sister Anne, wife of Sir John Danvers (fn. 80) (d. 1514). (fn. 81) From Anne (d. 1539) the manor pa
2 CONC ssed to her grandson Silvester Danvers (fn. 82) (d. 1551) and to his son Sir John. (fn. 83)
2 CONT
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173426968959 Stradling 1 (May 4, 2024; Anne Brannen, curator)
