1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Thomas de la Bere is the only known child of John de la Bere. So far, the name of his mother has not been discovered. He was just a small child when his father died and so his lands were looked after by John St Joh
2 CONC n [son of Elizabeth de la Bere & Oliver St John]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, Thomas died in 1414, while still a minor, the last direct male descendant of Sir John de la Bere & Agnes de Turberville. His lands from the de Turberville estate then went to the Gamage family, descended from the 4t
2 CONC h de Turberville sister, Sara.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquisition Post Mortem John de la Bere of Hereford and the adjacent Marches of Wales. 11 Henry IV [30 September 1409-29 September 1410. C 137/79/36 In Latin. Heir is Thomas de la Bere aged 7 years. JOHN DE LA BERE 743 Writ mandam
2 CONC us . ‡ 20 April 1410 [Wissyngsete]. <http://www.inquisitionspostmortem.ac.uk/view/inquisition/19-743> Regarding lands held of Thomas, late earl Marshal, son and heir of Thomas, late duke of Norfolk, a minor in royal custody. HEREF
2 CONC ORDSHIRE AND THE ADJACENT MARCH OF WALES . Inquisition [indented]. Hereford  2 May. [ap Harry]. Jurors: John Schellewik ; Hugh Watyes ; John Broyne ; Philip Fourches ; John Welynton ; William Forster ; Hugh Hanebury ; John Ward ; 
2 CONC Richard Smyth of Leominster ; John Payn ; Hugh Benet ; and John Chaundos . He held a fortified manor called Webley  in the lordship of Gower  called ‘Gowereslond’ in Wales of Thomas late Earl Marshal , son and heir of the duke of 
2 CONC Norfolk , then under age in the king’s ward, by knight service of the castle and lordship of Swansea  ; annual values, the site nil because the house and buildings were destroyed by the rebels, the manor 30s. He died on 24 Sept. 1
2 CONC 403 . Thomas his son and heir is aged 9 years. John Seint Johan, knight , has held and taken the profits, title unknown. C 137/79/36 mm. 1-2 http://www.inquisitionspostmortem.ac.uk/view/inquisition/24-125/
2 CONT
2 CONT William Gamage 1411-1414 Partition of the lands of Laurance Berkerolles in County Glamorgan, Wales between Joan, wife of Richard Vernon, Edward and John Stradeling, William Gamage and Thomas de la Bere; co-heirs  [to the de Turber
2 CONC ville lands] 13 Henry IV Nat Archives, Kew C47/9/32
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of his kinsman, John de la Bere, in May 1409 [IPM date, death was 1403], brought him [John st John] temporary custody of all the latter’s possessions in Glamorgan; and when the next heir, Thomas, a royal ward, died witho
2 CONC ut issue five years later the entire inheritance was duly partitioned, one half going to St. John’s cousin, John Basset, and the other to the MP himself. https://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1386-1421/member/st-john-si
2 CONC r-john-1360-1424
2 CONT
2 CONT 1414 Thomas de la Bere died as a minor on 28 October 1414, heir of Coity Castle 21 March 1414 - 20 March 1415 Thomas de la Beere, Inquisition Post Mortem, Gloucestershire & the marches of Wales C 138/8/37
2 CONT
2 CONT LAWRENCE BERKKEROLLES and THOMAS DE LA BEER 210. ‡ . Commission to John Derehurst, Robert Andrewe, Nicholas Sambourne the younger and Thomas Crekkelade [CPR 1413–16, p.263], as in writ devenerunt. 16 July 1414. [Wymbyssh]. Regardi
2 CONC ng lands which came into the king’s hand by the death of Laurence Berkerolles, chivaler, who held by knight service of Richard, late lord le Despenser, a minor in the ward of Henry IV, and by the minority of Thomas de la Beere, no
2 CONC w deceased, son of John, son of John de la Beere chivaler, son of Agnes, sister of Richard Turburvill, and one of the kin and heirs of the said Richard.
2 CONT
2 CONT GLOUCESTERSHIRE AND THE ADJACENT MARCH OF WALES Inquisition before John Derehurst and Robert Andrewe Cirencester 13 Nov.. Jurors: John Martyn ; John Sau...marys ; Thomas Snell ; Reynold Spycer ; Richard ?Drap[unclear: our] of Baun
2 CONC ton ; John Tame of Fairford ; John Sameborne ; Thomas Baroun ; Henry Pouyter ; Thomas Walleys ; William Cook of Fairford ; and John Harold .
2 CONT
2 CONT [Details of the holdings in Coychurch, Langeley, Waterton, Nolton, Coyty, Newcastle, Newland, Llanharry, Altrewe and Neath (all in Glam.) which came into the hands of Henry IV by the death of Lawrence, and remain in the hands of t
2 CONC he present king are given in CFR 1413–22, pp.99–101,
2 CONT
2 CONT as are the names of the heirs].
2 CONT
2 CONT C 138/8/37 mm. 1-2 http://www.inquisitionspostmortem.ac.uk/view/inquisition/20-210/
