1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The portrait used to illustrate Elizabeth Paulet is not of her. It is a Tudor era painting that was thought to be Anne Boleyn. See here for an article that shows the painting and another version with a ruff. https:
2 CONC //katherinethequeen.com/442464540
2 CONT This same portrait is claimed to be an image of many different women [as shown in a reverse google search].
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Paulet is claimed to be a daughter of John Paulet of Rodes, Someset who does not seem to exist.
2 CONT There seems to be no records of her marriage to Sir John St John.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of their 4 children; two do exist [Oliver & Agnes] but I think belong to a different set of parents. Joan & Isabel do not seem to exist.
2 CONT
2 CONT More research is needed but It may be that Sir John St John & Elizabeth Paulet are an "extra" generation that does not exist [outside of a Visitation].
2 CONT
2 CONT A Oliver St John did marry an Elizabeth Paulet in 1602, bring Paulet as a Christian name into the family.
