1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Christina (or Christian) Bruce (c. 1270 – 1356/1357) was a sister of King Robert I of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Along with the king's other female relatives, she was captured shortly after his rebellion. However she avoided the fate of Isabella MacDuff (the Countess of Buchan) and Mary Bruce who were imprisoned in cages, but was sent to a c
2 CONC onvent in Lincolnshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married three times. Her husbands were Gartnait, Earl of Mar, Christopher Seton, and Sir Andrew Murray.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1335, during the wars against Edward Balliol, she commanded the garrison of Kildrummy Castle during a siege by pro-Balliol forces led by David of Strathbogie.
2 CONT
2 CONT --from Wikipedia.org
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://historytheinterestingbits.com/2016/04/01/an-uncommon-sister-christian-bruce/?fbclid=IwAR1m6BXiTY_p7GLradTLyh_MxWDSzCz02u6yFl_QoAVjyE4j6ckUMga-MjY
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Third daughter; sister of King Robert I of Scotland who made her Lady of Garioch; married three times. Her second husband was Sir Christopher Seton, and her third was Sir Andrew Moray.; Sister of King Robert I of Scotland; mother 
2 CONC by Andrew Moray o f two sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Christina Bruce
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Christina (or Christian) Bruce (b. c1273 Seton, East Lothian, Scotland – 1356/1357) the the second daughter of Robert de Brus, jure uxoris Earl of Carrick and Marjorie of Carrick, and a older sister of King Robert I of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Along with the king's other female relatives, she was captured shortly after his rebellion. However she avoided the fate of Isabella MacDuff (the Countess of Buchan) and Mary Bruce who were imprisoned in cages, but was sent to a c
2 CONC onvent in Lincolnshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married three times. Her husbands were Gartnait, Earl of Mar, (d. 1305) Christopher Seton (d. 1306), and lastly Sir Andrew Murray.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1335, during the wars against Edward Balliol, she commanded the garrison of Kildrummy Castle during a siege by pro-Balliol forces led by David of Strathbogie.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Traquair Freedom's Sword 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Christian Bruce (sometimes referred to as "Christina"), lived from 1273 to 1357. An older sister of Robert the Bruce, she played an active part in the Wars of Independence against the English. The wider picture in Scotland at
2 CONC  the time is set out in our Historical Timeline.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Remains of Turnberry Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT The Remains of Turnberry Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Christian was the second of ten children born to Robert Bruce of Annandale and Marjorie, Countess of Carrick. In an unusually twist on what was probably a fairly common medieval story, her mother Marjorie had kidnapped and he
2 CONC ld Robert prisoner at Turnberry Castle until he agreed to marry her. The wedding took place in 1271, and the following year their first child Isobel was born. Christian followed a year later, and Robert, later known as Robert the 
2 CONC Bruce, appeared the year after that. Lady Christian may well have been born at Turnberry Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian married her first husband, Gartnait, Earl of Mar, in about 1295. He seems to have died some time after 1300, and Christian married Sir Christopher Seton. The Wars of Independence were viciously fought, and following the 
2 CONC defeat of Robert the Bruce in 1306 Lady Christina was captured by the English at Tain while fleeing to safety in Orkney. Other female relatives captured at the same time were imprisoned in cages, and her husband and two of her bro
2 CONC thers were executed. Lady Christina herself was imprisoned at the Gilbertine nunnery at Sixhills in Lincolnshire, where she would stay until after Robert the Bruce's victory at the Battle of Bannockburn in 1314.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time later, Christian married her third husband, Sir Andrew Murray. Sir Andrew was appointed Guardian of Scotland after the death of Robert the Bruce in 1329 and played a leading role in the 2nd War of Independence against Ed
2 CONC ward III of England, who wanted to place Edward Balliol on the Scottish throne. In 1335, English forces under David de Strathbogie, Earl of Atholl, besieged Kildrummy Castle in Aberdeenshire. The defenders were commanded by Lady C
2 CONC hristina Bruce, who held out until forces commanded by Sir Andrew Murray could march north and defeat David de Strathbogie at the Battle of Culblean on 30 November 1335.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Christian continued to play an important role in Scottish politics until her death in 1357 at the age of 84. She had two children with Gartnait, Earl of Mar, and two more with Sir Andrew Murray.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Christian Bruce, sister of Robert I, who granted her the lands of Garioch for her life, but whereby the Earls of Mar acquired the (feudal) Lordship of Garioch (not a peerage dignity) and were even latterly styled "Earls of Ga
2 CONC rioch". [Burke'sPeerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian Bruce, d. 1357; m. (1) Gratney, Earl of Mar, d. before 1305, son of Donald, Earl of Mar, by Helen or Elen, illegitimate daughter of Llewellyn ap Iorwerth, Prince of Wales, widow of Malcolm, Earl of Fife. [Magna Charta Su
2 CONC reties]
2 CONT --------------------
