1 NOTE 1.  Earl of Carrick, Robert Bruce was born at Turnberry Castle, Ayrshire, in 1274, of both Norman and Celtic ancestry.
2 CONT 2. He is rumored to had died of Leprosy.
2 CONT 3.  Original name: Robert VIII De Bruce, or Robert the Bruce king of Scotland (1306-29), who freed Scotland from English rule, winning the decisive Battle of Bannockburn (1314) and ultimately confirming Scottish independence in th
2 CONC e Treaty of Northampton (1328). (12/15/02 AC)
2 CONT http://www.deldom.com/bohanan/index.htm
1 NOTE Robert couldn't make up his mind on which side to take. That of the Normans or that of the Scots.
2 CONT In the end he betrayed William Wallace 1272-23 Aug 1305,
1 NOTE Robert I, King of Scots (11 July 1274 – 7 June 1329) usually known in modern English as Robert the Bruce (Medieval Gaelic: Roibert a Briuis; modern Scottish Gaelic: Raibeart Bruis; Norman French: Robert de Brus or Robert de Bruys;
2 CONC  ) was King of the Scots from 1306 until his death in 1329.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although his paternal ancestors were of Scoto-Norman heritage (originating in Brieux, Normandy)[1], his maternal ancestors were Scottish-Gaels. He became one of Scotland's greatest kings, as well as one of the most famous warriors
2 CONC  of his generation, eventually leading Scotland during the Wars of Scottish Independence against the Kingdom of England. He claimed the Scottish throne as a fourth great-grandson of David I of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT His body is buried in Dunfermline Abbey, while his heart is buried in Melrose Abbey. His heart was to be taken on crusade eventually to the Holy Land, but only reached Moorish Granada, where it acted as a talisman for the Scottish
2 CONC  contingent at the Battle of Teba.
1 NOTE Robert Bruce
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=32f8bf3f-db92-4666-9120-9f1d02becfd3&tid=6786050&pid=-1118010205
1 NOTE victor of Bannockburn 1314. Scotlands liberator & epic hero.
2 CONT Assumed the Crown and Royal Dignity of Scotland, thus bringing to an end
2 CONT the Second interregnum, in defiance of Edward I of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Baronage  (http://www.baronage.co.uk)
2 CONT Has extensive discussion about the Bruce origins at five different webpages:
2 CONT The Ancestry of Robert the Bruce I ~ The first Earls of Carrick
2 CONT http://www.baronage.co.uk/bphtm-03/bruce-01.html
2 CONT The Ancestry of Robert the Bruce ~ II. Bruces from Normandy
2 CONT http://www.baronage.co.uk/bphtm-03/bruce-02.html
2 CONT The Ancestry of Robert the Bruce   III ~ The Mysterious Blue Lion
2 CONT http://www.baronage.co.uk/bphtm-03/bruce-03.html
2 CONT Ancestry of Robert the Bruce, Part IV ~ The Blue Lion in Flanders
2 CONT http://www.baronage.co.uk/bphtm-03/bruce-04.html
2 CONT The Ancestry of Robert the Bruce  To summarise
2 CONT http://www.baronage.co.uk/bphtm-03/bruce-05.html
2 CONT
2 CONT "Could Robert de Bruges, as a younger son of the powerful Count ofLouvain, have accepted the post of Castellan from the Count of Flanders?Most certainly, yes! The Count of Flanders was then, de facto, probablythe most powerful sov
2 CONC ereign in northwest Europe. Bruges was strategicallyof huge importance. It would be a very attractive offer, especially to alandless younger son, and it could be expected to lead on to furtheropportunities (such as the offer to ac
2 CONC company the Count's daughter toNormandy, when she left to marry Duke William, the eventual Conqueror ofEngland, an offer that would entail a grant of land in the Cotentin"
2 CONT From Baronage at  http://www.baronage.co.uk/bphtm-03/bruce-05.html
2 CONT
