1 NOTE "THE BRUCE"; KING OF SCOTS 1306-1329; VICTOR AT BANNOCKBURN 1314; CAUSE OF
2 CONT DEATH (POSSIBLY) LEPROSY; GRANDSON OF ROBERT BRUCE (CLAIMANT TO THE SCOTTISH
2 CONT THRONE 1291) AND GREAT-GREAT-GRANDSON OF DAVID I
1 NOTE Robert I, (Roibert a Briuis in medieval Gaelic, Raibeart Bruis in modern Scottish Gaelic and Robert de Brus in Norman French), usually known in modern English today as Robert the Bruce (July 11, 1274  June 7, 1329), was King of S
2 CONC cotland (1306  1329). Although his paternal ancestors were of Scoto-Norman heritage, his maternal ancestors were Gaelic, and he became one of Scotland's greatest kings, and one of the most famous warriors of his generation, event
2 CONC ually leading Scotland during the Wars of Scottish Independence against England. He claimed the Scottish throne as a great-great-great-great grandson of David I of Scotland.
1 NOTE Notes on Robert the Bruce, King of Scotland:
2 CONT Crowned at scone in 1306. His place of birth uncertain; it is also reportedly Turnberry, Ayshire or in Essex. In 1296, as Earl of Carrick, he swore fealty to Edward I at Berwick. In 1297 he renewed that oath at Carlisle. Shortly a
2 CONC fterwards, with his Carrick vassals he joined the Scottish independence fighters under Sir William Wallace. In 1298, after Wallace�s defeat at Falkirk his lands were wasted by the English. One of the four Regents of Scotland in 12
2 CONC 99; he rejoined the battle against Edward in 1306. Originally supported by John Comyn, the nephew of John Baliol, they eventually quarrelled and Comyn was killed during the final rising of 1306 by Kirkpatrick. Bruce assembled his 
2 CONC forces and two months later was crowned at Scone, but was forced to retreat to Ireland. In 1307, the Bruce landed a force at Turnberry and defeated the English under the Earl of Pembroke at Loudon Hill. After the death of Edward I
2 CONC  in 1307, the English were cleared out of Scotland and all of the great castles recovered except Stirling, which the Governor promised to surrender if not relieved before 24 June. This led to the battle of Bannockburn when the Eng
2 CONC lish armies of some 100,000 men under King Edward II were totally routed by Robert the Bruce with 30,000. In 1317, Bruce passed over to Ireland to help his brother Edward and defeated the Anglo-Irish at the battle of Slane. Hostil
2 CONC ities continued until a truce in 1323 and then resumed in 1327 with a great Scottish inroad into the northern counties. The war at last closed with the Treaty of Northampton (1328) which recognised the independence of Scotland and
2 CONC  Bruce�s right to the throne. Bruce died of leprosy at Cardross Castle. His heart was to be carried to Palestine [this was the period of the Crusades] but Douglas who was carrying it was killed fighting the Moors in Spain. Bruce�s
2 CONC  heart was returned to Scotland and buried at Melrose Abbey. {Burke�s Peerage and Chamber�s Biographical Dictionary} [GADD.GED]
2 CONT
2 CONT King Robert I (Robert the Bruce)
2 CONT 1274 - 1329
2 CONT
2 CONT Eldest son of Robert de Bruce, 5th Lord Annandale and Earl 
2 CONT of Carrick, born in Essex (England). Despite somewhat wavering loyalty in his
2 CONT  earlier years, Bruce was a hero of the Wars of Independence,
2 CONT  eventually being crowned King of Scotland at Scone in 1306.
2 CONT  He defeated the English King Edward II at Bannockburn on 
2 CONT 24th June 1314. He is supposed to have been encouraged towards
2 CONT  perseverance and eventual victory by watching a spider build 
2 CONT a web in the cave in which he was hiding. Bruce died of leprosy 
2 CONT at Cardross Castle on the Firth of Clyde. His body was buried at 
2 CONT Dunfermline Abbey and his heart was interred at Melrose Abbey 
2 CONT after being carried to the Crusades. His daughter Marjorie 
2 CONT married Walter the High Steward, their son Robert II initiating 
2 CONT the Stewart line
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James Douglas ('The Good')
2 CONT
2 CONT c.1286 - 1330
2 CONT
2 CONT Freedom fighter. The son of Sir William Douglas (d.1298), who 
2 CONT had been a supporter of William Wallace (1274 - 1305), James was sent to France for safety in these dangerous times. He returned in 1306, fighting alongside King Robert I - 'the Bruce' - (1274 - 1329) in the Wars of Independence a
2 CONC nd having considerable success in south-west Scotland against the English King Edward I (1239 - 1307). He was knighted by Robert at Bannockburn. He became known as the 'Black' Douglas, a name which carried on through one branch of
2 CONC  his family. He took Robert's heart for a posthumous crusade against the Moors in Spain, where he died.
