2 CONT ==Marjorie Bruce==
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marjorie_Bruce
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse	Walter Stewart, 6th High Steward of Scotland Issue: Robert II of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	Robert I of Scotland Mother	Isabella of Mar
2 CONT
2 CONT Born	1296
2 CONT
2 CONT Died	2 March 1316 (aged 19–20)
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial	Paisley Abbey
2 CONT
2 CONT Marjorie Bruce or Marjorie de Brus (1296 – 2 March 1316) was the eldest daughter of Robert the Bruce, King of Scots by his first wife, Isabella of Mar, and the founder of the Stewart dynasty. Her marriage to Walter, High Steward o
2 CONC f Scotland gave rise to the House of Stewart. Her son was the first Stewart monarch, King Robert II of Scotland. Her father remarried, after a certain period of time, Elizabeth de Burgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Early life===
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother, Isabella, a nineteen-year-old noblewoman from the Clan Mar, died soon after giving birth to her. Her father was then the Earl of Carrick. Marjorie was named after her father's mother, Marjorie, Countess of Carrick.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to legend, her parents had been very much in love, and Robert the Bruce did not remarry until Marjorie was six years old. In 1302, a courtier named Elizabeth de Burgh became her stepmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 March 1306, her father was crowned King of Scots at Scone, Perthshire, and Marjorie, then nine years old, became a Princess of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Imprisonment (1306–1314)===
2 CONT
2 CONT The Priory of Watton, where Marjorie was imprisoned under Edward I
2 CONT Three months after the coronation, in June, 1306, her father was defeated at the Battle of Methven. He sent his female relatives (his wife, two sisters and Marjorie) north with his supporter the Countess of Buchan, but by the end 
2 CONC of June the band of Bruce women were captured and betrayed to the English by the Earl of Ross.
2 CONT
2 CONT As punishment, Edward I sent his hostages to different places in England. Princess Marjorie went to the convent at Watton; her aunt Christina Bruce was sent to another convent; Queen Elizabeth was placed under house arrest at a ma
2 CONC nor house in Yorkshire (because Edward I needed the support of her father, the powerful Earl of Ulster, her punishment was lighter than the others'); and Marjorie's aunt Mary Bruce and the Countess of Buchan were imprisoned in woo
2 CONC den cages, exposed to public view, Mary's cage at Roxburgh Castle and Countess Isabella's at Berwick Castle. For the next four years, Marjorie, Elizabeth, Christina, Mary and Isabella endured solitary confinement, with daily publi
2 CONC c humiliation for the latter two. A cage was built for Marjorie at the Tower of London, but Edward I reconsidered and instead sent her to the convent.[1] Christopher Seton, Christina's husband, was executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward I died on 7 July 1307. He was succeeded by his son, Edward II, who subsequently held Marjorie captive in a convent for about seven more years. She was finally set free around 1314, possibly in exchange for English noblemen 
2 CONC captured after the Battle of Bannockburn (23 June – 24 June 1314).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage and death===
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Stewart, 6th High Steward of Scotland distinguished himself in the battle and was rewarded the hand of the adolescent princess. Her dowry included the Barony of Bathgate in West Lothian. The original site of Bathgate Castle
2 CONC , which was part of the dowry, can be found on the grounds of Bathgate Golf Club. The site is protected by the Historic Scotland organisation and the Club is debarred from carrying out any excavation work on the site without prior
2 CONC  permission. Every year on the first Saturday of June, the town of Bathgate celebrates the marriage of Marjorie and Walter in their annual historical pageant, just before the town's procession and Newland festival. Local school ch
2 CONC ildren are given the parts of Marjorie, Walter and other members of the court. After the pageant, everyone joins the procession along with Robert the Bruce on horseback.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later, on 2 March 1316, Marjorie was riding in Gallowhill, Paisley, Renfrewshire while heavily pregnant. Her horse was suddenly startled and threw her to the ground at a place called "The Knock." She went into premature 
2 CONC labour and delivered the child at Paisley Abbey, surviving the birth by a few hours at most.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was nineteen at the time of her death, like her mother, who was also nineteen years old when she died in childbirth.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the junction of Renfrew Road and Dundonald Road in Paisley, a cairn marks the spot near to where Marjorie reputedly fell from her horse. While the reputed place of her death is now referred to as Knockhill Road, with nearby roa
2 CONC ds of Bruce Way, and Marjorie Drive named in her honour. She is buried at Paisley Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son succeeded his childless uncle David II of Scotland in 1371 as King Robert II. Her descendants include the House of Stuart and all their successors on the throne of Scotland, England and the United Kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marjorie in fiction===
2 CONT
2 CONT The young adult novel Girl in a Cage by Jane Yolen and Robert J. Harris features Marjorie Bruce as its protagonist. In it, Marjorie is imprisoned in a cage. Although there is a preface stating that it is fictional, many have taken
2 CONC  it to be a true story.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References===
2 CONT
2 CONT # Scott, Ronald McNair, Robert the Bruce, p. 87
2 CONT
2 CONT ===External links===
2 CONT
2 CONT Bannockburn article (National Trust for Scotland) contains some information on Marjorie Bruce.
2 CONT Bathgate Castle and Old Hall Knowe
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Bruce, King of Scotland, was born the 11th of July 1274, and died June 7, 1329. He married first Isabella, eldest daughter of Donald, tenth Earl of Marr. Their daughter, Marjory, Princess Royal of Scotland, fell into the ha
2 CONC nds of the English 1306, and was detained a prisoner in charge of Henry Percy till 1314, when she was conducted to Scotland by Walter, the sixth high steward of Scotland, to whom she was married in 1315. She died in March, 1316. H
2 CONC er husband, Walter, born in 1294, brought a noble body of men to the aid of Bruce. In the battle of Bannockburn he and his cousin, Sir James Douglas, commanded the Third division. The same year he was appointed to receive, on the 
2 CONC borders, the Queen of King Robert, Marjory, his daughter, and other illustrious Scottish prisoners. On that occasion he formed an attachment for the Princess. He died April 9, 1326. "Had he lived," says an old writer, "he might ha
2 CONC ve equaled Randolph and Douglas; but his course of glory was short." The only child of the Princess Marjory was Robert Stuart, King of Scotland, born March 2, 1316.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  The Descent Of General Robert Edward Lee From Robert The Bruce, Of Scotland.  By Professor Wm. Winston Fontaine, of Louisville.  (From the Southern Historical Society Papers)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marjorie Bruce or Marjorie de Brus (December, 1296 – 2 March 1316) was the eldest daughter of Robert the Bruce, King of Scots by his first wife, Isabella of Mar, and the founder of the Stewart dynasty. Her marriage to Walter, High
2 CONC  Steward of Scotland gave rise to the House of Stewart. Her son was the first Stewart monarch, King Robert II of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source=
2 CONT http://www.patrickspeople.co.uk/ancestors%20of%20isabella%20gordon/2152.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The wife of Donald of the Isles, was Princess Margaret Stuart, granddaughter of King Robert the Bruce, through his daughter, the Princess Marjorie. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dundonald,_South_Ayrshire
2 CONT
2 CONT The village is mostly known for Dundonald Castle, which was built in the 14th century by king Robert II, on the ruins of a castle built earlier (in 1260 by his grandfather, Alexander Comyn. It served the Scottish kings for 150 yea
2 CONC rs
2 CONT
2 CONT In the late 13th century, Walter Stewart, the 6th High Steward of Scotland, who fought alongside Sir William Wallace and King Robert the Bruce in the Scottish Wars of Independence, was rewarded for his loyalty to King Robert the B
2 CONC ruce by a marriage with Princess Marjorie Bruce, the king's daughter. Walter and Marjorie had a son, Robert Stewart, who became Earl of Strathearn and Menteith.  Robert Stewart nearly didn't make it into this world. His mother, Pr
2 CONC incess Marjorie, was thrown from her horse while she was pregnant. She died from her injuries and Robert was born by an emergency Caesarean section. When Robert the Bruce's son, King David II, died without any male heir then Rober
2 CONC t Stewart, Earl of Strathearn and Menteith, was next in line for the throne.  He became King Robert II of Scotland, and founder of the Royal House of Stewart, which ruled Scotland for over three hundred years and in 1603 under Kin
2 CONC g James VI became the ruling dynasty of the combined thrones of the United Kingdom. King James VI of Scotland became known as King James I of the United Kingdom and was the patron of the King James Bible. The primary residence of 
2 CONC the early Scottish kings was Stirling Castle.  In the days of Robert the Bruce, Stirling castle would have been primarily a wooden structure.  It was in the time of King Robert II that a stone structure began to replace the earlie
2 CONC r wooden one.  The North Tower of Robert II's era still stands as part of the castle today.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert The BRUCE King Of Scots b: 11 JUL 1274 in Turnberry, Ayrshire, Scotland.  He married firstly to Isabella Of MAR b: 1276 in Aberdeenshire, Scotland.  
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following daughter: 
2 CONT # Has Children Marjorie BRUCE Princess Of Scots b: ABT 1297 in Scotland.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Marjorie was only child of Robert I "the Bruce" King of Scotland from his marriage to Isabella of Mar. Marjorie was held hostage in the Tower of London by King Edward the Longshanks in order to compel surrender from her father, Ro
2 CONC bert the Bruce. Marjorie was later freed and married Sir Walter Stewart who was a young knight and sixth family holder of the High Steward of Scotland. This marriage produced a son Robert Stewart who was to later become Robert II,
2 CONC  King of Scotland (1371-90). Robert's birth was considered something of a miracle since he was delivered by caesarean section.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Marjorie died from a fall from her horse.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Marjorie married in 1315 in Scotland to Walter STEWART 6th High Steward Of Scotland b: 1293 in Scotland.  
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following child: 
2 CONT # Has Children Robert STEWART King Robert II of Scots b: 02 MAR 1316 in Paisley, Renfrewshire, Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT King Robert The Bruce married secondly in 1302 to Elizabeth DE BURGH b: ABT 1280 in Ulster, Ireland.  
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children:
2 CONT #  Margaret BRUCE b: ABT 1317 in Scotland
2 CONT #  Matilda BRUCE b: ABT 1320 in Scotland
2 CONT #  David BRUCE, King of Scots b: 05 MAR 1324 in Dunfermline Palace, Fifeshire, Scotland.  David was the only surviving son of his father, King Robert "the Bruce". He was married to Princess Joan of England at the age of four(!) an
2 CONC d ascended to the throne of Scotland at age five. Thomas Randolph, earl of Moray, was appointed as his guardian. On Randolph's death, David became vulnerable to attack by Edward Balliol, who was being backed by Edward III. David a
2 CONC nd Joan fled for safety first to Dumbarton Castle and then to France, where they lived in exile for seven years. David led an invasion into Northumberland and captured Hexham, but was captured and taken prisoner by the English, wh
2 CONC ere he was imprisoned for eleven years. Meanwhile Robert the Steward (later King Robert II) ruled in his absence. David died without children and the throne passed to Robert the Steward.
2 CONT #  John BRUCE b: OCT 1327 in Scotland.  He died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marjorie Bruce or Marjorie de Brus (1296 – 2 March 1316) was the eldest daughter of Robert the Bruce, King of Scots by his first wife, Isabella of Mar, and the founder of the Stewart dynasty. Her marriage to Walter, High Steward o
2 CONC f Scotland gave rise to the House of Stewart. Her son was the first Stewart monarch, King Robert II of Scotland. Her father remarried, after a certain period of time, Elizabeth de Burgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother, Isabella, a nineteen-year-old noblewoman from the Clan Mar, died soon after giving birth to her. Her father was then the Earl of Carrick. Marjorie was named after her father's mother, Marjorie, Countess of Carrick.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to legend, her parents had been very much in love, and Robert the Bruce did not remarry until Marjorie was six years old. In 1302, a courtier named Elizabeth de Burgh became her stepmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 March 1306, her father was crowned King of Scots at Scone, Perthshire, and Marjorie, then nine years old, became a Princess of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Imprisonment (1306–1314) The Priory of Watton, where Marjorie was imprisoned under Edward I
2 CONT
2 CONT Three months after the coronation, in June, 1306, her father was defeated at the Battle of Methven. He sent his female relatives (his wife, two sisters and Marjorie) north with his supporter the Countess of Buchan, but by the end 
2 CONC of June the band of Bruce women were captured and betrayed to the English by the Earl of Ross.
2 CONT
2 CONT As punishment, Edward I sent his hostages to different places in England. Princess Marjorie went to the convent at Watton; her aunt Christina Bruce was sent to another convent; Queen Elizabeth was placed under house arrest at a ma
2 CONC nor house in Yorkshire (because Edward I needed the support of her father, the powerful Earl of Ulster, her punishment was lighter than the others'); and Marjorie's aunt Mary Bruce and the Countess of Buchan were imprisoned in woo
2 CONC den cages, exposed to public view, Mary's cage at Roxburgh Castle and Countess Isabella's at Berwick Castle. For the next four years, Marjorie, Elizabeth, Christina, Mary and Isabella endured solitary confinement, with daily publi
2 CONC c humiliation for the latter two. A cage was built for Marjorie at the Tower of London, but Edward I reconsidered and instead sent her to the convent.[1] Christopher Seton, Christina's husband, was executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward I died on 7 July 1307. He was succeeded by his son, Edward II, who subsequently held Marjorie captive in a convent for about seven more years. She was finally set free around 1314, possibly in exchange for English noblemen 
2 CONC captured after the Battle of Bannockburn (23 June – 24 June 1314). Marriage and death Paisley Abbey
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Stewart, 6th High Steward of Scotland distinguished himself in the battle and was rewarded the hand of the adolescent princess. Her dowry included the Barony of Bathgate in West Lothian. The original site of Bathgate Castle
2 CONC , which was part of the dowry, can be found on the grounds of Bathgate Golf Club. The site is protected by the Historic Scotland organisation and the Club is debarred from carrying out any excavation work on the site without prior
2 CONC  permission. Every year on the first Saturday of June, the town of Bathgate celebrates the marriage of Marjorie and Walter in their annual historical pageant, just before the town's procession and Newland festival. Local school ch
2 CONC ildren are given the parts of Marjorie, Walter and other members of the court. After the pageant, everyone joins the procession along with Robert the Bruce on horseback.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later, on 2 March 1316, Marjorie was riding in Gallowhill, Paisley, Renfrewshire while heavily pregnant. Her horse was suddenly startled and threw her to the ground at a place called "The Knock." She went into premature 
2 CONC labour and delivered the child at Paisley Abbey, surviving the birth by a few hours at most.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was nineteen at the time of her death, like her mother, who was also nineteen years old when she died in childbirth.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the junction of Renfrew Road and Dundonald Road in Paisley, a cairn marks the spot near to where Marjorie reputedly fell from her horse. While the reputed place of her death is now referred to as Knockhill Road, with nearby roa
2 CONC ds of Bruce Way, and Marjorie Drive named in her honour. She is buried at Paisley Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son succeeded his childless uncle David II of Scotland in 1371 as King Robert II. Her descendants include the House of Stuart and all their successors on the throne of Scotland, England and the United Kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.electricscotland.com/history/women/wih10.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marjorie_Bruce
2 CONT
