1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Douglas Richardson [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Plantagenet_Ancestry_A_Study_In_Colonial/kjme027UeagC?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=David%20Strathbogie Plantagenet Ancestry]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Strathbogie,_Earl_of_Atholl
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Strathbogie (c. 1266 - 7 November 1306) was warden and Justiciary of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years and family
2 CONT
2 CONT John was born in Atholl, Perthshire, Scotland around 1266. He was the son of David I Strathbogie, Earl of Atholl (d. 1270), by his spouse Isabel, daughter of Richard de Dover, Baron of Chilham, Kent. John de Strathbogie first appe
2 CONC ars on record as his father's son and heir in 1282. He was a great-great-grandson of King John of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life and military service
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1284, he joined with other Scottish noblemen who acknowledged Margaret of Norway as the heir to King Alexander. In 1296, he fought on the Scottish side at the Battle of Dunbar, where he was captured and sent to the Tower of Lon
2 CONC don. After a year's confinement there he was set free on condition that he served King Edward I of England in Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT He did homage for his manor of Lesnes, Kent, in 1305 but subsequently returned to Scotland, and in 1306 joined Robert the Bruce in his rebellion against English overlordship, and his English possessions were forfeited. He took par
2 CONC t in the coronation of The Bruce in that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Execution
2 CONT
2 CONT In the subsequent English invasion of Scotland in 1306, he was taken prisoner at the Battle of Methven. John, Earl of Atholl, was hanged in London, Middlesex, England on 7 November 1306, on a gallows 30 feet higher than ordinary. 
2 CONC This was to signify his higher status than his fellow prisoners, no earl had been executed in England for 230 years. His body was burnt and his head fixed on London Bridge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT John married Marjory (also known as Margaret), daughter of Donald, 6th Earl of Mar. They had two sons and a daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT David de Strathbogie, 10th Earl of Atholl (d. 28 December 1326)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John de Strathbogie, Knight.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel, wife or mistress of Edward de Brus, Earl of Carrick.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT   John of Strathbogie, 9th Earl of Atholl  was the son of David of Strathbogie, 8th Earl of Atholl and Isabel  of Chilham.1  He married Margaret (?), daughter of Donald, 6th Earl of Mar and Helen  ferch Llywelyn.1  He died on 7 No
2 CONC vember 1306 at London, England, executed, with his body burnt and his head fixed on London Bridge (and his titles forfeited).1
2 CONT
2 CONT      John of Strathbogie, 9th Earl of Atholl succeeded to the title of 9th Earl of Atholl [S., c. 1115] on 6 August 1270.1 On 5 February 1283/84 he was one of the nobles who acknowledged Margaret, Maid of Norway, as their Sovereig
2 CONC n.1 In 1292 he swore fealty to King Edward I.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Battle of Dunbar on 28 April 1296 at Dunbar, East Lothian, Scotland, fighting on the side of the Scots, who were defeated. He was taken prisoner to London.1 On 31 July 1297 he was ordered to be released from the T
2 CONC ower of London.1 On 27 March 1306 he assisted at the coronation of King Robert Bruce of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p10786.htm#i107856
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p388.htm#i11636
