1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.thepeerage.com/p10255.htm#i102543
2 CONT ----
2 CONT From Stirnet.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Wake, 1st Lord of Liddell (b 1268, d before 10.04.1300)
2 CONT
2 CONT   	TCP is not clear about the identity of his wife other than to name her as Joan. It indicates that she may have been a daughter of either John FitzBernard of Kingsdown or William de Fenes, a Count of Spain. BE1883 does not name 
2 CONC his wife at all. The following marriage is suggested by 'Tudor' and 'Mathematical' and is being followed on a provisional basis only and may not be secure.
2 CONT
2 CONT   	m. (before 24.09.1291) Joan de Fiennes (dau of William, Baron of Fiennes)
2 CONT
2 CONT   	(i) 	Thomas Wake, 2nd Lord of Liddell (b c20.03.1297/8, dsp 30/1.05.1349)
2 CONT
2 CONT   	m. (before 09.10.1316) Blanche Plantagenet (b 1305, d before 12.07.1380, dau of Henry Plantagenet, 3rd Earl of Lancaster)
2 CONT
2 CONT   	(ii) 	John Wake (a 06.1320, dsp before 1349)
2 CONT
2 CONT   	(iii) 	Margaret Wake 'of Liddell' (b c1299, d 29.09.1349) 	
2 CONT
2 CONT HJY
2 CONT
2 CONT   	m1. John Comyn, Lord of Badenoch (dsps Bannockburn 24.06.1314)
2 CONT
2 CONT   	m2. (1325) Edmund Plantagenet, 1st Earl of Kent, Earl of Arundel (b 05.08.1301, d 19.03.1330) 	
2 CONT
2 CONT HJY
2 CONT
2 CONT   	Either Margaret married again (as follows) or it was a sister (Elizabeth) who married ...
2 CONT
2 CONT   	m(3). Sir Eustace Dampredicourt
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT John Wake, 1st Baron Wake of Liddell
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p10255.htm#i102543
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.celtic-casimir.com/webtree/7/6965.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT John Wake, 1st Lord (Baron), so created by writ of summons to Parliament 24 June 1295; campaigned in Gascony between 1288 and 1297 and against the Scots 1297-1300, Jt Captain of March of Scotland in Cumberland and Westmorland 1297
2 CONC , fought at Battle of Falkirk 1298; married by 24 Sep 1291 Joan (died just prior to 26 Oct 1309), allegedly daughter of Sir John FitzBernard, of Kingsdown, Kent or William de Fenes, a Spanish Count, and possibly a relative of Edwa
2 CONC rd I, and died just prior to 10 April 1300. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Wake, 2nd Baron Wake of Liddell (1297 – 31 May 1349), English baron, belonged to a Lincolnshire family which had lands also in Cumberland, being the son of John Wake (died 1300), who was summoned to parliament as a baron in
2 CONC  1295, and the grandson of Baldwin Wake (died 1282), both warriors of repute.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among Thomas Wake's guardians were Piers Gaveston and Henry, Earl of Lancaster, whose daughter Blanche (d. 1380) he married before 1317. This lady was the niece of Thomas, Earl of Lancaster, and her husband was thus attached to th
2 CONC e Lancastrian party, but he did not follow Earl Thomas in the proceedings which led to his death in 1322. Hating the favourites of Edward II Wake joined Queen Isabella in 1326 and was a member of the small council which advised th
2 CONC e young king, Edward III; soon, however, he broke away from the queen and her ally, Roger Mortimer, and in conjunction with his father-in-law, now earl of Lancaster, he joined the malcontent barons.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was possibly implicated in the plot which cost his brother-in-law, Edmund of Woodstock, 1st Earl of Kent, his life in 1330, and he fled to France, returning to England after the overthrow of Isabella and Mortimer. Edward III ma
2 CONC de him governor of the Channel Islands and he assisted Edward Bruce to invade Scotland, being afterwards sent on an errand to France. In 1341 he incurred the displeasure of the king and was imprisoned, but he had been restored and
2 CONC  had been employed in Brittany and elsewhere when he died childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT His estates passed to his sister Margaret (d. 1349), widow of Edmund, Earl of Kent, and her son John, 3rd Earl of Kent (d. 1352), and later to the Roland family. Wake founded a monastery Haltemprice Priory for the Austin canons at
2 CONC  Newton near Cottingham, East Riding of Yorkshire where he is buried.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References
2 CONT
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
2 CONT
