1 OCCU 2nd Baron Braose, Lord Braose of Gower, Lord of Gower and Lord of Bramber
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =William de Breuse (Braose), 2nd Lord Brewose=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''son of Sir William de Breuse, 1st Lord Brewose, and Aline de Multon''' '''[Note: MedLands has changed its mind about this and now assigns all four children to Mary de Ros.]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3.htm#JohnBriouseMMargaretB
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir WILLIAM de Breuse''' (-before 1 May 1326).  '''Lord Brewose'''.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly ---.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly '''ELIZABETH de Sully''', daughter of Sir RAYMUND de Sully of Sully, co. Glamorgan & his wife --- ([1295/96]-before 24 Aug 1328).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William & his first wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          '''ALINE de Breuse''' (-before 20 Jul 1331).  A manuscript record of the Mowbray family states that “Johannes filius [Roger]” married “Elianoram filiam domini Willielmi de Brewes in Wallia”[1291].  m firstly (Swansea 1
2 CONC 298) JOHN de Mowbray, son of ROGER de Mowbray Lord Mowbray & his wife Rohese de Clare (4 Sep 1286-hanged York 23 Mar 1322).  m secondly Sir RICHARD de Peshale .
2 CONT
2 CONT ii)         '''JOAN de Breuse''' (-before 23 Jun 1324).  m firstly (1301 or before) JAMES de Bohun, son of --- (-1306).  m secondly Sir RICHARD Foliot of Gressenhall, Norfolk, son of ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =<nowiki>-------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT Main residence was Oystermouth Castle in Gower
2 CONT
2 CONT Led siege of Emlyn in 1288, near Cardigan
2 CONT
2 CONT 1293 Fought in Gascony
2 CONT
2 CONT Three children, two of which predeceased him
2 CONT
2 CONT Second wife was Elizabeth de Sully
2 CONT
2 CONT 1297 won Gower for his service to king in Flanders
2 CONT
2 CONT 1300 Fought in Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT 1300 Summoned to Parliament
2 CONT
2 CONT Fought in Welsh wars beginning in 1307
2 CONT
2 CONT Gower seized by Edward II in 1320, leading William to join barons revolt
2 CONT
2 CONT Lost in Battle of Boroughbridge, and lost all holdings
2 CONT
2 CONT *____________________
2 CONT *'''William de Braose, (sometimes William de Briouze, William de Breuse, William de Brewes or William de Brewose; c. 1260–1326) was the second Baron Braose, as well as Lord of Gower and Lord of Bramber. He was held as a hostage af
2 CONC ter being captured in 1264 during the Second Barons' War and records of some of his childhood expenses survive from his time as a hostage. He first entered royal service in 1286 and, in 1291, he succeeded his father as baron. He c
2 CONC ontinued in royal military service, serving in Scotland as well as in Wales. Protracted disputes over his lands embroiled him throughout his life and at the end of his life helped spark a revolt against King Edward II of England's
2 CONC  favourites, the Despensers. He married twice, and his heirs were his daughter Aline and his grandson John de Bohun.
2 CONT *'''Braose was the son of William de Braose, 1st Baron Braose and his first wife, Aline, daughter of Thomas de Multon.[1] He was likely born around 1260, as his age was given as about 46 in 1307. Other events prove that he was bor
2 CONC n prior to 1264, as he was captured in that year. This came about during the Second Barons' War (1264–1267) during the reign of King Henry III of England, as the elder Braose had sided with the king during Simon de Montfort's rebe
2 CONC llion. The younger Braose was a hostage in the custody of Montfort's wife, Eleanor. Her household accounts include expenses related to the younger William's care.[2]
2 CONT *'''Sometime around 1285, Braose confirmed grants of land by his ancestors to the religious house of Sele Priory.[3][a] In 1286 Braose was in the king's service, for unspecified duties overseas. It is possible that these included 
2 CONC accompanying the king, Edward I, to Paris where Edward performed homage to the new French king, Philip IV, for Edward's French lands.[2] Braose played a significant role in King Edward's Welsh wars. In the winter of 1287–8 he comm
2 CONC anded the force blockading Emlyn castle. His men also provided the escort for the transport of a huge siege engine from Dryslwyn to Emlyn. The arrival of the engine, with 480 great stones as ammunition, persuaded the defenders of 
2 CONC the castle to surrender peaceably.[5]
2 CONT *'''The younger Braose succeeded his father before 1 March 1291, when he did homage for his father's lands.[1] He received custody of his father's lands on 2 March 1291, which had been placed into the custody of Robert de Tibetot 
2 CONC on 12 January 1291.[6] He was summoned a number of times to Parliament from 1291 until 1322 as Baron Braose. He was the second Baron Braose, as well as Lord of Gower and Lord of Bramber.[1]
2 CONT *'''After his father's death, Braose continued to serve Edward. He contributed both money and personal military service in Edward's wars in Wales, Scotland, and France.[2] He saw service in Gascony in 1294.[3] In 1297 he took part
2 CONC  in a military campaign in Flanders. As a reward for his service in Flanders, he received the wardship of John de Mowbray, who Braose eventually married to his daughter Aline.[2] From 1298 to 1306 he was involved in the Scottish w
2 CONC ars, and was at the Battle of Falkirk on 22 July 1298.[3] Besides the military service, he served the king in 1301 by signing a letter from the leading barons of England to Pope Boniface VIII in which the barons decried papal inte
2 CONC rference in the royal rights of England.[2]
2 CONT *'''Braose captured the Welsh rebel William Cragh in 1290, whose miraculous resurrection after being hanged was attributed to Thomas de Cantilupe.[7] This led in 1307 to Braose giving testimony to papal commissioners inquiring int
2 CONC o the events surrounding Cragh's hanging and whether or not it would support the canonisation of Cantilupe.[8]
2 CONT *'''It was most likely Braose who commissioned a condensed copy of Domesday Book, now Public Record Office manuscript E164/1. This copy has a marginal notation of "Br" next to the estates owned by Braose's ancestor, the first Will
2 CONC iam de Braose.[9]
2 CONT *'''Braose was embroiled in a dispute over his lordship of Gower in 1299 when the Bishop of Llandaff, John de Monmouth, brought a case against Braose to the king. Although the case was adjudicated in 1302, the resulting decision w
2 CONC as overturned. In 1304 Braose secured King Edward's confirmation of earlier grants and charters granting Braose special rights and liberties in Gower. He managed this because he was serving the king in Scotland at the time, and th
2 CONC us had easy access to the king. In 1305, however, Braose miscalculated and insulted a royal judge,[10] using "gross and contumelious words" to describe the royal official.[11] This episode caused the case of Gower to be reopened i
2 CONC n 1306, and Braose was only able to settle the issue again by the grant of rights to his men in Swansea and Gower.[10]
2 CONT *'''In 1320 King Edward II of England confiscated the lordship of Gower on the grounds that Braose had given it to his son-in-law Mowbray without royal permission. Over the preceding years Braose had promised Gower to a number of 
2 CONC persons,[12] including Humphrey de Bohun, the Earl of Hereford, Hugh Despenser the Younger, and Roger Mortimer of Wigmore. Mowbray then in late 1319 took custody of Gower to protect his rights. Despenser persuaded the king in 1320
2 CONC  to take Gower into royal hands in October, and was appointed keeper of the honour in November.[13] The other lords in the Welsh Marches resented this seizure, feeling that the king's excuse for it was not applicable. The seizure 
2 CONC was one of the precipitating causes of the baronial rebellion that led to the exile of the Despensers in 1321.[12] In 1322 Gower was given to the younger Despenser again, who then traded it for the honours of Usk and Caerleon. Bra
2 CONC ose was then induced to sue the new holder of Gower for the return of the barony in April 1324, which action succeeded in June 1324. Braose then promptly gave Gower to the elder Despenser, returning the property to the Despenser f
2 CONC amily once more.[14] The lordship of Gower eventually ended up in the hands of the Beauchamp family, but it was not until the 1350s that the issue was decided.[15]
2 CONT *'''The name of Braose's first wife was Agnes,[16] but her family is not known. His second wife was Elizabeth, the daughter and heiress of Raymund de Sully. He had two daughters with his first wife, but no children with his second
2 CONC  wife, who outlived him.[1] It appears that there was a son named William, who was the subject of a military summons from King Edward in 1311, but nothing further is mentioned of him after 1315. In 1316 a settlement of William the
2 CONC  father's estates made no mention of this son making it likely that the son died before this date.[17]
2 CONT *'''Braose died not long before 1 May 1326[1] and his heirs were his daughter Aline and his grandson John de Bohun.[18] Aline, the elder daughter,[13] married John de Mowbray and Richard de Peschale. The second daughter, Joan, mar
2 CONC ried James de Bohun and Richard Foliot. Mowbray received the lands of Gower and Bramber before Braose's death.[1]
2 CONT *'''Braose was known as a man often in debt and as being unable to manage his cash flow well.[17] Thomas Walsingham stated in his chronicle that Braose was "very rich by descent but a dissipater of the property left to him".[19]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Braose,_2nd_Baron_Braose
2 CONT *_____________________________
2 CONT *'''Sir William de Breuse, 2nd Lord Brewes1
2 CONT *'''M, #151692, d. circa 1 May 1326
2 CONT *Last Edited=30 Apr 2008
2 CONT *'''     Sir William de Breuse, 2nd Lord Brewes was the son of Sir William de Breuse, 1st Lord Brewes and Aline de Multon.1 He married, firstly, Agnes (?) before 1298.2 He married, secondly, Elizabeth de Sully, daughter of Sir Ray
2 CONC mund de Sully, before 24 April 1317.2 He died circa 1 May 1326.1
2 CONT *'''     He succeeded to the title of 2nd Lord Brewes [E., 1290] circa 6 January 1290/91.1
2 CONT *'''Children of Sir William de Breuse, 2nd Lord Brewes and Agnes (?)
