1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Wikitree: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Camoys-8 Margaret (Braose) de Braose (abt. 1285–bef. 1319)]
2 CONT * "Margaret de Brewes, daughter of William de Brewes and Mary Ros, married Ralph de Camoys, Knt." – Richardson. ''Magna Carta Ancestry: A Study in Colonial and Medieval Families'', 2nd ed., Vol. 1, pp. 313-325 BREWES.
2 CONT * '''Research Notes'''
2 CONT * This profile was created as daughter of William's first wife, Aline de Multon, with birth/death as 1250/1359,[2] but their only known child was William (born c1261). Margaret was the daughter of his third wife, Mary Ros. William
2 CONC  and Mary were married "in or before 1271",[citing source above] so Margaret's birth year was changed to "after 1271".
2 CONT * The profile for her husband, Ralph de Camoys, has a birth year of 1283, so Margaret's birth year was changed to "about 1285". Source citation for [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Camoys-8 Ralph's profile] notes that he remarried "
2 CONC before 1319", so Margaret had died before then:
2 CONT ** "The following is a post by Douglas Richardson on soc.genealogy.medieval with regard to the second wife of Ralph de Camoys.
2 CONT ** "Sir Ralph de Camoys is known to have married twice. He married (1st) in 1303 to Margaret de Brewes, daughter of William de Brewes, Knt., of Bramber, Sussex, by his 3rd wife, Mary, daughter of Robert de Roos, Knt. He had one so
2 CONC n, Thomas, Knt., by this marriage. Margaret was living in 1304-1305, when she and Roger conveyed the manor of Little Bookham, Surrey to her mother, Mary de Brewes [Reference: List of Inquisitions ad Quod Damnum, Part I (Lists and 
2 CONC Indexes, No. XVII): 83]. She was likely still living in 1307, when Ralph was summoned "with his consort" to attend the Coronation of King Edward II. Following Margaret's death, Sir Ralph de Camoys married (2nd) sometime before 131
2 CONC 9 a certain Elizabeth,...."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret is first recorded in the 1280 fine between her father and Amicia de Rivers concerning the descent of Chesworth, Grinstead and Bidlington in Sussex. She is noted again, living with her parents in Gower in 1290 when she pro
2 CONC vides the thread for "measuring" the corpse of William Cragh. This was part of the ritual which invoked the help of St Thomas de Cantilupe in restoring life to this hanged man. (The full story of this meticulously documented medie
2 CONC val miracle is told in The Hanged Man by Robert Bartlett.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret was married to Ralph de Camoys in 1303. Her mother granted Little Bookham, Surrey, to them. Ralph granted Flockthorpe, Norfolk to Margaret. Ralph and Margaret made a life grant of Little Bookham to her mother, Mary, in 13
2 CONC 06 with the service of rendering a rose each year. In 1307, Ralph and Margaret were summoned to attend the coronation of Edward II in the train of the King and Queen. Between 1308 and 1310 Ralph performed military service against 
2 CONC the Scots. Ralph married his second wife, Elizabeth, in 1319 and later became constable of Windsor Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Courtesy of Virgin Media
