2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Giffard'''
2 CONT
2 CONT From From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Giffard_(died_1299)
2 CONT
2 CONT John Giffard, Baron Giffard of Brimsfield (1232–1299) was an English nobleman prominent in the Second Barons' War and in Wales. His initial gift of land in Oxford led to the foundation of Gloucester College, Oxford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Involvement in military actions
2 CONT Giffard was active in the campaigns against Llywelyn ap Gruffudd in 1257–1258 and 1260–1261. In 1263, with others of Simon de Montfort's party,[1] Giffard besieged the sheriff of Gloucester in Gloucester Castle. Also in that year,
2 CONC  with others, he abducted Peter of Aigueblanche, the Bishop of Hereford, confining him to Eardisley Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1264 Giffard controlled Kenilworth Castle, and successfully attacked Warwick Castle, occupied by William Maudit, 8th Earl of Warwick. Captured at the Battle of Lewes, he changed sides, and fought for Henry III at the Battle of 
2 CONC Evesham in 1265. After Lewes, Giffard joined the retinue of Gilbert de Clare, earl of Gloucester and Hertford, and his association with the powerful Marcher Lord was rewarding in money and property.
2 CONT
2 CONT Giffard was subsequently a staunch king's man, for Henry and Edward I. He fought at the decisive Battle of Orewin Bridge (1282).[2] Edward granted him Welsh castles, including Carreg Cennen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Giffard was born on 19 January 1232, the son of Elias Giffard IV of Brimpsfield, Gloucestershire and his second wife, Alice, sister of John Mautravers, of Lytchett Matravers, Dorset.
2 CONT
2 CONT Giffard married, firstly, Maud de Clifford, daughter of Walter de Clifford, of Clifford, Hertfordshire, and widow of William III Longespée. He caused a scandal, for which he was heavily fined, in 1271 when he abducted her while ne
2 CONC gotiations for the marriage were in progress. Their daughter, Katherine, married Nicholas Audley (1258–1299), son of Ela Longespée and James de Audley. Their daughter Eleanor married Fulk le Strange, 1st Baron Strange of Blackmere
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Giffard married secondly, in 1286, Margaret, widow of John de Neville, of Hallingbury, Essex.[3] Margaret was a granddaughter of Ralph Belet and Sibyl de Cormeilles. Their son John (1287–1322) was executed by Edward II as a rebel,
2 CONC  and Brimpsfield Castle was destroyed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Giffard died at his house at Boyton, Wiltshire, on 29 May 1299 and was buried at Malmesbury Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT # Including Roger de Clifford[disambiguation needed].[1]
2 CONT # Orewin Bridge and the Fall of Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References===
2 CONT
2 CONT Concise Dictionary of National Biography
2 CONT Complete Peerage, Volume 5, pp. 639-44
2 CONT
2 CONT =<nowiki>--------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT
2 CONT John Giffard, then in his 17th year (born 1232) during whose minority the Queen had a grant of his lands, towards the maintenance of Prince Edward. (Afterwards Edward I.) In 41st of Henry III, this John Giffard was commanded to be
2 CONC  at Bristol with horse and arms, thence to march into South Wales against Llewellyn ap Griffith. In six years subsequently he was constituted Governor of St. Brianels Castle, and warden of the forest of Dean in Gloucester; but soo
2 CONC n after taking part with the rebellious barons (these rebellions continued long into the reign of Henry III, son of King John) was among those whom the Archbishop of Canterbury ordered to be excommunicated. He was at the battle of
2 CONC  Lewes, under the baronial banner, but adopting a different course at Evesham, he obtained pardon for his former treason, in consideration of the services which he then rendered to the royal cause. In the 55th of Henry III, 1271, 
2 CONC Maud de Longspee, widow of William de Longspee (see Longspee line below), daughter and heiress of Walter Clifford, having by letter complained to the king, that this John Giffard had taken her by force from her manor house, and ca
2 CONC rried her to his Castle of Brimsfield, where he kept her in restraint: he was summoned before the king, when denying the charge, but confessing his marriage with the lady without royal license, he made his peace by paying a fine o
2 CONC f 300 marks. In 10th of Edward I, 1282, John Giffard was in the expedition made by Gilbert de Clare, Earl of Gloucester, against Llewellyn, ap Griffith, Prince of North Wales; and observing that Llewllyn had separated, with a smal
2 CONC l party, from the body of the army, he joined Edward Mortimer, and slaying the Prince, despatched his head to the king, who caused it to be set upon the Tower of London, crowned with ivy. In the 18th year of the same reign, 1290, 
2 CONC he was constituted Governor of Dynevor Castle in Wales, and having been summoned to Parliament as a baron from June 24, 1295, to April 10, 1299, he died in the latter year and was buried at Malmesbury. He married 1st Maud Clifford
2 CONC  de Longspee, widow of Wm. de Longspee, and had two daughters, Catherine and Alinore. He married 2nd Alicia de Mautravers, who d. s. p., and 3rd, Margaret Neville, and had an only son, John, who succeeded him in 1299, who was hang
2 CONC ed for high treason at Gloucester 1322, when the barony fell under the attainder, but was revived and may now be considered to be vested in the descendants and representatives of his half sisters Catherine and Eleanor 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT Moriarty in NEHGR 75:129 discoverd fine of John Giffard le Boef ol Twyfor, in the time of Edward I, in which John is called 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was knight of the shire for co. Bucks, in a Parliament at Westminster -- one of the earliest Parliaments.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the role of arms, time of Edward I, arms of John Giffard le Boef are given as Gules, three lions pasaant in pale argetn and a label for difference
2 CONT
