2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3D-K.htm#_Toc389046296
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         JOHN Giffard .  Lord Giffard of Brimpsfield.  m firstly MATILDA de Clifford, daughter of ---.  John & his wife had children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         CATHERINE Giffard (1272-after 1322).  The Book of Lacock names Catharinam filiam Johannis Giffard as wife of Nichum de Audele, son of Jacobus de Audele 1248].  She became a nun at Ledbury[1249].  m (1299 or before) NICH
2 CONC OLAS de Audley, son of JAMES de Audley of Heleigh, Staffordshire & his wife Ela Longespee (before 1258-28 Aug 1299). 
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===biographical notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.medievalhistories.com/myths-of-medieval-anchoritism-and-the-reclusion-of-katharine-de-audley/
2 CONT
2 CONT >Katharine de Audley, a wealthy Marcher heiress, was the daughter of a baron, John Giffard, who had been a leading campaigner against the Welsh 1264 5. In 1299 her husband Nicholas of Audley died and she withdrew to an anchorhold 
2 CONC at Ledbury. In this way she avoided to be forcefully married to another Marcher lord or  which was customary to Welsh lords. Such dynastic exchanges of women were often part of the prevalent colonial strategy of forceful encultura
2 CONC tion or assimilation of the Welsh into the world of the Marcher Lords.
2 CONT
2 CONT >However, Katharine of Audley, avoided this fate by refusing to remarry. Instead she presumably spent the next decade raising her three children and not least securing their wealth and inheritance. However, in 1308 her eldest son 
2 CONC died and after that a series of grants and entries in the Close Rolls testify to her attempts to secure the future of her daughter and second son. Finally in 1313 her inheritance was as witnessed by a series of senior clerics secu
2 CONC red for her children; at the same time she was ensured a substantial sum for her life as a future anchorite in Ledbury, whereto she according to legend was drawn  by the peeling of the bells of the church of St. Martin and All sai
2 CONC nts. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT >Here she lived the life of a living saint, the so-called death-within-life in a liminal sacred space, the anchorhold, which may still be seen at the North of the Church. Unfortunately the 100 pounds she was due each year graduall
2 CONC y dried up, while her children died and shifting managers either mismanaged her estates or simply defrauded the woman. In the end, we hear about her on two final occasions: first when she inherits responsibility for an orphaned gr
2 CONC andson, in spite of her enclosure; the second probably a last attempt to win back her lost inheritance, whether for the child or for her own future sustenance. Probably she died around 1326  27.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://ldysinger.stjohnsem.edu/@texts2/1230_ancren_rule/01_introd-anchor.htm from [http://www.historyfish.net/anchorites/clay_anchorites_seven.html Rotha Mary Clay's The Hermits and Anchorites of England] page 75 in chapter s
2 CONC even 
2 CONT
2 CONT >No more is heard of the Lady Audley until 1312, when she gave away into lay hands a portion of her maternal inheritance. Since the deed is witnessed at Ledbury by the bishop and the vicar, it may be presumed that she had already 
2 CONC taken up her abode there, or was about to be enclosed by the bishopÂ  In 1323, Katherine de Audele, recluse of Ledbury, was receiving 30 pounds a year through the sheriff, and as the sum was paid out of lands which were in the cus
2 CONC tody of her husband executor, it seems probable that she had made some arrangement about her property in order to obtain a pension
2 CONT
2 CONT >Around these prosaic facts the following poetic legend grew up. In obedience to a vision which bade her not to rest until she came to a town where the bells should ring untouched by man, Katherine and her maid Mabel wandered from
2 CONC  place to place, following out of Worcestershire into Herefordshire the hoof-marks of the lady's mare which had been stolen prints still shown in the sandstone at Whelpley Brook The expected miracle was manifested at Ledbury, and 
2 CONC there, it may be under the shadow of the bell-tower, the Lady Katherine determined to remain.  The story is familiar through Wordsworth's sonnet ....
2 CONT
2 CONT >The title Saint Catherine of Ledbury is a late addition to the tale, suggested, doubtless, by the dedication-name of the hospital of St. Katharine, a house founded years before the birth of Katherine de Audley 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igmpostem.cgi?op=show&app=jweber&notify=GNxuisph2u6HE7oK3j8mo6-KejFRdqwL&key=I01094&return= postem]
2 CONT
2 CONT >John Giffard m2 Margaret, wdw of Sir John de Neville of Hallingbury etc., [no parents given but] to whom he appears to be related as papal dispensation was applied for, being related in the 3rd-4th degree. The bishop found there 
2 CONC was no impediment to the marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >By this m2 he left '''John s&h''', and his heirs were his four half sisters (therefore by Maud de Clifford) were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1)'''Katherine''' m Sir Nicholas Daudeleye who seems to be son of James d'Audley/Alditheley by Ela dau of William Longespee II  
2 CONT * 2)Alianore m Sir Fulk Lestraunge of Whitchurch (Lord Lestrange)  
2 CONT * 3)Maud m William son of Geoffrey de Joinville/Geneville, dsp  
2 CONT * 4)Elizabeth dvp 
2 CONT
