2 CONT >>>>a)         [MARY .  The editor of Monumenta de Insula Manniæ shows Mary as daughter of King Ragnall, brother of King Magnus, and his wife “Mary, daughter of Alexander de Ergadia Lord of Lorn”, adding that King Ragnall’s widow 
2 CONC “afterwards became the second wife of Malise earl of Strathearn”.  He also shows the marriage of the daughter Mary to “John de Waldeboef” by whom she had “William Waldeboef”.  He cites no sources for this information[1358].  Camde
2 CONC n’s Britannia (written in 1607) records that “Mary daughter of Reginald king of Man…preferred a suit for the island to [John King of England]”, which is clearly garbled as King John died in 1216[1359].  The same source adds that “
2 CONC her son’s son John Waldebeof…prosecuted his grandmother’s claim in parliament 33 Edward the first” unsuccessfully and that “William de Montacute his kinsman, descended from the kings of Man, did by arms” but mortgaged the island t
2 CONC o “Anthony Bec bishop of Durham and patriarch of Jerusalem”.  According to the Complete Peerage, the fourth (not second) wife of Malise Earl of Strathearn was the widow of Magnus (not Ragnall) King of Man[1360].  Unfortunately it 
2 CONC cites no sources either.  However, if the Complete Peerage is correct in its listing of Earl Malise’s four wives, it would be chronologically consistent for his wife Mary to have been the widow of Magnus rather than Ragnall.  Unti
2 CONC l more information comes to light, it is considered more appropriate to show Mary as the possible daughter of King Magnus than of King Ragnall.  m JOHN de Waldeboef, son of ---.] 
