2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Somerled-King-of-the-Isles/6000000002043212109 SOMERLED] (-killed in battle 1164).  Balfour Paul says that the first reference to Somerled records him as living in the district of Morven with his fathe
2 CONC r Gillebride, but he does not cite the precise source[1019].  Lord of Argyll.  He acquired control of the Western Isles and assumed the title "King of the Isles".  The Chronicle of Melrose records that Somerled Lord of Argyll land
2 CONC ed at Renfrew in 1164, after 12 years of rebellion against Malcolm IV King of Scotland, with a large army from Ireland but was defeated and killed with his son[1020].  The Extracta ex Cronicis Scocie records that "Sumerlendus regu
2 CONC lus Ergadie" rebelled against King Malcolm for 12 years, landed at Renfrew from Ireland, and was killed "et filium suum Gillecolanem", among passages dealing with events in 1164[1021].  The Annals of Ulster record that "Somharlidh
2 CONC  Mac Gilla-Adhamhnain and his son were killed" in 1164[1022].  The Book of Clanranald records that that no battle was fought and that Somerled was killed in his tent, after which his followers dispersed[1023].  '''m  [https://www.
2 CONC geni.com/people/Ragnhildr-%C3%93l%C3%A1fsd%C3%B3ttir-of-Man/6000000019032360275?through=6000000002043212109 --- of Man], illegitimate daughter of OLAV King of Man & his mistress ---.'''  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records t
2 CONC hat “Olavus filius Godredi Crovan” had many concubines by whom he fathered “filios tres…Reignaldum, Lagmannum et Haraldum et filias multas”, adding that one daughter married “Sumerledo regulo Herergaildel”[1024].  Balfour Paul nam
2 CONC es her "Ragnhildis" but he does not cite the corresponding primary source[1025].  Somerled & his wife had five children: 
2 CONT >*a)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Dougal-MacRory-1st-of-Dunollie-and-of-Lorn/6000000005398287081 DUGALD] (-after 1175).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names “Dulgallum, Reginaldum, Engus et Olavum” as the four sons of 
2 CONC “Sumerledo regulo Herergaildel” and his wife the daughter of Olav King of Man[1026]. 
2 CONT >*b)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Ragnall-mac-Somhairle-Lord-of-the-Isles/6000000001745043401?through=6000000002043212109 REGINALD] .  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names “Dulgallum, Reginaldum, Engus et Olavum” as the
2 CONC  four sons of “Sumerledo regulo Herergaildel” and his wife the daughter of Olav King of Man[1027].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Engus, son of Somerled, defeated his brother Reginald in 1192[1028]. 
2 CONT >*c)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Angus-of-Bute-Arran/6000000003078496086?through=6000000002043212109 ANGUS] (-killed 1210).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names “Dulgallum, Reginaldum, Engus et Olavum” as the four son
2 CONC s of “Sumerledo regulo Herergaildel” and his wife the daughter of Olav King of Man[1029].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Engus, son of Somerled, defeated his brother Reginald in 1192, adding in a later passage tha
2 CONC t Engus was killed in 1210[1030].  Balfour Paul states that Angus inherited "Bute, with a part of Arran, and the Rough Bounds (Garmoran) extending from Ardnamurchan to Gleneig"[1031].  m ---.  The name of Angus’s wife is not known
2 CONC .  Angus & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT >*d)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Olav/6000000158790979880 OLAV] .  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names “Dulgallum, Reginaldum, Engus et Olavum” as the four sons of “Sumerledo regulo Herergaildel” and his wife the daug
2 CONC hter of Olav King of Man[1035]. 
2 CONT >*e)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Gilliecolum-mac-Somairle/6000000016832419050?through=6000000002043212109 GILLECOLAN] (-killed in battle 1164).  The Chronicle of Melrose records that he was killed in battle with his fathe
2 CONC r[1036].  The Extracta ex Cronicis Scocie records that "Sumerlendus regulus Ergadie" rebelled against King Malcolm for 12 years, landed at Renfrew from Ireland, and was killed "et filium suum Gillecolanem", among passages dealing 
2 CONC with events in 1164[1037]. 
2 CONT >*f)          [https://www.geni.com/people/Gall-mac-Somairle/6000000000436323738?through=6000000002043212109 GALL Macsgillin] .  Balfour Paul names "Olave and Gall Macsgillin" as two other possible sons of Somerled, but does not c
2 CONC ite the corresponding primary source[1038].  Olav is named in the Chronicon Manniæ (see above) but not Gall.] 
2 CONT >*g)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Bethoc-Prioress-of-Iona/6000000003646023627?through=6000000002043212109 BEATRICE] .  Balfour Paul names "Beatrice prioress of Iona" as the daughter of Somerled, but does not cite the corre
2 CONC sponding primary source[1039].  Olav is named in the Chronicon Manniæ (see above) but not Gall.] 
