2 CONT
2 CONT The Kings of the Isle of Man
2 CONT
2 CONT compiled by Stewart Baldwin
2 CONT
2 CONT Table 4:  The kings of Chronicon Regum Manniae prior to Godred Crovan
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of the Kings of Man (Chronicon Regum Manniae, abbreviated "CRM") is the principle native source for the history of the kings of Man. It gives two kings of Man before Godred Crovan, Godred [called "ii" here] and his s
2 CONC on Fingal.  It names an otherwise unknown Sitric as the father of Godred ii, but if that name is wrong, it is possible that he was the same person as Goffraidh son of Amlaibh (Olaf) son of Ragnall, the king of Dublin who died in 1
2 CONC 075 (see the appendix below).  AU records the death at Man of a certain Sitric son of Amlaib in the year 1073, but he seems too late to identify with the father of Godred.
2 CONT
2 CONT                  Sitric(?)
2 CONT
2 CONT                     |
2 CONT
2 CONT                  Godred ii, d. ca 1070/5
2 CONT
2 CONT                  king of Man
2 CONT
2 CONT                     |
2 CONT
2 CONT                  Fingal, king of Man
2 CONT
2 CONT                  dethroned by Godred Crovan
2 CONT
2 CONT (.gif version of Table 4)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ancestry of Godred Crovan
2 CONT
2 CONT The ancestry of Godred Crovan (d. 1095), king of Dublin and Man, is not well documented, and there are differing opinions regarding his parentage and immediate ancestors.  Rather than try to give a definitive solution to the probl
2 CONC em, the basic evidence will be outlined, and several possible alternatives will be given, along with their strengths and weaknesses.  Since the basic raw data is itself somewhat contradictory, none of the suggested alternatives wi
2 CONC ll fit all of the primary evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT The basic raw data is as follows.  First, the Annals of Tigernach [AT] for the year 1091 refer to him as the son of the son of Harald ["Goffraidh mac Maic Arailt, rí Atha Cliath."].  Then, there is the Chronicle of the Kings of Ma
2 CONC n [CRM], which states that Godred was the son of Harald the Black of "Ysland" (Iceland), without further identifying this Harald.  Finally, there is the Welsh collection of  Norse pedigrees in "Achau Brenhinoedd a Thywysogion Cymr
2 CONC u" [ABT, in EWGT, pp. 95-110], which includes a genealogy of the kings of Man, as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT ABT.6c:  Rhanallt m. Gwythryg m. Afloyd m. Gwrthryt mearch m. Harallt ddu m. Ifor gamle m. Afloyd m. Swtrig.
2 CONT
2 CONT Changing the names from these Welsh forms to the more familiar English forms gives:
2 CONT
2 CONT Reginald [king of Man, d. 14 Feb 1229], son of
2 CONT
2 CONT Godred [king of Man, d. 10 Nov 1187], son of
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf [king of Man, d. 29 June 1153], son of
2 CONT
2 CONT Godred [Crovan, king of Dublin and Man, d. 1095], son of
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald ddu [i.e., the Black], son of
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivar gamle [i.e., the Old], son of
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf [presumably Olaf Cuaran, king of Dublin and York], son of
2 CONT
2 CONT Sitric [d. 927]
2 CONT
2 CONT It may be that the above genealogy was composed during the reign of Reginald (d. 1229), since he is the latest person mentioned in the genealogy.  There is no way of knowing whether copying mistakes were made between that time of 
2 CONC composition and the surviving manuscripts.
2 CONT
2 CONT We now list several possibilities regarding the ancestry of Godred Crovan, along with the strengths and weaknesses of each possibility (some of which are valid for more than one case, and are therefore repeated).  While there are 
2 CONC other scenarios which could be listed, they would seem less likely than the ones given below.
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibility 1:  The genealogy of ABT is to be accepted as it is.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Strengths:  It requires no emendation of the genealogy in ABT.  It agrees with the Chronicle of the kings of Man in making Godred the son of Harald "the Black".
2 CONT
2 CONT   Weaknesses:  No son of Olaf Cuaran named Ivar is known from the Irish records.  The generations are a bit long (but not drastically so).  It disagrees with the Annals of Tigernach, which make Godred the grandson of a certain Har
2 CONC ald.
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibility 2:  In the process of copying the pedigree, a "Harald" was accidently omitted between Ivar and Olaf Cuaran, so that the pedigree should read Godred son of Harald the Black son of Ivar son of Harald son of Olaf [Cuaran]
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT   Strengths:  Olaf Cuaran had a son named Harald, who in turn had a son named Ivar, both known from the Irish annals, so the agreement with the Irish annals would be excellent.  It agrees with the Chronicle of the kings of Man in 
2 CONC making Godred the son of Harald "the Black".  The chronology fits better than Possibilities 1 and 3.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Weaknesses:  It requires an emendation of the pedigree in ABT.  It disagrees with the Annals of Tigernach, which make Godred the grandson of a certain Harald.
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibility 3:  In the process of copying the pedigree, Harald and Ivar were accidently switched, so that the pedigree should read Godred son of Ivar son of Harald son of Olaf [Cuaran].
2 CONT
2 CONT   Strengths:  Olaf Cuaran had a son named Harald, who in turn had a son named Ivar, both known from the Irish annals, so the agreement with the Irish annals would be excellent.  It agrees with the Annals of Tigernach, which call G
2 CONC odred the grandson of a certain Harald.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Weaknesses:  It requires an emendation of the pedigree in ABT.  The generations are a bit long (but not drastically so).  It disagrees the the Chronicle of the kings of Man, which make Godred the son of Harald the Black.
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibility 4:  The pedigree in ABT is wrong, and Godred was not a descendant of Olaf Cuaran, but was instead descended somehow from the kings of the Isles who ruled in the late tenth and early eleventh centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Strengths:  It would explain Godred's claim to the kingship of Man.  The known names used by the early dynasty of the kings of the Isles were Guthfrith (i.e., Godred), Harald, Lagman, Olaf, and Rognvald, which were exactly the n
2 CONC ames which were common in the family of Godred Crovan (including the rare name Lagman), so this possiblity has some onomastic support.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Weakness:  It requires abandoning the manuscript genealogy of ABT, so there is no direct supporting evidence.  The onomastic argument is mitigated somewhat by the fact that the names Guthfrith, Harald, Olaf, and Rognvald were al
2 CONC l common among the Hiberno-Norse in general, so that only the rare name Lagman carries significant weight in the onomastic argument.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before I was aware of the genealogy in ABT, I favored possibility 4.  Now that I know about the ABT genealogy, I think Possibility 2 is the most likely one.  However, I think that none of the four possibilities can be ruled out, g
2 CONC iven the currently known evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Discussions of this material can also be found in Broderick (1980), Duffy (1992), and Thornton (1996).  I have not yet seen a copy of the Broderick article.
2 CONT
