1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Primary Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Aug. 1. 1303 Arbroath <br>
2 CONT
2 CONT Beginning at the bottom of page 47 at Membrane 5: https://archive.org/details/calendarofclose05grea/page/47/mode/1up
2 CONT
2 CONT To Walter de Glouc[estria], escheator beyond Trent. Order to restore to '''Hugh de Aldithele and Isolda, his wife''', a third of the manor of Great Markeleye, which is held of the king in chief, together with the issues received f
2 CONC rom it since it was taken into the king's hands, as they have shown the king that whereas they have long held the said part in her dower of the free tenement of '''Walter Balun, her first husband''', and they have now acquired the
2 CONC  right and fee of the said part to them and to Hugh's heirs from John de Balun, kinsman and heir of Walter, to whom the said part ought to have reverted after her death, without obtaining the king's licence, the escheator has take
2 CONC n that part into the king's hands by reason of the acquisition aforesaid, and the king wishes to show favour to Hugh for the good service rendered by him and his ancestors and because he is with the king in his service in Scotland
2 CONC . The part is to be held by them in form aforesaid until further orders. By p.s.
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Close Rolls, Edward I: August 1303', in Calendar of Close Rolls, Edward I: Volume 5, 1302-1307, ed. H C Maxwell Lyte (London, 1908), pp. 47-51. British History Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/cal-close-rolls/edw1/vol5/pp4
2 CONC 7-51 [accessed 15 January 2018].
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquisitions Post Mortem, Edward II, File 94
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh’s mother is listed as Ela in his IPM.
2 CONT
2 CONT 657.	HUGH DAUDELEYE alias DE AUDELEYE.
2 CONT Writ of certiorari to John Loveday and others, on the petition of James Daudeleye for livery of the '''manor of Stretton''', '''which Ela Daudeleye gave to Hugh Daudeleye''', whose heir he is, and to the heirs of his body; which m
2 CONC anor was taken into the king’s hand by the rebellion of the said Hugh, who has now died, 9 March, 19 Edward II.
2 CONT OXFORD.	Inq. Saturday after the Annunciation, 19 Edward II.
2 CONT Stretton. The manor was given by Ela de Audeleie to Hugh de Audeleye her son and the heirs of his body by her charter on Monday after St. Frideswide, 1 Edward I, and the said Hugh peacefully continued his seisin thereof until thro
2 CONC ugh his rebellion it was taken into the king’s hand. The manor is held of Eblo le Estraunge and Alesia his wife, as of her right, by service of 1/3 knight’s fee. 
2 CONT James de Audeleie is his son and heir.
2 CONT Writ of certiorari to John Inge and others, on the petition of James de Audeleye for a fourth part of the manor of Mere, co. Stafford, which was taken into the king’s hand by the rebellion of the said Hugh his father, now deceased
2 CONC , 17 March, 19 Edward II.
2 CONT STAFFORD.	Inq. Thursday in Easter week, 19 Edward II.
2 CONT Mere. William de Mere enfeoffed the said Hugh of a fourth part of the manor, and of a fourth part of a fourth part, and of 4a. land and 12a. turbary in Mere by Assheleye, in 11 Edward II, and the said Hugh in the same year granted
2 CONC  the same back, by fine levied in the king’s court, to the said William for life with reversion to the said Hugh and his heirs; and after the death of the said William the king’s escheator seized the same into the king’s hand by r
2 CONC eason of the rebellion of the said Hugh, who was at that time in the king’s prison at Wallyngford, and there they still remain. The lands &c., are held of Thomas de Halghton, by service of 1/4 knight’s fee. 
2 CONT James his son, is his next heir. 
2 CONT Petition to the king and council from the aforesaid James for livery of the lands &c. abovesaid, which are in the king’s hand through the quarrel of the earl of Lancaster. Endorsed. Let this petition be sent to the chancery, and i
2 CONC f it be so, let the said James be restored to his heritage with the issues &c.
2 CONT C. Edw. II. File 94. (19.)
2 CONT
2 CONT >E E S Sharp and A E Stamp, 'Inquisitions Post Mortem, Edward II, File 94', in Calendar of Inquisitions Post Mortem: Volume 6, Edward II (London, 1910), pp. 406-412. British History Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-p
2 CONC ost-mortem/vol6/pp406-412 [accessed 21 August 2023] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy
2 CONT
2 CONT Medlands 6 June 2024 Update English Lords A–C 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3.htm#HughAudleyMIsoltMortimer
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMES Audley of Heleigh, Staffordshire, son of HENRY de Audley & his wife Bertrade Mainwaring ([1220]-[11 Jun] 1272).  The Complete Peerage records that he died by "breaking his neck"[19].  m ELA Longespee, daughter of WILLIAM Lon
2 CONC gespee & his wife Idoine de Camville (-before 22 Nov 1299). 
2 CONT
2 CONT James Audley & his wife had six children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         '''HUGH de Audley''' ([1267]-[Wallingford Castle] [Nov 1325/Mar 1326]).  He was summoned to Parliament 15 May 1321, whereby he is held to have become Lord Audley. ...  m ([1288/89]) as her second husband,''' ISOLDA le R
2 CONC ous''', widow of WALTER de Ballon of Much Marcle, Herefordshire, daughter of ROGER le Rous & his wife --- ([before 1273]-after 1336).  Her birth date is estimated from the birth of her son by her second marriage, dated to [1289] (
2 CONC see below).  The Complete Peerage names Isolda as the daughter of Edmund Mortimer and his wife Margaret de Fiennes (although she is not listed among their children in another part of the same work[57])[58].  This suggestion is chr
2 CONC onologically impossible if Isolda gave birth to her son Hugh Audley in [1289][59].  In a previous version of the present document, it was therefore supposed that Isolda was Edmund Mortimer's illegitimate daughter, or was possibly 
2 CONC Edmund's sister, assuming that she was a member of the Mortimer family[60].  The problem is resolved in Some corrections and additions to the Complete Peerage, which says that “Isolt was not a Mortimer, but was the daughter of Rog
2 CONC er le Rus or Rous”[61], citing several sources including “Roger le Rous” settling the manor of Eastington, Gloucestershire on “Walter de Balun and Isolt his wife” in Jun 1287 and “Isolt, daughter of Roger le Rus” claiming Eastingt
2 CONC on “as her right against Reynold de Balun (Walter’s brother and heir” in the Court of Common Pleas in 1289[62].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Hugh & his wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *a)         '''JAMES Audley of Stratton Audley''' (-before 1 Mar 1334, bur Langley Abbey, Norfolk).  Mistress: (after 24 Jun 1314) EVA de Clavering, widow firstly of THOMAS Audley and secondly of THOMAS de Ufford, daughter of JOHN
2 CONC  de Clavering Lord Clavering & his wife Hawise de Tibetot (-30 Sep 1369, bur Langley Abbey, Norfolk). ...  James had four illegitimate children by his mistress:  ...
2 CONT *b)        ''' HUGH Audley''' ([1289][69]-10 Nov 1347, bur Tonbridge Priory).  He was summoned to Parliament 30 Nov 1317, whereby he is held to have become Lord Audley.  He was created Earl of Gloucester 16 Mar 1337.  m (Windsor 2
2 CONC 8 Apr 1317) as her second husband, MARGARET de Clare, widow of PIERS Gavaston Earl of Cornwall, daughter of GILBERT de Clare Earl of Gloucester and Hereford & his second wife Joan of England ([1293]-9 Apr 1342). ... Lord Hugh & hi
2 CONC s wife had one child:  ...
2 CONT *c)         '''ALICE''' (-13 Jan 1375, bur Durham Cathedral Church).  A mid-15th century manuscript records that "Radulfum de Neuille" married "Alesia, filia Hugonis de Audeley"[71].  “Michael de la Pole miles, dominus de Wingfeil
2 CONC d” founded Kingston-upon-Hull priory, for the souls of “…Radulphi de Nevill patris et Alesiæ matris…Johannis [de Nevill domini de Raby] et Matildæ quondam uxoris eiusdem Johannis”, by charter dated 18 Feb 1378[72].  m firstly (131
2 CONC 7) RALPH de Greystoke of Greystoke, son of ROBERT FitzRalph & his wife Elizabeth --- (15 Aug 1299-Gateshead 14 Jul 1323, bur Newminster).  He was summoned to Parliament in 1322 whereby he is held to have become Lord Greystoke.  m 
2 CONC secondly (1327) RALPH Neville of Raby Lord Neville, son of RANDOLPH Neville Lord Neville & his first wife Eupheme --- ([before 1291]- 5 Aug 1368, bur Durham). 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
