2 CONC d Marchamley, Shropshire, Horseheath, Cambridgeshire, etc., itinerant justice, Sheriff of Shropshire and Staffordshire, 1261, 1270, Justiciar of Ireland, 1270-2, Keeper of Newcastle-under-Lyme Castle, son and heir of Henry de Audl
2 CONC ey, of Heleigh, Audley, etc., Staffordshire, by Bertrade, daughter of Ralph de Mainwaring (or Mesnilwarin). She had the manor of Stratton (in Stratton Audley) and part of Wretchwick (in Bicester), Oxfordshire in free marriage. The
2 CONC y had five sons, James, Knt., Henry, William, Knt., Nicholas, Knt., and Hugh, Knt. [Lord Audley], and one daughter, Joan. He had one illegitimate son, James, by his mistress, Alice de Mohun, widow successively of William de Clinto
2 CONC n (died before 1237) and Robert de Beauchamp (died about 1264), of Hatch Beauchamp, Somerset. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1263 he [James de Audley] was granted the wardship of the lands and heirs of Robert de Beauchamp, tenant in chief. The following year he was granted the lands and heirs of John, son of Robert de Beauchamp, a minor, deceased, wh
2 CONC o had married his daughter, Joan, “who is near her time or has already given birth to an heir.” He took an active part on the King’s side against the Barons in 1264, and was engaged in the Evesham campaign in 1265. He went on pilg
2 CONC rimage to Galicia in 1268. Sometime previous to 1272, he and his mistress, Alice de Beauchamp, had a joint grant of lands in Horseheath and Streatley, Cambridgeshire. SIR JAMES DE AUDLEY died in Ireland about 11 June 1272 by break
2 CONC ing his neck. In 1273 his widow, Ela, sued her son, James son of James de Audley for a third of the manors of Audley and Heleigh, except the castles of the manors, and a third of the vills of Betley, Bucknall, Talk, Wryneford, and
2 CONC  Balterley, Staffordshire, etc.; 
