2 CONT === Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Legh-54
2 CONT
2 CONT John was the son of John de Legh[1] and his wife, Matilda, daughter of John de Ardene.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT John was the father of:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Matilda Legh, who was married to Richard, son of John de Radecliff; they were living in 1355-7;[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On 24 June, 1338, John, son of John de Legh, kt, released to his grandmother, Elena de Legh', all his right, etc, in a moiety of the vill of Modburlegh', for life, with reversion to him or his heirs immediately after her death, an
2 CONC d in return "she will provide honourable maintenance for him in all things concerning his position," witnessed by the grantor's uncles, William de Legh, kt, Robert de Legh', Peter de Legh and Gilbert de Legh'.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT John was dead before29-30 Edward III, during which time his daughter sued his father's widow for property which his maternal grandfather had given to his parents and their heirs.[2] In 29-30 Edward III [25 January 1355-24 January 
2 CONC 1357], at Chester, Richard, son of John de Radecliff, and Matilda his wife, sued Isabella, formerly wife of John de Legh Chivaler, for land in Modberlegh, which John de Ardene gave to John de Legh for his life, with remainder to J
2 CONC ohn, son of John de Legh, and Matilda, daughter of John de Ardene, and to the heirs of the bodies of John de Legh and Matilda:[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT * John de Legh was the father of:
2 CONT * John de Legh who married Matilda, daughter of John de Ardene, they were the parents of:
2 CONT * John, who was the father of:
2 CONT * Matilda, who was married to Richard de Radecliff, the plaintiffs in 1355-6.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT In The Visitation of Cheshire in the Year 1580 Made by Robert Glover, a Richard Leigh is said to be the son of John Ligh and his wife, Isabel, daughter and heir of Sir Richard Sandbach, and grandson of Sir John Leigh of Bouthes; a
2 CONC nd Richard and his wife, Maud Arderne, had one daughter, their sole heir, Maude who was married to Richard Radcliff of Wordsall.[3] However, this is disproved by the contemporary court case on the Chester Plea Rolls of 29-30 Edwar
2 CONC d III [25 January, 1355-24 January, 1357], in which Richard, son of John de Radecliff, and Matilda his wife state that her father was John, and her grandparents John de Legh, and Matilda, daughter of John de Ardene.[2]
