1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joan De Stockport was born.  Joan married Sir Nicholas De Eton and had a child:  Cicely De Eton.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "Joan the elder, married, first, Sir Nicholas de Eton, and secondly, Sir John de Ardern, who had some of her lands for his life only, and others were settled upon him and his heirs. Hence, Nicholas, son of Sir Nicholas de Eton the
2 CONC  younger, released to Sir John Ardern all his right to the Manors of Stockport, &c. and to the Advowsons of the Churches of the said Manors in 1332 and 1335." [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan de Eton was the oldest daughter of Richard de Stockport, and was the sole and affirmed heiress. See chart attached. Sir Nicholas de ETON Knight 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 was born 1285 in Eaton, Cheshire, England. He died 1325 in Eaton, C
2 CONC heshire, England. Nicholas married Joan de STOCKPORT on 1314 in Eaton, Cheshire, England. Joan would have kept her family COA, as did all aristocratic women. Imposing 21st century ideas of identity does  not work here. This is ver
2 CONC y important in clan lineages. Joan's descendants might have joined Stockport with Eaton, but the Stockport's were definitely in the superior position regarding land holdings and Joan would have been very proud of her family coat o
2 CONC f arms. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT In 20 and 21 Edward I which was 20 November, 1291-19 November, 1293, Cecilia Stockport, who was the wife of Richard de Stockport, sued Lucy, who was the wife of Roger de Stockport, for dower of 3 messuages and 20 acres of land in 
2 CONC Hatteresl'; 9 messuages and 95 acres of land in Wernith'; 3 messuages and 50 acres of land in Bredebury; and 4 messuages and 42 acres of land in Romel. Lucia vouches to warranty Joan and Matilda, daughters and heirs of Richard de 
2 CONC Stokeport.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # Peter Turner, comp, "Appendix 4. Welsh Records. Calendar of Deeds, Inquisitions, and Writs of Dower, Enrolled on the Plea Rolls of the County of Chester; Henry III and Edward I", The Twenty-Sixth Annual Report of the Deputy Keep
2 CONC er of the Public Records [18 February 1865], (London: Her Majesty's Stationery Office, 1865), 42, e-book HathiTrust (https://hdl.handle.net/2027/mdp.39015039450500?urlappend=%3Bseq=358%3Bownerid=34524627-357 accessed 9 May, 2022).
2 CONT * 1.  Jane MacDaniels Genealogy
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
