1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *''''''Catherine Arderne1
2 CONT *F, #21544
2 CONT *Father     Sir Thomas de Arderne d. c 1 Jul 1391
2 CONT *Mother     Katherine Stafford
2 CONT *''''''     Catherine Arderne married Sir Henry Delves, son of Richard de Delves, Constable of Heleigh Castle and Amicia. Catherine Arderne married William Chetilton. Catherine Arderne married Ralph Wetenal.
2 CONT *''''''Family      Sir Henry Delves d. 1397
2 CONT *Children
2 CONT **John Delves, Lord Walton-upon-Trent+ d. bt 1393 - 1394
2 CONT **Johanna Delves2
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S6524] Unknown author, The History of the County Palatine and City of Chester, by George Ormerod, 1819, p. 268; Burke's Dormant and Extinct Baronetcies, p. 156.
2 CONT * 2.[S10297] Unknown author, History of the County Palatine and City of Chester, by George Omerod, 1819., p. 268.
2 CONT *http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p717.htm#i21544
2 CONT *______________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT *''''''Full text of "History of the family of Wrottesley of Wrottesley, co. Stafford"
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/historyoffamilyo00wrot/historyoffamilyo00wrot_djvu.txt
2 CONT *''''''History of the family of Wrottesley of Wrottesley, co. Stafford
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/details/historyoffamilyo00wrot
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/historyoffamilyo00wrot#page/91/mode/1up
2 CONT *	Sir Hugh de Wrottesley, K.G., A.D. 1333 to A.D. 1381. 
2 CONT *   Sir Hugh de Wrottesley, who now succeeded to his inheritance, is shewn to be son of the last Sir William by the deeds above printed, a suit in Banco of Easter term 13 Ed. II, and another suit on the Staffordshire Assize Roll o
2 CONC f 13 Ed. III. He was born in the early part of the year 1314, but is found to be a Knight and in full possession of his estates in January 1334.  
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/historyoffamilyo00wrot#page/142/mode/1up
2 CONT *   Shortly after the death of Mabel ap Rees, Sir Hugh had married for a third time. His choice on this occasion was Isabella, the daughter of Sir John Arderne of Aldford, co. Chester. Sir John was lord of Elford in Staffordshire,
2 CONC  but his principal estates lay in Cheshire, where he represented Bigod one of the great Chester feudatories of A.D. 1086, and held his lands by Barony.1  The father and mother of Isabella had both died in 1349, the year of the Gre
2 CONC at Pestilence, leaving two sons, Thomas and Walcheline, under age, and three daughters, Matilda, ''''''Katherine'''''' and Isabella. Sir John also left a son, Peter, by a former wife, but under a special settlement made with the l
2 CONC icense of the King as Prince of Wales, the whole of the Aldford fee passed to Thomas, the son of his third wife, Ellen de Bulkeley. This Thomas was born during the lifetime of the second wife, and was clearly illegitimate. He firs
2 CONC t appears on the Cheshire Plea Kolls under the name of Thomas Ellensone, but he subsequently assumed the name of Arderne, and will appear in these pages as Sir Thomas de Arderne at a later date. The daughters of Sir John Arderne w
2 CONC ere born in wedlock, and the Arderne estates had been settled by a Fine on the two sons in succession and their issue in tail male, and failing such with remainder to the daughters and their issue.2  Walcheline, the younger brothe
2 CONC r of Thomas, died leaving no issue, and the male line of Sir Thomas failed after two generations. The attempts of the descendants of the daughters to make good their claim to the Arderne inheritance under this entail will be descr
2 CONC ibed later on. 
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/historyoffamilyo00wrot#page/153/mode/1up
2 CONT *   On the 6 December 1380, license was granted to ''''''John Burdele (Boydell), Kt., and to Katherine, his wife'''''', to alienate to Hugh de Wrottesleye, Kt., and to Isabella, his wife, and to their issue, a messuage and thirty 
2 CONC acres of land in Budworth, in Le Frith, and on the following 14 December a writ to the Eschaetor of co. Chester directs him to deliver to Hugh de Wrottesleye that part of the land of Alina, formerly the wife of Thomas de Alvandele
2 CONC gh, which belonged to Isabella, wife of the said Hugh, and likewise the other part which belonged to Katherine, wife of John Burdele, Kt., and which had been sold by her and the said John'''''', to Hugh and Isabella, and which lan
2 CONC ds had fallen to the said Isabella and ''''''Katherine, as kinswomen and heirs of the said Alina''''''.1  This is the latest appearance of Sir Hugh on any public document. 
2 CONT *	1 Calendar of Welsh and Cheshire Recoi'ds, printed. (Holls series). ''''''Kalherine had been previously married to Thomas de Mascy, and seems to have been the eldest sister, for in 1368 Thomas de Mascy and Katherine, his wife'''
2 CONC ''', Robert de Legh, the younger, and Matilda, his wife, and Isabella, sister of Matilda, sued Thomas de Venables, of Alvandelegh, for waste in Teverton, near Tarporley, which was of their inheritance. 
2 CONT *_____________________
