2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Wettenhall-2
2 CONT
2 CONT John was the son of John de Wetenhale who was Lord of Wetenhale in 43 and 53 Hen. III. He "married Agnes, daughter of Sir John Arderne of Alvanley and Aldford, knight. This John de Wetenhale, senior, and Agnes his wife, obtained t
2 CONC he manor of Wetenhale, by fine from Margery, daughter to John de Arderne, 11 Edw II. [8 Jul 1317 - 7 Jul 1318]"[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a knight during the reign of Edward II.[2] He and Agnes "resided chiefly at Dorfold".[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sons:[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Robert de Wettenhall2 d. a 1316 m. 	Amicia de Alpraham
2 CONT # John, m. Maude, daughter of Robert Fulleshurst
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John died before 21 Edward III when his widow, Agnes, transferred the manor of Wetenhale to Robert de Paris.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wettenhall is now part of Crewe and Nantwich district, in Cheshire, England [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: There was a different John de Wetenhale, keeper of Delamere Forest[5] in Cheshire, England, who was paid 12d for each wolf taken in the forest: in 1303-4, he took three wolves and Richard de Dalton took one, for which John r
2 CONC eceived a total of 4s.[5] John was also paid 40 marks for well and faithfully keeping the forest, which he was assigned to do by Sirs J de Haveringe, W de Bliburg and Thomas de Cantebrug, after the taking of the bailiwicks of Rich
2 CONC ard Doun and Warin le Grovenour, foresters of Delamere.[5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/ww/wettenhall1.php#con1
2 CONT
2 CONT Ormerod ('Wettenhall of Wettenhall') identifies the arms of this family as "Vert, a crosse engrailed Ermine" and adds that its "Ancient arms" were "Vert, a bend Ermine".
2 CONT
