1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====FMG: English Lords [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3D-K.htm#FulkFitzWarinIIdied1198B D–K]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''FULK FitzWarin''' [II], son of FULK FitzWarin [I] & his wife --- (-[1195/98]). The Pipe Roll records the manor of Alveston to "Fulko son of Fulko fitz Warin" in Michaelmas 1171[588]. The Red Book of the Exchequer, listing scuta
2 CONC ge payments in [1190/91], records "Fulco filio Garini" paying "x s, i militem" in Warwickshire, Leicestershire[589]. The 1194/95 Pipe Roll records "Fulcho f Warini et Hawisa uxor eius" in Wiltshire[590]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ([1155/65] or later) '''HAWISE de Dinan''', daughter and co-heiress of JOSCELIN de Dinan & his wife Sibylla de Lacy ([1140/50]-1226 or after). The Historia sancti Petri Gloucestriæ records that "Hawyse de Dynan" exchanged 
2 CONC property donated by her father, that “Jozo de Plukenya" confirmed the donation of "Jazonis avi sui", that "Hugo de Plukenya" confirmed the donation of "Jazonis de Dynan" and donated further property, and that "Jozo de Plukeneya fi
2 CONC lius Hugonis" confirmed the foregoing[591].  Hawise’s suggested date of birth is estimated from the first marriage before 1125 of her mother, who was then presumably already of child-bearing age, and her mother’s second marriage a
2 CONC fter 1138. If this estimate is correct, it is likely that Hawise was married [1155/65], although the first marriage of her son Fulk FitzWarin [III] in 1207 suggests the later part of this date range and even an extended period aft
2 CONC er her marriage before Fulk [III] was born.  Her family origin and marriage are confirmed by two lawsuits recorded in 1194 in which, in the first, "Hawisia de Dinat…Fulkon fil Warin" and "--- Sibill de Dinat uxore" are named, and 
2 CONC in the second "Fulko fil Warin…Hawiss uxor sue" and "Sibill uxor Hug de Plugenai"[592].  The 1194/95 Pipe Roll records "Fulcho f Warini et Hawisa uxor eius" in Wiltshire[593].  "Hawis de Dinan et Sibill soror eius" paid a fine for
2 CONC  "de Bokeland, Corfton in Sumerset et de Hertilande in Devon…vs Oliverum de Dinan", dated 1204[594].  The primary source which confirms her date of death has not been identified. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fulk [II] & his wife had six children:...
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT www.findagrave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Hawise de Dinan
2 CONT BIRTH unknown
2 CONT England
2 CONT DEATH 1226
2 CONT Whittington, Shropshire Unitary Authority, Shropshire, England
2 CONT BURIAL
2 CONT Burial Details Unknown 
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID 74748329
2 CONT
2 CONT awise was the daughter and coheir of Joscelin de Dinan and Sybilla Talbot.
2 CONT She married Fulk "Brunin" Fitzwarin sometime around 1160.
2 CONT On 6 Nov 1194, Hawise de Dinan names her husand as Fulk fitz Warine in a suit of mort d'ancestre.
2 CONT In the year ending Michaelmas 1198, Hawise de Dinan was fined 80 merks that she might not be obliged to remarry. Now a widow, she renewed her claim upon Stanton soon after the accession of King John. In 1201, the Berkshire Pipe Ro
2 CONC ll, gives Sibil (her sister), widow of Hugh de Plugenai, and Hawise, mother of Fulk fitz Warine, as coparcerners in Lamburn. In 1204, Hawise de Dinan and her sister Sibil are apparently claiming, against Olver de Dinana, estates i
2 CONC n Bokeland and Corfton in Somersetshire and at Hertilande in Devonshire.
2 CONT Hawise and Fulk were the parents of Fulk Fitzwarin whom the legend of Robin Hood was inspired and a daughter named Eve who married Thomas de Londres
2 CONT
