1 NAME John /le Strange/ III
2 GIVN John
2 SURN le Strange
2 NSFX III
2 _MARNM le Strange
1 NAME John III le Strange
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1194
2 ADDR
3 CITY Ness Cheswardine
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE MAR 1269
2 PLAC Knockin
2 ADDR
3 CITY Oswestry
3 STAE England
1 OCCU Baron, Lord of Knockyn, Constable of Montgomery Castle 1236-48 and Cheshire 1241-1
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John III Lestrange was the son of John II Lestrange and Amice.[2] 
2 CONT He married Lucy Tregoz, daughter of Robert Tregoz.[1] 
2 CONT He died before 26 March 1269.[1]
2 CONT He supported King Henry III against Simon De Montfort.[1] 
2 CONT In 1213 he was old enough to attend on King John.[1] 
2 CONT In 1214 he undertook military service against the French.[1] 
2 CONT On 25 May 1231 he was granted by King Henry III the manor of Wrockwardine, Shropshire.[1] 
2 CONT In 1232 he played a part in negotiations between King Henry III and Prince Llywelyn the Great.[1] 
2 CONT He held the office of Constable of Bridgnorth, Chester and Shrewsbury Castles between 1233 and 1240.[1] 
2 CONT He held the office of Constable of Montgomery Castle in 1235.[1] 
2 CONT He held the office of Sheriff of Shropshire and Staffordshire between 1236 and 1248.[1] 
2 CONT He held the office of Sheriff of Cheshire from 1241 to 1242.1 He held the office of Justice of Cheshire in 1240/41.[1]
2 CONT Children of John III Lestrange and Lucy Tregoz:
2 CONT
2 CONT     John IV Lestrange - died before: 26 Feb 1275/76 [2]
2 CONT     Hamon Lestrange - died: 1274/75 [2]
2 CONT     Roger Lestrange 1st & Last Lord Strange of Ellesmere - died: 31 Jul 1311 [2]
2 CONT     Robert Lestrange - died: 12 Oct 1276 [2]
2 CONT     Hawise Lestrange - died::21 Feb 1286/87 [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations:
2 CONT
2 CONT     [S37] BP2003 volume 3, page 3473. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT     [S37] BP2003. [S37]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Some accounts say that he was born in Ness and Cheswardine, Shropshire. Alternately, it appears that his family was already in possession of Knockin Castle by the time he was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT From wikipedia (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knockin):
2 CONT
2 CONT Knockin
2 CONT
2 CONT *Unitary authority 	Shropshire
2 CONT *Ceremonial county 	Shropshire
2 CONT *Region 	West Midlands
2 CONT *Country 	England
2 CONT
2 CONT Knockin is a village and civil parish located on the B4396 in Shropshire, England. It comprises mainly historic detached buildings in a rural setting. Much of Knockin was owned by the Earl of Bradford until it was sold off in lots
2 CONC  to meet other financial demands. The Earl still owns the cricket pitch and other small pockets of land in the area. The local public house is called the Bradford Arms and displays the Bradford family coat of arms. The pub also ha
2 CONC s an impressive clock with three faces, hung outside above the main entrance. The motto displayed on the sign is that of the Bridgeman Earls of Bradford "Nec temere nec timide" (Neither rashly nor timidly) indicating a policy of p
2 CONC rogress with prudence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Church of St Mary
2 CONT
2 CONT The parish church of St Mary at Knockin was founded by Ralph Le Strange between 1182 and 1195. It has a Norman chancel, nave and north aisle but the building was heavily restored in 1847. Its graveyard was consecrated in 1817 and 
2 CONC before then all burials took place at Kinnerley. The bricked-up doorway which can be seen in the chancel is because the building was once a chapel to an adjacent castle and the entrance recalls the time when the priest entered fro
2 CONC m the fortress side.
2 CONT
2 CONT All that remains of Knockin Castle today is a large tree-covered mound of earth. It was a moated site between two knolls and may have been built by Henry II in the 12th century. A little while after his death it passed into the ha
2 CONC nds of John le Strange, whose family were also responsible for building the church which stands close to the castle site. Like most Shropshire castles which are now only marked by grassy sites, its stones live on in a number of bu
2 CONC ildings in the area.
2 CONT *________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT *John Le Strange, Sheriff of Shropshire and Staffordshire
2 CONT *b. circa 1194, d. 1276
2 CONT *Father	John Le Strange II2 b. circa 1168, d. 1269
2 CONT *Mother	Amice (?)2 b. circa 1172
2 CONT *     John Le Strange, Sheriff of Shropshire and Staffordshire was born circa 1194 at Ness And Cheswardine, Shropshire, England.2 He was the son of John Le Strange II and Amice (?).2 John Le Strange, Sheriff of Shropshire and Staf
2 CONC fordshire was sheriff of counties Shropshire and Staffordshire.3 He was Governor of Castle Shrewsbury.3 He married Lucy de Tregoz, daughter of Robert II de Tregoz, Baron of Ewyas Harold and Julia de Cantilupe.2 Arms: D'argent, à d
2 CONC eux léopards de gueules.4 John Le Strange, Sheriff of Shropshire and Staffordshire died in 1276.5
2 CONT *Family
2 CONT *	Lucy de Tregoz b. circa 1210, d. 1294
2 CONT *Children	
2 CONT ** John Le Strange, Lord of Knockyn+ 3
2 CONT ** Hawyse Le Strange+ 6
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *   1. [S215] Revised by others later George Edward Cokayne CP, XII/1:341.
2 CONT *   2. [S1121] LDS Submitters, "AFN: 4X44-4P", Ancestral File.
2 CONT *   3. [S1216] C.B., L.L.D., Ulster King of Arms Sir John Bernard Burke, B:GA, pg. 979.
2 CONT *   4. [S1403] Armoriaux, online www.heraldique-europeenne.org/Armoriaux/index.html, Rôle d'armes Glover: 168. Jean le Strange (John Lestrange).
2 CONT *   5. [S603] C.B., LL.D., Ulster King of Arms Sir Bernard Burke, B:xP, pg. 515.
2 CONT *   6. [S1388] Tudor Place, online http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/index.htm, Strange.
2 CONT *_____________________
2 CONT *'''John Le STRANGE
2 CONT *'''Born: ABT 1194, Ness And Ches, Shropshire, England
2 CONT *'''Died: 26 Mar 1269, Knockyn, Warwick, England
2 CONT *Father: John Le STRANGE
2 CONT *Mother: Amice ?
2 CONT *'''Married: Lucy De TREGOZ (b. ABT 1210 - d. 1294)
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. John Le STRANGE (1º B. Strange of Knockin)
2 CONT **2. Hamon Le STRANGE
2 CONT **3. Robert Le STRANGE
2 CONT **4. Catherine Le STRANGE
2 CONT **5. Hawise Le STRANGE
2 CONT **6. Roger Le STRANGE of Ellesmere
2 CONT **2. Herbert Le STRANGE
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/STRANGE.htm#John Le STRANGE3
2 CONT *____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT He was sheriff of counties Shropshire and Staffordshire.  He was Governor of Castle Shrewsbury [C.B., L.L.D., Ulster King of Arms Sir John Bernard Burke, compiler, The General Armory of England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales (Baltim
2 CONC ore, MD: reprint 1967 by Genealogical Publishing Company, 1884), pg. 979].
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms: D'argent, à deux léopards de gueules.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	    * Born: Bef 1193, Ness & Cheswardine, Shropshire, England 3
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Wifr: Lucy De Tregoz
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Died: Bef 12 Oct 1269, Knockin, , Shropshire, England 2
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Buried: Sheriff Of Shropshire & Staffordshire
2 CONT
2 CONT     * John Le Strange, Lord of Knockyn
2 CONT
2 CONT John III Lestrange; old enough to attend on King John in 1213 and to undertake military service against the French 1214; granted by Henry III the manor of Wrockwardine, Salop 25 May 1231; played a part in negotiations between Henr
2 CONC y III and Prince Llywelyn The Great 1232; Constable of Montgomery Castle 1235 and Bridgnorth, Chester and Shrewsbury Castles 1233-40; Sheriff of Salop and Staffs 1236-48 and Cheshire 1241-2, Justice of Chester 1240 or 41; supporte
2 CONC d Henry III against Simon de Montfort (unlike? his son); married? Lucy, daughter of Robert Tregoz, and died by 26 March 1269. [Burke's Peerage]
2 CONT *__________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT John le Strange, who was a person of great note in his time. In 16th of King John, his father being then living, he was in the Wars of Poitou in France. In 15th of Henry III, 1231, he obtained a grant of the inheritance of Wrockwa
2 CONC rdine for the yearly rent of œ8, to be paid to the King and his heirs and successors. In 21st of Henry III, 1237, he was appointed Sheriff of Salop and Stafford and constituted Governor of the Castles of Salop and Brugges. He was 
2 CONC afterwards one of the Baron's Marchers and had command to reside in the Marshes in order to resist incursions by the Welsh. In the contest between Henry III and the Barons he adhered with great fidelity to the King and obtained fo
2 CONC r his loyalty a grant of the lands of Walter de Muscegros, which had been forfeited in that rebellion.
2 CONT *____________________
