1 NAME Henry /le Foun/
2 GIVN Henry
2 _MARNM le Foun
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1244
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Copied from the Stradling family website "www.stradling.org.uk/docs/
2 CONT
2 CONT "Victoria County History of Warwickshire."
2 CONT
2 CONT Certain lands in Losley [Loxley, of Robin Hood fame] were sold with the manor of Walton Mauduit by the Earl of Warwick to Simon de Wauton, Bishop of Norwich (1258-66). The bishop conveyed them to John de Wauton, who was probably h
2 CONC is great-nephew. Subsequently John de Wauton received from Walter Giffard, Archbishop of York (1266-79), a grant of the two Walton manors and all his land in Loxley; this was possibly the carucate and 100s. in rents which Henry le
2 CONC  Foun and Isabel his wife quitclaimed to the archbishop in 1278. The archbishop was perhaps acting as trustee for John de Wauton whose daughter and heir Maud, was in 1279 a ward of Godfrey Giffard, Bishop of Worcester, Walter's yo
2 CONC unger brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Simon was elderly when he became bishop and may well have been married when young. John de Wauton died in 1277, and his widow Isabel married Henry le Foun. John's heir, his daughter Maud, was a child, and in 1278 Henry and Isa
2 CONC bel conveyed the manors of Walton and other lands to Walter Giffard, Archbishop of York.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Patent Rolls of the Reign of Henry III.: 1247-1258 By Great Britain. Public Record Office, H. C. Maxwell Lyte (Sir.) Page 459 [https://books.google.com/books?id=bivg6Arg23kC&lpg=PA459&ots=n5BuXLIsEe&dq=Henry%20le%20Foun&pg=PA
2 CONC 459#v=onepage&q=%E2%80%9CHenry%20le%20Foun%E2%80%9D&f=true GoogleBooks]
2 CONT
2 CONT Pardon, for a fine of 200 marks which Robert le Vavassur made, to the said Robert, William his son, Alexander de Wandesle, Walter de Ufton, John de Scarlet, Simon le Porter, '''Henry le Foun,''' William de Morton, Stephen de Shipl
2 CONC e, Robert de Wike, Henry Revel, John Yedrich, and William de Ufton, sometime bailiffs and ministers of the said Robert, of all trespasses in the forests, hays, woods, castles, manors, works and other bailiwicks and wardenships, wh
2 CONC ich he held of the king to the day of the Conversion of St. Paul, 40 Henry III, and release to them of the king's rancour and indignation; on condition that they stand their trial in the king's court, according to law and the cust
2 CONC om of the realm, touching trespasses committed against others than the king when the said Robert was sheriff of Nottingham and Derby or other bailiff of the king, if any will proceed against them.
2 CONT
