1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Not a documented spouse of https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Bryan-Cornwall/6000000004382732989?through=6000000003243285723
2 CONT Lady Maude le Strange is a figure who appears in various genealogical records, though there is often conflicting or unverified information regarding her specific placement within the le Strange family line.
2 CONT Based on the available records, here is a summary of the context surrounding this name:
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical Confusion
2 CONT Contradictory Records: There is no single, universally accepted historical record confirming a daughter named Maude le Strange born in 1321 to Roger le Strange, 4th Baron Strange of Knockin.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Baron’s Family: Roger le Strange, 4th Baron Strange of Knockin (1301–1349), was married to a woman named Maud (sometimes referred to as Maud de Coucy) and later to Joan de Ingham. While his descendants are well-documented (inc
2 CONC luding his son, Roger, the 5th Baron), genealogies listing a daughter named Maude born in 1321 often rely on user-contributed family trees rather than contemporary historical documentation.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Possible Misidentification: In some historical contexts, "Maude le Strange" is associated with other branches of the family. For example, some records point to a Maude le Strange who married Brian de Cornwall, though she is someti
2 CONC mes cited as the daughter of Fulk le Strange, 1st Lord Strange of Blackmere, rather than Roger of Knockin.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Distinctions in the le Strange Lineage
2 CONT It is helpful to distinguish between the two primary titles of the family, as they are frequently confused:
2 CONT
2 CONT Strange of Knockin: This is the line of Roger le Strange, 4th Baron Strange of Knockin.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Strange of Blackmere: This is a separate branch founded by Fulk le Strange (d. 1324).
2 CONT —-
2 CONT There seems to be considerable confusion as to just which Maud Le Strange married Sir Brian Cornwall. History of Parliament Online claims she was the daughter of Fulk, 1st Baron Strange of Blackmere; but it is not clear that he ha
2 CONC d any daughter named Maud. A more traditional account is that she was the daughter of Roger, 4th Baron Strange of Knockin; but this does not account for Sir John Cornwall being his father's heir born in 1366.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps the true answer - this is nothing but a wild guess - is that the Black Death did away with Sir Brian's entire earlier family, and he was forced to remarry (we don't know whom) in order to get a surviving son and heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''INQUIRIES:''' (Ken Shelley, 22 Oct 2024) 
2 CONT >'''1.''' What value does the above wild guess offer''‽
2 CONT >'''2.''' What rationale is there for keeping her birth place as Blakemere (Fulk's locale of residence) while this profile remains attached as a daughter to Roger and Maud, whose residence was in Shropshire‽
2 CONT >'''3.''' Which '''top-tier''' source proves that Roger had a daughter named Maude‽ '''—''' "Our Royal Titled Noble and Commoner Ancestors and Cousins" doesn't qualify. Medlands doesn't source her inclusion in the family. ''The Hi
2 CONC story of Parliament'' online doesn't inline-source the relationships. Thus, neither qualifies as a secondary source or even tertiary source! 
2 CONT >'''4.''' Is it legitimate to maintain connections to parents or spouse simply to keep others from bewailing that their unproven ancestries at this junction deserve severing‽ '''—''' She shows as my 19th great grandmother, but if 
2 CONC she can't be proven thus, I have now qualms in dismissing her in my person tree.
2 CONT >''' Frankly,''' the dilemmas detailed above probably may '''never''' be resolved by a miraculous, never before seen record-find.
2 CONT <nowiki>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
