2 CONC e…Eleanor la Estraunge, afterwards espoused to Fulk le Estraunge, who begat of her John le Estraunge, now aged 19 years and more, and in the king’s wardship"[944].  Lord Strange (of Blackmere).  m as her first husband, ANKARET Bot
2 CONC eler, daughter of WILLIAM Boteler of Wem, Shropshire & his wife --- (-( Oct 1361).  She married secondly Sir Thomas de Ferrers.  John & his wife had children: 
2 CONT **a)         FULK Le Strange ([1330/31]-30 Aug 1349).  Lord Strange (of Blackmere).  m as her first husband, ELIZABETH de Stafford, daughter of RALPH de Stafford Earl of Stafford & his second wife Margaret de Audley (1333 or after
2 CONC -7 Aug 1375).  She married secondly John Lord Ferrers.  She married thirdly as his first wife, Sir Reginald de Cobham (Lord Cobham of Sterborough). 
2 CONT **b)         JOHN Le Strange (Whitchurch [Easter] 1332-12 May 1361).  Lord Strange (of Blackmere).  m MARY [Isabel] FitzAlan of Arundel, daughter of RICHARD FitzAlan Earl of Arundel & his first wife Isabel le Despencer (-29 Aug 13
2 CONC 96).  John & his wife had children: 
2 CONT ***i)          JOHN Le Strange (-3 Aug 1375).  Lord Strange (of Blackmere).  m (after 6 Sep 1369) as her first husband, ISABEL de Beauchamp, daughter of THOMAS Beauchamp Earl of Warwick & his wife Katherine de Mortimer (-29 Sep 14
2 CONC 16, bur Campsey Priory).  The will of "Thomas Beauchamp Earl of Warwick", dated 6 Sep 1369, chose burial “in the quire of the Collegiate Church of our Lady at Warwick”, bequeathed property to “my two sons Thomas and William...Isab
2 CONC el my daughter...to her marriage...Margaret my daughter a nun at Shouldham...Katherine the daughter of my son Guy a nun at Shouldham...my daughter Alice...my daughter Clifford...Sir John Beauchamp...Sir Roger Beauchamp...Sir Guy d
2 CONC e Brienne...”[945].  Her marriage is recorded in the Complete Peerage[946].  She married secondly (12 Jun 1376 or before) as his second wife, William de Ufford Earl of Suffolk.  She became a nun 21 Mar 1382.  The will of "Thomas B
2 CONC eauchamp Earl of Warwick", dated 1 Apr 1400, bequeathed property to “Richard my son and heir...my daughter Beauchamp...my brother of Bergavenny...my sisters of Suffolk and Clifford and...my niece Katherine Beauchamp the nun at Sho
2 CONC uldham”[947].  John & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT ****(1)       ELIZABETH Le Strange ([1374]-23 Aug 1383).  Baroness Strange.  m as his first wife, THOMAS Mowbray, son of JOHN Mowbray Lord Mowbray & his wife Elizabeth de Segrave (22 Mar 1366-Venice 22 Sep 1399, bur Venice, abbey 
2 CONC of St George).  He succeeded his brother in 1383 as Lord Mowbray, Lord Segrave.  Earl of Nottingham 1383.  He was created Duke of Norfolk 29 Sep 1397, and succeeded to the earldom of Norfolk in 1399 on the death of his maternal gr
2 CONC andmother. 
2 CONT ***ii)         ANKARET Le Strange ([1361]-1 Jun 1413).  She succeeded her father 23 Aug 1383 as Baroness Strange, suo iure.  m firstly (before 23 Aug 1383) RICHARD Talbot, son of GILBERT Talbot Lord Talbot & his first wife Pernel 
2 CONC Butler ([1361]-London 8/9 Sep 1395).  He was summoned to Parliament 3 Mar 1384, in consequence of his marriage to the heiress of Strange, whereby he is held to have become Lord Talbot[948].  m secondly ([8 Mar/4 Jul] 1401) as his 
2 CONC second wife, THOMAS Neville Lord Furnivalle, son of JOHN Neville Lord Neville & his first wife Matilda Percy (-14 Mar 1407, bur Worksop Priory). 
2 CONT **c)        ''' [MATILDA .  m BRIAN de Cornwall''', son of Sir EDMUND Cornwall of Kinlet & his wife Elizabeth de Brampton.] 
2 CONT From: https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3P-S.htm#EblesLestrangedied1324B
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT Maud (surname unknown) was the wife of Sir Bryan de Cornwall of Kinlet (c. 1315–1392), a prominent Shropshire landowner and public official. While her name and marriage are documented through the property and administrative record
2 CONC s associated with the manor of Kinlet, her maiden name and parentage are not preserved in any contemporary 14th-century records.
2 CONT Her primary historical significance is her status as the matriarch of the family line that held the manor of Kinlet during the late 14th century. Because no primary documentation survives to identify her father or maiden name, her
2 CONC  lineage remains historically unverified.
2 CONT Historical records regarding an individual named "Maud le Strange" who was born in 1323 and died circa 1400 are inconsistent and often conflated with other noblewomen of the era. Genealogical databases frequently present conflicti
2 CONC ng information due to the difficulty of documenting individuals from this perio.
2 CONT Points of Historical Confusion:
2 CONT Identity Conflation: Many sources confuse the wives of Sir Bryan de Cornwall (c. 1315–1392). While some genealogies identify his wife as Maud le Strange (often with dates such as 1320/1321–1344), other records identify his wife as
2 CONC  Maud de Mohun (1335–1400).
2 CONT Maud le Strange: Some records place a "Maude le Strange" as the wife of Bryan de Cornwall, born around 1320 or 1321 and dying young in 1344. In some pedigrees, she is tentatively suggested as a daughter of Fulk le Strange, though 
2 CONC this remains uncertain and debated among genealogists.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Not documented
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Not documented
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Birth: c. 1320–1325 (Estimated based on marriage and child-bearing years)
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: c. 1390–1400
2 CONT Place of Death: Kinlet, Shropshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage and Issue===
2 CONT Spouse: Sir Bruan de Cornwall (c. 1315–1392)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT 1. Isabelle Cornwall (c. 1348–1424); spouse: Sir John Le Blount.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Sir John Cornwall (c. 1350–1414).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Factual Information and Relevance===
2 CONT Estate Management: Marriage to Sir Brian de Cornwall established her role within the manor of Kinlet, which served as the primary seat for the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Absence of Proof: Despite various claims in post-medieval heraldic visitations, no Inquisition Post Mortem, land charter, or contemporary legal document provides evidence of her maiden name or parentage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Record Integrity: Genealogical claims linking her to the Le Strange or De Mohun families are considered speculative and are not supported by primary 14th-century evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Verifiable Sources and Records===
2 CONT Calendar of Patent Rolls: Records of the reign of Edward III and Richard II documenting Sir Brian de Cornwall's land holdings and administrative duties.
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Parliament Online: Biographies of Sir Brian de Cornwall and his descendants, which document the descent of the manor of Kinlet without establishing Maud’s maiden name.
2 CONT
2 CONT National Archives (UK): Records regarding the Inquisition Post Mortem for the Cornwall family, which establish the descent of property to his heirs but do not provide maternal lineage.
