2 CONT FITZWARIN
2 CONT The exploits of the early generations of this family are recorded in the highly romanticised Legend of Fulk Fitz Warin, whose early passages are clearly legendary.  Many of the later genealogical details in the Legend are corrobor
2 CONC ated by charter evidence as noted below, although the Legend conflates Fulk FitzWarin [I] and Fulk FitzWarin [II] into one person.  The FitzWarin family’s extensive landholding interests in Shropshire are reviewed by Eyton in diff
2 CONC erent parts of his History of Shropshire, as shown below. 
2 CONT A.      ORIGINS
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT B.      LORDS FITZWARIN
2 CONT FULK FitzWarin [V], son of FULK FitzWarin [IV] & his [second] wife Constance de Tosny (14 Sep 1251-24 Nov 1315).  An enquiry, dated “Thursday after St. Mark the Evangelist, 1 Edw I”, into the age of "Fulk son of Warin" includes te
2 CONC stimony that "he will be 22 on the day of the Exaltation of the Holy Cross next" adding that at his birth his father was “much congratulated because all his other children were girls”[648].  He was summoned to Parliament from 1295
2 CONC  whereby he is held to have become Lord FitzWarin. 
2 CONT m (before 25 Feb 1277) MARGARET, daughter of GRUFFYDD ap Gwenwynwyn & his wife Hawise le Strange (-11 May 1336).  The Complete Peerage indicates inquisitions dated 25 Feb 1276 (O.S.) which records Fulk FitzWarin [IV] holding land 
2 CONC at Bauseley [in Alberbury, county Montgomery] until “Gruffydd ap Wennonwen” occupied the premises following a dispute with Thomas Corbet, until transferring it to Fulk FitzWarin [V] who married his daughter, noting that FulkFitzWa
2 CONC rin [V] was later dispossessed following other disputes[649].  Inquisitions following a writ dated 28 Jun “10 Edw III”, after the death of "Margaret late the wife of Fulk le Fitz Waryn", records "Alweston...held for life of the in
2 CONC heritance of Walter, son and heir of Walter de Gloucestre deceased, a minor" with “Walter son of the said Walter de Gloucestre deceased aged 21 years and more is his next heir”[650]. 
2 CONT Fulk [V] & his wife had children: 
2 CONT *1.         FULK FitzWarin [VI] (-before 6 Jun 1336).  Lord FitzWarin.  m ELEANOR de Beauchamp, daughter of JOHN de Beauchamp of Somerset, Lord Beauchamp & his wife Cecile de Vivonne (-after 18 Nov 1341).  Fulk [VI] & his wife had
2 CONC  children: 
2 CONT **a)         FULK FitzWarin [VII] (-25 Jul 1349).  Lord FitzWarin.  m ---.  The Complete Peerage states that he married “(it is said) Joan da. of Henry (de Beaumont) Earl of Buchan by Alice, da. of Alexander Comyn” but cites no ev
2 CONC idence[651].  Fulk [VII] & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT ***i)          FULK FitzWarin [VIII] (Whittington 2 Mar 1341-12 Feb 1374).  Lord FitzWarin.  m MARGERY Audley, '''daughter of JAMES Audley Lord Audley & his second wife Isabel ---.'''  Inquisitions dated 23 Nov 1392 relating to th
2 CONC e manor of Tawstok name "Margaret the third sister of Nicholas [d’Audelee Chivaler]" and her grandson "Fulk Fitz Waryn son of Fulk" as heirs of Nicholas[652].  Fulk [VIII] & his wife had children: 
2 CONT ****(a)       FULK FitzWarin [IX] (Combe Martin, Devon 2 Mar 1362-8 Aug 1391, bur Whittington).  Lord FitzWarin.  The will of "Fulk Fitzwarine Knight", dated 8 Aug 1391, chose burial “in...the church of Whittington”, bequeathed pr
2 CONC operty to “Philip Fitzwarine my uncle” and appointed “Elizabeth my wife and the said Philip” as his executors[653].  m firstly as her first husband, ELIZABETH Cogan, daughter of WILLIAM Cogan of Bampton, Devon & his second wife Is
2 CONC abel Loring of Chalgrave, Bedfordshire ([1373/74]-29 Oct 1397).  The will of "Fulk Fitzwarine Knight", dated 8 Aug 1391, bequeathed property to “Philip Fitzwarine my uncle” and appointed “Elizabeth my wife and the said Philip” as 
2 CONC his executors[654].  She married secondly (pardon for marrying without royal licence 11 Feb 1393) as his first wife, Hugh de Courtenay of Goodrington and Stancombe, Devon. 
2 CONT From: https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISHNOBILITYMEDIEVAL3D-K.htm#_Toc165960596
