2 CONT Children of Nicholas de Audley, Knt., by Joan Martin:
2 CONT i. JAMES DE AUDLEY, Knit., 2nd Lord Audley [see next]
2 CONT ii. ALICE DE AUDLEY, married (1st) RALPH BASSETT, of Drayton, Staffordshire [see SHIRLEY8]: (2nd) HUGH DE MEYNELL, Knt., of Langley Meynell (in Kirk Langley), Derbyshire [see SHIRLEY 8].74
2 CONT 8. '''JAMES DE AUDLEY, Knt., 2nd Lord Audley, of Heleigh (in Audley), Staffordshire, Redcastle (in Weston), Edgmond, Ford, and Newport, Shropshire, Tackbear (in Bridgeule), Cornwall, Barnstaple, Bovey Tracey, Combe Martin, Drtingt
2 CONC on, Fremington, Holsworthy, Kingston, Marwell (in Ringmore), Langacre (in Broadclyst), North Holne, Nymet Tracey, South Molton, and Torrington, Devon, Blagdon, Downend, Nether Stowey, Puriton, Staunton, and West Lydford, Somerset,
2 CONC  etc.''', son and heir, born at Kneesal, Nottinghamshire 7 Jan. 1313/4. He was co-heir in 1326 to his mother's brother, William Martin, by which he inheritted the barony of Barnstaple, 
2 CONT https://books.google.com.au/books?id=kjme027UeagC&pg=PA150#v=onepage&q&f=false
2 CONT Pg. 150
2 CONT Devon, and the castle and lordship of Newport (in Camois), Pembrokeshire, Wales. He was also heir in 1322 to his great uncle, John Giffard, Knt., 2nd Lord Gifford, by which he inherited a moiety share of the manor oa Ashton, Wilts
2 CONC hire. He was summoned to Parliament from 25 Jan 1329/30 to 8 Aug. 1386. he married (1st) before 13 June 1330 JOAN DE MORTIMER, daughter of Roger de Mortimer, Knt., 1st Earl of March (descendant of King John), by Joan, daughter and
2 CONC  co-heiress of Peter de Geneville (or Joinville), Knt. [see MORTIMER 8 for her ancestry]. They had two sons, Nicholas, Knt. [3rd Lord Audley] and Roger, Knt., and two daughters, Joan and Margaret. His wife, Joan, was living in 133
2 CONC 7. he was heir in 1343 to his aunt, Eleanor Martin, widow of Philip de Columbers, Knt., Lord Columbers. In 1343 he sued William de Champernoun for the next presentation to the church of Ilfracombe, Devon. '''He married (2nd) befor
2 CONC e 1346-7 (date of settlement) ISABEL ____, said to be daughter of''' Robert Fitz Walker, Lord Fitz Walter. '''They had four sons''', James, Knt., Thomas, Knt., Oliver, and Roland, and one daughter Blanche. '''In 1346-7 he settled 
2 CONC the manors of Combe Martin, Nymet Tracey, and Bovey Tracey, Devon on himself and Isabel his wife''', with remainder to James their son and the heirs male of his body. He presented to the churches of Blagdon, Somerset, 1347 and 135
2 CONC 3, Christon, Somerset, 1362, and Spaxton, Somerset, 1362. His next heir in 1348 to his Longespee cousin, Alice de Lacy, Countess of Lancaster and Leicester. In 1348 he conveyed half an acre of land in Marchamley, Shropshire to Joh
2 CONC n de Wottenhull, clrk, and common pasture for his animals in the demsne of the said vill. '''In 1353 he and his wife, Isabel, settled the manors of Nymet Tracy (in Bow) and North Holne, Devon on themselves and the heirs of their b
2 CONC ody. The same year they settled the manors of Barnstple, Combe Martin, Dartington, Fremington, and Sourth Molton, Devon, together with the hundreds of Fremington and Sourth Molton, Devon, on themselves for life, with reversion to 
2 CONC their son''' James. They same year he settle the manor of Blagdon, Lydford, and Staunton, Somerset, Bovey Tracey, North Lew, Holsworthy, and Langacre (in Broadclyst), and Tackbear (in Bridgerule), Cornwall on himself for life, wit
2 CONC h reversion to the king. In 1353-4 he quitclaimed the manor of Winkleigh, Devon to the king. He presented to the church of Fremington, Devon in 1354. He was summoned to a Council in 1359. by writ directed Jacobo de Audeley, de Hee
2 CONC lee. '''In 1359 he and his wife, Isabel, settled the manors of Kingston and Marwell (in Ringmore), Devon on themselves for life, with successive remainders to their sons''', Oliver, Thomas, and Roland. In 1359-60 he settle a 1/5th
2 CONC  share of the manor of Chipping Torrington, Devon on Fulk Corbet for life. '''In 1360-1 he settled a 1/5th share of the manor of Kilmington, Devon on himself and his wife, Isabel, and the heirs of their bodies, and the site of a m
2 CONC ill and lind in Nymet St. George, Devon on Robert Hacche. His wife Isabel, was living in 1366'''. In 1374 he sued John de Chesturton, Roger Vernon and four others for forcibly breaking into his parks at Heleigh and Audley, Staffor
2 CONC dshire, and chasing and take his game. '''SIR JAMES DE AUDLEY, 2nd Lord Audley, died''' at Heleigh (in Audley), Staffordshire 1 April 1386, and was buried at Hulton Abbey, Staffordshire. He left a will dated 1385.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT https://books.google.com.au/books?id=kjme027UeagC&pg=PA152#v=onepage&q&f=false
