2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000067697722841& Inquisitions Post Mortem] for Warin de Insula, knight. Writ: 30 June, 6 Richard II [1382]. He died on 28 June [1382]. Margaret wife of Thomas de Berkeleye, knight, h
2 CONC is daughter by Margaret, his wife, is their next heir. She is 20 years of age and more.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The manor of Charleton, Devon''' <br>
2 CONT According to an Inquest of 1276 ([https://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vol2/pp105-111 4 Ed. I. No. 165]), Reginald de Valletorta gave £20 worth of lands at Charleton, Devon, with his sister Isabel in free marriage 
2 CONC to Thomas Corbet, who gave the same to William Pipard, father of Thomas Pipard with his daughter Katherine in free marriage. William Pipard died seised of Charleton in 1267 ([https://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vo
2 CONC l1/pp203-208 Inq. 51 Hen. III. No. 650]), and was succeeded by his son, Thomas Pipard, who died seised of it in 1283 ([http://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vol2/pp276-284 Inq. 11 Ed. I. No. 470]). In 1300 William, s
2 CONC on of Thomas Pipard, died seised of it and was succeeded by his uncle John Pipard. ([http://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vol3/pp447-456 Inq. 28 Ed. I. No. 598]). In 1365 Margery, widow of William Pipard, died seise
2 CONC d of it ([http://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vol11/pp452-468 Inq. 38 Ed. III. No. 597]) ; in 1376 Margaret, wife of Warin de Lisle, died seised of it ([https://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vol14/pp
2 CONC 164-182 Inq. 49 Ed. III. No. 151]) ; and in 1383 Warin de Lisle, died seised of it ([https://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vol15/pp307-319 Inq. 6 Ric. II. No. 807]). Taken from "Report & Transactions of the Devonshi
2 CONC re Association Vol 43" with alterations & additions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charleton continued down through Berkeley, Beauchamp, Talbot and Grey descendants until 1519 when Elizabeth Grey, Viscountess Lisle, wife of Henry Courtenay 1st Marquess of Exeter died without children. Elizabeth was succeeded by 
2 CONC her aunt, another Elizabeth Grey, who married 1st Edmund Dudley and 2nd Arthur Plantagenet ([http://www.british-history.ac.uk/letters-papers-hen8/vol3/pp72-77 Letters and Papers Hen. VIII Vol. 3 No. 220]). In 1531/2 Elizabeth's gr
2 CONC andson John Dudley, knight, later 1st Duke of Northumberland sold the manor in fee farm (sale of the freehold subject to a fixed annual payment) to Henry Courtenay, Marquess of Exeter ([http://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/det
2 CONC ails/r/C5521632 Ancient Deeds 23 Hen.VIII]; [http://www.british-history.ac.uk/letters-papers-hen8/vol5/pp415-430 Letters and Papers Henry VIII Vol. 5 No. 897] & [http://www.british-history.ac.uk/letters-papers-hen8/vol13/no2/pp409
2 CONC -426 Vol. 13 Part 2 No. 990]). In 1538 the Marquess of Exeter was attained for high treason and all his estates were forfeited to the crown. Arthur Plantagenet, Lord Lisle wrote to Cromwell begging to have possession of Charleton 
2 CONC and other manors during his life if the Marquess of Exeter was found guilty ([http://www.british-history.ac.uk/letters-papers-hen8/vol13/no2/pp409-426 Letters and Papers Hen. VIII Vol. 13 Part 2 No. 990]). Courtenay was found guil
2 CONC ty, however, in 1540 Lord Lisle was also arrested for treason. He languished in the Tower of London for two years until the king decided to release him. Upon receiving the news he suffered a heart attack and died two days later. I
2 CONC n 1553 Charleton was granted to William Herbert, 1st Earl of Pembroke ([https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?q1=%22Charleton%2C%20Devon%22;id=msu.31293027026412;view=image;seq=175;start=1;sz=10;page=search;num=169 Cal. Patent Rolls
2 CONC . Vol. 5 1547-1553. p. 169]) and in 1561 he sold it to John Peter of Exeter, merchant for £1200 ([http://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/fdb51ba2-e2ac-45fc-a21b-9b083f7a2ac1 Bargain and Sale 69/M/2/46]). Researched by 
2 CONC Charlene Newport www.findagrave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT BARONESS LISLE of Kingston Lisle and BARONESS TEYES; Margaret de Lisle was born in the little village of Kingston Lisle, to Warin, de Lisle, the 2nd Baron de Lisle and Margaret Pypard.
2 CONT
2 CONT www.wikipedia.com
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1367 Thomas married Margaret de Lisle, 3rd Baroness Lisle  (1360-1392), daughter of Warine de Lisle, 2nd Baron Lisle  (d.1382) and Margaret Pipard.
