2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *1.[S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 966. Hereinafter cited as Burke's Pe
2 CONC erage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *''''''A genealogical and heraldic dictionary of the landed gentry of ..., Volume 3 By John Burke, Sir Bernard Burke
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=9NDTAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA83&lpg=PA83&dq=Hugh+Croft&source=bl&ots=kiIv9n5r9F&sig=NH4j3CCGVOca2jh1qT179fVbxs8&hl=en&ei=-Kx3Te-RHoe-sAOxls3IBA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=2&ved=0CBgQ6AEwATgy#v=onep
2 CONC age&q=Hugh%20Croft&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 83
2 CONT *''''''SIR JOHN DE CROFT,* Knt. of Croft Castle, co. Hereford, Capt. of Merk Castle, near Calais who was frequently employed in negotiations in Flanders, between 1402 and 1404. He m. Janet, dau. and presumed co-heir of Owen ap Gri
2 CONC ffith Vychan, Lord of Wlyndowrdwy, in Merionethshire, the renowned OWEN GLENDOWER, representative of the Princess of Powys, (see HUGHES OF GWERCLAS,) and was father of WILLIAM DE CROFT, whose son was
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *''''''The gentleman's magazine, Volume 5
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=u1NIAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA90&lpg=PA90&dq=Hugh+Croft&source=bl&ots=3_sQ3RJ6XE&sig=2g0U5twBFqHXiqAeVFNoYArmuBY&hl=en&ei=-Kx3Te-RHoe-sAOxls3IBA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CBYQ6AEwADgy#v=onep
2 CONC age&q=Hugh%20Croft&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 90
2 CONT *Obituary. -- Sir T. E. Croft, Bart.
2 CONT *SIR THOMAS ELMSLEY CROFT, BART.
2 CONT * Nov. 29. At Hastings, aged 37, Sir Thomas Elmsley Croft, Bart. the eldest son that survived of Sir Richard Croft, M.D. the sixth Baronet, by Margaret, daughter of Dr. Thomas Denman, and sister of the present Lord Denman, the Lor
2 CONC d Chief Justice.
2 CONT *The family of Croft* is one of the most ancient and distringuished of English gentry. The immediate ancestor of the late Baronet, Bernard de Croft, is recorded in Domesay-book as having held the tenement of Croft, afterwards Crof
2 CONC t-Castle in Herfordshire, before the Conquest. His descendant Sir Hugh de Croft was created a Knight of the Bath in the 33rd Edward I. and represented the county of Hereford in Parliament in 1315. His great grandson ''''''Sir John
2 CONC  de Croft, was frequently employed on diplomatic missions, married Janet, third daughter and co-heir of the renowned Owen Glendower''''''. Their grandson Sir Richard Croft, Knight Banneret, was a zealous adherent of the House of Y
2 CONC ork, and took the young Prince of Wales prisoner at the battle of Tewkesbury: he was Treasurer of Henry the Seventh's household, and Steward of that of Prince Arthur. His son Sir Edward frequently . . . . 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *''''''Croft Castle
2 CONT *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croft_Castle
2 CONT *  Croft Castle [1] is a manor house and associated buildings near the village of Yarpole in Herefordshire, England some 7 km (4 mi) to the north-west of Leominster (grid reference SO449655).
2 CONT *  11th century origin
2 CONT *A building has been on the site from the 11th century and it has from this time been the home of the Croft family and Croft Baronets.
2 CONT *The Croft family were closely linked to their neighbours the Mortimers of Wigmore and later Ludlow. The Battle of Mortimer's Cross took place on Croft lands nearby in 1461.
2 CONT *The present building originated as a castle in the 14th century and has been much altered since.
2 CONT *''''''It was the home of a John Croft who married one of Owain Glyndwr's daughters. In the 15th century the Croft family adopted the Welsh Wyvern crest, a wounded black dragon, seen as a subtle allusion to their Glyndwr heritage.
2 CONT *Croft Castle was restored after slighting in the Civil War.
2 CONT *Some members of this Croft family
2 CONT *Sir Richard Croft (1429/30-1509), royal official for Kings Edward IV, Edward V, *Richard III, and Henry VII
2 CONT *Thomas Croft (c.1435-1488), shipowner and patron of Atlantic exploration
2 CONT *Sir James Croft (c.1518-1590), lord deputy of Ireland and leading conspirator in Wyatt's Rebellion
2 CONT *Herbert Croft (1603-1691), bishop of Hereford, chaplain to King Charles I and dean of the chapels Royal to Charles II
2 CONT *William Croft (c.1678-1727), organist and composer
2 CONT *Sir Herbert Croft (1751-1816), writer and lexicographer
2 CONT *Sir Richard Croft (1762-1818), physician and man-midwife
2 CONT *Sir Henry Page Croft (1881-1947), 1st Baron Croft, soldier and politician, Under-Secretary of State for War 1940-1945
2 CONT *Sir James Herbert Croft (1907-1941), died on active service with No 1 Commando
2 CONT *Andrew Croft (1906-1998), explorer and member of Special Operations Executive
2 CONT *Manor house
2 CONT *It now consists of a stone quadrangular manor house with a small castellated round tower at each corner [2] and a small square tower flanking the north side [3]. The castle is under the care of the National Trust and members of t
2 CONC he Croft family still live within it.
2 CONT *The main building shares some similarities to Treago Castle, also in Herefordshire.
2 CONT *The church
2 CONT *The castle and 13th century St. Michaels church adjacent [4], lie in 1500 acres (6 km²) of glorious Herefordshire countryside.
2 CONT *Inside the church is the fine altar tomb of Sir Richard Croft (1430-1509), high official to four monarchs and his wife born Eleanor Cornewall, before her remarriage the widow of Sir Hugh Mortimer of Kyre Wyard and Martley Worcest
2 CONC ershire, killed in action at the Battle of Wakefield.
2 CONT *External links 
2 CONT *Croft Castle and Parkland - Official site at National Trust
2 CONT *Croft Castle on Herefordshire Council website
2 CONT *Croft Castle on Castle UK website
2 CONT *Croft Castle on Castlewales
2 CONT *BBC Hereford & Worcester feature including 360 panoramic of Croft Castle
