0 @N6000000002188658154@ NOTE Philip Basset
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip Basset
1 CONT Chief Justiciar of England
1 CONT In office
1 CONT May 1261 – July 1263[1]
1 CONT Monarch Henry III
1 CONT Preceded by Hugh le Despencer, 1st Baron le Despencer
1 CONT Succeeded by Hugh le Despencer, 1st Baron le Despencer[1]
1 CONT Born c. 1185[2]
1 CONT Died 19 October 1271[2]
1 CONT Political party Royal
1 CONT Spouse Hawise, sister of Archbishop Walter de Gray
1 CONT Relations Hugh le Despencer, 1st Baron le Despencer, Son-in-law
1 CONT Children Aline Basset
1 CONT Margery Basset[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip Basset (c.1185 - 19 October 1271) was the Justiciar of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip was the son of Alan Basset of High Wycombe in Buckinghamshire and his wife, Alice, the daughter of Stephen Gray. He inherited the manor of Wycombe and served as the Justiciar of England between the two terms served by his s
1 CONC on-in-law, Hugh le Despencer, 1st Baron le Despencer.[3] He served during the period that Henry III regained control of the government from the Barons.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Hugh le Despencer, 1st Baron le Despencer Chief Justiciar
1 CONT 1261-1263 Succeeded by
1 CONT Hugh le Despencer, 1st Baron le Despencer
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] reference
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ a b Susan Higginbotham. "The Last Justiciar: Hugh le Despenser in the Thirteenth Century" (html). Retrieved on 2008-02-15.
1 CONT    2. ^ a b c "Basset of England" (html). geneajourney.com. Retrieved on 2008-03-03.
1 CONT    3. ^ "TITLE OF "JUSTICIAR" (PRIME MINISTER)" (html). Baronial Order of Magna Charta. Retrieved on 2008-02-15.
