0 @N6000000002187905131@ NOTE Llywelyn the Great
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn the Great  
1 CONT Reign c. 1195 - 11 April 1240 
1 CONT Predecessor Dafydd ab Owain 
1 CONT Successor Dafydd ap Llywelyn 
1 CONT Spouse Joan, Lady of Wales 
1 CONT Issue Dafydd ap Llywelyn
1 CONT Gruffydd ap Llywelyn
1 CONT Elen ferch Llywelyn
1 CONT Gwladus Ddu
1 CONT Marared ferch Llywelyn
1 CONT Gwenllian ferch Llywelyn
1 CONT Angharad ferch Llywelyn
1 CONT Susanna ferch Llywelyn 
1 CONT Royal House Aberffraw 
1 CONT Father Iorwerth Drwyndwn 
1 CONT Mother Marared ferch Madog 
1 CONT Born c. 1173 
1 CONT Died 11 April 1240 
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn the Great (Welsh Llywelyn Fawr, IPA pronunciation ??wel??n), full name Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, (c. 1173 – April 11, 1240) was a Prince of Gwynedd in North Wales and eventually de facto ruler over most of Wales. He is someti
1 CONC mes called Llywelyn I of Wales.[1] By a combination of war and diplomacy he dominated Wales for forty years, and was one of only two Welsh rulers to be called 'the Great'.
1 CONT
1 CONT During Llywelyn's boyhood Gwynedd was ruled by two of his uncles, who had agreed to split the kingdom between them following the death of Llywelyn's grandfather, Owain Gwynedd, in 1170. Llywelyn had a strong claim to be the legiti
1 CONC mate ruler and began a campaign to win power at an early age. He was sole ruler of Gwynedd by 1200, and made a treaty with King John of England the same year. Llywelyn's relations with John remained good for the next ten years. He
1 CONC  married John's illegitimate daughter Joan in 1205, and when John arrested Gwenwynwyn ab Owain of Powys in 1208 Llywelyn took the opportunity to annex southern Powys. In 1210 relations deteriorated and John invaded Gwynedd in 1211
1 CONC . Llywelyn was forced to seek terms and to give up all his lands east of the River Conwy, but was able to recover these lands the following year in alliance with the other Welsh princes. He allied himself with the barons who force
1 CONC d John to sign Magna Carta in 1215. By 1216 he was the dominant power in Wales, holding a council at Aberdyfi that year to apportion lands to the other princes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following King John's death, Llywelyn concluded the Treaty of Worcester with his successor Henry III in 1218. During the next fifteen years Llywelyn was frequently involved in fighting with Marcher lords and sometimes with the kin
1 CONC g, but also made alliances with several of the major powers in the Marches. The Peace of Middle in 1234 marked the end of Llywelyn's military career as the agreed truce of two years was extended year by year for the remainder of h
1 CONC is reign. He maintained his position in Wales until his death in 1240, and was succeeded by his son Dafydd ap Llywelyn.
1 CONT
