0 @N6000000002188011969@ NOTE William Maudit, 8th Earl of Warwick
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT William Maudit (or Mauduit), 8th Earl of Warwick (abt 1220 – 8 January 1267), was an English nobleman and participant in the Barons' War.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Alice de Beaumont (daughter of the 4th Earl) and William de Maudit, and so himself the grandson of Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick. His father was the lord of Hanslape and hereditary chamberlain of the e
1 CONC xchequer, a title that went back to another William Maudit who held that office for Henry I.
1 CONT
1 CONT He adhered to Henry III in the wars with the barons. He was surprised in his own castle, Warwick Castle by John Giffard, the governor of Kenilworth Castle. The walls of the castle were destroyed and the countess taken prisoner to 
1 CONC Kenilworth, and only released on payment of a ransom nineteen hundred marks.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died without issue and the estates then passed to his sister Isabel de Maudit who married William de Beauchamp and inherited the title of the Earl of Warwick by right of his wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article or section includes a list of references or external links, but its sources remain unclear because it lacks inline citations.
1 CONT You can improve this article by introducing more precise citations where appropriate. (February 2008)
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Beaumont, J.P., Edward T.. The Beaumonts in History. A.D. 850-1850. Oxford. 
1 CONT     * Hollister, C. Warren (1978). "The Origins of the English Treasury". English Historical Review 73: 262–275. doi:10.1093/ehr/XCIII.CCCLXVII.262. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * FMG on William Mauduit
1 CONT
1 CONT Peerage of England
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Margaret de Newburg and
1 CONT
1 CONT John du Plessis
1 CONT Earl of Warwick
1 CONT 1253 – 1267 Succeeded by
1 CONT William de Beauchamp
