0 @N6000000002188684157@ NOTE Eleanor de Montfort
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor de Montfort, Princess of Wales and Lady of Snowdon (1252 - 19 June 1282) was a daughter of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester and Countess Eleanor of England.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 Marriage to Llywelyn ap Gruffydd
1 CONT     * 3 Capture and imprisonment by Edward I
1 CONT     * 4 Married life
1 CONT     * 5 Death and legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor's maternal grandparents were John of England and his queen consort Isabella of Angoulême. Her maternal uncles included Henry III of England and Richard, 1st Earl of Cornwall. Her maternal aunts included Joan of England, Qu
1 CONC een of Scotland, and Isabella of England, and Joan, Lady of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Eleanor was thirteen years old, her father Earl Simon and brother Lord Henry were killed at the Battle of Evesham (4 August 1265). According to the chroniclers, Nicholas Trivet, William Rishanger and others, Earl Simon had ea
1 CONC rlier made an alliance with Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, whereby it was agreed that Llywelyn and Eleanor would marry. After Earl Simon's death, his family was forced to flee the Kingdom of England: Countess Eleanor took her daughter to t
1 CONC he safety of the Dominican nunnery at Montargis, France, a Montfort foundation.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage to Llywelyn ap Gruffydd
1 CONT
1 CONT Countess Eleanor died in Spring 1275, and shortly afterwards Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, the Prince of Wales, and Eleanor de Montfort married by proxy, (per nuncios) per verba de presenti (Canon law endorsed a marital bond that was made
1 CONC  in this way, with the full consent of both of the individuals, before witnesses).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Capture and imprisonment by Edward I
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor began the sea voyage from France to north Wales, avoiding making a land passage through England. The two ships carrying Eleanor, her brother Amaury and their entourage, sailing off the south coast of England, were captured
1 CONC  by sailors from the port of Bristol, just off the Isles of Scilly. Six named men together with the crews of four ships of Bristol were rewarded with a payment of 220 marks (Calendar Patent Rolls, 1272-81, 161; PRO Liberate Rolls 
1 CONC C62). 'Thomas Larchdeacon', 'Thomas the Archdeacon', who masterminded the capture on behalf of her first cousin Edward I of England was paid £20 in May 1276 by the king's orders, through the sheriff of Cornwall. (Calendar of Close
1 CONC  Rolls, 1272-79, 292).
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor was taken by ship to Bristol, then held prisoner at Windsor for nearly three years (PRO: Liberate Rolls, C62/ 52). In 1278, following the signing of the Treaty of Aberconwy, she was released.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Married life
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor and Llywelyn were formally married (secundum formam ecclesie) at the cathedral door, as was the custom, of the cathedral church at Worcester, on the Feast Day of St Edward, 1278; Edward gave the bride, his cousin, away and
1 CONC  paid for the wedding feast. Before the wedding mass was celebrated, Edward insisted that Llywelyn should put his seal to an adjustment to the agreement that they had previously made. Llywelyn had no alternative but to comply, and
1 CONC  he later stated that he did it under duress, 'moved by the fear that can grip a steadfast man' (see Registrum Epistolarum Fratis Johannis Peckham Archiepiscopi Cantuariensis, Lambeth Palace Archives).
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the ceremony, Eleanor became officially known as Princess of Wales and Lady of Snowdon. (see Calendar of Patent Rolls, 1272-81, 306; CPR, 1281-92, 11; Calendar of Ancient Correspondence, 75-76; Foedera I, ii, 576, 584, 5
1 CONC 87).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death and legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor died in childbirth on 19 June 1282 (see The chronicle of Bury St Edmunds, 74-76) at the royal home Garth Celyn, Aber Garth Celyn on the north coast of Gwynedd; her body was carried across the Lafan Sands to the Franciscan 
1 CONC Friary of Llanfaes, Anglesey (sse Brut y Tywysogion, Peniarth MS20, 223; Peniarth MS20Tr, 117). The Friary, on the opposite shore of the Menai to Garth Celyn, had been founded by llywelyn Fawr, the grandfather of Llywelyn ap Gruff
1 CONC udd, in memory of his wife Joan.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 12 July 1282, members of Eleanor's personal household were given 'safe-conduct' to travel to England (see Calendar of Welsh Rolls, 234).
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn was killed on 11 December 1282. The child, Gwenllian of Wales, was captured the following year by the armies of King Edward I of England, and taken to be held at Sempringham Priory in Lincolnshire.
0 @N6000000003658776268@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT ELEANOR DE MONTFORT, born about 1258.  In 1275 she married by proxy LLYWELYN AP GRUFFYDD, Prince of Wales, lord of Snowdon, 2nd son of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, by his wife, Senena.  At the close of 1275, she set out with her brother,
1 CONC  Amaury, for WAles, but their ship was captured in the Bristol Channel.  Eleanor was subsequently imprisoned at Bristol, and afterwards at Windsor Castle.  She was released in 1278, when Llywelyn submitted to King Edward I.  She m
1 CONC arried Llywelyn at Worcester in a public ceremony 13 Oct. 1278 in King Edward I's presence.  They had one daughter, Gwenllian (nun at Sempringham).  Between 1256 and 1257 Llywelyn reunited North Wales from the Dovey to the Dee, an
1 CONC d annexed extensive territories in the middle marches.  In 1258 he declared himself Prince of Wales.  He was defeated in the war of 1277, which left him only Gwynedd west of Conway.  His wife, Eleanor, died in childbirth 19 June 1
1 CONC 282.  LLYWELYN AP GRUFFYDD, Prince of Wales, was killed 11 Dec. 1282, while campaigning in the Builth region.  His body was interred by the Cistercians of Cwn Hir.
0 @N6000000002188684175@ NOTE Gwenllian of Wales
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gwenllian ferch Llywelyn (June 12, 1282 – June 7, 1337) was the child of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, the last native Tywysog Cymru (Prince of Wales). She is sometimes confused with Gwenllian ferch Gruffudd, who lived two centuries earli
1 CONC er.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Lineage
1 CONT     * 2 At the mercy of King Edward I
1 CONT     * 3 Confinement in the seclusion of Lincolnshire priories
1 CONT     * 4 Confinement for fifty years
1 CONT     * 5 Old age & death
1 CONT     * 6 Fate of her male cousins
1 CONT     * 7 Primary source references
1 CONT     * 8 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Lineage
1 CONT
1 CONT Gwenllian (pronounced gwen-LLEE-an) was born in the Welsh royal home Garth Celyn, Aber Garth Celyn (now known as Pen y Bryn Abergwyngregyn) near Bangor, Gwynedd, on or about June 19, 1282. Her mother was Llywelyn's wife, Eleanor d
1 CONC e Montfort, who died giving birth to the baby. She was descended from dual Royal bloodlines; not only was she the daughter of the reigning Welsh Tywysog, but her maternal great-grandfather was King John of England, making Gwenllia
1 CONC n a member of the House of Plantagenet.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] At the mercy of King Edward I
1 CONT
1 CONT Just a few months after Gwenllian's birth, on 11 December 1282, with north Wales encircled by the massive army of Edward I of England, her father Llywelyn, lured into a trap, was put to death. Her uncle, Tywysog Dafydd ap Gruffudd
1 CONC , assumed guardianship of the baby, but in June 1283 he and his family were captured at 'Nanhysglain,' a secret hiding place in a bog by Bera mountain in the uplands above Garth Celyn. Tywysog Dafydd, 'severely injured,' was taken
1 CONC  to Edward, then moved under guard to Shrewsbury, where he was later executed.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Confinement in the seclusion of Lincolnshire priories
1 CONT
1 CONT Gwenllian, together with the daughters of Tywysog Dafydd, were confined for life in remote, secluded priories in Lincolnshire. It is to be supposed that they were spared due to their young ages; Gwenllian was barely a year old. Gw
1 CONC enllian's Plantagenet descent probably worked in her favour as well.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Confinement for fifty years
1 CONT
1 CONT Gwenllian was placed in the Gilbertine Priory at Sempringham, where she was held behind its high walls until her death 54 years later. In committing her to a convent, Edward's aim was not only to prevent her marrying and having ch
1 CONC ildren who might claim the Principality but also to hide her from the outside world, lest she become a symbol for rebellion by the Welsh. He chose the Gilbertines in Lincolnshire because of their remote location and because they w
1 CONC ere an order with large communities, where the children would not stand out. It has been speculated by archaeologists involved in the excavations at Sempringham that the girls may have been taken there by sea rather than land; Sem
1 CONC pringham at that time was close to the coast. (This would have reduced the risk of ambush or kidnap by supporters of the princes of Gwynedd.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Only once in records was Gwenllian's royal rank ever acknowledged. When writing to the Pope, attempting to secure more money for the Sempringham priory from the Church, the English King noted that "...herein is kept the Princess o
1 CONC f Wales, whom we have to maintain." It is difficult to know whether Gwenllian herself ever truly understood her status or importance; what is known is that no one ever taught her how to spell her own name. Possibly the English nun
1 CONC s had no idea themselves. Records show the princess writing her name alternately as "Wencilane" and "Wentliane."
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Old age & death
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward III of England, Edward I's grandson, endowed Gwenllian with a pension of £20 per year, the sum simply needed to pay the priory for her board and lodgings. Her death was recorded by the priory's chronicler Piers Langtoft.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Fate of her male cousins
1 CONT
1 CONT Dafydd's two young sons, direct heirs to the Principality/Kingdom of Wales, were taken to Bristol Castle where they were held prisoner; Llywelyn ap Dafydd died there in 1287, four years after capture, and was buried in the Dominic
1 CONC an Church; Owain ap Dafydd survived his brother; the King of England ordered a cage made of timber, bound with iron, in which to hold the prince Owain ap Dafydd more securely at night. Owain was never released from imprisonment.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Edward I took the title of "Prince of Wales" for the Crown, bestowing it upon his son, Edward, who was crowned in Caernarfon in 1301 at age 17. Hence the title passed as a grace title bestowable by the English, and later Brit
1 CONC ish, monarchy to this day.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Primary source references
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Calendar of Patent Rolls, 1281-92, 321 ((Inquiry of 1289 concerning the custody of the Welsh royal children)
1 CONT     * Calendar of Papal Letters, ii, 185, 273
1 CONT     * Calendar of Memoranda Rolls, 1226-7, no. 2160
1 CONT     * Calendar of Close Rolls, 1327-30, 65, 175, 273, 322, 438
1 CONT     * Public Record Office, London E101/351/9 (Letter, noting provision made for the needs of the Welsh royal children, 11 November 1283)
1 CONT     * Calendar Ancient Petitions, 458 (letter from Gwenllian)
1 CONT     * Robert Manning (a canon at Sempringham then at Sixhills) see The Works of Thomas Hearne, 4 vols (London, 1810)
1 CONT     * Annales Prioratus de Dunstaplia, 293-4
1 CONT     * Accounts of Bristol Castle
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * The The Princess Gwenllian Society has been formed in honour of this princess, and a memorial to her has been erected on the site of the former priory at Sempringham.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * The Aber Trust / Ymddiriedolaeth Aber owns and is restoring the medieval gatehouse at Garth Celyn, Aber Garth Celyn, the place of Gwenllian's birth.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Music composed about Princess Gwenllian includes Llio Rhydderch's harp suite Gwenllian and Toby Griffen's song, Gwenllian's Lament/Cradlesong
