0 @N6000000001220390322@ NOTE [mebl.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT One source indicates none of these daughters is proven except Elen/Helen.[beaufort.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT ES III:356b credits John with two illegitimate children by Agatha de Ferre rs
1 CONT - Joan and Richard.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Magna Charta Barons" by Charles H. Browning, copyright 1969 (I'm unsu re of the overall reliability of this source), gives Joan's mother as Agat ha de Ferrers, daughter of Robert de Ferrers, fourth Earl of Derby.
1 CONT
1 CONT The journal "The Plantagenet Connection" has published an ahnentafel of El izabeth Plantagenet, wife of Henry VII, which gives Joan's mother's na me as Constance (Clementia) of Brittany.  This is the same Constance who w as marrie
1 CONC d to Geoffrey Plantagenet, John's brother.  Considering the well- known animosity between Constance and John and her attempts to press her o wn son Arthur's interests as far as the throne of England were concerned ( whom John murd
1 CONC ered with his own hands) the relationship seems unlikely.  T he Constance that history portrays does not seem as one who John could eas ily seduce or coerce into bed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Others suggest her mother was Clemantina, the wife of Henry Pinel.
1 CONT
1 CONT Penman, Sharon Kay.  "Here Be Dragons".  New York: Ballatine Books, June
1 CONT 1993.
1 CONT Penman, Sharon Kay.  "Falls The Shaow".  New York:  Ballatine Books, May
1 CONT 1989.
1 CONT Lewis, Marlyn (compiler for "The Plantagenet Connection").  "Ahnentafel fo r
1 CONT Elizabeth Plantagenet (Wife of Henry Tudor):  Generations 1-15".  Arvad a, CO:
1 CONT  Heliotrope Communications, 1996.
1 CONT Wurts, John C.  "Magna Charta".  Copyright 1945.
