0 @N6000000008640505548@ NOTE known 9 wives, 29 children, many dates approximate
1 CONT
1 CONT REF: British Monarchy Official Website: After William's death whilehunting in the New Forest in 1100, his younger brother, Henry I(reigned 1100-35), succeeded to the throne. By 1106 he had capturedNormandy from his brother, Robert
1 CONC , who then spent the last 28 years ofhis life as his brother's prisoner. An energetic and decisive ruler,Henry centralised the administration of England and Normandy in theroyal court, and extended royal powers of patronage.
0 @N6000000008799888167@ NOTE [Jeremiah Brown.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [more information in the royal database: http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/cgi-bin/gedlkup/n=royal?royal01391]
1 CONT
1 CONT (from Pemble website http://www.my-ged.com/db/page/pemble/4999)
1 CONT
1 CONT Longest reign of any of the Norman kings of England, 35 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry played both sides in his brothers' quarrel. Consequently, they both mistrusted Henry and signed a pact barring Henry from the crown. Henry's hopes rose when Robert went with the crusades
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was in the woods hunting on August 2, 1100, when William died. Henry moved quickly and was crowned on August 5, 1100.  Robert was captured on his return from the holy land and spent the remaining 28 years of his life as Henr
1 CONC y's prisoner.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death in 1125 of his daughter Matilda's husband, Emporer Henry V of Germany, he forced the nobles to accept her as Queen on his death. She was then forced to marry the 16 year old Geoffrey of Anjou (founder of the Planta
1 CONC genet dynasty) to continue the Angevin alliance.  The marriage was unpopular among the Norman barons and Henry was forced to acquire another oath of Allegiance from them.
1 CONT
