0 @N6000000004533071058@ NOTE Some people have Hugh's death as 1120, and at least one other has"aft 1123".  I believe that his death may have been well after 1123,otherwise Beatrice would not have divorced him, and later marriedWilliam de Say.
1 CONT
1 CONT No printed source that I have indicates that Hugh married Emma deLacy. Emma's sister Agnes certainly m. Geoffrey Talbot.
0 @N6000000003216125741@ NOTE In early December 1074 Roger de Montgomery was created Earl of Shrewsbury or Shropshire. At this time earldoms tended to be for counties but many holders of the office took the name of the couty town as their title. Roger was the 
1 CONC son of another Roger de Montgomery who was seigneur of St. Germain-de-Montgomery in the Calvados region of Normandy. He was a prominent nobleman and one of a close group around William the Conqueror well before 1066. He stayed in 
1 CONC Normandy and did not take part in the invasion of England though a year after Hastings he crossed the Channel and received lands in Sussex. He is thought to have built the Castle of Montgomery (now in Powis) and the old Welsh coun
1 CONC ty of Montgomeryshire had its name from the family.His son, the 2nd Earl of Shrewsbury, was killed by a missile thrown from a Norse raiding party attacking the coast of Anglesey. he was termed as "Gentle" by the English and Norman
1 CONC s but "the Hammer" by the Welsh. On his death the title passed to his brother, Robert de Belleme.  It was he who constructed Bridgnorth Castle. He foolishly rebelled against Henry I in 1102 and was deprived of the earldom and all 
1 CONC his English and Welsh lands.Many years passed before the Talbot Family were eventually granted the title.
0 @N6000000003949751066@ NOTE Younger son of William, Count of Exmes. Sometime after the death of his bro. Godfry, Count of Brionne and Eu, came into possession of the latter comte, by which name he was thereafter know. Hugh Taleboth, c. 1035, granted a charte
1 CONC r in
1 CONT favor of Trinite du Mont, Rouen, which was witnessed by Gilbert d'Eu. William Talbot, son of Hugh Taleboth, is mentioned in the foundation charter of Treport, executed in 1059 by Robert, Comte d'Eu. [Falaise Roll, p. 50]
