0 @N6000000002188658135@ NOTE Hugh le Despencer, 1st Baron le Despencer
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh le Despencer
1 CONT Chief Justiciar
1 CONT In office
1 CONT 1260 – May 1261[1]
1 CONT Monarch Henry III
1 CONT Preceded by Hugh Bigod
1 CONT Succeeded by Philip Basset[1]
1 CONT Chief Justiciar
1 CONT In office
1 CONT July 1263 – August 4, 1265[1]
1 CONT Monarch Henry III
1 CONT Preceded by Philip Basset
1 CONT Succeeded by none
1 CONT Born 1223
1 CONT Died August 4, 1265
1 CONT Evesham
1 CONT Political party Barons
1 CONT Spouse Aline Bassett
1 CONT Children Hugh le Despencer
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir Hugh (1223 – August 4, 1265) was an important ally of Simon de Montfort during the reign of Henry III. He served briefly as Justiciar of England in 1260 and as Constable of the Tower of London.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Hugh le Despenser I and was summoned to Parliament by Simon de Montfort, and so might be deemed a baron, though the legality of that assembly is doubtful. He remained allied with Montfort to the end, and was pres
1 CONC ent at the Battle of Lewes. He was killed fighting on de Montfort's side at the Battle of Evesham. He was slain by Roger Mortimer, 1st Baron Wigmore; this caused a feud to begin between the Despenser and Mortimer families.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his wife, Aline Bassett, he was father of Hugh the elder Despenser. She was the daughter of Philip Basset, who had also served as Justiciar.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ a b c Susan Higginbotham. "The Last Justiciar: Hugh le Despenser in the Thirteenth Century" (html). Retrieved on 2008-02-15.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
