0 @N6000000003219694282@ NOTE (Research):    TITLE: Earl of WINCHESTER
1 CONT     NICK: "The Elder"
1 CONT     NOTE:    ... the son of Hugh le Despenser [q. v.], the justiciar of the barons, who fell at Evesham, by his wife Aliva, daughter of Philip Basset, was born in 1262, for he was twenty-one on 1 March 1283. He served with Edmund,
1 CONC  earl of Cornwall, in the Welsh war, and soon afterwards was fined two thousand marks for marrying Isabel, daughter of William Beauchamp, earl of Warwick, and widow of Patrick of Chaworth, without the king's license. In 1294 he wa
1 CONC s with the king in Gascony, and the next year received a summons to parliament. He marched with Edward into Scotland, was present at the battle of Dunbar, took part in the expedition to Flanders in 1297, and was employed to treat 
1 CONC for peace between Edward and the king of the Romans and the king of France. The next year he again served in Scotland, and was sent on an embassy to Boniface VIII. He took part in other Scotch campaigns, and in the negotiations wi
1 CONC th France which preceded the peace of 1303. In 1305 he was sent to Clement V at Lyons, and obtained a bull absolving the king from the oaths which he had taken to his peop le. At the coronation of Edward II he carried part of the 
1 CONC royal insignia. When in 1308 the barons leagued themselves together against Gaveston, he stood alone in upholding the king's favourite. His conduct was put down to avarice, he was regarded as a deserter from the common cause, and 
1 CONC the parliament which met at Northampton procured his dismissal from the council (Vita Edwardi II, ii. 158; Annales Paulini, i. 264). His disgrace was not of long duration; he received the castles of Devizes and Marlborough, and be
1 CONC came the chief adviser of the king. In 1312 he was sent with Aymer de Valence, earl of Pembroke [q. v.], and others to endeavour to secure London for the king. The commissioners arrested some of the citizens, a tumult was raised, 
1 CONC and they were forced to leave the city (Annales Londondienses, i. 215). On the death of Gaveston, Despenser became the chief man of the court party, and encouraged the king to form plans of revenge against the barons. He was bitte
1 CONC rly hated by the Earl of Lancaster, and was excluded from the general pacification of 1313. He accompanied the king on his unfortunate expedition to Scotland in 1314, and when the defeat of Bannockburn placed Edward at the mercy o
1 CONC f Lancaster, was forced to withdraw from the court and the council. In 1318 the king seemed on the point of making a vigorous effort to overthrow the power of Lancaster, and Despenser, with the other lords of the same party, atten
1 CONC ded the parliament at Northampton armed, and at the head of his retainers. A pacification followed, greatly to the king's disadvantage, and he stood alone in refusing to bend to the earl's will. About this time his son, Sir Hugh l
1 CONC e Despenser [q. v.], joined the king's side. Both the Despensers received many large grants from the crown; they were generally hated, and were accused of many acts of oppression and wrongful dealing. Although both, and especially
1 CONC  the son, succeeded Gaveston in the royal favour, they had little in common with him. Unlike Gaveston, they were of noble family, and were connected with many great baronial houses. They held the most prominent place in the party 
1 CONC opposed to the unscrupulous designs of Lancaster, and sought their own advancement through alliance with the crown, while the earl carried on an equally selfish policy by thwarting and limiting the royal power. Greedy and ambitiou
1 CONC s, they used the influence they gained over the king for their own aggrandisement. The wealth and honours he showered upon them strengthened the hatred in which they were held. In the case of Gaveston, the hatred of the barons was
1 CONC  mixed with contempt for the upstart foreigner; in the case of the Despensers, it was near akin to fear. It appears impossible to decide whether the father or the son was the more to blame.
0 @N6000000003232501280@ NOTE Sir Hugh and his son were supporters of King Edward II and his favorite, Piers Gaveston, against the intrigues of Queen Isabella and Mortimer.  Both were executed when the Queen's armies were victorious
0 @N6000000003615871409@ NOTE Executed 
1 CONT The son of Sir Hugh Despenser (the elder). Hugh the younger was to pl
1 CONT ay an important role at the side of Edward II in military aspects a
1 CONT nd as an advisor. Initially Hugh has been on the side of the Ordainers w
1 CONT ho had drawnup Ordinances to limit the king's powers. After the dea
1 CONT th of Gaveston, Hugh had joined the king's supporters and became increasi
1 CONT ng close to him.
0 @N6000000003658791936@ NOTE From Magna Carta Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT ISABEL DE BEAUCHAMP, married (1st) PATRICK DE CHAWORTH, of Kidwelly, Carmarthenshire, Wales, Baron fo Kempsford, Gloucestershire. ... He died shortly before 7 July 1283.  She married (2nd) after 28 May 1281 (grant of his marriage)
1 CONC  HUGH LE DESPENSER, Knt., of Loughborough, Arnesby, Ryhall, etc., Wycombe, Compton-Basset, and Wootton-Basset, Maplederwell, Hampshire, Parlington, Barrowby, Hillam, and Shippen [Ho], Yorkshire, etc., Justice of the Forest South o
1 CONC f Trent, son and heir of Hugh le Despenser, Knt., of Loughborough, Leicetershire, by Aline, daughter and heiress of Philip Basset, Knt.  He was born 1 March 1260/1.  They had two sons ... and three daughters. ... In 1286 and again
1 CONC  in 1287, he appointed attorneys, he going beyond seas.  In 1289 he was going beyond seas with Roger le Bigod, Earl of Norfolk.  In 1293 he had license to enclosed 30 acres of wood adjoining his park at Vastern, Wiltshire.  IN 129
1 CONC 4 he was going beyond seas as the king's envoy.  He was summoned to Parliament from 24 June 1295 by writs directed Hugoni le Despenser.  In 1295 he was going beyond seas on the king's service.  The following year he was going beyo
1 CONC nd seas on an embassage for the king.  IN 1297 he was granted 20 oaks fit for timber by the king.  The same year he was going beyond seas with the king on his service.  In 1298 Maud le Barber of Garscherch testified in London cour
1 CONC t that Saer le Barber said that Sir Hugh le Despenser "kept more robbers with him than any man in England."  In 1300 he was going to the court of Rome on the king's special affairs.  In 1301 Ralph Pipard granted him the manor of G
1 CONC reat Haseley, Oxfordshire.  In Feb. 1306 Hugh received a papal indult to have a portable altar.  His wife, Isabel, died shortly before 30 May 1306.  IN the quarrel about Peter de Gavaston in 1308, Hugh alone sided with the king ag
1 CONC ainst the Barons, who induced the King to promise to dismiss Hugh from Court.  He was present at the Battle of Bannockburn 24 June 1314, and accompanied the King in his flight to Dunbar, and thence by sea to Berwick.  IN May and J
1 CONC une 1321 the Barons of the Welsh Marches and their adherents ravaged the ladns of the young Despenser in Wales and those of the elder throughout the country.  In August of that year both Despensers were accused by Parliament, chie
1 CONC fly on account of the son's misconduct, of many misdeeds, including appropriating royal power to themselves, counselling the King evilly, and replacing god ministers by bad ones.  They were then disinherited and exiled from the re
1 CONC alm.  The elder Hugh accordingly retired about to the Continent.  The sentence on the Despensers was pronounced unlawful at a provincial council of the clergy about 1 Jan. 1321/2.  In March folloiwng, the elder Hugh accompanied th
1 CONC e King against the contrariants, and was present at the judgement on the Earl of Lancaster.  The proceedings against the Despensers were annulled and cancelled, and his lands restored 7 May 1322.  He was created Earl of Winchester
1 CONC  10 May 1322, and granted £20 yearly from the issues of Hampshire, together with the castle and manor of Brimpsfield, Gloucestershire, and the manors of Badgeworth and Syde, Gloucestershire, Ashton Giffard, Codford St. Peter, Sher
1 CONC rington, and Stapleford, Wiltshire (formerly belonging to John Giffard), as well as other lands formerly belonging to Thomas Mauduit, Henry le Tyeys, Warin de Lisle, and John de Kyngeston.  On the King's flight to Wales in October
1 CONC  1326 the Earl was dispatched to defend Bristol, which, however, he at once surrendered on the arrival of the Queen.  The next day, 27 Oct. 1326, SIR HUGH LE DESPENSER, Earl of Winchester, was tried--without being allowed to speak
1 CONC  in his own defence--condemned to death as a traitor, and hanged on the common gallows, all his honours forfeited.
