0 @N6000000001353928734@ NOTE Roger de Mortimer, Lord of Wigmore, a feudal baron who, like his predecessors, was in constant strife with the Welsh. At one time he sustained a great defeat in conjunction with Hugh de Say, but in the end he was victorious and to
1 CONC ok twelve of their principal leaders in one battle. He also enlarged considerably his territories and drove thieves androbbers from those pars. Being at one time present at the solemn anniversary of his father, he confirmed all hi
1 CONC s grants to the canons of Wigmore, adding, of his own gift, a spacious and fruitful pasture lying adjacent to the abbey, called the Treasure of Mortimer, upon which occasion, his steward remonstrating with him for parting with so 
1 CONC valuablea treasure, he replied, "I have laid up my treasure in that field, where thieves cannot steal or dig, or moth corrupt."
