0 @N6000000002187959186@ NOTE Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick (1153 – 12 December 1204) was the younger son of Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick and Gundred de Warrenne, daughter of William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey and Elizabeth de Vermando
1 CONC is. He was also known as Waleran de Newburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his brother's death an impostor arose, claiming to be the deceased Earl; he gave Waleran a great deal of trouble in maintaining his claim. He does not appear to have been a great soldier, for he paid scutage money to escape 
1 CONC military service in Wales. His position in the Court is attested by his bearing the right hand Sword of State at the Coronation of King John, 27 May 1199.
1 CONT
1 CONT He liberally supported the hospital of St. Michael, Warwick and gave to the nuns of Pinley land at Claverdon, and land at Brailes to the nuns at Wroxall.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He married before 1190 Margaret, daughter of Henry de Bohun, 1st Earl of Hereford and Maud of Essex and had children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry de Beaumont, 5th Earl of Warwick, his heir. 
1 CONT Waleran de Beaumont of Gretham and Cotismore. 
1 CONT Gundred de Beaumont. She and her cousin Mabel became nuns at the Abbey of Pinley. 
1 CONT Alice de Beaumont (died before 1263), married William de Maudit, Baron of Hanslape, Chamberlain to the King. They children were: 
1 CONT William Maudit, 8th Earl of Warwick; 
1 CONT Isabel de Maudit, married William de Beauchamp, Baron Emley. Their son was William de Beauchamp, 9th Earl of Warwick. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Edward T. Beaumont, J.P. The Beaumonts in History. A.D. 850-1850. Oxfor
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William de Beaumont Earl of Warwick
1 CONT 1184 – 1204 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry de Beaumont 
0 @N6000000003051193088@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick", retrieved 23 Oct 07]
1 CONT Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick (1153 - 12 December 1204) was the younger son of Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick and Gundred de Warrenne, daughter of William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey and Elizabeth de Vermando
1 CONC is. He was also known as Walerian de Newburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his brother's death an impostor arose, claiming to be the deceased Earl; he gave Waleran a great deal of trouble in maintaining his claim. He does not appear to have been a great soldier, for he paid scutage money to escape 
1 CONC military service in Wales. His position in the Court is attested by his bearing the right hand Sword of State at the Coronation of King John, 27 May 1199.
1 CONT
1 CONT He liberally supported the hospital of St. Michael, Warwick and gave to the nuns of Pinley land at Claverdon, and land at Brailes to the nuns at Wroxall.
1 CONT
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He married first to Margery, daughter of Henry d'Oily and Maud de Bohun and had children:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. Henry de Beaumont, 5th Earl of Warwick, his heir.
1 CONT 2. Waleran de Beaumont of Gretham and Cotismore.
1 CONT 3. Gundred de Beaumont. She and her cousin Mabel became nuns at the Abbey of Pinley.
1 CONT
1 CONT His second wife was Alice de Harcourt, widow of John de Limesy, Lord of Cavendish, daughter of Robert de Harcourt and had one child:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. Alice de Beaumont (died before 1263), married William de Maudit, Baron of Hanslape, Chamberlain to the King. Their children were:
1 CONT a. William Maudit, 8th Earl of Warwick;
1 CONT b. Isabel de Maudit, married William de Beauchamp, Baron Emley. Their son was William de Beauchamp, 9th Earl of Warwick.
0 @N6000000003949681303@ NOTE Waleran de Beaumont [elder brother William, 3rd Earl of Warwick, dsp in the Holy Lands (15 Nov?) 1184], 4th Earl of Warwick; married 1st Margery, daughter of Humphrey de Bohun by Margaret, eldest daughter and ultimate coheir of 1s
1 CONC t Earl of Hereford of the 1141 creation. The 4th Earl of Warwick married 2nd c1196 Alice, apparently daughter of Robert de Harcourt, of Stanton (subsequently Stanton Harcourt), Oxon, though, if so either Alice and the 4th Earl's m
1 CONC arriage date of c1196 is far too early or her father's marriage date of c1200 is far too late, and widow of John de Limesy, and died 24 Dec 1203(?). [Burke's Peerage]   This nobleman, Dugdale says, "had much ado a great part of hi
1 CONC s time touching his inheritance; there starting up one who feigned himself to he his brother, Earl William, deceased in the Holy Land, which occasioned him no little trouble and vexation; so that it is thought by some that the gra
1 CONC nt which he made to Hubert, archbishop of Canterbury, then chancellor of England, of the advowson of all the prebendaries belonging to the collegiate church, in Warwick, to hold during his life, was to purchase his favour in that 
1 CONC weight business." His lordship m. 1st, Margery, dau. of Humphrey de Bohun, Earl of Hereford, by whom he had issue, Henry, his successor, Waleran, and Gundred. He m. 2ndly, Alice, dau. of John de Harcourt, and widow of John de Lime
1 CONC si, by whom he had an only dau., Alice. The earl d. in 1205 and was s. by his elder son, Henry de Newburgh. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 399, Newburg
1 CONC h, Earls of Warwick]
1 CONT Survived until Dec. 1204, and left a son, Henry, still a minor. [Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1961 ed., Vol. 23, pg. 375, EARLS OF WARWICK]   aka Waleran de Newburgh , 4th Earl of Warwick
1 CONT WALERAN, EARL OF WARWICK, brother and heir, being 2nd son of the 2nd Earl. He was with Henry II at Geddington, Northants, February 1187/8, was present at the Coronation of Richard I at Westminster, 3 September 1189, and was with t
1 CONC he King at Geddington later in the same month. In 1195 he paid 20 marks for livery of the 3rd penny of the pleas of co. Warwick, and in 1196 £100 to be allowed to return to England. In 1199 he swore fealty to King John and is said
1 CONC  to have carried the right hand sword at his Coronation. He granted for his life to Hubert, Archbishop of Canterbury, the advowson of all the prebendaries of the collegiate church in Warwick; he was a benefactor of the Hospital of
1 CONC  St. Michael, Warwick, of the nuns of Pinley, co. Warwick, and of those at Wroxall, Co. Warwick; and he confirmed the gift of his grandfather, Earl Henry, to Préaux.
1 CONT He married, 1stly, Margery, daughter of Humphrey DE BOHUN, by Margaret, 1st daughter and eventually coheir of MILES (of Gloucester), EARL OF HEREFORD and CONSTABLE OF ENGLAND. He married, 2ndly, circa 1196, Alice, widow of John DE
1 CONC  LIMESY, lord of Cavendish, Suffolk, daughter of Robert DE HARCOURT, of Bosworth, co. Leicester, and Stanton, Oxon., by Isabel, daughter and heir of Richard DE CAMVILLE, of Stanton. He died before 13 October 1204, it is said on 24
1 CONC  December 1203. Alice was living, September 1212. [Complete Peerage XII/2:363-4, (transcribed by Dave Utzinger)]
0 @N6000000005076821160@ NOTE Waleran de Newburgh, 4th Earl of Warwick was the son of Roger de Newburgh, 2nd Earl of Warwick and Gundred de Warenne.1 He married, secondly, Alice de Harcourt, daughter of John de Harcourt.1 He died in 1205.1
1 CONT      Waleran de Newburgh, 4th Earl of Warwick gained the title of 4th Earl of Warwick.
1 CONT Child of Waleran de Newburgh, 4th Earl of Warwick and Alice de Harcourt
1 CONT Alice de Newburgh+ 1
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S22] Sir Bernard Burke, C.B. LL.D., A Genealogical History of the Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire, new edition (1883; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland: Genealogical Publishing Company, 1978), p
1 CONC age 399. Hereinafter cited as Burkes Extinct Peerage.
