0 @N6000000003615512321@ NOTE  Despenser, Hugh le (1262-1326),  English nobleman, son of Hugh leDespenser. A supporter of the king against the barons, he fought underEdward I, for whom he secured a papal release from an oath to refrainfrom excessive taxation. 
1 CONC When the barons forced Edward's son andsuccessor, Edward II, to banish his favorite, Piers Gaveston, leDespenser supported Gaveston and in 1312 succeeded him as the royalfavorite. Edward II made him earl of Winchester in 1322, and
1 CONC  leDespenser, together with his son, Hugh, acquired vast wealth. Thebarons established a coalition to challenge Edward II and leDespenser, and open warfare broke out between the king and the barons.At the battle of Boroughbridge i
1 CONC n 1322, the king's forces defeated thecoalition. The elder le Despenser became virtual ruler of England,instituted several important administrative reforms, and concludedpeace with Scotland in 1323. In 1326 Isabella, queen consort
1 CONC  ofEngland, formed an alliance with the barons against Edward and the leDespensers in favor of Edward's and Isabella's son, young PrinceEdward. In a general uprising, the forces of Edward II were defeatedand both father and son le
1 CONC  Despenser were hanged.
1 CONT Source: "Despenser, Hugh le (1262-1326)," Microsoft(R) Encarta(R) 98Encyclopedia. (c) 1993-1997 Microsoft Corporation. All rightsreserved.
0 @N6000000003615534622@ NOTE Imported from &quot;The Tillotson Project&quot;: tilston@pe.net
0 @N6000000003615871446@ NOTE *  Titles Lord Despenser; 2nd Baron Despenser 
1 CONT *  Cause of Death Hanged, drawn, and quartered for his support of Edwa
1 CONT rd II
1 CONT DE SPENSER, HUGH (c. 1287 - 1326), courtier. Known as 'the younger' to dis
1 CONT tinguish him from his father, like whom he was a leading figure at Edwa
1 CONT rd II's court, he was clearly the King's favourite by 1321. His acquisitiv
1 CONT eness, especially in the Welsh Marches, led to the formation of an anti-De
1 CONT spenser coalition; after this was defeated at Boroughbridge (Yorks.) in 13
1 CONT 22, his greed and arrogance reached new heights. He alienated public opini
1 CONT on to such an extent that, when Roger Mortimer andIsabella of France inva
1 CONT ded in 1326, no one raised a finger to help him, and he and his father we
1 CONT re executed by the new regime.
0 @N6000000003658791905@ NOTE From Magna Carta Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT HUGH LE DESPENSER, Knt., styled "the younger", of Hanley Castle, Worcestershire, King's Chamberlain, Constable of Odiham Castle, Keeper of the castle and town of Dryslwyn, and Cantref Mawr, Carmarthenshire, Keeper of the castle an
1 CONC d town of Portchester, Keeper of the castle, town and barton of Bristol, Keeper of the castles, manor, and lands of Brecknock, Hay, Cantref Selyf, etc., Breconshire, and Huntington, Herefordshire, son and heir.  he married at West
1 CONC minster shortly after 14 June 1306 ELEANOR DE CLARE, daughter of Gilbert de Clare, Knt., Earl of Gloucester and Hertford, by his 2nd wife, Joan of Acre, daughter of Edward I, King of England. ... She was born at Caerphilly, Glamor
1 CONC gan in Oct. 1292.  They had four sons ... and five daughters. ... He accompanied the King to Pontoise in 1313.  She was co-heiress in 1314 to her brother, Gilbert de Clare, Knt., Earl of Gloucester and Hertford, by which she inher
1 CONC ited the lordship of Glamorgan in Wales, including the castles of Llanblethian, Kenfeg, Neath, Llantrisant, Caerphilly, and Whitchurch, and the manor of Stanford in the Vale, Berkshire.  He was summoned to Parliament from 29 July 
1 CONC 1314 to 10 Oct. 1325, by writs directed Hugoni le Despenser juniori.  From Nov. 1317 his influence at court rapidly increased, and, by the end of the following year, he won a lasting ascendancy over the king's mind.  In 1320 the k
1 CONC ing seized the lordship of Gower in Wales, as a means of conferring it on him.  The dispute over this seizure united a powerful group of Welsh marcher lords in a coalition against Hugh; moreover, his rule in Glamorgan was intensel
1 CONC y unpopular with the Welsh.  Civil war broke out May 4, 1321, and the lordship of Glamorgan was quickly overrun and devastated.  Hugh and his father were subsequently banished by Parliament 14 August 1321.  The royalist counter-of
1 CONC fensive against the baronial opposition began in Oct. 1321.  Following the royalist victory at the Battle of Boroughbridge 16 March 1321/2, Parliament in May 1322 reversed the sentences on the Despensers, who were allowed to retur
1 CONC n from exile.  Hugh recovered the lordships of Glamorgan and Gower.  From 1322 to 1326 he directed England's internal and foreign policy.  By fraud and violence, he accumulated enormous possessions in both England and Wales.  The 
1 CONC revolution of 1326 was the inevitable result of the misgovernment of the Despensers.  In Sept. 1326 Queen Isabel (wife of King Edward II) and Roger de Mortimer landed at Orwell, Suffolk, with a force of 700 mercenaries hired from 
1 CONC the Count of Hainault.  In a matter of weeks, the government of the Despensers collapsed.  On Oct. 2, King Edward II left London and fled to south Wales, accompanied by Hugh the younger.  They were captured near Llantrisant, Glamo
1 CONC rgan 16 Nov. 1326.  SIR HUGH LE DESPENSER, 2nd Lord le Despenser, was taken to Hereford, and executed there 24 Nov. 1326.  In 1330 his bones were collected and buried at Tewkesbury Abbey, Gloucestershire.  His widow, Eleanor, was 
1 CONC treated with remarkable leniency and her inheritance was restored to her 22 April 1328.  Sometime shortly before 26 Jan. 1328/9, she was abducted from Hanley Castle, Worcestershire by WILLIAM LA ZOUCHE MORTIMER, Knt., 1st Lord Zou
1 CONC che of Richard's Castle, whom she afterwards married without royal license. ... They had two sons. ... Sir William la Zouche Mortimer, 1st Lord Zouche of Richard's Castle, died 28 Feb. 1336/7, and was buried at Tewkesbury Abbey, G
1 CONC loucestershire.  His widow, Eleanor, died testate 30 June 1337.
0 @N6000000004287199051@ NOTE !"Sureties of the Magna Charta", line 14; "Sir Hugh le DESPENSER, hanged & quartered, 24 Nov 1326, Lord Despenser; m 1306 aft 14 June, Alinore de CLARE (34-5). (CP IV, 267). His mother, Isabel is from line (4-5). Line 34-5; "Alian
1 CONC ore de CLARE b Caerphilly, Oct 1292, d 30 June 1337; m 1st 1306, aft 14 June, Sir Jugh le DESPENSER, Knt. (14-6); m/2 William la ZOUCHE de Mortimer, Lord Zouche. (CP IV 267).
0 @N6000000000337389224@ NOTE Source #1:  T. Anna Leese, "Blood Royal:  Issue of the Kings and Queens of Medieval England, 1066-1399:  The Normans and Plantagenets" (Heritage Books, Inc, 1996) - executed;  a favorite of Edward II
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  Marcellus Donald Alexander R. von Redlich, "Pedigrees of Some of the Emperor Charlemagne's Descendants" (Baltimore:  Genealogical Publishing Co, Inc, 1988 reprint of 1941 edition), pp. 257-258
1 CONT
1 CONT he was hanged on November 24, 1326
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #3: http://www.encyclopedia.com/printablenew/48045.html
1 CONT
1 CONT About 1318 the younger Despenser, who had earlier supported the barons, joined his father and the king, soon gaining more influence with Edward than had the elder Hugh. Both Despensers became involved in a quarrel with the barons,
1 CONC  who formed a league against them and brought about their banishment in 1321. In 1322, however, they returned to England, and after the baronial defeat at Boroughbridge they were the real rulers of the kingdom. The elder Despenser
1 CONC  was created earl of Winchester in 1322. Their rule was notable for several important administrative reforms and the conclusion of peace with Scotland (1323), but their greed was enormous and they were bitterly hated by the barons
1 CONC . Both Despensers were executed after the invasion of Queen Isabella in 1326.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #3: Gary Boyd Roberts, "The Royal Descents of 500 Immigrants to the American Colonies or the United States, Who Were Themselves Notable or Left Descendants Notable in American History" (Baltimore:  Genealogical Publishing C
1 CONC o, Inc., 2001) p. 277.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #4:  Frederick Lewis Weis, "Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700" - Seventh Edition, with additions and corrections by Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., assisted by Davis Faris (Baltimore, M
1 CONC D:  Genealogical Publishing Co, 1995), p. 74
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #5:   George Edward Cokayne, "The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant or Dormant," New Edition, Revised and Much Enlarged, Edited by The Hon. Vicary Gibbs and H. A. D
1 CONC oubleday (London:  The St. Catherine Press, 1926), Vol IV, pp. 267-271
0 @N6000000005076836069@ NOTE 3rd Lord le Despenser
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh le Despenser, 3rd Lord le Despenser was born circa 1290. He was the son of Hugh le Despenser, Earl of Winchester and Isabella de Beauchamp. He married Eleanor de Clare, daughter of Gilbert de Clare, 6th Earl of Gloucester and
1 CONC  Joan of Acre, in 1306. He died on 29 November 1326, execution.1 He was buried at Tewkesbury Abbey, Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire, England.
1 CONT      Hugh le Despenser, 3rd Lord le Despenser gained the title of 3rd Lord le Despenser.
1 CONT Children of Hugh le Despenser, 3rd Lord le Despenser and Eleanor de Clare
1 CONT Elizabeth le Despencer+ d. 13 Jul 13892
1 CONT Edward Despenser+
1 CONT Isabel le Despencer+
1 CONT Hugh Despenser, 4th Lord le Despenser b. 1308, d. 8 Feb 1348/49
1 CONT Isabel le Despenser+ b. c 1313, d. 13753
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 83. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
1 CONT [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct 
1 CONC or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume II, page 130. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
1 CONT [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 243.
