0 @N6000000003949834190@ NOTE John de Braose, Lord of Gower, born c1197, died 18 July 1232 at Bamber, co Sussex from a fall from a horse; married 1219 Margaret, died 1263, daughter of Llewellyn ap Iorwerth, Prince of Wales. [Magna Charta Sureties]
1 CONT Nicknamed "Tadody" by the Welsh when he was hidden in Gower as a child after King John had his father and grandmother killed. He was later in the custody of Engelard de Cigogny (castellan of Windsor) along with his brother Giles. 
1 CONC Cigogny was ordered to give the two boys up to William de Harcourt in 1214. At this time John became separated from his brother. He was present at the signing of the Magna Charta in 1215.
1 CONT John disputed his uncle Reginald's claim to the Braose lands, sometimes resorting to arms. Llewelyn, Prince of Wales, helped him to secure Gower (1219). In 1221, with the advice and permission of Llewelyn, he repaired his castle o
1 CONC f Abertawy (Swansea). He purchased the Rape of Bramber from Reginald and his son William in 1226. In that year John confirmed the family gifts to Sele Priory, near Bramber, and to the Abbey of St FLorent, Saumur, and added others.
1 CONC  After the death of Reginald (1228) he became Lord of Skenfrith, Grosmont, and Whitecastle, the three Marcher castles, by charter from the king but he lost these in 1230 to Hugh de Burgh at the same time as Gower became a subtenan
1 CONC cy of de Burgh's Honour of Carmarthen and Cardigan.   See Castle of Abertawy, Swansea
1 CONT John had been privately nursed by a Welsh woman at Gower. This John had grants of lands from King Henry III and was also possessed of the Barony of Brembye, in Sussex, where he died in 1231, by a fall from his horse, his foot stic
1 CONC king in the stirrup. He married, it is stated, Margaret, dau. of Llewellyn, Prince of Wales, by whom (who m. afterwards Walter de Clifford) he had a son, his successor, William de Braose. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forf
1 CONC eited and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, England, 1883, p. 72, Braose, Baron Braose, of Gower]
0 @N6000000000437050647@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Vii; Lord
0 @N6000000001220382410@ NOTE [2753682.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT !Bro. of Richard de Brews. [Magna Charta Sureties]
1 CONT
1 CONT WAITE, FOSTER LINE - 21st ggrandfather
1 CONT
1 CONT !Of age 15 Jul 1245. [Magna Charta Sureties]
1 CONT
1 CONT As you follow the coast westwards out of Swansea towards Mumbles, the rugged 13th century kee p of Oystermouth Castle looms on its grassy mound above the town, guarding the landward appro ach to the peninsula. Several castles have
1 CONC  stood on this site, including one owned by the D e Braose family, on whom King John had bestowed the lordship. The Welsh burnt the castle--an d its
1 CONT replacement. ["Celtic Coast" by Susan Elkin, REALM, July/August 1997, p. 76]
1 CONT
1 CONT Only 12 at his father's death, he became a ward of Henry III's chief minister, Peter de Rivau x. But within two years a warrant was out for Peter's arrest and his fall was imminent.
1 CONT    The king demanded the return of Peter's ward and the surrender of the de Braose lands. Pet er de Rivaux flatly refused and sent a message to say that William de Braose was too ill at p resent. The king's messenger reported, how
1 CONC ever, that "he had found the youth sound and well , riding a large black Spanish horse, with Peter, from the town of Rosse towards London."
1 CONT    William was finally placed in the wardship of the king's brother, Richard of Cornwall. H e was raised at Henry's court and was probably introduced to his first wife there. She was an other royal ward and heiress, Alina de Moult
1 CONC on. When he came of age, the young lord met wit h a barrage of lawsuits from the women of the family, including his mother and sister. Late r the children and relatives of his three wives added further claims for a portion of the 
1 CONC deB raose inheritance.
1 CONT    William was removed from the turmoil of his family's earlier history and became a committe d royalist. His Welsh roots were forgotten and even relations with his mother appear to hav e deteriorated. She married Water de Cliffor
1 CONC d and the couple issued several claims against Wi lliam arising from her first marriage. William was called to answer charges that he seized hi s mother's
1 CONT goods as they were transported towards Chichester and imprisoned her men.
1 CONT    William went to some lengths to make provision for all his children and involved the Count ess of Devon in the legal processes. Descendants of his son Peter lived at Chesworth in Horsh am, sussex and others became the de Braose
1 CONC s of Wiston, near Steyning. William also negotiate d some excellent marriages.
1 CONT    Lawsuits over land and money continued throughout William's life. His debts soared when Si mon de Montfort attempted to ruin him. As a committed royalist,William had fought against th e de Montforts and devastated Simon de Mont
1 CONC fort Jr's manor of Sedgewick (which was in the rap e of Bramber). In the months after his victory at the Battle of Lewes, Simon de Montfort se t his sights on revenge as well as personal expansion in Sussex.
1 CONT    William de Braose failed to appear at the Tower of London when a de Montfort "court" summo ned him to hear the charges. In June 1264 damages were awarded against William for the outrag eous sum of 10,000 marks. In addition, his
1 CONC  son/heir and his castle at Knepp, already seized b y Simon de Montfort, would be held until arrangements for payment were made. The young Willia m de Braose was held at Eleanor de Montfort's household until her husband's defeat a
1 CONC t the bat tle of Evesham in 1265.
1 CONT    In 1278 Williams' port of Shorham was protesting to the king about their lord's extortiona te methods of raising revenue and abuses of custom duties. He was accused of taking "from eve ry ship one cask before the mast and one b
1 CONC ehind and likewise took prise of wax and other merc handise against the will of the merchants and in the king's despite." William denied the char ge but his
1 CONT financial affairs had become a source of scandal and earned him a scoundrel's reputation.
1 CONT    A Swansea historian (W.H. Jones) is quoted in the Oystermouth Castle Guidebook, referrin g to the de Braose lords of Gower: "The de Braoses were a licentious clan of freebooters, wh o appear to have been so habituated to duplic
1 CONC ity and chicanery as to render it impossible t o be straightforward and honest in their dealings with their neighbours." William died in 129 1 and was buried at Findon, Sussex. ["Barons de Braose" <http://freespace.virgin.net/doug
1 CONC .tho mpson/BraoseWeb/stage.htm]
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of John de Braose and Margaret verch Llewelyn; m. Isabel de Clare; father of William de B raose who m. Aline de Multon. [Fred Lathrop@blast.net, 1 Feb 2002]
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Margred verch Llywelyn and John de Broase; m. Aline Multon, Mary de Ros and Agnes de M oels.  [dfox <pifox@shentel.net, 30 Mar 2002]
