0 @N6000000003463812373@ NOTE As for Sir Edmund de Mortimer, his early history is well documented in
1 CONT the records.  He was born about 1251-4 (aged 28-30 in 1282, aged 40
1 CONT in 1301).  He was placed as a youth in the church.  He was appointed
1 CONT Canon of Hereford in 1263 and Treasurer of York in 1265.  In 1268 his
1 CONT father appointed attorneys in the cause in progress at the papal court
1 CONT between him and Amaury de Montfort as to the treasurership of York.  He
1 CONT occurs as Treasurer of York in 1270.  In 1274 the king committed his
1 CONT houses at Oxford (later called Beaumont Palace) for him "to dwell in
1 CONT them."  On the death of his father in 1282, he ceased to pursue a
1 CONT clerical career.  He vacated his position as Canon of Hereford in Nov.
1 CONT 1282, and was granted livery of his father's lands 24 Nov. 1282.  In
1 CONT Dec. 1282 he and his brothers, Roger, William, and Geoffrey, were among
1 CONT royal forces who defeated and killed Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, Prince of
1 CONT Wales, at Builth.  Sir Edmund married about 1286 Margaret de Fiennes,
1 CONT daughter of William de Fiennes, Knt., of Wendover, Buckinghamshire,
1 CONT Martock, Somerset, seigneur of Fiennes in Guisnes, by Blanche, daughter
1 CONT of Jean de Brienne, Count of Montfort, Grand Butler of France
1 CONT (descendant of King Henry II).  Margaret was near kinswoman of Queen
1 CONT Eleanor of Castile, wife of King Edward I.  They had five sons, Roger,
1 CONT Knt. [1st Earl of March], John, Hugh (clerk), Walter (clerk), and
1 CONT Edmund (clerk), and four daughters, Maud (wife of Thebaud de Verdun,
1 CONT Knt., 2nd Lord Verdun), Joan (nun at Limbrook), Elizabeth (nun), and
1 CONT Eleanor (wife of William de Kyme, Knt.).
0 @N6000000003949751203@ NOTE  Lord of Wigmore, who m. Margaret, dau. of Sir William de Fiennes. In the 10th Edward I [1282], he s. his father and the next year, doing his homage, had livery of his lands. He was afterwards constantly employed in the Welsh wars
1 CONC  and was summoned to parliament as a baron from 8 June, 1294, and from 23 June, 1295, to 2 June, 1302. His lordship was mortally wounded in 1303 at the battle of Buelt, against the Welsh, and dying almost immediately at Wigmore Ca
1 CONC stle, was buried in the abbey there. He left issue, Roger, his successor; John, accidentally slain in a tournament at Worcester, 12th Edward 11 [1319], by John de Leybourne, being not more than eighteen years of age; Hugh, a pries
1 CONC t, rector of the church at Old Radnor; Walter, a priest, rector of Kingston; Edmund, a priest, rector of Hodnet, and treasurer of the cathedral at York; Maud, m. to Theobald de Verdon; Joan and Elizabeth, nuns. His lordship was s.
1 CONC  by his eldest son, Roger Mortimer, 2nd baron. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage Ltd, London, England, 1883, p. 384, Mortimer, Barons Mortimer, of Wigmore, Earls of March]
0 @N6000000004287287417@ NOTE !Title is; Lord of Wigmore; Sir Knight.
0 @N6000000004533182827@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Edmund Mortimer (1351—27 December 1381), 3rd earl of March and 1stEarl of Ulster, was son of Roger Mortimer, 2nd Earl of March, by hiswife Philippa, daughter of William Montacute, 1st Earl of Salisbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT Early Life
1 CONT Being an infant at the death of his father, Edmund, as a ward of thecrown, was placed by Edward III of England under the care of Williamof Wykeham and Richard Fitzalan, 10th Earl of Arundel.
1 CONT
1 CONT The position of the young earl, powerful on account of his possessionsand hereditary influence in the Welsh marches, was rendered still moreimportant by his marriage in 1368 at the age of 17 to the 13 year oldPhilippa, the only ch
1 CONC ild of Lionel of Antwerp, Duke of Clarence, thirdson of Edward III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lionel's wife, Elizabeth, was daughter and heiress of William deBurgh, 6th Lord of Connaught and 3rd Earl of Ulster, and Lionel hadhimself been created Earl of Ulster before his marriage. Edmundinherited the title Earl of Ulster o
1 CONC n Lionel's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Therefore, the Earl of March not only represented one of the chiefAnglo-Norman lordships in Ireland in right of his wife Philippa, butPhilippa's line was also the second most senior line of descent in thesuccession to the crown, a
1 CONC fter Edward, the Black Prince and his son,King Richard II of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT This marriage had, therefore, far-reaching consequences in Englishhistory, ultimately giving rise to the claim of the House of York tothe crown of England contested in the Wars of the Roses; Edward IVbeing descended from the third
1 CONC  son of Edward III asgreat-great-grandson of Philippa, countess of March, and in the maleline from Edmund of Langley, fifth son of Edward III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund's son Roger Mortimer, 4th Earl of March would become heirpresumptive to the English crown during the reign of Richard II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Political Advancement
1 CONT Mortimer, now styled Earl of March and Ulster, became Marshal ofEngland in 1369, and was employed in various diplomatic missionsduring the next following years. He was a member of the committeeappointed by the Peers to confer with
1 CONC  the Commons in 1373—the firstinstance of such a joint conference since the institution ofrepresentative parliaments—on the question of granting supplies forJohn of Gaunt’s war in France.
1 CONT
1 CONT He participated in the opposition to Edward III and the court party,which grew in strength towards the end of the reign, taking thepopular side and being prominent in the Good Parliament of 1376 amongthe lords who supported the Pr
1 CONC ince of Wales and opposed the CourtParty and John of Gaunt. The Speaker of the Commons in this parliamentwas March’s steward, Peter de la Mare, who firmly withstood John ofGaunt in stating the grievances of the Commons, in support
1 CONC ing theimpeachment of several high court officials, and in procuring thebanishment of the king’s mistress, Alice Perrers. March was a memberof the administrative council appointed by the same parliament afterthe death of Edward, t
1 CONC he Black Prince to attend the king and advisehim in all public affairs.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign of Richard II
1 CONT On the accession of Richard II, a minor, in 1377, the Earl became amember of the standing council of government; though as father of theheir-presumptive to the crown he wisely abstained from claiming anyactually administrative off
1 CONC ice. The most powerful person in the realmwas, however, John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster, whose jealousy ofMarch led to the acceptance by the latter of the Lieutenancy ofIreland in 1379. March succeeded in asserting his authority 
1 CONC in easternUlster, but failed to subdue the O’Neills farther west. Proceeding toMunster to put down the turbulency of the chieftains of the south,March died at Cork on 27 December 1381. He was buried in WigmoreAbbey, of which he ha
1 CONC d been a benefactor, and where his wife Philippawas also interred.
1 CONT
1 CONT The earl had three sons and two daughters, the elder of whom,Elizabeth, married Henry Percy "Hotspur", son of the Earl ofNorthumberland. His eldest son, Roger, succeeded him as 4th Earl ofMarch and Ulster. His second son, Edmund p
1 CONC layed an important part, inconjunction with his brother-in-law Hotspur, in the fortunes of OwainGlyndwr. The other daughter was Philippa, who married RichardFitzalan, 11th Earl of Arundel.
