0 @N6000000003615495699@ NOTE Sheriff of Salop and Stafford 
1 CONT "That this family of Alditheley, vulgarly called Audley, " says Dugdale,
1 CONT "came to be great and eminent, the ensuing discourse will sufficiently
1 CONT manifest: but that the rise thereof was no higher than King John's time,
1 CONT and that the first who assumed this surname was a branch of that ancient
1 CONT and noble family of Verdon, whose chief seat was at Alton Castlein the
1 CONT northern part of Staffordshire, I am very inclined to believe; partlyby
1 CONT reason that Henry had the inheritance of Alditheley given him by Nicholas
1 CONT de Verdon, who d. in the 16th Henry III [1232], or near that time; and
1 CONT partly for that he bore for his arms the same ordinary as Vernon did. .
1 CONT .so thatprobably the ancestor of this Henry first seated himself at
1 CONT Alditheley: for that there hath been an ancient mansion there, the large
1 CONT moat, northwards from the parish church there (somewhat less than a
1 CONT furlong, and upon the chief part of a fair ascent), do sufficiently
1 CONT manifest."
1 CONT Henry de Alditheley, to whom Dugdale alludes above, being in great favour
1 CONT with Ranulph, Earl of Chesterand Lincoln (the most powerful subject of
1 CONT England in his time), obtained from that nobleman a grant of Newhall in
1 CONT Cheshire with manors in Staffordshire and other parts--and for his
1 CONT adhesion to King John, in that monarch's strugglewith the
1 CONT insurrectionary barons, a royal grant of the lordship of Storton in
1 CONT Warwickshire, part of the possessions of Roger de Summerville. In the
1 CONT first four years of King Henry III [1216 - 1220], he executed the office
1 CONT of sheriff for the counties of Salop and Stafford as deputy for his
1 CONT patron, the greatEarl Ranulph. In the 10th of Henry III [1226], this
1 CONT Henry de Alditheley was appointed governor of the castles of Carmarthen
1 CONT and Cardigan and made sheriffthe next year of the counties of Salop and
1 CONT Stafford and constable of the castles of Salop and Bridgenorth, which
1 CONT sheriffalty he held for five years. Uponhis retirement from office, he
1 CONT had a confirmation of all such lands whereof he was then possessed as
1 CONT well those granted to him by Ranulph, Earl of Chester, and Nicholas de
1 CONT Verdon, as those in Ireland given him by Hugh de Lacy, Earl of Ulster,
1 CONT whose constable he was in that province. He subsequently obtained divers
1 CONT other territorial grants from the crown, but, notwithstanding, when
1 CONT Richard Mareschall, Earl of Pembroke, rebelled and made an incursion into
1 CONT Wales, the king, Henry III, thought it prudent to secure the persons of
1 CONT this Henry and all the other barons-marchers. He was afterwards, however,
1 CONT constituted governor of Shrewsbury in place of John de Lacy, Earl of
1 CONT Lincoln, and, on the death of John, Earl of Chester, governor of the
1 CONT castle of Chester, and also that of Beeston, then called the "Castle on
1 CONT the Rock, " and soon after made governor of Newcastle-under-Lyne. This
1 CONT powerful feudal baron m. Bertred, dau. of Ralph de Meisnil-warin, of
1 CONT Cheshire, and had a son, James, and a dau., Emme, who m. Griffith ap
1 CONT Madoc, Lord of Bromefield, a person of great power in Wales. He d. in
1 CONT 1236, having founded and endowed the Abbey of Hilton near to his castle
1 CONT at Heleigh, in Staffordshire, for Cistercian monks, and was s. by his
1 CONT son, James de Alditheley. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited
1 CONT and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, Middlesex, England,
1 CONT 1883, p. 15, Audley, Barons Audley, of Heleigh]
1 CONT Henry de Alditheley was in great favor with Ranulph de Meschines, Earl of
1 CONT Chester and Lincoln (the most powerful subject in England in his time).
1 CONT Henry de Alditheley obtained from Ranulph de Meschines a grant of Newhall
1 CONT in Cheshire, with manors in Staffordshire and other parts, and for his
1 CONT adhesion to King John, in that monarch's struggle with the
1 CONT insurrectionary barons, a royal grant of the lordship of Storton, in
1 CONT Warwickshire, part of the possessions of Robert of Summerville. In the
1 CONT four first years of the reign of King Henry III.
0 @N6000000003949733372@ NOTE Henry of Aldithley, 2nd son of Adam of Aldithley, (who d. bet. 1203 and 1211) by Emma, daughter of Ralf fitz Orm, of Darlaston, Staffs; was b. about 1175; with his father, he was witness to a charter of Harvey Bagot in 1194. He bo
1 CONC ught large estates from Eleanor Malbank in 1214; in 1227 he acquired the manors of Edgmund and Newport, and in 1230 that of Ford, all in Salop, and all held by him direct from the Crown, though not by military or knight service. H
1 CONC e was Under Sheriff of Salop and co. Stafford 1217-20, and Sheriff 1227-32; was in command of the Welsh Marches 1223-46. He built the castle of Heligh, co. Stafford; and Red Castle, Salop. In 1223 he founded Hulton Abbey. He was a
1 CONC ppointed Custodian of Chester and Beeston Castle, 22 June 1237, on the extinction of the the earldom of Chester. He m. in 1217, Bertred, daughter of Ralf Mainwaring, Seneschal of Chester. He d. in 1246, shortly bef. Nov. His widow
1 CONC  was living in 1249. She was buried in Hulton Abbey. [Complete Peerage I:337 XIV:50]
