1 CONT undertook for the king therein. The next year we findhim with Roger de
1 CONT Mortimer and the other barons-marchers, giving battle to Llewellyn,
1 CONT Prince of Wales, and afterwards joining the Earl of Gloucester at Evesham
1 CONT in rescuing the king, who had become captive to the Earl of Leicester at
1 CONT the battle of Lewes. In the 52nd year of Henry III, he performed a
1 CONT pilgrimage to the shrine of St. James in Galicia, in Spain, and the
1 CONT following yearembarked in the Crusade with the king and Prince Edward.
1 CONT He married Ela, daughter of William Longespee, Earl of Salisbury. She was
1 CONT living in 1282.
0 @N6000000003265459378@ NOTE
1 CONT James de Alditheley was a great favourite of Richard, Earl of Cornwall, at whose coronation as king of Almaigne he assisted. This nobleman had livery of his lands in the 31st Henry III [1247], and was constituted in two years afte
1 CONC rwards constable of Newcastle-under-Lyne. Being one of the lords-marchers he was actively employed for some years against the Welsh and was appointed governor of the castles of Salop and Bridgenorth and sheriff of the counties of 
1 CONC Salop and Stafford. In the 47th of Henry III [1263] he was made justice of Ireland; and in the same year, upon the misunderstanding between the king and the barons regarding the provisions of Oxford, being referred to arbitration 
1 CONC of the monarch of France, he was one of the noblemen who undertook for the king therein. The next year we find him with Roger de Mortimer and the other barons-marchers giving battle to Llewelyn, Prince of Wales, and afterwards joi
1 CONC ning the Earl of Gloucester at Evesham in rescuing the king, who had become captive to the Earl of Leicester at the battle of Lewes. In the 52nd of Henry III [1268], his lordship performed a pilgrimage to the shrine of St. James i
1 CONC n Galicia, and the following year embarked in the Crusade. His death, occasioned by breaking his neck, occurred soon afterwards (1271). He had a dau. Joan, who m. John, son of Robert de Beauchamp, to whose child, prior to its birt
1 CONC h, the said John then being deceased, his lordship was appointed guardian. He had also five sons, the youngest of whom, Hugh, is supposed to have been the Hugh Alditheley who had summons to parliament 15 May 1321, and whose son be
1 CONC came Earl of Gloucester. His lordship was s. by his eldest son, James de Alditheley, who d. s. p. in 1272, and was s. by his brother, Henry, who d. without issue in 1275, and was s. by his brother, William, who d. without issue is
1 CONC  1275, was s. by his brother, Nicholas, who died in 1299. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, England, 1883, p. 15, Audley, Barons Audley, of Heleigh]
1 CONT
1 CONT NOTE: John Burke, History of the Commoners of Great Britain and Ireland, Vol. IV, R. Bentley, London, 1834, p. 757, Stansfeld, of Burley Park, names two additional sons, William, Lord of Blore and Grindon, co. Stafford, and Sir Ad
1 CONC am, surnamed FitzWolfric.
1 CONT
