0 @N6000000001353926683@ NOTE John le Strange, Lord of Knokyn. This feudal baron, in the time of his father, was deputy governor of Winchester Castle, and in the 48th Henry III [1264], he was constituted governor of the castle of Montgomery. He subsequently es
1 CONC poused the baronial cause and, after the triumphof the barons at Lewes, was reinstated in the governorship of Montgomery Castle. In the 3rd Edward I [1375], he surrendered to his brother,Robert, his entire right in the manor of Wr
1 CONC ockwurdine. His lordship m. Joane, one of the daus. and heirs of Roger de Someri, Baron of Dudley, by his 1st wife, Lady Nichola de Albini, sister and co-heir of Hugh, 4th Earl of Arundel, and d. in 1276, when all his lands were s
1 CONC eizedupon by the crown, but in two years afterwards, they were restored tohis son and heir, John le Strange. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 516, Stran
1 CONC ge, Barons Strange of Knokyn]
