1 CONT * Some authors state, that this great man was buried at Bisham priory which he had founded. This error probably was caused by the fact that his son built a magnificent monument to his memory, in that Abbey, which was however demol
1 CONC ished by Henry VIII at the dissolution.  Not only Edmondson's Heraldry but Glover's Ordinary of Arms, and also the very high authority of Dugdale, assert that he was buried at White Friars, London. Edmondson's Heraldry, Vol. I, p.
1 CONC  51, states that he became 21 years old in the 19th of Edward II, which would place his birth A. D. 1304; the same authority also states, that he owned the Manor of Cookham in Berkshire. Lipscombe's Bucks states that the hamlet of
1 CONC  Boveney anciently belonged to Cookham in Berkshire, and the inference is that this Earl of Salisbury may have been possessed of Boveney also.
1 CONT
1 CONT Vol. I, P. 51, Glover's ordinary of Arms, Edmondson's Heraldry, says, this earl owned Fulmere in Buckinghamshire.  Lipscombe's History of Bucks says, he sold it, in or before 1335. This was a hamlet and Chapelry of Datchet in Stok
1 CONC e Hund. Bucks, near Stoke Poges and not far from Boveney.
1 CONT
1 CONT This great man, who died so young and who also accomplished so much in his busy career, also found time to establish at Bisham in Berkshire, on the banks of the Thames 4 miles from Maidenhead, a Monastery, and he also founded a pr
1 CONC iory (in 1338) for Canons of the order of St. Augustine, in the words of his charter, "dedicated to Our Lord and the Virgin."  This priory was re-founded by Henry VIII for an abbot and 13 Benedictine Monks.  Here Henry VIII. confi
1 CONC ned one of his wives; afterward, it was a favorite resort of Queen Elizabeth; here was buried the wife of the founder, Katherine de Grandison, the Countess of Salisbury, and the inscription upon her tomb stated, that her father wa
1 CONC s "descended out of Burgundy, cousin German to the Emperor of Constantinople, the King of Hungary and Duke of Bavaria."
1 CONT
1 CONT Here William Montacute, the 2nd Earl of Salisbury (son of the founder), was interred. By his will he directed that a monument should there be erected to the memory of his father, which was done upon a magnificent scale.
1 CONT
1 CONT His wife Catherine was daughter of William (and sister and heir to Otho) lord Grandison by Sibylla, daughter and heir of John de Tregoz, a great Baron.  She was a brave woman, worthy of such a brave and noble man as was her husban
1 CONC d the Earl of Salisbury. She  nobly defended and aided with heroic valor the defense of the castle of Werk, with her husband's brother, Sir Edward Montacute, who was its Governor, and also bravely defended her own Castle of Salisb
1 CONC ury from King David of Scotland, with the aid of William Montacute, her husbands cousin, while her husband was a prisoner of war in France as before mentioned in the history of Sir Simon No. VIII.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "The bones of John Montacute, 3rd Earl of Salisbury, who was beheaded, were brought from Cirencester, (by order of his widow) and reinterred at Bisham Priory." Crosse's Antiquities. Here also were laid the "mortal parts" of the 4
1 CONC th and last Earl of Salisbury, General Thomas Montacute, killed at the siege of Orleans (1428). Here, also rest the remains of John, Marquis of Montacute, killed at the battle of Barnet in 1470, and also his brother Richard Nevill
1 CONC e, Earl of Warwick, called the "king maker." Here also sleeps that unfortunate youth Edward Plantaganet, son of the Duke of Clarence, beheaded in 1499 for attempting an escape from confinement.
1 CONT  It was this countess of Salisbury who, while dancing with King Edward, lost her garter, which gave rise to the founding of the order of the garter, (and not Joan Plantaganet, the Fair Maid of Kent, as was stated in the " Montague
1 CONC s at Hadley.") See History of the Orders of British Knighthood by Sir N. Nicholas. Rambles about Eton and Harrow, by Alfred Rimmer, London, 1882, et al.
1 CONT
1 CONT They had seven children, three sons and four daughters.  Of the sons, William, the eldest, succeeded his father and became the second Earl of Salisbury, but was killed in a tilting match at Windsor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir John Montacute, the second son, married Margaret Monthermer, granddaughter of Ralph and Joan of Acres, daughter of King Edward I, and his son became 3rd Earl of Salisbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT Of Robert Montacute, the third and youngest son, the records give no history.
0 @N6000000001876703988@ NOTE William Montagu, 1st Earl of Salisbury was also known as William de Montacute.2 
1 CONT He gained the title of  3rd Lord Montagu. 
1 CONT He gained the title of  1st Earl of Salisbury on March 16 1337. 
1 CONT He was crowned King of Man in 1344.
1 CONT Wikipedia:
1 CONT William was born in Cassington in Oxfordshire, the eldest son of William de Montacute, 2nd Baron Montacute and Elizabeth Montfort. He succeeded to his father's title in 1319.
1 CONT Montacute accompanied Edward III in repelling the Scottish invasion of 1327. The next year he served Edward on diplomatic missions to the King of France (Charles IV or Philip VI) and to the Pope John XXII. In 1330, at the behest o
1 CONC f the King, Montacute and some of his men arrested Roger Mortimer, and after Mortimer's execution Montacute received a good part of the forfeited estates.
1 CONT Montacute took part in the Second War of Scottish Independence (1333 - 1357) of the following years, notably at the Battle of Halidon Hill in 1333. In the same year the King gave Montacute the English claim to the Isle of Man.
1 CONT He was created Earl of Salisbury on 16 March 1337. In 1338 the new earl went on a lengthy diplomatic mission, first to France to declare King Edward claims to the French crown, and then to visit many of the German princes who migh
1 CONC t ally against France. Later that year he was made Marshall of England.
1 CONT During the next two years, he was one of the commanders of the English forces in Flanders, until he was captured by the French near Lille . He was released later in 1340 as part of a prisoner exchange, on the condition that he nev
1 CONC er again fight against the French. It was probably sometime after his return to England that he conquered the Isle of Man, which had been held by the Scots. He won and was crowned to King of Mann in 1344.
1 CONT Lord Salisbury died from injuries said to have been received during a Windsor tournament. He was buried at Bisham Priory in Berkshire, which he had founded in 1337 adjoining his home, Bisham Manor.
1 CONT Citing
1 CONT * Gransden, Antonia (1972). "The alleged rape by Edward III of the countess of Salisbury". English Historical Review 87. 
1 CONT * Hunt, William (1894). "William de Montacute or Montagu, third Baron Montacute and first Earl of Salisbury". Dictionary of National Biography 38: 212ΓÇô213.
0 @N6000000002188316879@ NOTE William Montacute, 1st Earl of Salisbury
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT William Montacute (alias Montagu), King of the Isle of Man, 1st Earl of Salisbury and 3rd Baron Montagu (1301 – January 30, 1344) was an English nobleman and loyal servant of King Edward III.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Life
1 CONT     * 2 Family
1 CONT     * 3 References
1 CONT     * 4 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT
1 CONT William was born in Cassington in Oxfordshire, the eldest son of William de Montacute, 2nd Baron Montacute and Elizabeth Montfort. He succeeded to his father's title in 1319.
1 CONT
1 CONT Montacute accompanied Edward III in repelling the Scottish invasion of 1327. The next year he served Edward on diplomatic missions to the King of France (Charles IV or Philip VI) and to the Pope John XXII. In 1330, at the behest o
1 CONC f the King, Montacute and some of his men arrested Roger Mortimer, and after Mortimer's execution Montacute received a good part of the forfeited estates.
1 CONT
1 CONT Montacute took part in the Second War of Scottish Independence (1333 - 1357) of the following years, notably at the Battle of Halidon Hill in 1333. In the same year the King gave Montacute the English claim to the Isle of Man.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was created Earl of Salisbury on 16 March 1337. In 1338 the new earl went on a lengthy diplomatic mission, first to France to declare King Edward claims to the French crown, and then to visit many of the German princes who migh
1 CONC t ally against France. Later that year he was made Marshall of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the next two years, he was one of the commanders of the English forces in Flanders, until he was captured by the French near Lille. He was released later in 1340 as part of a prisoner exchange, on the condition that he neve
1 CONC r again fight against the French. It was probably sometime after his return to England that he conquered the Isle of Man, which had been held by the Scots. He won and was crowned to King of Mann in 1344.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lord Salisbury died from injuries said to have been received during a Windsor tournament. He was buried at Bisham Priory in Berkshire, which he had founded in 1337 adjoining his home, Bisham Manor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Catherine Grandison, daughter of William de Grandison, 1st Baron Grandison. Legend holds that Edward III was in love with her, and she may have been the "Countess of Salisbury" from whose dropped garter the Order of the
1 CONC  Garter derives its name. In another version of the story, the king forced his attentions on the countess, but this is thought to have originated in French propaganda. The children of William and Catherine were:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Elizabeth Montacute (b. before 1325); married Hugh le Despencer, 2nd Baron le Despencer before 27 April 1341.
1 CONT     * John Montacute, (1327-1396); father of John Montacute, 3rd Earl of Salisbury.
1 CONT     * William Montacute, 2nd Earl of Salisbury (1329-1397)
1 CONT     * Anne Montacute, (b. 1330); married John De Grey on 12 June 1335.
1 CONT     * Philippa Montacute (b. 1332); married Roger Mortimer, 2nd Earl of March.
1 CONT     * Sibyl Montacute (b. before 1339); married Edmund FitzAlan about 1356.
1 CONT
1 CONT Montacute was succeeded by his eldest surviving son, William.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Gransden, Antonia (1972). "The alleged rape by Edward III of the countess of Salisbury". English Historical Review 87. 
1 CONT     * Hunt, William (1894). "William de Montacute or Montagu, third Baron Montacute and first Earl of Salisbury". Dictionary of National Biography 38: 212-213. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Royal Berkshire History: William Montacute, Earl of Salisbury
1 CONT
