0 @N6000000004533239833@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT William Montacute, 1st Earl of Salisbury, 3rd Baron Montacute(1301-1344) was an English nobleman.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son of William Montacute, 2nd Baron Montacute, andsucceeded his father as baron in 1319.
1 CONT
1 CONT Montacute accompanied Edward III in repelling the Scottish invasion of1327. The next year he served the king on diplomatic missions to theking of France and to the Pope.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1330, at the behest of the king, Montacute and some of his menarrested Roger Mortimer, and after Mortimer's execution Montacutereceived a good part of the forfeited estates.
1 CONT
1 CONT Montacute took part in the Scottish campaigns of the following years,notably at the Battle of Halidon Hill in 1333.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in 1333 the king gave Montacute the English claim to the Isle ofMan.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was created earl of Salisbury on March 16, 1337.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1338 the new earl went an a lengthy diplomatic mission, first toFrance to declare king Edward claims to the French crown, and then tovisit many of the German princes who might ally against France. Laterthat year he was made Mar
1 CONC shall of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the next two years he was one of the commanders of the Englishforces in Flanders, until he was captured by the French near Lille. Hewas released later in 1340 as part of a prisoner exchange, on thecondition that he never ag
1 CONC ain fight against the French.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was probably sometime after his return to England that he conqueredthe Isle of Man, which had been held by the Scots.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Catherine Grandison, daughter of William Grandison, 1stBaron Grandison. Legend holds that Edward III was in love with her,and that it was her dropped garter from which the Order of the Garterderives its name. The matter
1 CONC  is disputed, but the garter at least ismore likely to have belonged to Joan of Kent, who was engaged to herson. There is also a story that the king raped the countess, but thisis probably a fruit of French propaganda.
1 CONT
1 CONT Montacute was succeeded by his eldest son, William Montacute, 2nd Earlof Salisbury. One of his daughters, Philippa, married Roger Mortimer,2nd Earl of March.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Antonia Gransden, "The alleged rape by Edward III of the countess ofSalisbury", English Historical Review 87 (1972)
