0 @N6000000001503810088@ NOTE     *
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Parentage and ancestry
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert fitzHamon (b ca 1045-1055, d. March 1107 Falaise, Normandy) was a cousin of William the Conqueror, although the details of his descent from the Norman dukes is uncertain. His family held the lordships of Torigny, Creully, M
1 CONC ézy, and Evrecy in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is said to be a son of Hamon, Count of Corbeil, himself a grandson of Richard I of Normandy and thus cousin to Robert I of Normandy (the Conqueror's father). However, fitzHamon is also described as the grandson of Hamo Dentatus
1 CONC  ('The Toothy', i.e., probably buck-toothed).[1]. The second explanation might make sense if his father were also named Hamon and thus confused with the grandfather and namesake Hamon, Count of Corbeil.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hamon was younger brother of William, Count of Corbeil (possibly also known as William, Count of Arques), and as such he was a descendant of Richard I, Duke of Normandy, the great-grandfather of William II, Duke of Normandy, "The 
1 CONC Conqueror". Robert and his father were thus kin to William the Conqueror although this connection does not appear to have profited Robert significantly until 1087 when his cousin William Rufus ascended the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert's mother is said to have been Halwisa (or Hawisa) alias Elisabeth d'Avoye, widow of Hugh Magnus, Prince of France, and daughter of one Henri l'Oiseteur.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Career in England and Wales
1 CONT
1 CONT Few details of Robert's career prior to 1087 are available; if he was Hamon's grandson rather than his son, this is not so surprising.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert probably did not fight at Hastings, and does not appear in the Domesday Book, although some relatives may. He first comes to prominence as a supporter of William Rufus during the Rebellion of 1088. After the revolt failed h
1 CONC e was rewarded with great estates in Gloucestershire and elsewhere. Some of these had belonged to the late Queen Matilda, and were supposed to be inherited by Rufus's younger brother Henry (the future Henry I); nevertheless Fitzha
1 CONC mon remained on good terms with Henry.
1 CONT
1 CONT The chronology of Fitzhamon's conquest of Glamorgan is uncertain, but it probably took place in the decades after he received Gloucester.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Welsh prince Iestyn ap Gwrgan (Jestin), prince or Lord of Glamorgan, called in the assistance of Robert Fitzhamon. Fitzhamon defeated the prince of South Wales Rhys ap Tewdwr in battle in 1090. With his Norman knights he then 
1 CONC took possession of Glamorgan, and "the French came into Dyned and Ceredigion, which they have still retained, and fortified the castles, and seized upon all the land of the Britons." Rhys's daughter Nest became the mistress of Kin
1 CONC g Henry I of England and allegedly was mother of Robert, 1st Earl of Gloucester who married Mabel, Fitzhamon's daughter and heiress and thus had legitimacy both among the Welsh and the Norman barons.[2] (Robert of Caen's mother is
1 CONC  however unknown to historians and genealogists).
1 CONT
1 CONT Iestyn did not profit long by his involvement with the Normans. He was soon defeated and his lands taken in 1091. Robert Fitzhamon then built Cardiff Castle on the site of an old Roman fort in 1091; his descendants would inherit t
1 CONC he castle and lands.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Founder of Tewkesbury Abbey (1092)
1 CONT
1 CONT He also refounded Tewkesbury Abbey in 1092. The abbey's dimensions are almost the same as Westminster Abbey. The first abbot was Giraldus, Abbot of Cranborne (d. 1110) who died before the abbey was consecrated in October 1121. The
1 CONC  abbey was apparently built under the influence of his wife Sibylle (or Sibilla)[3], said to be a beautiful and religious woman like her sisters.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Fitzhamon and His Kings
1 CONT
1 CONT Legend has it that Robert had ominous dreams in the days before Rufus' fatal hunting expedition, which postponed but did not prevent the outing. He was one of the first to gather in tears around Rufus' corpse, and he used his cloa
1 CONC k to cover the late king's body on its journey to be buried in Winchester. How much of these stories are the invention of later days is unknown.
1 CONT
1 CONT In any case Fitzhamon proved as loyal to Henry I as he had been to his predecessor, remaining on Henry's side in the several open conflicts with Henry's brother Robert Curthose. He was one of the three barons who negotiated the 11
1 CONC 01 truce between Henry I and Robert Curthose.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1105 he went to Normandy and was captured while fighting near his ancestral estates near Bayeux. This was one of the reasons Henry crossed the channel with a substantial force later that year. Fitzhamon was freed, and joined He
1 CONC nry's campaign, which proceeded to besiege Falaise. There Fitzhamon was severely injured in the head; although he lived two more years he was never the same mentally. He was buried in the Chapter House at Tewkesbury Abbey, which h
1 CONC e had founded and considerably enriched during his lifetime.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Fitzhamon married circa 1087-1090 Sibyl (or Sybille), apparently the youngest daughter of Roger of Montgomery, 1st Earl of Shrewsbury by his first wife Mabel of Bêlleme, by whom he is said to have had four daughters. His eldest da
1 CONC ughter Mabel inherited his great estates and married circa 1119 Robert, 1st Earl of Gloucester. Fitzhamon's Gloucestershire property thus became the nucleus of the Earldom of Gloucester later given to his son-in-law. Fitzhamon is 
1 CONC sometimes called Earl of Gloucester, but was never so created formally.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another daughter Isabella (or Hawisa) is said to have been married to a count from Brittany, but no further details exist. His widow and two other daughters (unnamed) are reported to have entered a convent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert fitzHamon's great granddaughter Isabel of Gloucester married John of England.
0 @N6000000002187738924@ NOTE Robert Fitzhamon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Robert Fitzhamon (died March 1107) was Lord of Gloucester and the conqueror of Glamorgan.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a cousin of William the Conqueror, although the details of his descent from the Norman dukes is uncertain. His family held the lordships of Torigny, Creully, Mézy, and Evrecy in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert probably did not fight at Hastings, and does not appear in the Domesday Book, although some relatives may. He first comes to prominence as a supporter of William Rufus during the Rebellion of 1088. After the revolt failed h
1 CONC e was rewarded with great estates in Gloucestershire and elsewhere. Some of these had belonged to the late Queen Matilda, and were supposed to be inherited by Rufus's younger brother Henry (the future Henry I); nevertheless Fitzha
1 CONC mon remained on good terms with Henry.
1 CONT
1 CONT The chronology of Fitzhamon's conquest of Glamorgan is uncertain, but it probably took place in the decades after he received Gloucester.
1 CONT
1 CONT Legend has it that Robert had ominous dreams in the days before Rufus' fatal hunting expedition, which postponed but did not prevent the outing. He was one of the first to gather in tears around Rufus' corpse, and he used his cloa
1 CONC k to cover the late king's body on its journey to be buried in Winchester. How much of these stories are the invention of later days is unknown.
1 CONT
1 CONT In any case Fitzhamon proved as loyal to Henry I as he had been to his predecessor, remaining on Henry's side in the several open conflicts with Henry's brother Robert Curthose. He was one of the three barons who negotiated the 11
1 CONC 01 truce between Henry I and Robert Curthose.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1105 he went to Normandy and was captured while fighting near his ancestral estates near Bayeux. This was one of the reasons Henry crossed the channel with a substantial force later that year. Fitzhamon was freed, and joined He
1 CONC nry's campaign, which proceeded to besiege Falaise. There Fitzhamon was severely injured in the head; although he lived two more years he was never the same mentally.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fitzhamon married Sibyl, daughter of Roger of Montgomery, 1st Earl of Shrewsbury, and Mabel of Bêlleme, by whom he probably only had one child, Mabel, who inherited his great estates and married Robert of Gloucester. Fitzhamon's G
1 CONC loucestershire property became the nucleus of the Earldom of Gloucester later given to his son-in-law.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT C. Warren Hollister, Henry I 
1 CONT Lynn Nelson, The Normans in South Wales, 1070-1171 (see especially pp. 94-110 in chapter 5) 
