0 @N6000000003051286205@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Hugh de Kevelioc, 3rd Earl of Chester", retrieved 21 Jun 08]
1 CONT Hugh de Kevelioc, 3rd Earl of Chester (1147 - June 30, 1181) was the son of Ranulph de Gernon, 2nd Earl of Chester and Maud of Gloucester, daughter of Robert, 1st Earl of Gloucester (otherwise known as Robert de Caen, the illegiti
1 CONC mate son of Henry I of England, making her Henry's granddaughter).
1 CONT
1 CONT He is thought by some to have taken his name from Kevelioc in Monmouth as his birthplace, but others think that instead he was born in, and took the name of, the cwmwd of Cyfeiliog (in modern Powys) in the southern part of the Kin
1 CONC gdom of Powys, Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was underage when his father's death in 1153 made him heir to his family's estates on both sides of the channel. He joined the baronial Revolt of 1173-1174 against King Henry II of England, and was influential in convincing the
1 CONC  Bretons to revolt. After being captured and imprisoned after the Battle of Alnwick, he finally got his estates restored in 1177, and served in King Henry's Irish campaigns.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1169 he married Bertrade de Montfort of Evreux, daughter of Simon III de Montfort. She was the cousin of King Henry, who gave her away in marriage. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Ranulph de Blondeville de Meschines, 4th Earl of Chester
1 CONT Maud of Chester (1171-1233), married David of Scotland, 8th Earl of Huntingdon
1 CONT Mabel of Chester, married William d'Aubigny, 3rd Earl of Arundel
1 CONT Agnes of Chester (died November 2, 1247), married William de Ferrers, 4th Earl of Derby
1 CONT Hawise of Chester (1180-1242), married Robert II de Quincy
1 CONT A daughter, name unknown, who was briefly married to Llywelyn Fawr
1 CONT He also had an illegitimate daughter, Amice of Chester, who married Ralph de Mainwaring.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh of Kevelioc died 30 June 1181 at Leek, Staffordshire, England.
0 @N6000000003319660196@ NOTE
1 CONT (Hugh de Meschines de Kyveliock) 5th Earl Palatine of Chester
0 @N6000000003949733412@ NOTE Hugh, styled "OF KEVELIOC," EARL OF CHESTER, also VICOMTE D'AVRANCHES, &c., in Normandy, son and heir born at Kevelioc [?Machynlleth], co. Merioneth. He joined in the rebellion against King Henry II, set on foot by Henry, the son 
1 CONC of that King, and was taken prisoner at Alnwick, 13 July 1174. He was deprived of his Earldom, and was again in rebellion both in England and Normandy, but, in January 1177, was restored. He married, in 1169, Bertrade, then aged 1
1 CONC 4 (the King giving her away in marriage "because she was his own cousin "), daughter of Simon de Montfort, Count D'EVREUX by his 1st wife, Maud. He died at Leek, co. Stafford, 30 June 1181, aged about 34 and was buried at St. Werb
1 CONC urg's, Chester. His widow died 1227, aged about 7I. [Complete Peerage III:167, XIV:170, (transcribed by Dave Utzinger)]
1 CONT This nobleman, Hugh (Keveliok), 3rd Earl of Chester, joined in the rebellion of the Earl of Lancaster and the King of Scots against King Henry II, and in support of that monarch's son, Prince Henry's pretensions to the crown. In w
1 CONC hich proceeding he was taken prisoner with the Earl of Leicester at Alnwick, but obtained his freedom soon afterwards upon the king's reconciliation with the young prince. Again, however, hoisting the standard of revolt both in En
1 CONC gland and Normandy, with as little success, he was again seized and then detained a prisoner for some years. He eventually, however, obtained his liberty and restoration of his lands when public tranquility became completely reest
1 CONC ablished some time about the 23rd year of the king's reign. His lordship m. Bertred, dau. of Simon, Earl of Evereux, in Normandy, and had issue, I. Ranulph, his successor; I. Maud, m. to David, Earl of Huntingdon, brother of Willi
1 CONC am, King of Scotland, and had one son and four daus., viz., 1. John, surnamed le Scot, who s. to the Earldom of Chester, d. s. p. 7 June, 1237; 1. Margaret, m. to Alan de Galloway, and had a dau., Devorguilla, m. to John de Baliol
1 CONC , and was mother of John de Baliol, declared King of Scotland in the reign of Edward I; 2. Isabel, m. to Robert de Brus, and was mother of Robert de Brus, who contended for the crown of Scotland, temp. Edward I; 3. Maud, d. unm.; 
1 CONC Ada, m. to Henry de Hastings, one of the competitors for the Scottish crown, temp. Edward I; II. Mabill, m. to William de Albini, Earl of Arundel; III. Agnes, m. to William de Ferrers, Earl of Derby; IV. Hawise, m. to Robert, son 
1 CONC of Sayer de Quincy, Earl of Winchester.
1 CONT The earl had another dau., whose legitimacy is questionable, namely, Amicia,* m. to Ralph de Mesnilwarin, justice of Chester, "a person," says Dugdale, "of very ancient family," from which union the Mainwarings, of Over Peover, in
1 CONC  the co. Chester, derive. Dugdale considers Amicia to be a dau. of the earl by a former wife. But Sir Peter Leicester, in his Antiquities of Chester, totally denies her legitimacy. "I cannot but mislike," says he, "the boldness an
1 CONC d ignorance of that herald who gave to Mainwaring (late of Peover), the elder, the quartering of the Earl of Chester's arms; for if he ought of right to quarter that coat, then must he be descended from a co-heir to the Earl of Ch
1 CONC ester; but he was not; for the co-heirs of Earl Hugh married four of the greatest peers in the kingdom."
1 CONT The earl d. at Leeke, in Staffordshire, in 1181, and was s. by his only son, Ranulph, surnamed Blundevil (or rather Blandevil) from the place of his birth, the town of Album Monasterium, modern Oswestry, in Powys), as 4th Earl of 
1 CONC Chester.
0 @N6000000004286817472@ NOTE !From "Ancestral Roots of 60 Colonist" by WEIS, 6th ed, line 125, page 113; "Hugh of Kevelioc b 1147, Kevelioc, Co Monmouth d 1181 Leeke, Co Stafford, Earl of Chester, Vicomte d'Avranches in Normandy; m 1169 Bertrade, dau of Simon
1 CONC  de Montfort, Count of Evreux. (CP III 167; V 670 chart IV)." From line 127, page 113; "Hugh of KEVELIOC (125-28) Earl of Chester; m Bertrade de Montfort. (CP II 167)."
