1 CONT
1 CONT This nobleman, Hugh (Keveliok), 3rd Earl of Chester, joined in therebellion of the Earl of Lancaster and the King of Scots against KingHenry II, and in support of that monarch's son, Prince Henry'spretensions to the crown. In whic
1 CONC h proceeding he was taken prisonerwith the Earl of Leicester at Alnwick, but obtained his freedom soonafterwards upon the king's reconciliation with the young prince.Again, however, hoisting the standard of revolt both in England 
1 CONC andNormandy, with as little success, he was again seized and thendetained a prisoner for some years. He eventually, however, obtainedhis liberty and restoration of his lands when public tranquilitybecame completely reestablished s
1 CONC ome time about the 23rd year of theking's reign. His lordship m. Bertred, dau. of Simon, Earl of Evereux,in Normandy, and had issue, I. Ranulph, his successor; I. Maud, m. toDavid, Earl of Huntingdon, brother of William, King of S
1 CONC cotland, andhad one son and four daus., viz., 1. John, surnamed le Scot, who s. tothe Earldom of Chester, d. s. p. 7 June, 1237; 1. Margaret, m. to Alande Galloway, and had a dau., Devorguilla, m. to John de Baliol, andwas mother 
1 CONC of John de Baliol, declared King of Scotland in the reignof Edward I; 2. Isabel, m. to Robert de Brus, and was mother of Robertde Brus, who contended for the crown of Scotland, temp. Edward I; 3.Maud, d. unm.; Ada, m. to Henry de 
1 CONC Hastings, one of the competitorsfor the Scottish crown, temp. Edward I; II. Mabill, m. to William deAlbini, Earl of Arundel; III. Agnes, m. to William de Ferrers, Earl ofDerby; IV. Hawise, m. to Robert, son of Sayer de Quincy, Ear
1 CONC l ofWinchester.
1 CONT
1 CONT The earl had another dau., whose legitimacy is questionable, namely,Amicia,* m. to Ralph de Mesnilwarin, justice of Chester, "a person,"says Dugdale, "of very ancient family," from which union theMainwarings, of Over Peover, in th
1 CONC e co. Chester, derive. Dugdaleconsiders Amicia to be a dau. of the earl by a former wife. But SirPeter Leicester, in his Antiquities of Chester, totally denies herlegitimacy. "I cannot but mislike," says he, "the boldness andignor
1 CONC ance of that herald who gave to Mainwaring (late of Peover), theelder, the quartering of the Earl of Chester's arms; for if he oughtof right to quarter that coat, then must he be descended from aco-heir to the Earl of Chester; but
1 CONC  he was not; for the co-heirs ofEarl Hugh married four of the greatest peers in the kingdom."
1 CONT
1 CONT The earl d. at Leeke, in Staffordshire, in 1181, and was s. by hisonly son, Ranulph, surnamed Blundevil (or rather Blandevil) from theplace of his birth, the town of Album Monasterium, modern Oswestry, inPowys), as 4th Earl of Che
1 CONC ster.
1 CONT
1 CONT * Upon the question of this lady's legitimacy there was a long paperwar between Sir Peter Leicester and Sir Thomas Mainwaring---andeventually the matter was referred to the judges, of whose decisionWood says, "at an assize held at
1 CONC  Chester, 1675, the controversy wasdecided by the justices itinerant, who, as I have heard, adjudged theright of the matter to Mainwaring." [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant andExtinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, pp. 
1 CONC 365-6,Meschines, Earls of Chester]
0 @N6000000005597651372@ NOTE !From "Ancestral Roots of 60 Colonist" by WEIS, 6th ed, line 125, page 113; "Hugh of Kevelioc b 1147, Kevelioc, Co Monmouth d 1181 Leeke, Co Stafford, Earl of Chester, Vicomte d'Avranches in Normandy; m 1169 Bertrade, dau of Simon
1 CONC  de Montfort, Count of Evreux. (CP III 167; V 670 chart IV)." From line 127, page 113; "Hugh of KEVELIOC (125-28) Earl of Chester; m Bertrade de Montfort. (CP II 167)."
0 @N6000000006422120012@ NOTE !From "Ancestral Roots of 60 Colonist" by WEIS, 6th ed, line 125, page 113; "Hugh of Kevelioc b 1147, Kevelioc, Co Monmouth d 1181 Leeke, Co Stafford, Earl of Chester, Vicomte d'Avranches in Normandy; m 1169 Bertrade, dau of Simon
1 CONC  de Montfort, Count of Evreux. (CP III 167; V 670 chart IV)." From line 127, page 113; "Hugh of KEVELIOC (125-28) Earl of Chester; m Bertrade de Montfort. (CP II 167)."
0 @N6000000008799857763@ NOTE [Jeremiah Brown.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [from Ancestry.com 139798.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh (Keveliok) de Meschines, surnamed from the place of his birth in Merionethshire, was the third Earl of Chester. He joined in the rebellion of the Earl of Leicester and the King of Scots against King Henry II, and in support o
1 CONC f that monarch's son, Prince Henry's pretensions to the crown. In that proceeding he was taken prisoner along with the Earl of Leicester at Alnwick, but obtained his freedom soon afterwards, upon the King's reconciliation with the
1 CONC  young prince. Again, however, hoisting the banner of revolt, both in England and in Normandy, with as little success he was again seized and detained as prisoner for some years. He eventually obtained his liberty and restoration 
1 CONC of his lands when public tranquillity became completely re-established some time about the 23rd year of the King's reign.
0 @N6000000007329696606@ NOTE [Descent from Battle of Hastings, Kenneth J. Hart] :  [see notes under De
1 CONT Quincy, Saer]
0 @N6000000003658774532@ NOTE HUGH DE COURTENAY, Knt., of Okehampton, Devon, Waddesdon, Buckinghamshire, etc., son and heir, born 25 March 1251.  He married ELEANOR LE DESPENSER, daughter of Hugh le Despenser, of Loughborough, Leicestershire, Ryhall, Rutland, 
1 CONC Parlington, Yorkshire, etc., Justiciar of England, by Aline, daughter and heiress of Philip Basset, Knt., of Wycombe, Buckinghamshire, and Wootton Basset, Wiltshire.  Her maritagium included the manors of Wootton and Dunstredon.  
1 CONC They had three sons ... and four daughters. ... He was in the Army of West Wales in 1282.  He was summoned to attend the King at Shrewsbury 28 June 1283.  SIR HUGH DE COURTENAY died at Colecombe (or Cullicombe), Devon 28 Feb. 1291
1 CONC /2.  His widow, Eleanor, presented to the church of Hemington, Somerset in 1316, the church of Parkham, Devon in 1322, and the church of Wrington, Somerset in 1327.  She died at London when returning from Canterbury 30 Sept. 1328.
1 CONC   They were buried at Cowick Priory near Exeter, Devon.
0 @N6000000005783672017@ NOTE [ss.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Baron of Okehampton.
1 CONT Buried at Cowick, Devon.
