0 @N6000000003658774517@ NOTE From Magna Carta Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT HUGH DE COURTENAY, Knt., of Okehampton, Devon, Waddesdon, Buckinghamshire, etc., son and heir, born 14 Sept. 1276 (came of age in 1297).  He married 1292 AGNES DE SAINT JOHN, daughter of John de Saint John, Knt., of Basing, Hampsh
1 CONC ire, by Alice, daughter of Reynold Fitz Peter, Knt., of Blaen Llyfni (in Cathedine), Breconshire, Wales.  They had four sons ... and two daughters. ... He was heir in 1293 to his cousin, Isabel de Rivers, Countess of Aumale and De
1 CONC von.  He served in the Scottish wars, 1298-1303.  He was summoned to Parliament from 6 Feb. 1298/9 by writs directed Hugoni de Curtenay.  He was in the Scottish wars, and was present at the Siege of Caerlaverock Castle in 1300.  H
1 CONC e was knighted by the Prince of Wales 22 May 1306, a Banneret 1308.  He was chosen one of the Lords Ordainers in 1313, and was a member of the King's Council 9 August 1318.  On 22 Feb. 1334/5 he was declared Earl of Devon.  SIR HU
1 CONC GH DE COURTENAY, Earl of Devon, died testate at Tiverton, Devon 23 Dec. 1340.  His widow, Agnes, died 11 June 1345.  They were buried at Cowick Priory near Exeter, Devon.
0 @N6000000005783672027@ NOTE [ss.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Baron of Okehampton, High Admiral of the West Seas, created Earl of
1 CONT Devon 1335.
1 CONT Sir Hugh was in the wars with Scotland and at the siege of
1 CONT Carlaverock in 1300.  Knighted by the Prince of Wales 1306, a
1 CONT Banneret in 1308; the King's Council 1318; Warden of the Coasts of
1 CONT Devon & Cornwall in 1324 & 1336.
1 CONT Buried at Cowick, Exeter.
