0 @N6000000004287066826@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Wraxall, Sir
1 CONT Was made Baron by the Westminster Parliament on March 4, 1309. He was
1 CONT a gr eat soldier and close companion of the King. Hisrole as one of
1 CONT the foremo st Chieftains who in 1300 assisted in the assault that came
1 CONT to be kno wn as the 'seige of Carlaverock' was celebrated in
1 CONT minstrelry with the fol lowing:
1 CONT "Sir Ralph de Georges there I saw
1 CONT One newly bound to Knighthoods law
1 CONT Down on the earth was prostrate thrown
1 CONT More than once struck by some great stone
1 CONT Or staggered by the rushing crowd
1 CONT Still to recede he was tooproud
1 CONT Upon his arms and surcoats fold
1 CONT Was Mascally of blue and gold."
1 CONT (see "De Georges from Gaurges in the Cotentin, Normandy, 1065", p. 17)
1 CONT -------------------
1 CONT BARONY of GORGES (I) 1309
1 CONT Sir Ralph de Gorges, of Wraxall (Somerset), Bradpool (Dorset),
1 CONT Braunton (D evon), Knighton (Isle of Wight), etc. was son and heir of
1 CONT Sir Ralph de Gor ges, of the same, presumably by Maud, his wife (b),
1 CONT and grandson of anoth er Sir Ralph de Gorges, by his wife Ellen,
1 CONT daughterand coheir of Ives de M orville.  He served in the wars in
1 CONT Gascony and was taken prisoner, remaini ng in France till after 2 Apr
1 CONT 1299.  He appears to have returned befo re 20 Mar 1300 and had a
1 CONT military summons against the Scots, 24 June 130 0, being present at
1 CONT the siege of Carlaverock in July, shortly after he h ad received
1 CONT knighthood.  He was sheriff of Devon in 1306, and again in 130 8.  He
1 CONT wassummoned to Parliament from 4 Mar 1308/9 to 18 Sep 1322, by wri ts
1 CONT directed Radulpho de Gorges, whereby he is held to have become LORD
1 CONT GOR GES.  In 1311 he wassupervisor of the array in Somerset and Dorset
1 CONT and lea der of the levies.  Thereafter he had the usual summonses for
1 CONT military ser vice, and on 1 Feb 1320/1 wasappointed to the office of
1 CONT Justiciar of Irela nd, with an allowance of 500 marks a year, a
1 CONT mandate issuing the same d ay to the person holding the place of Roger
1 CONT de Mortimer (of Wigmore), la te Justiciar, to make delivery to the
1 CONT said Ralph.  A few days later, 6 Fe b, he had letters of protection
1 CONT for one year as Ralph de Gorges, the elde r, going on the King's
1 CONT service to Ireland.  On 5 Mar, the Mayor of Brist ol and others were
1 CONT ordered to have 2 ships at that port for the passa ge of the new
1 CONT Justiciar and his men. He, however, never reached Ireland, b ut became
1 CONT involved in the Despenser troubles in Wales, and was, as a knig ht of
1 CONT SirHugh Despenser, the younger, holding Caerphilly Castle, from whi ch
1 CONT (after its capture) he was expelled wouded.  On 2 Jul 1321 he had a
1 CONT gra nt of 500 marks towards his ransom, he having been lately taken
1 CONT prison er in the King's service, and on 12 Nov 1321 had a protection
1 CONT as Ral ph de Gorges, the elder, lately on the King's service in the
1 CONT Marches of Wa les, and in that cause taken and imprisoned.On 16 Nov
1 CONT his own and his ance stors debts were respited till Easter because of
1 CONT his captivity, and on 3 D ec Roger de Mortimer of Wigmore was ordered
1 CONT to release him.  This appea rs to have been done before 15 Feb 1321/2,
1 CONT when he and John de Cliveden we re ordered to raise 1,000 foot men in
1 CONT Somerset and Dorset, the order bei ng increased (25 Mar) to 2,000, to
1 CONT be conducted to the King at Newcastle-o n-Tyne.  He appears himself to
1 CONT have joined the Scots expedition in Aug 132 2, and was summoned for
1 CONT military service for the last time 3 Apr 1323, bei ng discharged from
1 CONT attendance 2 Jun 1323.
1 CONT He m. before 20 Nov 1301, Eleanor (f).  Hewas living 15 July, but was
1 CONT de ad before 27 Sep 1323.  His widow was granted asdower the manor of
1 CONT Wraxa ll and one-third of the manor of Braunton (25 Jan 1323/4) by
1 CONT assignmen ts of 1 and 20 Mar 1323/4, enrolled 18 Dec 1324.  The manors
1 CONT ofBradpool, L itton, and Combe, with the hundred of Beaminster, etc.
1 CONT had already been se ttled on her husband and herself cojointly.  She
1 CONT m., in or before 1326, Jo hn Peche (1st Lord Peche), and was living
1 CONT 1334.  [Complete Peerage VI:9-1 2, XIV:342]
1 CONT (b) On 27 June 1297, Maud, late the wife of Ralph de Gorges, had
1 CONT assignme nt of dower for a third of her husband's lan
