0 @N6000000002187959140@ NOTE Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Roger de Beaumont, 2nd Earl of Warwick (1102 – 12 June 1153), was the elder son of Henry de Beaumont, 1st Earl of Warwick and Marguerite, daughter of Geoffrey II of Perche and Beatrix of Montdidier. He was also known as Roger de N
1 CONC ewburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was generally considered to have been a devout and pious man; a chronicle of the period, the Gesta Regis Stephani, speaks of him as a "man of gentle disposition". The borough of Warwick remembers him as the founder of the Hospi
1 CONC tal of S. Michael for lepers which he endowed with the tithes of Wedgnock, and other property; he also endowed the House of the Templars beyond the bridge. In the reign of Stephen he founded a priory dedicated to S. Kenned at Llan
1 CONC gennilth, Co. Glamorgan and he attached it as a cell to the Abbey of S. Taurinus at Evreux in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT He visited the Holy Land several times and took part in the Second Crusade, and he apparently joined the expedition under Conrad III of Germany after the Siege of Lisbon.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He married 1130 Gundred de Warrenne, daughter of William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey and Elizabeth de Vermandois and had children:
1 CONT
1 CONT William de Beaumont, 3rd Earl of Warwick. 
1 CONT Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick. 
1 CONT Henry de Beaumont, was Dean of Salisbury in 1205. 
1 CONT Agnes de Beaumont, married Geoffrey de Clinton, Chamberlain to the King and son of Geoffrey, the founder of Kenilworth Castle and Priory. 
1 CONT Margaret de Beaumont. 
1 CONT Gundred de Beaumont, married: 
1 CONT Hugh Bigod, 1st Earl of Norfolk; 
1 CONT Roger de Glanville. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Edward T. Beaumont, J.P. The Beaumonts in History. A.D. 850-1850. Oxfor
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry de Beaumont Earl of Warwick
1 CONT 1123 – 1153 Succeeded by:
1 CONT William de Beaumont 
0 @N6000000003051193074@ NOTE [Weis 82] Crusader
0 @N6000000003949907603@ NOTE  1st son and heir, was probably under age at his father's death and obtained the Earldom in 1123, before Easter [15 April), when his name first appears as a witness to a royal charter. He attested charters of Henry I, the two late
1 CONC st in 1131. After the accession of Stephen he was at the Easter Court [22 March 1135/6) at Westminster; in April he witnessed the King's charter of liberties at Oxford, and in the same year he was with Stephen at Winchester. After
1 CONC  the battle of Lincoln, 2 February 1140/1, he joined the Empress Maud of his own free will. He served with her at the siege of Winchester in 1141; but early in 1142 he was with Stephen at Stamford. He does not seem to have taken a
1 CONC ny active part in the Civil War; but at an unknown date he allowed Warwick Castle to be garrisoned by Stephen's troops, and in 1153 he was with the King when he heard that the garrison had been tricked by Henry's knights and the C
1 CONC astle surrendered. Although he was not to blame, it is said that he was so overcome with shame and grief that he died suddenly. He founded the Templars' House and St. Michael's Hospital, both in Warwick, completed the foundation o
1 CONC f Warwick Priory and was a benefactor to a large number of religious foundations.
1 CONT Roger de Newburgh, 2nd Earl of Warwick. This nobleman, in the contest between the Empress Maud and King Stephen, espoused the cause of the former, but his lordship is much more known by his munificent grants to the church than his
1 CONC  martial deeds. He married Gundred, daughter of William, Earl of Warren, and had issue, William and Waleran, successive earls, Henry, and Agnes. The earl died 12 June, 1153, and was succeeded by his eldest son, William de Newburgh
1 CONC , 3rd Earl of Warwick. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 399, Newburgh, Earls of Warwick]
0 @N6000000005076818845@ NOTE Roger de Newburgh, 2nd Earl of Warwick was the son of Henry de Newburgh, 1st Earl of Warwick and Margaret de Perche.2 He married Gundred de Warenne, daughter of William II de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey and Elizabeth de Vermandois
1 CONC .1 He died on 12 June 1153.1
1 CONT      Roger de Newburgh, 2nd Earl of Warwick gained the title of 2nd Earl of Warwick.
1 CONT Children of Roger de Newburgh, 2nd Earl of Warwick and Gundred de Warenne
1 CONT William de Newburgh, 3rd Earl of Warwick d. 15 Nov 11842
1 CONT Waleran de Newburgh, 4th Earl of Warwick+ d. 12052
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S22] Sir Bernard Burke, C.B. LL.D., A Genealogical History of the Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire, new edition (1883; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland: Genealogical Publishing Company, 1978), p
1 CONC age 569. Hereinafter cited as Burkes Extinct Peerage.
1 CONT [S22] Burke, Burkes Extinct Peerage, page 399.
