0 @N6000000003006249917@ NOTE Baron of Despencer 
0 @N6000000002188655925@ NOTE Hugh le Despencer, 2nd Baron le Despencer
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh le Despencer (1308 – 1349) was the eldest son and heir of Hugh the younger Despenser, son of Hugh the elder Despenser. His father and grandfather both were executed in 1326. His mother was Alianore de Clare, daughter of Gilbe
1 CONC rt de Clare, 7th Earl of Hertford.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh was restored to his grandfather's title of Baron le Despencer in 1338. He died without issue in 1349. His heir was his nephew, Edward le Despencer, 1st Baron le Despencer, who was created Baron le Despencer of a new creation 
1 CONC in 1375.
1 CONT
1 CONT This biography of a baron in the Peerage of England is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
1 CONT Peerage of England
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Hugh Despenser Baron le Despencer Succeeded by
1 CONT extinct
0 @N6000000002906267852@ NOTE ?? Line 3271: (New PAF RIN=6227)
1 CONT     1 TITL [BARON LE DESPENSER]/
1 CONT
1 CONT ?? Line 3274: (New PAF RIN=6227)
1 CONT     1 BIRT
1 CONT     2 DATE ABT 1308 (18-1326)
0 @N6000000003658791919@ NOTE From Magna Carta Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT HUGH LE DESPENSER, Knt., lord of Glamorgan and Morgannwyg, Wales, and of Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire, Maplederwell, Hampshire, Hanley, Worcestershire, etc., and, in right of his wife, of Barrow, Suffolk, son and heir, born about 1
1 CONC 308 (aged 18 in 1326).  He successfully defended Caerphilly Castle against Queen Isabel's forces in 1327, until he obtained pardon of his life.  He was imprisoned at Bristol Castle 15 Dec. 1328, and not released until 5 July 1331.
1 CONC   In 1332 he was about to go on a pilgrimage to Santiago.  In 1332 he was granted manor of Frithby, Leicestershire and lands in Hampshire and the Isle of Wight.  He had livery of his mother's lands 21 July 1337.  He was summoned t
1 CONC o Parliament from 15 Nov. 1338 to 1 Jan. 1348/9 by writs directed Hugoni le Despenser, whereby he is held to have become Lord Despenser.  He was in the Scottish wars, Nov. 1337-1338, and present at the Battle of Sluys 24 June 1340
1 CONC .  He married after 31 May 1341 (by dispensation dated 27 April 1341, he and her former husband being related in the 3rd degree of kindred) ELIZABETH DE MONTAGU, widow of Giles de Badlesmere, Knt., 2nd Lord Badlesmere (died 7 June
1 CONC  1338), and daughter of William de Montagu, Knt., 1st Earl of Salisbury, 3rd Lord Montagu, Marshal of England, by Katherine, daughter of William de Grandison (or Graunson), Knt., 1st Lord Grandison.  They had no issue.  During the
1 CONC  Breton civil war, he served as a captain in the English army which defeated the French at Morlaix in Oct. 1342.  He accompanied the King to France in July 1346, and was in the King's retinue at the Battle of Crécy.  He was presen
1 CONC t at the Siege of Calais, which surrendered 4 August 1347.  SIR HUGH LE DESPENSER, 3rd Lord Despenser, died 8 Feb. 1348/9, and was buried in Tewkesbury Abbey, Gloucestershire.  His widow, Elizabeth, married (3rd) before 10 July 13
1 CONC 50 (as his 2nd wife) Guy de Bryan (or Brian), K.G., Lord Bryan (died 17 August 1390).  She died at Ashley, Hampshire 30 (or 31) July 1359, and was buried with her 2nd husband at Tewkesbury Abbey.
0 @N6000000003658791874@ NOTE From Magna Carta Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT GILBERT LE DESPENSER, Knt., younger son.  He was granted the manor of Melton Mowbray, Leicestershire by his uncle, King Edward II, in 1322, subject to a life estate reserved to his mother, Eleanor.  As an adult, he was granted the
1 CONC  manor of Broad Town (in Cliffe Pypard), Wiltshire by his older brother, Hugh le Despenser.  GILBERT LE DESPENSER died without issue 23 April 1381.
0 @N6000000000337436767@ NOTE Source #1: Gary Boyd Roberts, "The Royal Descents of 500 Immigrants to the American Colonies or the United States, Who Were Themselves Notable or Left Descendants Notable in American History" (Baltimore:  Genealogical Publishing C
1 CONC o, Inc., 2001) p. 277.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  Frederick Lewis Weis, "Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700" - Seventh Edition, with additions and corrections by Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., assisted by Davis Faris (Baltimore, M
1 CONC D:  Genealogical Publishing Co, 1995), p. 74
1 CONT
