0 @N6000000003615871431@ NOTE [janet skelton.FTW] 
1 CONT Maurice married in August 1338 (when aged about 8), Elizabeth, daught
1 CONT er of Hugh Despenser, the younger by Eleanor, daughter and coheirof Gilbe
1 CONT rt de Clare, Earl of Gloucester and Hertford, ''but though thus marri
1 CONT ed at 8, yet he had no issue by her till about 12 or 13 years after.
1 CONT '' He died at Berkeley Castle, ''never thoroughly cured of the wounds he r
1 CONT eceived at Poitiers,'' 8 June 1368, aged 37 and upwards, and was buried wi
1 CONT th his mother at St. Augustine's, Bristol. Inquisition post mortem 12 Ju
1 CONT ly (1368) 42 Edward III. His widow died 13 July 1389, and was buri
1 CONT ed at St Botolph's, London. Source: CPII: 130
1 CONT Elizabeth Despenser, Lady Berkeley, was probably the youngest of Hugh Desp
1 CONT enser the younger and Eleanor de Clare's children. After her father's down
1 CONT fall and execution, she escaped the fate of 3 of her elder sisters- bei
1 CONT ng sent to a nunnery for life - due to her extreme youth. In August 133
1 CONT 8, young Elizabeth Despenser was delivered by the prioress of Wix to the c
1 CONT are of her aunt Elizabeth de Burgh, Lady of Clare. The young girl was pa
1 CONT rt of her aunt's household for about a year and a half, when she was se
1 CONT nt to Tewkesbury to live with the Berkeleys. Elizabeth Despenser maintain
1 CONT ed a close relationship with her aunt up until the latter's death in 136
1 CONT 0. This information is from Frances Underhill's book "For Her Good Estat
1 CONT e: The Life of Elizabeth de Burgh."
1 CONT Elizabeth Despenser and Maurice, Lord Berkeley, had seven children that su
1 CONT rvived infancy. But apparently only two (sons) had issue themselves.
