0 @N6000000000682289025@ NOTE TITLE: COUNTESS OF ARUNDEL
0 @N6000000002188011341@ NOTE Eleanor of Lancaster
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor of Lancaster (sometimes called Eleanor Plantagenet1) (about 1315 – 11 January 1372) was born in Arundel, West Sussex, England, the fifth daughter of Henry, Earl of Lancaster (c. 1281-1345) and his wife Maud Chaworth (1282-
1 CONC 1322).
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 First marriage and offspring 
1 CONT 2 Second marriage 
1 CONT 3 Sources 
1 CONT 4 Notes 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT First marriage and offspring
1 CONT Sometime between September 1 and November 6, 1330, she married John Beaumont, 2nd Lord Beaumont, son of Henry Beaumont, 4th Earl of Buchan (c. 1288 - 1340) and his wife Alice Comyn (c. 1291-1349). They had two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry Beaumont, 3rd Lord Beaumont, born late 1339 
1 CONT Matilda Beaumont (died July 1467), married Hugh de Courtenay 
1 CONT Eleanor was a lady-in-waiting to Queen Philippa, and was in service to her in Ghent when her son Henry was born. John de Beaumont died in a tournament on 14 April 1342.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Second marriage
1 CONT On 5 February 1344 at Ditton Church, Stoke Poges, Buckinghamshire, she married Richard Fitzalan, 10th Earl of Arundel, 4th Earl of Surrey, known by the soubriquet of "Copped Hat", Justiciar of North Wales, Governor of Carnarvon Ca
1 CONC stle, Admiral of the West.2
1 CONT
1 CONT His previous marriage, to Isabel le Despenser, had taken place when they were children. It was annulled by Papal mandate as she, since her father's attainder and execution, had ceased to be of any importance to him. Pope Clement V
1 CONC I obligingly annulled the marriage, bastardized the issue, and provided a dispensation for his second marriage to the woman with whom he had been living in adultery (the dispensation, dated 4 March 1344/1345, was required because 
1 CONC his first and second wives were first cousins).
1 CONT
1 CONT The children of Eleanor's second marriage were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard (1346-1397), who succeeded as Earl of Arundel 
1 CONT John Fitzalan(bef 1349-1379) 
1 CONT Thomas Arundel, Archbishop of York (c. 1350-February 19, 1413) 
1 CONT Joan Fitzalan (bef. 1351-April 17, 1419), married Humphrey de Bohun, 7th Earl of Hereford 
1 CONT Alice Fitzalan (1352-March 17, 1416), married Thomas Holland, 2nd Earl of Kent 
1 CONT Eleanor died at Arundel and was buried at Lewes Priory in Lewes, Sussex, England. Her husband was buried beside her; in his will Richard requests to be buried "near to the tomb of Eleanor de Lancaster, my wife; and I desire that m
1 CONC y tomb be no higher than hers, that no men at arms, horses, hearse, or other pomp, be used at my funeral, but only five torches...as was about the corpse of my wife, be allowed."
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Fowler, Kenneth. The King's Lieutenant, 1969 
1 CONT Nicolas, Nicholas Harris. Testamenta Vetusta, 1826. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1The surname "Plantagenet" has been retrospectively applied to the descendants of Geoffrey V, Count of Anjou and Empress Matilda without historical justification: it is simply a convenient, if deceptive, method of referring to peo
1 CONC ple who had, in fact, no surname. The first descendant of Geoffrey to use the surname was Richard Plantagenet, 3rd Duke of York (father of both Edward IV of England and Richard III of England) who apparently assumed it about 1448.
1 CONC
1 CONT 2also called Richard de Arundel. 
