0 @N6000000003658760421@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT JOAN (or JEANNE) OF ENGLAND, born at Angers Oct. 1165.  She married (1st) at Palermo ... WILLIAM (or GUGLIELMO) II le Bon, King of Sicily. ... They had one son, Bohemond. ... Joan ... married (2nd) at Rouen, Normandy ... (as his 4
1 CONC th wife) RAYMOND V. ... They had one son, Raymond VII ... and one child who died at birth.  JOAN, Queen of Sicily, Duchess of Narbonne, etc., died testate at Rouen 24 Sept. 1199.  No living descendants.
0 @N6000000003177905107@ NOTE 1  AUTH Sl
0 @N6000000005076664362@ NOTE Joan Plantagenet was born in October 1165 at Angers Castle, Angers, Anjou, France.1 She was the daughter of Henry II 'Curtmantle' d'Anjou, King of England and Eleanor, Duchesse d'Aquitaine. She married William II, King of Naples a
1 CONC nd Sicily, son of William I, King of Naples and Sicily, on 13 February 1177 at Palermo Cathedral, Palermo, Sicily, Italy.1 She married Raimond VI, Comte de Toulouse, son of Raimond V, Comte de Toulouse and Constance de Toulouse, i
1 CONC n October 1196 at Rouen, Caux, France.1 She died on 4 September 1199 at age 33 at Fontevraud Abbey, Fontevraud, France, childbirth.1 She was buried at Fontevraud Abbey, Fontevraud, France.1
1 CONT      As a result of her marriage, Joan Plantagenet was styled as Queen Joan of Sicily on 13 February 1177.1
1 CONT Child of Joan Plantagenet and William II, King of Naples and Sicily
1 CONT Bohemond, Duca d'Apulia b. 1181, d. 11812
1 CONT Children of Joan Plantagenet and Raimond VI, Comte de Toulouse
1 CONT Raimond VII, Comte de Toulouse+ b. c 1197, d. 12491
1 CONT Wilhelmina de Toulouse b. 11982
1 CONT Richard de Toulouse b. 1199, d. 11992
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 62. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
1 CONT [S106] Royal Genealogies Website (ROYAL92.GED), online ftp://ftp.cac.psu.edu/genealogy/public_html/royal/index.html. Hereinafter cited as Royal Genealogies Website.
0 @N6000000005599114603@ NOTE In August of 1176, Joan left from Winchester for France and her marria ge to William II 'le Bon' of Sicily.  Her dowry for the marriage to Wi lliam was siezed by William's illegitimate nephew Tancred.  In 1189, T ancred also put h
1 CONC er under house arrest at Palermo.  Upon his arrival i n Sicily, enroute to the Holy Land, Joans' brother Richard I of Englan d demanded her release.  She was quickly released and with her dowry d elivered to Richard at Messina.  W
1 CONC hen her mother returned to Normandi e in April of 1191, Joan was placed in charge of the care of Berengari a.  On the voyage from Sicily, Joan and Berengaria's vessel was lost i n a storm and separated from Richards fleet who had 
1 CONC found harbor at Rh odes.  Joan and Berengaria's vessel found safety at Limassol on Cyprus .
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan was the fourth wife of Raymond of Toulouse.  By 1191, a widow an d dispossed by her husbands successor Tancred, she met her brother Ric hard at Naples and accompanied him and his bride Berengaria to the cru sade.  She and Ber
1 CONC engaria took an immediate liking to each other.  On 12 September 1192, Richard I dispatched Joan homeward on a ship bound for Sicily that was commanded by Steven de Turnham, Richards new Admir al. She and Berengaria returned from 
1 CONC the crusade via Messina.  In Sici ly, they were lavishly entertained by Tancred.  From Sicily, they set sail for Naples, and from there overland to Rome.  They idled in Rome until December of 1192.  She and Berengaria were accompa
1 CONC nied from Rom e not to Pisa, then to Genoa by an escort of Cardinals provided by Pop e Celestine.  From Genoa, they took ship for Marseilles.  At Marseille s, they were met by Alfonso II King of Aragon who gave them safe condu ct 
1 CONC through his territory and arranged for Raymond Count of St. Giles t o escort them on to Poitou.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1197, after her brother Richard arranged her marriage to Raymond d e Toulouse, he granted her the rights to Agen and Quercy as her dower .  Her marriage to Raymond was not a happy and he did not treat her we ll.  In May of 1199
1 CONC , Joan, then pregnant, joined her mother at Niort .  She was attacked by some of Raymond's vassels.  She raised an army to suppress the revolt and laid siege to a rebel stronghold near Casse e.  A fire was set to the camp by some 
1 CONC of her knights and Joan was bur ned in the fire and barely escapted.  Eleanor sent her to Fontevrault to be cared for by the nuns.   Just prior to her death, Joan requeste d to take the veil, which was granted by Hubert Walter - A
1 CONC rchbishop o f Canterbury.  On source indicates that Joan and her young infant may have both died at Beaucaire instead of at Rouen (?).
1 CONT NOTE
1 CONT GEDCOM created by TMG...
