0 @N6000000002188655499@ NOTE John Talbot, 1st Earl of Shrewsbury
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John Talbot, 1st Earl of Shrewsbury (1384/1390 – 17 July 1453) was an important English military commander during the Hundred Years' War, as well as the only Lancastrian Constable of France.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Family
1 CONT     * 2 Children
1 CONT     * 3 2nd Marriage
1 CONT     * 4 Early career
1 CONT     * 5 Service in France
1 CONT     * 6 The English Achilles
1 CONT     * 7 Cultural influence
1 CONT     * 8 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT He was second son of Richard, 4th Baron Talbot, by Ankaret, heiress of the last Lord Strange of Blackmere.
1 CONT
1 CONT Talbot was married on 12 March 1406 to Maud Nevill, daughter and heiress of Thomas Nevill, 5th Baron Furnivall, the son of John Neville, 3rd Baron Neville de Raby. He was summoned to Parliament in her right from 1409.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT
1 CONT The couple had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Lady Joan Talbot
1 CONT     * John Talbot, 2nd Earl of Shrewsbury (c. 1413 – 11 July 1460)
1 CONT     * Sir Christopher Talbot (d. 10 July 1460)
1 CONT     * Hon. Thomas Talbot (died before his father in Bordeaux)
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1421 by the death of his niece he acquired the Baronies of Talbot and Strange.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] 2nd Marriage
1 CONT
1 CONT He married, secondly, Lady Margaret Beauchamp, daughter of Richard de Beauchamp, 13th Earl of Warwick and Elizabeth de Berkeley, on 6 September 1425 and had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Sir Lewis Talbot
1 CONT     * John Talbot, 1st Viscount Lisle (c. 1426 – 17 July 1453)
1 CONT     * Sir Humphrey Talbot (before 1453 – c. 1492)
1 CONT     * Lady Elizabeth Talbot (before 1453). She married John de Mowbray, 4th Duke of Norfolk.
1 CONT     * Lady Eleanor Talbot (d. 1468) married to Thomas Butler and King Edward IV of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early career
1 CONT
1 CONT From 1404 to 1413 he served with his elder brother Gilbert in the Welsh war or the rebellion of Owain Glyndwr. Then for five years from February 1414 he was lieutenant of Ireland, where he held the honour of Wexford. He did some f
1 CONC ighting, and had a sharp quarrel with the Earl of Ormonde. Complaints were made against him both for harsh government in Ireland and for violence in Herefordshire. From 1420 to 1424 he served in France. In 1425, he was lieutenant 
1 CONC again for a short time in Ireland.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Service in France
1 CONT
1 CONT So far his career was that of a turbulent Marcher Lord, employed in posts where a rough hand was useful. In 1427 he went again to France, where he fought with distinction in Maine and at the Siege of Orléans. He fought at the Batt
1 CONC le of Patay where he was captured and held prisoner for four years.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was released in exchange for the French leader Jean Poton de Xaintrailles. Talbot was a daring and aggressive soldier, perhaps the most audacious Captain of the Age. He and his forces acted as a kind of fire brigade ever ready 
1 CONC to retake a town and to meet a French advance. His trademark was rapid aggressive attacks. In January 1436 he led a small force including Kyriell and routed La Hire and Xaintrailles at Ry near Rouen. The following year at Crotoy, 
1 CONC after a daring passage of the Somme, he put a numerous Burgundian force to flight. In December 1439, following a surprise flank attack on their camp, he dispersed the 6000 strong army of the Constable Richemont, and the following 
1 CONC year he retook Harfleur. In 1441 he pursued the French army 4 times over the Seine and Oise rivers in an unavailing attempt to bring it to battle.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The English Achilles
1 CONT
1 CONT He was appointed in 1445 by Henry VI of England (as king of France) as Constable of France. Taken hostage at Rouen in 1449 he promised never to wear armour against the French King again, and he was true to his word. He was defeate
1 CONC d and killed in 1453 at the Battle of Castillon near Bordeaux, which effectively ended English rule in the duchy of Gascony, a principal cause of the Hundred Years' War. His heart was buried in the doorway of St Alkmund's Church, 
1 CONC Whitchurch, Shropshire.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT The victorious French generals raised a monument to Talbot on the field called Notre Dame de Talbot. And the French Chroniclers paid him handsome tribute:
1 CONT
1 CONT "Such was the end of this famous and renowned English leader who for so long had been one of the most formidable thorns in the side of the French, who regarded him with terror and dismay" - Matthew d'Escourcy
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Talbot is generally remembered as a great soldier, some have raised doubts as to his generalship. In particular, charges of rashness have been raised against him. Speed and aggression are key elements in granting success 
1 CONC in medieval war, and Talbot's numerical inferiority necessitated surprise. Furthermore, he was often in the position of trying to force battle on unwilling opponents. At his defeat at Patay in 1429 he was advised not to fight ther
1 CONC e by Sir John Fastolf, who was subsequently blamed for the debacle, but the French, inspired by Joan of Arc, showed unprecedented fighting spirit - usually they approached an English position with great circumspection. The charge 
1 CONC of rashness is perhaps more justifiable at Castillon where Talbot, misled by false reports of a French retreat, attacked their entrenched camp frontally - facing wheel to wheel artillery and a 6 to 1 inferiority in numbers.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is portrayed heroically in William Shakespeare's Henry VI, Part I: "Valiant Lord Talbot, Earl of Shrewsbury, Created, for his rare success in arms"
1 CONT Political offices
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT New Creation Lord High Steward of Ireland
1 CONT 1446–1453 Succeeded by
1 CONT The Earl of Shrewsbury
1 CONT Peerage of England
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT New Creation Earl of Shrewsbury
1 CONT 1442–1453 Succeeded by
1 CONT John Talbot
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ankaret Talbot Baron Talbot
1 CONT 1421–1453 Succeeded by
1 CONT John Talbot
1 CONT Baron Strange of Blackmere
1 CONT 1421–1453
1 CONT Peerage of Ireland
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT New Creation Earl of Waterford
1 CONT 1446–1453 Succeeded by
1 CONT John Talbot
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Cultural influence
1 CONT
1 CONT John Talbot is shown as a featured character in Koei's video game known as 'Bladestorm: The Hundred Years' War', appearing as the left-arm of Edward, the Black Prince, in which he assists the former and the respective flag of Engl
1 CONC and throughout his many portrayals.
1 CONT
1 CONT Talbot appears as one of the primary antagonists in the PSP game Jeanne d'Arc.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
