0 @N6000000003216133442@ NOTE Lord Talbot 1421. Lord Furnival, Lord Strange of Blackmere, Earl of Salop, Earl of Waterford.  1st Earl of Shrewsbury.
1 CONT He spent much of his life campaigning in France. In a period when weak government at home was costing the English control of France, Talbot stands out as one of the very few effective commanders. He was finally killed, with John h
1 CONC is son, at the Battle of Castillon, the last battle of the Hundred Years War. I doubt his sacrifice was appreciated by the government at home as the Wars of the Roses were beginning to get under way at that time.
0 @N6000000003615547466@ NOTE http://wc.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=DESC&db=glencoe&id=I3678
1 CONT
1 CONT DESCENDANT OF CHARLEMAGNE, ALFRED THE GREAT, WILLIAM THE CONQUEROR, HENRY I, EMPRESS MATILDA, HENRY II, KING JOHN, EDWARD I, SIR WILLIAM MARSHALL, STRONGBOW DE CLARE, AND LADY GODIVA
1 CONT
1 CONT     DESCENDANT OF MALCOLM III, DAVID I, AND THE ANCIENT KINGS OF SCOTLAND
1 CONT
1 CONT     DESCENDANT OF LLEWELYN THE GREAT PRINCE OF WALES, AND KING BRIAN BORU, IRELAND'S GREATEST KING
1 CONT
1 CONT     7TH LORD TALBOT, 1ST EARL OF SHREWSBURY, EARL OF WATERFORD, MARSHALL OF FRANCE, LORD LIEUTENANT OF IRELAND, 10TH LORD STRANGE OF BLACKMERE
1 CONT
1 CONT     Lord Talbot is one of the most illustrious figures in the whole of English history. He distinguished himself in the wars of Kings Henry V and Henry VI. The mere mention of his name brought terror to the French on account of hi
1 CONC s numerous victories there. In 1422, he was created the 1st Earl of Shrewsbury by Henry VI in reward for his service. He was finally defeated by Joan of Arc at the Siege of Orleans near Petay in 1429 where he was taken prisoner an
1 CONC d detained for 4 years before a ransom was paid. He continued his brilliant military career upon his release, was named Marshall of France and Lord Lieutenant (Viceroy) of Ireland. He built Whites Castle there in County Kildare.
1 CONT
1 CONT     BATTLE OF ORLEANS, 1429
1 CONT     At the age of 17, Joan of Arc led her troops in a glorious victory over the English. Fear of her soldiers was so intense that the English under commander Sir John Fastolfe fled the battlefield. Fastolfe was stripped of the Ord
1 CONC er of the Garter for his cowardice. Lord Talbot held his ground, but lost the battle and was captured. William Shakespeare took the name of the historical Fastolfe and turned him into Sir John Falstaff, the colorful rogue of HENRY
1 CONC  IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Lord Talbot moved the chief residence of the Talbot family from Goodrich Castle to Alton Towers (now an amusement park in Staffordshire).
1 CONT
1 CONT     In his 80's, Lord Talbot was sent by Henry VI to do battle once again in France. The people of Bordeaux were so taken by his love of fine wine that they named the area Talbot d'Aux in his honor. At the Battle of Chastillon in 
1 CONC 1453, the last battle of the 100 Years' War, Lord Talbot died from a head wound received from a battle axe.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Lord Talbot was immortalized in Shakespeare's play, HENRY VI, PART ONE. In Act I, Sc. iv, Talbot relates the story of his imprisonment by the French:
1 CONT
1 CONT     SALISBURY
1 CONT
1 CONT     Talbot, my life, my joy, again return'd!
1 CONT     How wert thou handled being prisoner?
1 CONT     Or by what means got'st thou to be released?
1 CONT     Discourse, I prithee, on this turret's top.
1 CONT
1 CONT     TALBOT
1 CONT
1 CONT     The Duke of Bedford had a prisoner
1 CONT     Call'd the brave Lord Ponton de Santrailles;
1 CONT     For him was I exchanged and ransomed.
1 CONT     But with a baser man of arms by far
1 CONT     Once in contempt they would have barter'd me:
1 CONT     Which I, disdaining, scorn'd; and craved death,
1 CONT     Rather than I would be so vile esteem'd.
1 CONT     In fine, redeem'd I was as I desired.
1 CONT     But, O! the treacherous Fastolfe wounds my heart,
1 CONT     Whom with my bare fists I would execute,
1 CONT     If I now had him brought into my power.
1 CONT
1 CONT     SALISBURY
1 CONT
1 CONT     Yet tell'st thou not how thou wert entertain'd.
1 CONT
1 CONT     TALBOT
1 CONT
1 CONT     With scoffs and scorns and contumelious taunts.
1 CONT     In open market-place produced they me,
1 CONT     To be a public spectacle to all:
1 CONT     Here, said they, is the terror of the French,
1 CONT     The scarecrow that affrights our children so.
1 CONT     Then broke I from the officers that led me,
1 CONT     And with my nails digg'd stones out of the ground,
1 CONT     To hurl at the beholders of my shame:
1 CONT     My grisly countenance made others fly;
1 CONT     None durst come near for fear of sudden death.
1 CONT     In iron walls they deem'd me not secure;
1 CONT     So great fear of my name 'mongst them was spread,
1 CONT     That they supposed I could rend bars of steel,
1 CONT     And spurn in pieces posts of adamant:
1 CONT     Wherefore a guard of chosen shot I had,
1 CONT     That walked about me every minute-while;
1 CONT     And if I did but stir out of my bed,
1 CONT     Ready they were to shoot me to the heart.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Shakespeare on the death of Lord Talbot from HENRY VI:
1 CONT     "But where's the great Alcides of the field, Valiant Lord Talbot, Earl of Shrewsbury, Created, for his rare success in arms, Great Earl of Washford, Waterford and Valence; Lord Talbot of Goodrig and Urchinfield, Lord Strange o
1 CONC f Blackmere, Lord Verdun of Alton, Lord Cromwell of Wingfield, Lord Furnival of Sheffield, The thrice-victorious Lord of Falconbridge; Knight of the noble order of Saint George, Worthy Saint Michael and the Golden Fleece; Great ma
1 CONC rshal to Henry the Sixth, Of all his wars within the realm of France?" [Lucy, Act IV, Scene VII]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     Historical figures from Shakespeare's HENRY VI, PART I who appear in this database include:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Henry VI, King of England (1421-1471)
1 CONT     Margaret of Anjou, Queen of England (1429-1482)
1 CONT     Humphrey Plantagenet Duke of Gloucester (1390-1447)
1 CONT     John Plantagenet Duke of Bedford (1389-1435)
1 CONT     Cardinal Henry Beaufort, Bishop of Winchester (1375-1447)
1 CONT     Thomas Beaufort, Duke of Exeter (c. 1377-1426)
1 CONT     John Beaufort, Duke of Somerset (c. 1404-1444)
1 CONT     Richard Plantagenet, Duke of York (1411-1460)
1 CONT     Sir Richard Neville, Earl of Salisbury (1400-1460)
1 CONT     Richard "Kingmaker" Neville, Earl of Warwick (1428-1471)
1 CONT     William de la Pole, 4th Earl of Suffolk (1396-1450)
1 CONT     Lord John Talbot, Earl of Shrewsbury (1384-1453)
1 CONT     Sir John Talbot (1413-1460)
1 CONT     Edmund Mortimer, 5th Earl of March (1391-1425)
1 CONT     Sir John Fastolf (1377-1459, model for Falstaff)
1 CONT     Charles VII, King of France (1403-1461)
1 CONT     Regnier Duke of Anjou (1408-1480)
1 CONT
1 CONT     Interesting fact: Lord John's mother and mother-in-law died on the very same day.
1 CONT
