0 @N6000000001783323490@ NOTE 2nd Baron of Stafford
1 CONT
1 CONT He served in the military between 1330 and 1351 in Renowned Captain in French Wars of Edward III.(940) He served as Member of Parliament between 1337 and 1350. He died on 31 Aug 1372 in Turnbridge Castle, Kent, England. (95)(941) 
1 CONC (942) He was buried after 31 Aug 1372 in Priory of Turnbridge, Kent, England.(943) (944) He was married to Katherine HASTANG in 1327. (945)(321)
1 CONT
1 CONT 2nd Baron Stafford, was born in 1299. He was summoned to parliament, from January 1337, to November 1350. He gained majority in the 17th year of King Edward II., and then doing his homage, had livery of his father's lands, and the
1 CONC  next ensuing year, being made a knight "by bathing, and other sacred ceremonies, had robes, etc., as a banneret, allowed out of the king's wardrobe for the solemnity;" after which he soon became a personage of celebrity in the wa
1 CONC rs of King Edward III. He was engaged in Scotland for some years, and then he embarked for Brittany, where he was made prisoner at the siege of Nantes. In the 19th year of King Edward III., he was sent into Gascony, with Henry of 
1 CONC Lancaster, Earl of Derby, commanding the forces that attacked Bergerath by sea. The next year he was constituted Seneschal of Aquitaine, when John, son and heir of Philip, King of France, coming to besiege Aguillon, where his lord
1 CONC ship resided, he stoutly defended that palace against the whole power of the French, until King Edward came to his relief, and forced the enemy to raise the siege. After this, joining his troops with the army of King Edward III., 
1 CONC he had a principal command in the van of the English army at the battle of Cressy. When Calais surrendered, he was appointed with Sir Walter Manny, and the Earl of Warwick, to take possession of that place for the king, and subseq
1 CONC uently he was one of the ambassadors deputed to the cardinals of Naples and Claremont, to treat of peace between King Edward and Philip de Valois, then assuming the title of King of France. The next year he had license to make cas
1 CONC tles of his manor houses at Stafford and Nadeley. About this time he was elected a Knight of the Garter, being one of the original members of that order. In the 24th year of the reign of King Edward III., he was in commission with
1 CONC  the bishop of Durham, and the Lords Percy and Nevill, to treat with the nobles of Scotland, at York, for a final peace between the two realms; for all which eminent services, he was created on March 5, 1351, Earl of Stafford, and
1 CONC  constituted lieutenant and captain-general of the Duchy of Aquitaine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ralph de Stafford died August 31, 1372; and was buried at Tunbridge, and was succeeded by his only surviving son, Hugh. (http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~pmcbride/james/f041.htm#I1556X2)
