1 CONT Sir Herbert Maxwell, A History of Dumfries and Galloway (Edinburgh,1896), pp. 47-48: One of the boyhood companions of David I (taken toEngland as a boy by his sister Matilda when she married Henry I) was"Fergus, of the line of Gal
1 CONC loway princes or native rulers. . . Hemarried Elizabeth, natural daughter of Henry I, and sister of Sibilla,who married Alexander I, brother of David."
1 CONT
1 CONT E. William Robertson, Scotland under her Early Kings (Edinburgh,1862), pp. 356-357: "At some remote era the Lord of Galloway becamedependant upon the king of Scotland, and Fergus, the first knownprince of the province, was an atte
1 CONC ndant on certain state occasions atthe royal court, whilst he acknowledged the superiority of hiscontemporary David by the payment of a certain tribute in time ofpeace, and by a contingent of turbulent soldiery in war; resembling,
1 CONC in other respects, an ally rather than a vassal, and enjoying aconsiderable degeee of independence within his hereditary dominions.He married Elizabeth, a natural daughter of Henry the First, andAfreca, his daughter by this union,
1 CONC  became the wife of Olave and themother of Godfrey, kings of Man and the Isles. [note: _Chron. St.Crucis_ 1160. The names of Fergus and his son, Uchtred, occur amongstthe witnesses to the grant of Perdeyc on the 7th July 1136. Reg
1 CONC .Glasg., No. 3, 7."
1 CONT
1 CONT K.J. Stringer, ed. Essays of the Nobility of Medieval Scotland(Edinburgh, 1985), p. 49, cites "G.W.S. Barrow, Bruce, p. 36, n. 2.Fergus of Galloway married an illegitimate daughter of Henry I." . . ."Roland's father Uhtred married
1 CONC  Gunnilda daughter of Waltheof, lord ofAllerdale in Cumberland."
1 CONT
1 CONT John Philip Wood, Peerage of Scotland (Edinburgh, 1813), vol. 1, pp.612-13: "Uchtred, youngest son, divided with Gilbert the extensiveinheritance of Fergus. They led their forces to the army of KingWilliam the Lion, when he invade
1 CONC d Northumberland in 1174." The nativesof Galloway rebelled. Gilbert and Uchtred gave homage to Henry II ofEngland. Gilbert's son Malcolm murdered Uchtred 22 September 1174, andwas resisted by Roland."
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir Herbert Maxwell, A History of Dumfries and Galloway (Edinburgh,1896), p. 54: "Fergus was succeeded in the lordship of Galloway by hisson Uchtred. . . . His brother Gilbert, having first torn out his eyesand brutally mutilated 
1 CONC him, put him to death." Blinding and castrationwas used in Celtic times to make a man ineligible for the kingship;Gilbert apparently thought himself a monarch and wanted his brotherout of the way.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robin Frame, Political Development of the British Isles 1100-1400(Oxford, 1990), p. 42: "During the 1180s the king [William the Lion]struck up an alliance with Lachlan (or 'Roland'), lord of Galloway,who had married into the Anglo
1 CONC -Norman family of Moreville, and whosechange of name neatly captures the interaction that was beginningbetween the native and foreign cultures. When in 1187 William wasagain faced by a northern uprising, it was Roland who captured
1 CONC  itsleader, Donald mac William, a distant kinsman of the king". p. 59:"Roland of Galloway's marriage to the Moreville heiress expanded thefamily's interests."
1 CONT
1 CONT John Philip Wood, Peerage of Scotland (Edinburgh, 1813), vol. 1, pp.612-13: "Roland, Lord of Galloway, the son of Uchtred. On the death ofhis uncle, Gilbert, in 1185, Roland rose in arms, and possessedhimself of all of Galloway." 
1 CONC Henry II threatened to invade in 1186;Roland agreed to swear fealty, give his three sons as hostages, andkeep Uchtred's lands. Gilbert's son Duncan got Carrick. "Rolandgreatly increased his lands by marrying Eva, Ela, or Helena, d
1 CONC aughterof Richard de Moreville, Constable of Scotland, who died 1196. Rolandinherited the office of Constable. Issue: 1. Alan. 2. Thomas, Earl ofAtholl. 3. ---, hostage in 1186. Daughter Ada married Sir WalterBisset."
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir Herbert Maxwell, _A History of Dumfries and Galloway_ (Edinburgh,1896), p. 56: "On the death of the cruel Gilbert in 1185, Roland, sonof Uchtred, claimed the lordship of Galloway. . . . Roland, the fatherof Alan and Thomas, ob
1 CONC tained extensive estates in the shires ofNorthampton, Huntingdon, and Bedford, in right of his wife, Elena deMoreville (Joseph Bain, "Calendar of Documents Relating to Scotland",vol. i, p. 47)."
1 CONT
1 CONT G.W.S. Barrow, _The Anglo-Norman Era in Scottish History_ (Oxford,1980), p. 17: "In 1200 Lachlan, alias Roland, son of Uhtred lord ofGalloway, remembered . . . that his wife Helen de Morville, heir ofher father Richard and of her 
1 CONC grandmother Beatrice de Beauchamp, wasentitled to four knights' fees respectively at Bozeat, Northants,Whissendine and Whitwell in Rutland, Offord in Huntingdonshire, andHoughton Conquest beside Bedford--the 5 hides at Houghton ha
1 CONC ving beenoriginally acquired by Hugh de Beauchamp, Beatrice's grandfather,probably not long before 1086."
1 CONT
1 CONT K.J. Stringer, Earl David of Huntingdon (1985), p. 146, says Roland'swidow Helena made gifts to St. Andrews Priory, Northampton, when herhusband was buried there after his sudden death in 1200.
1 CONT
1 CONT K.J. Stringer, ed. Essays of the Nobility of Medieval Scotland(Edinburgh, 1985): Roland died at Northampton a few days short ofChristmas 1200, is buried at the Priory of St. Andrew there.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Philip Wood, Peerage of Scotland (Edinburgh, 1813), vol. 1, pp.612-13: "Alan, Lord of Galloway, died 1233, buried at Dundrennan. Byhis first wife, whose name is not known, he had a daughter Helen,married to Roger de Quincy [d
1 CONC . 28 April 1264]. . . He married secondlyat Dundee in1209 Margaret, eldest daughter of David, Earl ofHuntingdon, brother of Malcolm IV and William the Lion, by whom he hadtwo daughters: 1. Dervegulde. 2. Christian, died without is
1 CONC sue 1246.He married thirdly in 1228 a daughter of Hugh de Lacy, without issue."
1 CONT
1 CONT K.J. Stringer, ed. Essays of the Nobility of Medieval Scotland(Edinburgh, 1985), p. 49, says Alan married a sister of John de Lacy,Constable of Chester and future Earl of Lincoln, Margaret, daughter ofEarl David of Huntingdon, and
1 CONC  Rose, daughter of Hugh de Lacy, Earl ofUlster. He does not say in that article why he thinks the first wifewas a sister of John de Lacy; he published an article called "A NewWife for Alan of Galloway"
1 CONT
1 CONT Robin Frame, Political Development of the British Isles 1100-1400(Oxford, 1990), p. 59: "Alan held lands in Lothian, Cumbria (fromwhere he imported tenants), Yorkshire, and the English east midlands;he also gained a title to large
1 CONC  areas of eastern Ulster from KingJohn, who was almost as much his lord as was William the Lion." p.104: "Galloway . . . lost its regal status only slowly. Its ruler,Fergus, who died in 1161, was frequently called princeps, and on
1 CONC cerex; he may, like Alexander I, have married an illegitimate daughterof Henry I. His successors abandoned the royal styles while retainingsome regal attributes. . . .Alan son of Roland had an ease ofmanoeuvre and a range of conta
1 CONC cts on either side of the Solway and theIrish Sea that marked him out as more than a baron of the king ofScots. It was only Alexander II's intervention upon his death in 1234,to exclude his bastard son and partition his lands betw
1 CONC een hisdaughters, that put a final end to Galloway's royal status; even thenits identity survived in a distinctive law-code, whose tolerance ofblood feud was offensive in the eyes of a conventional monarchy."
1 CONT
