0 @N6000000000437138988@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Lord of Galloway
0 @N6000000002187738747@ NOTE Uchtred of Galloway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Uchtred mac Fergusa (c. 1120 - September 22, 1174) was Lord of Galloway from 1161 to 1174, ruling jointly with his half-brother Gilla Brigte of Galloway. They were sons of Fergus of Galloway; their mothers' names are unknown, but 
1 CONC Uchtred may have been born to one of the many illegitimate daughters of Henry I of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a boy he was sent as a hostage to the court of King Máel Coluim IV of Scotland. When his father, Prince Fergus, died in 1161, Uchtred was made co-ruler of Galloway along with Gilla Brigte. They participated in the disastrous in
1 CONC vasion of Northumberland under William I of Scotland in 1174. King William was captured, and the Galwegians rebelled, taking the opportunity to slaughter the Norman and Saxon settlers in their land. During this time Uchtred was br
1 CONC utally mutilated, blinded, castrated, and killed by his brother Gilla Brigte and Gilla Brigte's son, Máel Coluim. Gilla Brigte then seized control of Galloway entire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Uchtred had married Gunhilda of Dunbar, and they were the parents of Lochlann and Eve of Galloway, wife of Walter de Berkeley.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Taylor, James. The Pictorial History of Scotland, 1859 
1 CONT John of Fordun (chronicler) 
0 @N6000000002315764532@ NOTE Lords of Galloway From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In 1174 Uchtred died after being brutally blinded and mutilated by his brother Gilbert and Gilbert's son, Malcolm. 
1 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------  ----------------------------- Uchtred of Galloway From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Uchtred mac Fergusa (c. 1120 - September 22, 1174) was Lord of Galloway from 1161 to 1174, ruling jointly with his brother Gilla Brigte of Galloway. They were sons of Fergus of Galloway; their mother's name is unknown, but she may
1 CONC  have been one of the many illegitimate children of King Henry I of England. 
1 CONT
1 CONT As a boy he was sent as a hostage to the court of King Máel Coluim IV of Scotland. When his father, Prince Fergus, died in 1161, Uchtred was made co-ruler of Galloway along with Gilla Brigte. They participated in King William I of
1 CONC  Scotland's disastrous invasion of Northumberland in 1174. King William was captured, and the Galwegians rebelled, taking the opportunity to slaughter the Norman and Saxon settlers in their land. During this time Uchtred was bruta
1 CONC lly mutilated, blinded, castrated, and killed by his brother Gilla Brigte and Gilla Brigte's son, Máel Coluim. Gilla Brigte then seized control of Galloway entire. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Uchtred had married Gunhilda of Dunbar, and they were the parents of Lochlann and Eve of Galloway, wife of Walter de Berkeley. [edit] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Taylor, James. The Pictorial History of Scotland, 1859
1 CONT     * John of Fordun (chronicler)
1 CONT     * Roger of Hoveden (chronicler)
0 @N6000000002315839517@ NOTE Lords of Galloway From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In 1174 Uchtred died after being brutally blinded and mutilated by his brother Gilbert and Gilbert's son, Malcolm. 
1 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------  ----------------------------- Uchtred of Galloway From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Uchtred mac Fergusa (c. 1120 - September 22, 1174) was Lord of Galloway from 1161 to 1174, ruling jointly with his brother Gilla Brigte of Galloway. They were sons of Fergus of Galloway; their mother's name is unknown, but she may
1 CONC  have been one of the many illegitimate children of King Henry I of England. 
1 CONT
1 CONT As a boy he was sent as a hostage to the court of King Máel Coluim IV of Scotland. When his father, Prince Fergus, died in 1161, Uchtred was made co-ruler of Galloway along with Gilla Brigte. They participated in King William I of
1 CONC  Scotland's disastrous invasion of Northumberland in 1174. King William was captured, and the Galwegians rebelled, taking the opportunity to slaughter the Norman and Saxon settlers in their land. During this time Uchtred was bruta
1 CONC lly mutilated, blinded, castrated, and killed by his brother Gilla Brigte and Gilla Brigte's son, Máel Coluim. Gilla Brigte then seized control of Galloway entire. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Uchtred had married Gunhilda of Dunbar, and they were the parents of Lochlann and Eve of Galloway, wife of Walter de Berkeley. [edit] 
