0 @N6000000002188616542@ NOTE Hugh I de Audley
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh de Audley (ca. 1250 – ca. 1336) was a member of the Audley-Stanley family and the father of Hugh de Audley, 1st Earl of Gloucester.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Lineage
1 CONT     * 2 Family
1 CONT     * 3 see also
1 CONT     * 4 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Lineage
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born in Audley in the English County of Staffordshire, the son of James of Aldithley (born c. 1225 in Audley, Staffordshire) and Ela Longspee (daughter of William II Longespee, and his great great grandfather was therefore 
1 CONC Henry II, King of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Isolda de Mortimer, the daughter of Hugh de Mortimer, grandson of Llywelyn the Great by whom he had a daughter, Alice de Audley (born c. 1304 in England), who married Ralph Fitzrobert de Greystoke and later, in 1326, Si
1 CONC r Ralph de Neville. He also had two sons, John and Hugh, who became 1st Earl of Gloucester.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * John de Aldithley (Audley) born circa 1293
1 CONT     * Hugh de Audley, 1st Earl of Gloucester born 1289 who married Margaret de Clare
1 CONT     * Alice de Audley born circa 1304 who married firstly Robert Fitzrobert de Greystoke and later, Ralph de Neville a member of the Neville family.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] see also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Audley-Stanley family
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * de Audley family genealogy
1 CONT     * de Audley family genealogy at RenderPlus.com
0 @N6000000003051155871@ NOTE [Burke 17] Hugh de Alditheley or Audley, brother it is presumed of Nicholas, Lord Audley of Heleigh, was summoned to parliament as "Hugh de Audley, Seniori" on 15 May, 1321, 14th Edward II.  His lordship had been engaged during th
1 CONC e reign of Edward I in the king's service, and was called "Senior," to distinguish him from his son.  Being concerned in the insurrection of Thomas, Earl of Lancaster, 15th Edward II., the baron was committed a close prisoner to W
1 CONC allingford Castle, but making his peace with the king he obtained his release, and suffered nothing further.  His lordship sat in the parliament of the 11th and 14th of Edward II.  He m. Isolda, widow of Walter Balim, and left two
1 CONC  sons, by the elder of whom he was succeeded.
0 @N6000000003949845766@ NOTE Hugh de Alditheley or Audley, brother of Nicholas, Lord Audley of Heleigh, was summoned to parliament as "Hugh de Audley, Seniori" on 15 May, 1321, 14th Edward II. His lordship had been engaged during the reign of Edward I in the 
1 CONC king's service and was called "Senior" to distinguish him from his son. Being concerned in the insurrection of Thomas, Earl of Lancaster, 15th Edward II [1322], the baron was committed a close prisoner to Wallingford Castle but ma
1 CONC king his peace with the king he obtained his release and suffered nothing further. His lordship sat in the parliament on the 11th [1318] and 14th [1321] of Edward II. He m. Isolda, widow of Walter Balim, and left two sons, by the 
1 CONC elder of whom, Hugh, he was succeeded. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 17, Audley, or de Aldithley, Barons Audley, and Subsequently Earl of Gloucester]
