1 CONC ed the favour of King Edward I, in whose Welsh wars he took a very active part. He d. in 1287,
0 @N6000000003949866201@ NOTE son and heir. In 1260 he was staying beyond the seas. In April 1264, he was captured at Northampton and imprisoned for a short time in Windsor Castle with his father and brother Robert. In June he had custody of Rockingham Castle 
1 CONC for a few days; and it was probably he, and not his father, who was forbidden to take part in a tournament at Dunstable in February 1264/5. He fought at Evesham for Simon de Montfort, 4 August, and was wounded and taken prisoner. 
1 CONC Having "appeased the King's indignation and rancour of mind," he was on 28 June 1267 pardoned for all trespasses at the time of the disturbance in the kingdom, and he recovered part of his father's lands. In 1268 he gave the advow
1 CONC son of Ponteland, Northumberland, which his father had bought, to his friend Walter de Merton for his newly founded college at Oxford; and in this year a grant of £50 per annum was renewed to him. In February 1271/2 he had protect
1 CONC ion for going on a pilgrimage to Santiago, and he went again in January 1274/5. In Nov. 1276 he was one of the magnates at a Council at Westminster concerned with Llewelyn and Welsh affairs, and in February following was going to 
1 CONC Wales in the King's service. He was going beyond the seas in January 1277/8, and again in 1281. He gave the manor of Greetham to the Bishop of Durham for a hospital for the sick and poor. In 1280 he granted to Queen Eleanor the ma
1 CONC rriage of his eldest son John. A market and fair at Uppingham were granted to him in June 1281. In April 1282 he was summoned to serve in person against the Welsh.
1 CONT He participated in his father's treasons and was taken prisoner at the battle of Evesham, but being allowed the benefit of the Dictum of Kenilworth, he was restored to his paternal inheritance -- and afterwards enjoyed the favour 
1 CONC of King Edward I, in whose Welsh wars he took a very active part. He d. in 1287, leaving a dau. Elizabeth (who m. 1st, William, son and heir of Simon de Montacute, and 2ndly, Sir Thomas de Furnival), and a son and heir, John de Mo
1 CONC ntfort, who was the next year summoned to parliament as a baron. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage Ltd, London, England, 1883, p. 377, Montfort, Barons Montfort]
0 @N6000000000435987505@ NOTE Chief Butler of Ireland
0 @N6000000000437030051@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> III (Knight)
0 @N6000000000437216961@ NOTE According to John O'Hart (Irish Pedigrees, p. 242) "The ancestors of the Butlers came from Normandy to England with William the Conqueror. Their original namewas Fitz-Walter, from Walter one of their ancestors; and Theobald Fitz-W
1 CONC altercame to Ireland with Henry the Second in 1172 and had the office of Chief Butler of Ireland conferred on him, the duty attached to which was to attend at thecoronation of the kings of England, and present them with the first 
1 CONC cup of wine. From the office of Butlership of Ireland they took the name of "Butler."
1 CONT
1 CONT In the reign of Edward the Third, Tipperary was formed into the "county palatineof Ormond" under the Butlers, who thus became so powerful that different branches of them furnished many of the most distinguished families in Ireland
1 CONC . "ThePalatine possesssed such royal privileges that he ruled in his Palatinate almost as a king. The Butlers were earls, marquises and dukes of Ormond, and also had the following titles in Tipperary: earls of Carrick, earls of Gl
1 CONC engall, viscounts of Thurles, viscounts of Skerrin and barons of Cahir." "In the reign of Henry VIII, they gained possession of a great part of Carlow" (p. 294). "The county Kilkenny became possessed mostly by the Butlers, earls o
1 CONC f Ormond" (p. 304).They were called earls of Ossory, and Gowvan, viscounts of Galway, and variousother titles derived from their extensive estates." (305 p.) Their war-cry wasButler Aboo! the word Aboo is the old Celtic for Victor
1 CONC y (p. 348).
1 CONT
1 CONT The wordOrmond is said to mean in Irish East Munster. According to "The Norman People"--a London volume of 1874 (p. 182) the Ormond family, through the Irish chief butler, is traceable to a Walter who came over with the Conqueror,
1 CONC  and in 1086 was owner of estates in Lailand, Lancashire. This Walter came from Glanville nearCaen. His arms were a chief indented.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is an inveterate tradition in diverse families of American Butlers that they are descended from collateral branches of the family of James Butler, the Duke of Ormond (1610--1688.) Thus, Dr. Geo. H. Butler of N. Y., writes regar
1 CONC ding his ancestor Thomas Butler born about 1674. "Although it has never been questioned, and there is no doubt of its truth, yet in the absence of the exact date of his birth and of parish records, positive proof of his descent fr
1 CONC om the house of Ormond,...|||
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT FROM:  The Family of Rev John Butler page 5
1 CONT
1 CONT ORIGIN OF THE NAME.
1 CONT
1 CONT ALTHOUGH the English generation of Rev. John Butler has not been traced, the following mention of the origin of the name of Butler by the present Marquise de Fontenoy, to be found ina magazine "The House Beautiful," Chicago, Febru
1 CONC ary 1907, is thought to be ofinterest.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Theobald Walter, Lord Ormonde, of Kilkenny Castle, Ireland, a brother of Hubert Walter, Archbishop of Canterbury, in the time of Richard Coeur de Lion, first assumed the surname of Butler after being invested with the hereditary 
1 CONC dignity of Chief Butler of Ireland by King Henry II."
1 CONT
1 CONT Respecting the antiquity of the name, the following bit of historical romance is cherished as being worth preserving.
1 CONT
1 CONT "The two leading Anglo-Irish families in Ireland havelong been the Fitz-Geralds and the Butlers. From being comrades in arms of theinvading Strongbow, they became by degrees rival barons, and fierce contestants for the vice-sovere
1 CONC ignty of their adopted country. In the Wars of the Roses,the Butlers sided with the white rose of Lancaster, and the Fitz-Geralds with the red rose of York. Factions gathered around the two great houses, and the bitter feud brough
1 CONC t forth death and bloodshed from as early as 1250 down to the Williamite Wars. The Butlers, whose chief had attained the dignity of Earl of Ormonde, succeeded in crushing the power of the elder branch of the Fitz-Geralds,Earls of 
1 CONC Desmond. It is told of a warlike Desmond that, while he was being borne prisoner on the locked shields of his feudal foe's clansmen, the Butlers taunted him with the bitter words: 'Where is now the
0 @N6000000001353959572@ NOTE Fulke Fitz-Warine had a castle at Adderbury, the ruins of which were remaining at the time Dugdale wrote. This Fulke was left by King Richard I to defend the Marches of Wales when that monarch set out himself for the Holy Land; an
1 CONC d in the 7th of the same reign [1196], he paid 40marks to the crown for livery of Whittington Castle, in conformity with the judgment then given in his favour by the Court of King's Bench.After the accession of John in 1199, howev
1 CONC er, this castle was forcibly seized by the crown and conferred upon another person, which act ofinjustice drove Fitzwarine and his brothers into rebellion and they were, in consequence, outlawed; but through the mediation of the E
1 CONC arl of Salisbury (the king's brother) and the bishop of Norwich, the outlawry was reversed and FitzWarine, upon paying 200 marks and two coursers, had livery of the castle as his hereditary right, command being given to the sherif
1 CONC f of Shropshire to yield him possession thereof accordingly. About this time he paid tot he crown 1,200 marks and two palfreys for permission to marry Maud., dau. of Robert Vavasour, and widow of Theobald Walter.
1 CONT In the 12th John [1211], he attended that prince into Ireland, and inthe 17th he had livery of his wife's inheritance lying in Amundernesse, in Lancashire. After this we find him active in the baronial cause and amongst those exco
1 CONC mmunicated by the Pope; nor did he make his peace until the 4th of Henry III [1220], when he compromised by paying £262 and two great coursers for the repossession of Whittington Castle, which, in the baronial conflict, had again 
1 CONC been alienated. Whereupon undertaking that it should not be prejudicial to the king, he had licence the next year to fortify the same; and he thenceforward evinced hisloyalty by the good services he rendered against the Welsh unde
1 CONC r William Marshal, Earl of Pembroke, and by his personal attendance upon theking himself in his army at Montgomery. He had subsequently military summonses upon several occasions and fought at the battle of Lewes, anno 1263, under 
1 CONC the royal banner, in which action he lost his life by being drowned in the adjacent river.
1 CONT This celebrated lord m. 1st, as already stated, Maud, dau. of Robert Vavasour, and 2ndly, Clarice -----. He left at his decease a dau. Eve who became 2nd consort of Llewelyn the great Prince of North Wales ap Iorwerth Drwyndun, an
1 CONC d a son, his successor, Fulke Fitz-Warine. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 213, Fitz-Warine, Barons Fitz-Warine]
1 CONT NOTE: Burke has compacted two generations here, as it was his son whod. at the Battle of Lewes. If the father had died at the Battle of Lewes, he would have been near 100 years of age. It is also my opinion that the second marriag
1 CONC e to Clarice referred to above is actually the marriage of his son to Constance de Toni.
