0 @N6000000003949658264@ NOTE John III old enough to attend on King John in 1213 and to undertake military service against the French 1214; granted by Henry III the manor of Wrockwardine, Salop 25 May 1231; played a part in negotiations between Henry III and P
1 CONC rince Llywelyn The Great 1232; Constable of Montgomery Castle 1235 and Bridgnorth, Chester and Shrewsbury Castles 1233-40; Sheriff of Salop and Staffs 1236-48 and Cheshire 1241-2, Justice of Chester 1240 or 41; supported Henry III
1 CONC  against Simon de Montfort (unlike? his son);  and died by 26 March 1269. [Burke's Peerage]  He was a person of great note in his time. In 16th of King John, his father being then living, he was in the Wars of Poitou in France. In
1 CONC  15th of Henry III, 1231, he obtained a grant of the inheritance of Wockwardine for the yearly rent of œ8, to be paid to the King and his heirs and successors. In 21st of Henry III, 1237, he was appointed Sheriff of Salop and Staf
1 CONC ford and constituted Governor of the Castles of Salop and Brugges. He was afterwards one of the Baron's Marchers and had command to reside in the Marshes in order to resist incursions by the Welsh. In the contest between Henry III
1 CONC  and the Barons he adhered with great fidelity to the King and obtained for his loyalty a grant of the lands of Walter de Muscegros, which had been forfeited in that rebellion.  Lord of Knockyn 
0 @N6000000004533255020@ NOTE LE Strange, John ( d. 1269 )
1 CONT
1 CONT Lord Marcher
1 CONT
1 CONT Third in desent from Roland le Strange ( who occurs as a witness in1112 ), held extensive estates in Shropshire and Norfolk , includingKnokyn in the former , and Hunstanton in the latter county. He servedin 1214 under King John in
1 CONC  Poitou , and between 1233 and 1240 wassuccessively appointed by Henry iii constable of the castles ofMontgomery , Shrewsbury , Bridgnorth , and Chester. He spent a longlife in defending the Welsh border as a lord marcher, and dur
1 CONC ing therebellion of Simon de Montfort stood fast to the crown. One of hisdaughters, Hawise , married Griffin ,prince of Powys; his younger son, Hamon , accompanied Prince Edward on the crusade of 1270, and in1272 married at Cyprus
1 CONC  Isabella d'Ibelin , widow of Hugh ii , King ofCyprus. Another son , Robert, was ancestor of the Lords Strange ofBlackmere. John le Strange died in 1269, and was succeeded by his sonJohn ( d 1275 ) , who by his marriage with Joan 
1 CONC de Someri , coheiressof Hugh de Albini , earl of Arundel ,added much to the influence andwealth of his family. His grandson, John, sixth baron of Knokyn ( d .1311 ), gave in 1310 his manor of Hunstanton to his younger brother ,Ham
1 CONC on, from whom the Norfolk Le Stranges come in direct descent.
1 CONT
1 CONT    ( Eyton's History of Shropshire, x. 259; Carthew's History ofLaunditch, i, 139 ; Assises de Jerusalem , ii.449, Paris, 1843;Historiens des Croisades, ii. 462. )
